    <node id="primary sources">
      <data key="d0">primary sources</data>
      <data key="d1">source_or_artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">Primary sources are original documents or materials that provide direct evidence about an individual event, object, person, place, or work of art. They can include diaries, speeches, manuscripts, letters, interviews, news film footage, autobiographies, and official records.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cabf9508dda25121a8ef708c836ab925</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210785</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Late Antique Period">
      <data key="d0">Late Antique Period</data>
      <data key="d1">time_period</data>
      <data key="d2">The Late Antique Period is a pivotal phase during which significant cultural, religious, and socio-political transformations took place in the Mediterranean world, marking a transition from Antiquity to the Middle Ages. This period notably includes the construction of the Hagia Sophia under the Byzantine Emperor Justinian I.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-50e25b5ca5c2528a7011ec84bc476f76&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-17ddaf0fb532418dfd517db22df102e9</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210791</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Justinian I">
      <data key="d0">Justinian I</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">Justinian I, an Eastern Roman emperor who reigned from 527 to 565 CE and was a devoted Christian, is a significant historical figure. During his rule, he is credited with constructing the Hagia Sophia in Constantinople, which became an important architectural landmark. Justinian's contributions extended beyond architecture; he also oversaw the drafting of laws addressing religious matters during his reign from 483 to 565 CE.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-622311a2741b1845b3a8c00c6e5d7b93&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50e25b5ca5c2528a7011ec84bc476f76&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-72d7bb40fa0aa36bf5b0bb66bf7975fe</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210794</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Iconography">
      <data key="d0">Iconography</data>
      <data key="d1">cultural_or_religious_movement</data>
      <data key="d2">Iconography refers to the use of symbolic images in religious art, which served as means of communication and motivation for religious community participation in the Byzantine period.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-50e25b5ca5c2528a7011ec84bc476f76</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210786</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Engineering Feats">
      <data key="d0">Engineering Feats</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">Engineering feats such as the construction of Hagia Sophia represent significant achievements and challenges faced during the late antique period.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-50e25b5ca5c2528a7011ec84bc476f76</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210786</data>
