    <node id="Interior of Hagia Sophia">
      <data key="d0">Interior of Hagia Sophia</data>
      <data key="d1">textbook_structure</data>
      <data key="d2">Interior of Hagia Sophia includes halos on figures and gold mosaics depicting Mary, Jesus, Justinian, and Constantine, highlighting Greek Christian iconography.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-aef10b168281ffac76d2b994317417e1</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210786</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hagia Sophia's Minarets">
      <data key="d0">Hagia Sophia's Minarets</data>
      <data key="d1">source_or_artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">Minarets were added to Hagia Sophia a thousand years after its initial construction. They are used for calling Muslims to prayer multiple times a day.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8060ed2198a5e2356c685f6f1ab343d6</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210786</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hagia Sophia's Many Influences">
      <data key="d0">Hagia Sophia's Many Influences</data>
      <data key="d1">source_or_artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">The building showcases influences from Judaism, Christianity, and Islam, with visible Greek iconography until recent renovations which replaced it with Arabic writing during prayer times.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8060ed2198a5e2356c685f6f1ab343d6</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210786</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Competing Narratives">
      <data key="d0">Competing Narratives</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">Competing narratives refer to different historical accounts or perspectives on events. This concept is central in evaluating how various groups might view and interpret Hagia Sophia's history and significance, especially noting the cultural transformations over time.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8060ed2198a5e2356c685f6f1ab343d6</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210786</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Crusades">
      <data key="d0">Crusades</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">The Crusades were a series of religious wars spanning the 11th to the 13th centuries, primarily focused on regaining control over Jerusalem and other holy lands from Muslim rule. These conflicts were marked by internal factionalism and intolerance among crusaders themselves. They persisted in the European imagination for much of the Middle Ages and shaped both historical and modern thought and geopolitics, with their legacies continuing to influence contemporary discourse. Although these wars began after the Abbasid period, they are primarily defined by their specific historical context characterized by religious and ideological motivations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a3cce5ba44faddd8f9fd27e8b4f71bef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c13c9d68535c84e9a2084524eeed3c86&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b150c3e9d15dec1eaca94b8168bda0ba&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8d439bb45b85431dc5d851d56b5b4932</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210784</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hagia Sophia (Istanbul)">
      <data key="d0">Hagia Sophia (Istanbul)</data>
      <data key="d1">geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">The iconic Byzantine church converted into a mosque, then back to being an Orthodox cathedral, before becoming a museum and finally a UNESCO World Heritage Site in Istanbul, Turkey.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8060ed2198a5e2356c685f6f1ab343d6</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210786</data>
    </node>
