      <data key="d0">Hagia Sophia Istanbul 2013" credit "Karelj/Wikimedia Commons</data>
      <data key="d1">source_or_artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">The source provides an image representing interior and exterior elements of Hagia Sophia in 2013, under the Public Domain license, capturing early Greek iconography and recent renovations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8060ed2198a5e2356c685f6f1ab343d6</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210786</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Authorship Analysis for Primary Sources">
      <data key="d0">Authorship Analysis for Primary Sources</data>
      <data key="d1">concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">When analyzing primary sources, historians must consider various factors such as author background, importance to the author, and possible motives behind their work. This concept is central in ensuring accurate interpretation of historical texts like those related to Hagia Sophia.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8060ed2198a5e2356c685f6f1ab343d6</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210786</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Textual Primary Sources">
      <data key="d0">Textual Primary Sources</data>
      <data key="d1">source_or_artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">These are written documents that can provide rich context and information for historians, as seen in the sources about Hagia Sofia’s history. They need careful evaluation to interpret accurately.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8060ed2198a5e2356c685f6f1ab343d6</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210786</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Evaluation of Historians' Methods">
      <data key="d0">Evaluation of Historians' Methods</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">Historians use methodical approaches to evaluate primary sources, assessing aspects like authorship and context, which is crucial for understanding the complex narrative of Hagia Sophia's transformations over time.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8060ed2198a5e2356c685f6f1ab343d6</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210786</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Hernán Cortés&quot;&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Hernán Cortés"&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;"historical_figure"&gt;</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;"Hernán Cortés was a Spanish conquistador who led an expedition to conquer the Aztec Empire. He played a critical role in the Spanish conquest of Tenochtitlán and negotiations with Moctezuma II."</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7ed06ed72d462e213997f13f347cff93</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210786</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Tenochtitlán&quot;&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Tenochtitlán"&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;"geographical_location"&gt;</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;"Tenochtitlán was the capital city of the Aztec Empire, located on an island in Lake Texcoco. It was renowned for its grandeur and impressive architecture."</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7ed06ed72d462e213997f13f347cff93</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210786</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Aztec Empire&quot;&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Aztec Empire"&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;"civilization_or_empire"&gt;</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;"The Aztec Empire was a powerful Mesoamerican civilization led by Moctezuma II until the Spanish conquest. It was known for its advanced society, rich culture, and grand ceremonies such as human sacrifices."</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7ed06ed72d462e213997f13f347cff93</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210786</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Spanish Conquest of the Aztec Empire&quot;&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Spanish Conquest of the Aztec Empire"&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;"historical_concept_or_event"&gt;</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;"The Spanish conquest of the Aztec Empire refers to the events that led to the fall of Tenochtitlán by Hernán Cortés and his forces, significantly altering the course of history in Central Mexico."</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7ed06ed72d462e213997f13f347cff93</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210786</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Massacre in the Great Temple&quot;&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Massacre in the Great Temple"&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;"historical_event"&gt;</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;"The Massacre in the Great Temple was a significant event during the Spanish conquest when Spanish soldiers killed Aztec warriors within the sacred temple premises, leading to a brutal loss of lives and devastation."</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7ed06ed72d462e213997f13f347cff93</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210786</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Charles V (King of Spain)&quot;&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Charles V (King of Spain)"&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;"historical_figure"&gt;</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;"Charles V was the King of Spain who received correspondence from Hernán Cortés regarding the Spanish conquest of the Aztec Empire. He played a significant role in the governance and expansion of the Spanish empire."</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7ed06ed72d462e213997f13f347cff93</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210786</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Moctezuma II (Aztec ruler)&quot;&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Moctezuma II (Aztec ruler)"&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;"historical_figure"&gt;</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;"Moctezuma II was the last Aztec ruler who negotiated with Hernán Cortés but ultimately faced the destruction of his city and death by Spanish forces."</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7ed06ed72d462e213997f13f347cff93</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210786</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Aztec religious practices, Huitzilopochtli worship&quot;&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Aztec religious practices, Huitzilopochtli worship"&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;"cultural_or_religious_movement"&gt;</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;"The Aztecs practiced complex religious rituals including human sacrifices to Huitzilopochtli, the warrior god. These practices were an integral part of their culture and political structure."</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7ed06ed72d462e213997f13f347cff93</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210786</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Letters from Cortés to Charles V&quot;&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Letters from Cortés to Charles V"&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;"source_or_artifact"&gt;</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;"Cortés wrote detailed letters to King Charles V detailing his actions in Mexico, including the fall of Tenochtitlán and negotiations with Moctezuma II. These documents provide critical historical records."</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7ed06ed72d462e213997f13f347cff93</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210786</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Miguel León-Portilla">
      <data key="d0">Miguel León-Portilla</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">Miguel León-Portilla is a Mexican philosopher and a leading authority on Aztec culture, history, and philosophy. He has authored numerous books that seek to convey the perspective of indigenous peoples during the Spanish conquest.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a90cbba1d3f0a8cc50e947b7f0000a37</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210786</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Roosevelt's Speech">
      <data key="d0">Roosevelt's Speech</data>
      <data key="d1">textual_source</data>
      <data key="d2">The 'Day of Infamy' speech delivered by Franklin D. Roosevelt on December 8, 1941, to the U.S. Congress, declaring war against Japan in response to the attack on Pearl Harbor.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a90cbba1d3f0a8cc50e947b7f0000a37</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210787</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Aztec History and Culture">
      <data key="d0">Aztec History and Culture</data>
      <data key="d1">civilization_or_empire</data>
      <data key="d2">The pre-Columbian civilization of the Aztecs, known for their rich cultural heritage, sophisticated agricultural practices, and advanced urban planning. Their history is significant in understanding the dynamics of conquest and colonialism.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a90cbba1d3f0a8cc50e947b7f0000a37</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210787</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Spanish Conquest">
      <data key="d0">Spanish Conquest</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_event</data>
      <data key="d2">A series of military campaigns by Spain against the Aztec Empire leading to its fall, marking a pivotal point in both native American and European history.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a90cbba1d3f0a8cc50e947b7f0000a37</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
