    <node id="Herodotus">
      <data key="d0">Herodotus</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">Herodotus, a Greek historian, is recognized as the first true historian due to his rational exploration of past events in search of explanations. He is also known as the Father of History and is renowned for his seminal work "The Histories," which provides detailed accounts of early Greek history alongside narratives of various cultures and peoples.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d1b923a222fa396828cfac46f7a026c9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7813b888d8493d9e1452119a8b6e9c14</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210796</data>
    </node>
    <node id="World History Knowledge">
      <data key="d0">World History Knowledge</data>
      <data key="d1">concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">Understanding and knowledge of world history can guide life goals, providing a broader perspective on personal growth and societal impact.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9c630ebd8401e6fe0f370117e96751cf</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210792</data>
    </node>
    <node id="1974">
      <data key="d0">1974</data>
      <data key="d1">time_period</data>
      <data key="d2">1974 marks the year when Donald Johanson and his colleague discovered the skeleton known as Lucy in Ethiopia.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-91a847d29a702803fcc6217de2a69c95</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210792</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ethiopia">
      <data key="d0">Ethiopia</data>
      <data key="d1">geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">Ethiopia, a kingdom that partially emerged through the efforts of missionary Frumentius, is also recognized as the site of important historical and archaeological significance. Notably, it may have been host to the founding of one or more of the first Christian monasteries. Additionally, Ethiopia has played a crucial role in paleoanthropological studies, being the location where significant discoveries of early human fossils were made during mapping expeditions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-91a847d29a702803fcc6217de2a69c95&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5874c3ff9fc6f9f51a9a1072e47f5bed</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210798</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Paleolithic Age">
      <data key="d0">Paleolithic Age</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">The Paleolithic Age, spanning from approximately 2.6 million years ago to around 10,000 BCE, was characterized by the early use of stone tools and represented an era of human history when societies lived in a nomadic lifestyle. This period saw the development of basic tools that were essential for survival and laid the foundational aspects of early human culture and technology.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-91a847d29a702803fcc6217de2a69c95&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5d60f602c690bd1716c66d15ec766306</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210798</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Donald Johanson">
      <data key="d0">Donald Johanson</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">Donald Johanson is the American paleoanthropologist who discovered the skeleton known as Lucy in Ethiopia.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-91a847d29a702803fcc6217de2a69c95</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210794</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Lucy">
      <data key="d0">Lucy</data>
      <data key="d1">source_or_artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">Lucy is the name given to a significant fossil discovery, an almost complete adult skeleton of early human dating back 3.2 million years.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-91a847d29a702803fcc6217de2a69c95</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210794</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Popular Beatles Song 'Lucy in the Sky with Diamonds'">
      <data key="d0">Popular Beatles Song 'Lucy in the Sky with Diamonds'</data>
      <data key="d1">cultural_or_religious_movement</data>
      <data key="d2">The song 'Lucy in the Sky with Diamonds' by the Beatles provided inspiration for naming the fossil Lucy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-91a847d29a702803fcc6217de2a69c95</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210794</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mapping Expedition">
      <data key="d0">Mapping Expedition</data>
      <data key="d1">textbook_structure</data>
      <data key="d2">A mapping expedition is described as part of the historical structure which led to significant discoveries like those of Lucy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-91a847d29a702803fcc6217de2a69c95</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210794</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mongol Empire">
      <data key="d0">Mongol Empire</data>
      <data key="d1">civilization_or_empire</data>
      <data key="d2">The Mongol Empire was an expansive empire that existed from the 13th to the early 14th century, founded by Genghis Khan (also known as Chinggis Khan) and subsequently expanded under his successors. This vast territory covered much of Eurasia and significantly influenced numerous societies and cultures through its extensive military conquests across Asia and Europe. Known for rapid expansion and implementation of various tactics and strategies, the Mongol Empire remained a powerful geopolitical force during its height of power, stretching from Eastern Europe to parts of Southeast Asia. The Mongols' nomadic lifestyle is often compared to that of the Bedouin tribes on the Arabian Peninsula, highlighting their mobile warfare and administration in diverse environments.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c5b0a78bf038c5aff1201d9ae1d7d241&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b48f1c75c48a630bdea282765ade371c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7083273cf1feba343b800d63e994ddde&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3047d60403fb89082b52beb809b2b4c2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4a7bd606960e970df3415af494e95c38</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210789</data>
    </node>
    <node id="2–3 million years ago ">
      <data key="d0">2–3 million years ago </data>
      <data key="d1">time_period</data>
      <data key="d2">2–3 million years ago marks an important period in human evolution when Homo habilis emerged and began to use tools.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-58508d91454500027eb30e21d1d863f7</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210795</data>
    </node>
    <node id="200,000 years ago ">
      <data key="d0">200,000 years ago </data>
      <data key="d1">time_period</data>
      <data key="d2">200,000 years ago is significant for the emergence of Homo sapiens, the modern human species.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-58508d91454500027eb30e21d1d863f7</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210795</data>
    </node>
    <node id="40,000 years ago top ">
      <data key="d0">40,000 years ago top </data>
      <data key="d1">time_period</data>
      <data key="d2">40,000 years ago represents a key period in prehistory when Neanderthals lived alongside early modern humans during the Upper Paleolithic era.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-58508d91454500027eb30e21d1d863f7</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210795</data>
    </node>
    <node id="40,000 years ago bottom ">
      <data key="d0">40,000 years ago bottom </data>
      <data key="d1">time_period</data>
      <data key="d2">40,000 years ago also denotes the time of the Aurignacian culture and the creation of artistic works like Venus figurines.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-58508d91454500027eb30e21d1d863f7</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210795</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Early Human Evolution and Migration ">
      <data key="d0">Early Human Evolution and Migration </data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">The concept of Early Human Evolution and Migration is a critical phase in human history, highlighting our ancestors' transformation from early forms into modern humans through gradual changes over millions of years.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-58508d91454500027eb30e21d1d863f7</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210795</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Paleolithic Age ">
      <data key="d0">Paleolithic Age </data>
      <data key="d1">time_period</data>
      <data key="d2">The Paleolithic Age spans the earliest known periods of human prehistory where key developments in human tool use and technological sophistication occurred.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-58508d91454500027eb30e21d1d863f7</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210795</data>
    </node>
    <node id="2 million years ago">
      <data key="d0">2 million years ago</data>
      <data key="d1">time_period</data>
      <data key="d2">This period marks the emergence of Homo erectus, a species more closely resembling modern humans and evolving from Homo habilis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-135eaddcc02a505fd05af47a63a3f85a</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210795</data>
    </node>
    <node id="East Africa">
      <data key="d0">East Africa</data>
      <data key="d1">geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">East Africa refers to regions along the eastern part of the African continent, including areas such as Kenya, Uganda, and Tanzania. This region holds significance in both prehistoric and religious contexts. Approximately two million years ago, Homo erectus emerged here, marking a pivotal moment in human evolution history. Additionally, East Africa was the birthplace of distinctive forms of monasticism during the first through sixth centuries, contributing to its rich cultural heritage.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0ce3eaaba21272a94462cd9692507262&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-135eaddcc02a505fd05af47a63a3f85a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5874c3ff9fc6f9f51a9a1072e47f5bed</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210801</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Homo habilis">
      <data key="d0">Homo habilis</data>
      <data key="d1">civilization_or_empire</data>
      <data key="d2">Homo habilis is the predecessor to Homo erectus, providing crucial evidence of early human ancestors through remains and replicas.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-135eaddcc02a505fd05af47a63a3f85a</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210796</data>
    </node>
    <node id="evolution">
      <data key="d0">evolution</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">The process by which Homo habilis evolved into Homo erectus is a key historical concept in understanding our species' origins.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-135eaddcc02a505fd05af47a63a3f85a</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210796</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Homo erectus">
      <data key="d0">Homo erectus</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">Homo erectus is an early human species that lived around 1.8 million years ago, with some evidence suggesting they reached Java but no confirmed presence in the Americas. This species represents a crucial step in human evolution, characterized by their upright posture and advanced tool use.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-97ab4cac183886ee18d87b065b2e0ad5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-135eaddcc02a505fd05af47a63a3f85a</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210801</data>
    </node>
    <node id="tool use">
      <data key="d0">tool use</data>
      <data key="d1">cultural_or_religious_movement</data>
      <data key="d2">The development of tool use among Homo erectus is indicative of the advancement in human technology and cultural practices.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-135eaddcc02a505fd05af47a63a3f85a</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
