    <node id="Chinggis Khan">
      <data key="d0">Chinggis Khan</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">Chinggis Khan, also known as Genghis Khan, was a central figure in the formation of the Mongol Empire during the 13th century. He played a pivotal role in both the early development and subsequent expansion of the empire through decisive military campaigns and comprehensive reforms such as the yasa (a body of laws). As the leader of the Mongols, Chinggis Khan successfully united various nomadic tribes under his rule, radically altering world history by transforming their relationships. His military prowess was notable in targeting key territories, as evidenced by his decision to bypass the Delhi Sultanate during an expedition against Khwarazmians.

Known for his comprehensive policies and unifying actions, Chinggis Khan is recognized as a historical figure who played crucial roles as both a unifier and empire builder during the Pax Mongolica era. His leadership was marked by decisive action and military strategies that significantly impacted geopolitical landscapes of Eurasia, ensuring the Mongol Empire's dominance through his comprehensive reforms and treatment policies toward captives and conquered peoples.

Thus, Chinggis Khan is revered for his pivotal roles in expanding the Mongol Empire, uniting nomadic tribes, and profoundly influencing world history through his leadership and military campaigns.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-65567519161cbb48515c5592fd7dfa04&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6804ed94e025d37cfcb352ad735e3ba4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4139a06e78d2c443c8f7fbe8a29ad646&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2820e332617ec1365aa6fbd6cf782fac&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4a7bd606960e970df3415af494e95c38</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210792</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Climate Change">
      <data key="d0">Climate Change</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">Climate change is recognized as a broad factor that significantly impacts weather patterns, contributing to events such as the "Little Ice Age" and severe dearths. Additionally, it has been suggested that vulnerable populations like Neanderthals may have been weakened by these climatic changes, potentially playing a role in their decline.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7bd3827bfd5334056018dfe31cb0eb60&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-121747db1427da4df60e1759acfeb7f9</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210803</data>
    </node>
