    </node>
    <node id="Altamira Cave">
      <data key="d0">Altamira Cave</data>
      <data key="d1">geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">Altamira Cave is a location in Spain where significant examples of Paleolithic art have been found, including detailed animal paintings.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6572665bc6dd9d8dc0ce12ff5b6bd183</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210800</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cave Paintings">
      <data key="d0">Cave Paintings</data>
      <data key="d1">source_or_artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">Cave Paintings are ancient artworks created within caves using natural pigments like charcoal and ochre, often depicting animals.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6572665bc6dd9d8dc0ce12ff5b6bd183</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210800</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Byzantine Empire">
      <data key="d0">Byzantine Empire</data>
      <data key="d1">civilization_or_empire</data>
      <data key="d2">The Byzantine Empire was a continuation of the Eastern Roman Empire, centered around Constantinople (modern-day Istanbul), and played a significant role from the late 4th century until its fall in 1453, facing internal challenges and external pressures including conflicts with Persia, the Seljuk Empire, Arab-Muslim armies, and the Abbasid Caliphate. The empire was known for constructing monumental structures such as Hagia Sophia during the reign of Emperor Justinian I and for its cultural and religious influence. Initially established on Greek foundations after the fall of Rome, it held significant territory in Asia Minor, the Balkans, and parts of Europe, playing an important role during events like the Crusades. The Byzantine Empire was a formidable sea force and utilized Greek fire as a defensive measure against various threats.

Władysław III represented the Byzantine Empire in his opposition to Murad II's forces, reflecting the empire's ongoing military and political struggles with external powers such as the Ottoman Empire. Despite territorial losses and economic challenges during periods like the Macedonian period, marked by ineffective rulers, dynastic conflicts, and Dynatoi power elites, it remained an influential cultural, political, and religious power through Late Antiquity and the late Middle Ages. The fall of Constantinople to the Ottomans in 1453 represented a significant shift in power, ending the Byzantine Empire's control over parts of Europe and Asia.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9cea715d402110c49c739e324bc54567&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-81aaa6bf9b333d06305b8e02f056d212&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3dc7e2b0bdcb5cf6ffad76150023fc3b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-99442760cad93ba2b6238a5063d5e7a3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9659ee5a3cec87c5f65828322cee3bbd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-72d7bb40fa0aa36bf5b0bb66bf7975fe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ac97c61bc79f0fafcdc0133b3f827bda&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c13c9d68535c84e9a2084524eeed3c86&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b7df43c8d0f05694f4eca13a6b562ce4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-51a6e6c0f619a3b37e3ee2a7e7f19b5a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3047d60403fb89082b52beb809b2b4c2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50e25b5ca5c2528a7011ec84bc476f76&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-17ddaf0fb532418dfd517db22df102e9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-754df6979479251d63e61b06ae37d304&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aeb38d9c22dbab062c46fb4a7bdfb465&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c7e3d55eb6e21abdcb5964c1eb485603&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-64b6a2af1c3cece75a3cbd9b707b8713&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b150c3e9d15dec1eaca94b8168bda0ba&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8adac32853d2cbcb9cfc409ace493450&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ef569534db6161860971327c3bc8b184&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f82fcaeaf8ded7ccd594a70788c5c5e9</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210786</data>
    </node>
