    </node>
    <node id="Jericho">
      <data key="d0">Jericho</data>
      <data key="d1">geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">Jericho is a Neolithic archaeological site that has been significant for its protective features over ancient times. During the Natufian period, Jericho featured notable structures such as a large ditch and thick stone wall. Additionally, Jericho is renowned for containing an early example of human history where an artisan covered a human skull with plaster and placed shells in the eye sockets approximately 9,000 years ago. The site also includes evidence of further Neolithic development, including walls possibly used as an observatory and likely linked to ancestor veneration practices.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4a2723d4803a5b150552aff52fb30ce2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2b63f6d20ffb060f0c413763b316d411&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-747e78751c03e9ed731d7029e8f71270</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210811</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ain Ghazal">
      <data key="d0">Ain Ghazal</data>
      <data key="d1">geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">Ain Ghazal is a nearby Neolithic settlement to Jericho, showcasing similar characteristics of early agricultural societies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-747e78751c03e9ed731d7029e8f71270</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210806</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Nahal Hemar">
      <data key="d0">Nahal Hemar</data>
      <data key="d1">geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">Nahal Hemar is another nearby Neolithic settlement like Ain Ghazal and Jericho, part of the broader Natufian cultural context.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-747e78751c03e9ed731d7029e8f71270</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210806</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Abu Hureyra">
      <data key="d0">Abu Hureyra</data>
      <data key="d1">geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">Abu Hureyra is a northward site on the Euphrates River, also belonging to the same archaeological period as Jericho and other Neolithic settlements.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-747e78751c03e9ed731d7029e8f71270</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210806</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Natufian Culture">
      <data key="d0">Natufian Culture</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">The Natufian culture refers to an early pre-agricultural society that inhabited regions such as Jericho, Ain Ghazal, Nahal Hemar, and Abu Hureyra during the Pleistocene-era transition into the Holocene.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-747e78751c03e9ed731d7029e8f71270</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210806</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mehrgarh">
      <data key="d0">Mehrgarh</data>
      <data key="d1">geographical_location,civilization_or_empire</data>
      <data key="d2">Mehrgarh is an early Neolithic settlement located in modern Pakistan that marks the earliest evidence of agriculture in South Asia.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-747e78751c03e9ed731d7029e8f71270</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210806</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Indus River">
      <data key="d0">Indus River</data>
      <data key="d1">geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">The Indus River basin serves as a crucial geographical location where the archaeological site of Mehrgarh is situated, playing a significant role in early agricultural practices and trade networks. Additionally, the river forms one of the boundaries of the influence area of the Kushan Empire, highlighting its importance in both ancient settlement development and historical regional politics.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bceaa7979e3d60d1deb8d4848dbe01fa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-747e78751c03e9ed731d7029e8f71270</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210810</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Yellow River">
      <data key="d0">Yellow River</data>
      <data key="d1">geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">The Yellow River is one of the two major river systems in ancient China, alongside the Yangtze River. Both these rivers played a crucial role in the development of Neolithic settlements due to their rich, agriculturally favorable environments. This makes the Yellow River and Yangtze River areas significant regions for the early growth of Chinese civilization.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-990942779714eb1b55832fae0d8365e4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-747e78751c03e9ed731d7029e8f71270</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210810</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Yangtze River">
      <data key="d0">Yangtze River</data>
      <data key="d1">geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">The Yangtze River is a significant geographical area that played a crucial role in early agricultural practices and the development of rice cultivation, alongside another major river system in ancient China—the Yellow River. This comprehensive description highlights the Yangtze River's importance as one of two pivotal river systems in ancient Chinese history, contributing significantly to the region’s early agricultural advancements.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-990942779714eb1b55832fae0d8365e4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-747e78751c03e9ed731d7029e8f71270</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210811</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Millet">
      <data key="d0">Millet</data>
      <data key="d1">textbook_structure</data>
      <data key="d2">Millet is a staple crop in Neolithic settlements along the Yellow River, providing sustenance and supporting population growth through agriculture.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-747e78751c03e9ed731d7029e8f71270</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210807</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Rice">
      <data key="d0">Rice</data>
      <data key="d1">textbook_structure</data>
      <data key="d2">Rice is a staple crop developed along the Yangtze River, forming part of the diverse agricultural economy of early China.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-747e78751c03e9ed731d7029e8f71270</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210807</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Settlements of early Neolithic China">
      <data key="d0">Settlements of early Neolithic China</data>
      <data key="d1">geographical_location,civilization_or_empire</data>
      <data key="d2">The earliest Neolithic settlements in China, such as those found along the Yellow and Yangtze rivers, exemplify how these regions supported the development of agriculture and trade in the region.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-747e78751c03e9ed731d7029e8f71270</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210807</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Long-distance Trade">
      <data key="d0">Long-distance Trade</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">Long-distance trade among Neolithic settlements like Mehrgarh is evident from the presence of materials such as lapis lazuli, indicating complex social and economic networks emerging with early agricultural societies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-747e78751c03e9ed731d7029e8f71270</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210808</data>
    </node>
    <node id="7500 BCE">
      <data key="d0">7500 BCE</data>
      <data key="d1">time_period</data>
      <data key="d2">Evidence of early cultivation of wild rice is found at Pengtoushan and Bashidang sites, which may have been settled around 7500 BCE.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-be218f59dc8386a1e2c745f10659dfa3</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
