      <data key="d0">Lower sea levels</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">The reduction in ocean water volume leading to increased land exposure and expanded habitable areas, which occurred during the last ice age.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5d60f602c690bd1716c66d15ec766306</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210808</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Archaeologists uncover evidence">
      <data key="d0">Archaeologists uncover evidence</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">Archeological discoveries such as shelters made of mammoth bones found in eastern Europe provide insights into Paleolithic life.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5d60f602c690bd1716c66d15ec766306</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210808</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hunter-gatherer groups">
      <data key="d0">Hunter-gatherer groups</data>
      <data key="d1">civilization_or_empire</data>
      <data key="d2">Groups that traditionally rely on hunting and gathering for subsistence. The San people are an example of Hunter-gatherers who face gender imbalance today.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5d60f602c690bd1716c66d15ec766306</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210809</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Agriculture adoption">
      <data key="d0">Agriculture adoption</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">The transition from hunter-gathering to agricultural practices, which led to more stable food sources and societies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5d60f602c690bd1716c66d15ec766306</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210809</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Rice agriculture first development">
      <data key="d0">Rice agriculture first development</data>
      <data key="d1">source_or_artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">Neolithic settlements in the Yangtze River valley were among the earliest to develop rice cultivation, a significant practice in early agricultural societies of East Asia.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5d60f602c690bd1716c66d15ec766306</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210809</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Maize cultivation">
      <data key="d0">Maize cultivation</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">The independent domestication and cultivation of maize in the Andean region during prehistory, contributing to diverse food systems.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5d60f602c690bd1716c66d15ec766306</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210809</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Men's tasks in agricultural communities">
      <data key="d0">Men's tasks in agricultural communities</data>
      <data key="d1">textbook_structure</data>
      <data key="d2">In early agricultural societies, men often performed roles associated with farming such as plowing or building infrastructure, though societal roles varied widely.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5d60f602c690bd1716c66d15ec766306</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210809</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Sumer">
      <data key="d0">Sumer</data>
      <data key="d1">geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">Sumer was an ancient civilization located in southern Mesopotamia, today's southern Iraq, known for its significant contributions to human history. This early civilization is recognized as one of the first to develop in the region during a crucial period, marked by remarkable advancements such as cuneiform writing and sophisticated urban planning. Sumerians pioneered these innovations, which laid foundational elements for future civilizations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4475a92fdbe351d1be51fc471c84fa37&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ce95f4fd988337875531ba7f4b862b38&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1c1a3500d7732daf2db9f25db73376bf</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210814</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ur">
      <data key="d0">Ur</data>
      <data key="d1">geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">Ur is an ancient city situated in Mesopotamia, renowned for its royal tomb which houses an intricately ornamented wooden box dating back to approximately 2550 BCE. This city also serves as a significant indicator of social stratification during the early period of Sumerian civilization, highlighting the development of hierarchical structures within this civilization.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5cc80f4856e6c9df805d419244928154&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1c1a3500d7732daf2db9f25db73376bf</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210814</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Art of Ur Box">
      <data key="d0">Art of Ur Box</data>
      <data key="d1">source_or_artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">Inlaid wooden box found in a royal tomb in the ancient city of Ur, dating back to around 2550 BCE and decorated with mosaic figures and borders made from red limestone, lapis lazuli, and marine shell.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1c1a3500d7732daf2db9f25db73376bf</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210810</data>
    </node>
