      <data key="d0">Poverty Point</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">Poverty Point is a large archaeological site in Louisiana, United States, with monumental construction similar to Göbekli Tepe.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2b63f6d20ffb060f0c413763b316d411</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210811</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Neolithic settlements">
      <data key="d0">Neolithic settlements</data>
      <data key="d1">time_period</data>
      <data key="d2">Neolithic settlements refer to the period characterized by agricultural production and increasing complexity in life areas such as religion and government organization.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2b63f6d20ffb060f0c413763b316d411</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210811</data>
    </node>
    <node id="8000 BCE">
      <data key="d0">8000 BCE</data>
      <data key="d1">time_period</data>
      <data key="d2">8000 BCE marks the period when the famous tower of Jericho was constructed. This time period provides context for understanding the historical developments and societal structures during the Neolithic era.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-00b045edff27a3849cb01d2c87c94a5f</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210811</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Jericho Tower">
      <data key="d0">Jericho Tower</data>
      <data key="d1">source_or_artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">The Tower of Jericho is a stone monument built around 8000 BCE in the Neolithic city of Jericho, known to be one of the earliest in world history. Its purpose remains uncertain despite careful excavation and analysis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-00b045edff27a3849cb01d2c87c94a5f</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210811</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Decorated Skull">
      <data key="d0">Decorated Skull</data>
      <data key="d1">source_or_artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">A decorated skull found in Neolithic Jericho, which may have been used as an object of veneration or ancestor worship by the ancient inhabitants. However, its exact meaning is debated among historians and archaeologists.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-00b045edff27a3849cb01d2c87c94a5f</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210811</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Neolithic Era">
      <data key="d0">Neolithic Era</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">The Neolithic era represents a significant period in human prehistory during which societies developed agriculture, lived in settled communities, and began creating complex artifacts like the tower of Jericho and decorated skulls.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-00b045edff27a3849cb01d2c87c94a5f</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210811</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tower of Jericho">
      <data key="d0">Tower of Jericho</data>
      <data key="d1">cultural_or_religious_movement</data>
      <data key="d2">The Tower of Jericho is considered by some to be a religious monument or observatory due to its construction and location, illustrating the complex interplay between architectural structures and spiritual beliefs in Neolithic societies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-00b045edff27a3849cb01d2c87c94a5f</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210811</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ancient Artisans'&lt; Work">
      <data key="d0">Ancient Artisans'&lt; Work</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">Artisans from the Neolithic period played a crucial role in creating significant artifacts such as the decorated skull. Their work reflects the craftsmanship of their time but also leaves room for interpretation regarding its intended purpose.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-00b045edff27a3849cb01d2c87c94a5f</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210811</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Jericho Village">
      <data key="d0">Jericho Village</data>
      <data key="d1">geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2"> Jericho is a Neolithic village located near the Jordan River known for its ancient history and archaeological significance.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4a2723d4803a5b150552aff52fb30ce2</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210811</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Çatalhöyük Village">
      <data key="d0">Çatalhöyük Village</data>
      <data key="d1">geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2"> Çatalhöyük is a prehistoric settlement located in Turkey, known for its well-preserved murals and architectural remains from the Neolithic era.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4a2723d4803a5b150552aff52fb30ce2</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210811</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;img-1&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;img-1&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">source_or_artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;img-1 refers to Section 2: Cross-Cultural Interaction in the Sahel, an integral part within a structured text or document. This section integrates various historical and cultural elements from different periods, including the Hellenistic period (3rd to 1st centuries BCE), the Achaemenid period through depictions of Ahura Mazda, famine and cold weather during the Little Ice Age in China, the Black Death, Meroe, and potentially encompasses multiple unknown images or artifacts. The image highlights a blend of cultural influences and represents distinct historical phases such as the Hellenistic and Zoroastrian periods, while also touching upon broader historical events like the Black Death and specific archaeological finds related to Meroe and the Paleolithic Age. It is worth noting that some descriptions suggest &lt;img-1 might be an unidentified image or artifact, although it likely contains elements from diverse historical contexts and cultural interactions within its portrayal of cross-cultural interaction in the Sahel region.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-42a7884af18b1be9360e3134246f2ec8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b8ad3f8265df80ff0cbd95a7914a6123&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a72dedb2114605de59f4327cf6088215&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3ae707c8e9f6cd312e0a53d74e93b4a9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-959d0e5d4763f5423c8c6e422ec0cdb2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-44aa89ddb763022c44c3e2c4c5f3c057&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1e09ce349d4a0e40bee9c4bf3685382c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5010e0f708f284a5e25e323f470ad4d6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2e05c323f2387cfb1c337fb65115e181&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5eed38a4f29c432367bba95f27296bdf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7891c6c99c50753a0d022eafb7d7196a</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210791</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Veneration of Bulls">
      <data key="d0">Veneration of Bulls</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2"> The veneration of bulls as evidenced by bucrania is a significant cultural and religious practice associated with Çatalhöyük.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4a2723d4803a5b150552aff52fb30ce2</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210811</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Neolithic Advancements">
      <data key="d0">Neolithic Advancements</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2"> Neolithic advancements in human society are exemplified through archaeological findings such as those from Jericho and Çatalhöyük.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4a2723d4803a5b150552aff52fb30ce2</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210811</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Fourth Millennium BCE">
      <data key="d0">Fourth Millennium BCE</data>
      <data key="d1">time_period</data>
      <data key="d2">This is a period in history extending from 4000 to 3000 BCE.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4475a92fdbe351d1be51fc471c84fa37</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210811</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Uruk">
      <data key="d0">Uruk</data>
      <data key="d1">geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">The city of Uruk is a significant ancient Mesopotamian city located in southern Mesopotamia. It emerged as one of the earliest and largest urban centers during the Sumerian period, with an estimated population of 20,000 by around 3500 BCE. By the end of the fourth millennium BCE, Uruk had grown to a considerable size with an estimated population of up to 50,000 people. The city served as a vital hub for trade, culture, and administration in ancient Mesopotamia.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4475a92fdbe351d1be51fc471c84fa37&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c204cf87ddccb43d29862abf65e2506f</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210817</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bronze Age">
      <data key="d0">Bronze Age</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">The Bronze Age spans the period from circa 3300 to 1200 BCE and is characterized by significant technological advancements and cultural exchanges primarily in Mediterranean regions. This era saw bronze supplant stone as the primary material for tools and weapons, initiating a new phase of societal development around 4000 BCE. Notably, this transition coincided with the flourishing of Sumerian civilization in Mesopotamia. These descriptions collectively highlight the technological innovation that defined the Bronze Age, its importance in regional trade and cultural interactions, and its pivotal role in the emergence of complex societies such as those found in ancient Mesopotamia.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4475a92fdbe351d1be51fc471c84fa37&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fe793fb21f15c20ae7e7d7a78a23923e</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210818</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Plow">
      <data key="d0">Plow</data>
      <data key="d1">source_or_artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">Invented during this time, this agricultural tool helped expand production by improving land cultivation methods, facilitating larger-scale farming operations in ancient Mesopotamia.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4475a92fdbe351d1be51fc471c84fa37</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210811</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Wheel">
      <data key="d0">Wheel</data>
      <data key="d1">source_or_artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">The wheel was a significant technological advancement in Sumerian society during the fourth millennium BCE, utilized for various purposes including transportation and machinery.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4475a92fdbe351d1be51fc471c84fa37</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210811</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Irrigation Techniques">
      <data key="d0">Irrigation Techniques</data>
      <data key="d1">textbook_structure</data>
      <data key="d2">Detailed techniques for managing water resources were developed, facilitating crop production and urbanization, contributing to the prosperity of Sumerian cities.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4475a92fdbe351d1be51fc471c84fa37</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210811</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cuneiform">
      <data key="d0">Cuneiform</data>
      <data key="d1">source_or_artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">Developed as a script in Sumer, this writing system became crucial for record-keeping, law, religion, and documentation, aiding in the complex administration and cultural development of the society.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4475a92fdbe351d1be51fc471c84fa37</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210811</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Irrigation Canals">
      <data key="d0">Irrigation Canals</data>
      <data key="d1">source_orArtifact</data>
      <data key="d2">These canals were a part of Sumerian agricultural infrastructure, used for watering fields to support the growing crops during the dry season. The design and maintenance of these canals indicate sophisticated water management techniques developed in Mesopotamia.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4475a92fdbe351d1be51fc471c84fa37</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210812</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Urban Planning">
      <data key="d0">Urban Planning</data>
      <data key="d1">textbook_structure</data>
      <data key="d2">The careful layout and design of Sumerian cities such as Uruk demonstrate advanced urban planning, incorporating public spaces, residential areas, and defensive structures to support a dense population and organized society.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4475a92fdbe351d1be51fc471c84fa37</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210812</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Neolithic Revolution">
      <data key="d0">Neolithic Revolution</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">The Neolithic Revolution marks a significant period in human history when societies began transitioning from nomadic hunting and gathering to settled agricultural lifestyles, leading to the establishment of permanent communities around 10,000 BCE. This pivotal change initially started in regions such as the Fertile Crescent and gradually spread to other areas including parts of America, although later than in the Old World. While iron metalworking played a role in this transition by contributing to the domestication of plants and animals, it was primarily during the late Stone Age that humans developed sedentary farming communities. This revolutionary change significantly influenced social structures and laid the groundwork for the development of early civilizations through agriculture and animal husbandry, establishing common cultural foundations not only in southern Africa but also across diverse regions where agriculture took root.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5d60f602c690bd1716c66d15ec766306&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e0b75181e879a7cb5f46e925d8f88288&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-038356ebaf9b5df91192c77bd51ac651&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-97ab4cac183886ee18d87b065b2e0ad5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-91a847d29a702803fcc6217de2a69c95</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210802</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Marketplace">
      <data key="d0">Marketplace</data>
      <data key="d1">geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">A bustling marketplace in a Sumerian city like Uruk would have been a hub of economic activity, trading goods both locally and from distant regions, reflecting the complex commercial networks established during the Bronze Age.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4475a92fdbe351d1be51fc471c84fa37</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210812</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cuneiform Script">
      <data key="d0">Cuneiform Script</data>
      <data key="d1">source_or_artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">Cuneiform Script refers to the writing system that emerged in Sumer around 3000 BCE and beyond, including clay tablets and other artifacts.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ce95f4fd988337875531ba7f4b862b38</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210812</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tokens">
      <data key="d0">Tokens</data>
      <data key="d1">textbook_structure</data>
      <data key="d2">Tokens are small clay representations used as part of an accounting system in ancient societies before the development of cuneiform script.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ce95f4fd988337875531ba7f4b862b38</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210812</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bullae">
      <data key="d0">Bullae</data>
      <data key="d1">source_or_artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">Bullae are hollow clay balls containing tokens and marked with pictures, representing a transitional step towards writing from prehistoric counting systems.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ce95f4fd988337875531ba7f4b862b38</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210812</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Sumerian civilization">
      <data key="d0">Sumerian civilization</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">Sumer is an ancient civilization located in southern Mesopotamia that emerged around 4500 BCE and developed into a complex state with advanced cultural, social, economic, and technological systems. It is known for its contributions to early writing, such as cuneiform script.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ce95f4fd988337875531ba7f4b862b38</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210812</data>
    </node>
    <node id="cuneiform script">
      <data key="d0">cuneiform script</data>
      <data key="d1">source_or_artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">Cuneiform Script refers to the ancient writing system that emerged in Sumer around 3000 BCE and was used to record economic transactions, laws, religious texts, and epics. It is written on clay tablets with a wedge-shaped stylus and includes pictographic symbols.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ce95f4fd988337875531ba7f4b862b38</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210812</data>
    </node>
    <node id="tokens">
      <data key="d0">tokens</data>
      <data key="d1">textbook_structure</data>
      <data key="d2">Tokens were small clay representations used as part of an early accounting system in Sumer before the development of cuneiform script. They symbolized commodities, quantities, or values that could be exchanged in economic transactions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ce95f4fd988337875531ba7f4b862b38</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210812</data>
    </node>
    <node id="bullae">
      <data key="d0">bullae</data>
      <data key="d1">source_or_artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">Bullae are hollow clay balls containing tokens and marked with pictures, representing a transitional step from prehistoric counting systems to early writing methods."</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ce95f4fd988337875531ba7f4b862b38</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210812</data>
    </node>
    <node id="clay tablets">
      <data key="d0">clay tablets</data>
      <data key="d1">source_orArtifact</data>
      <data key="d2">Clay tablets were a primary medium for writing in ancient Sumer, used to create official records and documents using the cuneiform script. They survived as important artifacts from this period."</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ce95f4fd988337875531ba7f4b862b38</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210813</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mesopotamian economy">
      <data key="d0">Mesopotamian economy</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">The Mesopotamian economy encompasses the trade, industry, agriculture, and commerce practices developed in ancient Mesopotamia. It included early accounting systems like those facilitated by tokens and represented by bullae."</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ce95f4fd988337875531ba7f4b862b38</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210813</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Sumerian language">
      <data key="d0">Sumerian language</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">The Sumerian language encompasses the linguistic system used by the Sumerians for their written communication, particularly through cuneiform script. It is an essential part of understanding the civilization's history and culture."</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ce95f4fd988337875531ba7f4b862b38</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210813</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Sumerian culture">
      <data key="d0">Sumerian culture</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">The Sumerian culture encompasses the broader societal, religious, and philosophical aspects of the Sumerians. It is deeply intertwined with the development of cuneiform script as a means of expressing cultural and religious values."</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ce95f4fd988337875531ba7f4b862b38</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210813</data>
    </node>
    <node id="clay materials">
      <data key="d0">clay materials</data>
      <data key="d1">source_orArtifact</data>
      <data key="d2">Clay materials were the primary substance used in the production of clay tablets. Their availability and properties were crucial for the development and preservation of cuneiform script."</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ce95f4fd988337875531ba7f4b862b38</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210813</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Figure 3.11 The Ziggurat of Ur&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Figure 3.11 The Ziggurat of Ur&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">The Ziggurat of Ur is a partially reconstructed ancient structure located near the modern city of Nasiriyah in Iraq, dating from approximately 2650 BCE to 2400 BCE, marking significant human endeavors and resources spent on construction during this period.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a8ea083f226618d1b7e5a85496f09e4d</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210813</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Sumeria&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Sumeria&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">civilization_or_empire</data>
      <data key="d2">Sumeria was an ancient civilization located in Mesopotamia, known for its advanced societal structure, religious practices centered around worship of deities, and influential cultural contributions such as the Epic of Gilgamesh. The Sumerians placed gods at the center of their society to ensure prosperity.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a8ea083f226618d1b7e5a85496f09e4d</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210813</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;lugals&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;lugals&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">Lugalshad was a title for rulers in early Sumerian cities during the Early Dynastic Period who emerged as warlords due to urban conflicts over resources, legitimizing their authority through religious institutions. They were seen as leaders who could fund and expand temples.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a8ea083f226618d1b7e5a85496f09e4d</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210813</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;First Dynasty of Ur&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;First Dynasty of Ur&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">time_period</data>
      <data key="d2">The First Dynasty of Ur occurred around 2650 BCE to 2400 BCE in ancient Mesopotamia, characterized by especially wealthy rulers known for magnificent beehive-shaped tombs with precious belongings and sacrificial servants.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a8ea083f226618d1b7e5a85496f09e4d</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210813</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Pu-Abi&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Pu-Abi&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">Pu-Abi was a notable ruler during the First Dynasty of Ur, buried in an elaborate headdress. She may have been queen or another significant female figure who had substantial social and religious importance.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a8ea083f226618d1b7e5a85496f09e4d</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210813</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Gilgamesh&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Gilgamesh&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">Gilgamesh is a legendary Sumerian king from Uruk, renowned for his exploits in the Epic of Gilgamesh, which reflects early mythological and cultural significance during this period. He is often seen as a semi-divine figure.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a8ea083f226618d1b7e5a85496f09e4d</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210813</data>
    </node>
    <node id="First Empire Era">
      <data key="d0">First Empire Era</data>
      <data key="d1">time_period</data>
      <data key="d2">First Empire Era refers to a period when significant early empires like Akkadian reached their zenith and began to expand their influence.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-03e05c621c24bf3d1e73a7ca94fea65b</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210813</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Akkadian Empire">
      <data key="d0">Akkadian Empire</data>
      <data key="d1">civilization_or_empire</data>
      <data key="d2">The Akkadian Empire was an ancient Mesopotamian civilization that significantly expanded under Sargon of Akkad. This empire, established by Sargon of Akkad, is recognized as one of the earliest and largest empires in Mesopotamia, renowned for its military campaigns and administrative innovations. Notable among these innovations are the standardization of weights and measures, which contributed to the empire's effective governance.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-03e05c621c24bf3d1e73a7ca94fea65b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3ec6731194e5e13d1ca2bd169094d176</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210818</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Sargon of Akkad">
      <data key="d0">Sargon of Akkad</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">Sargon of Akkad was the first emperor of the Akkadian Empire, who reached its greatest extent just before his death in 2279 BCE.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-03e05c621c24bf3d1e73a7ca94fea65b</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210814</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Syria">
      <data key="d0">Syria</data>
      <data key="d1">geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">Syria is a significant location in the Eastern Mediterranean, known for its strategic positioning near Mesopotamia and historical rivalry with city-states such as Ebla. The region has been integral to trade networks, connecting Mesopotamian goods like tin and copper with destinations such as Egypt or other areas. Syria was impacted by external conquests, notably during Timur’s military campaigns, which underscored its importance in the broader context of ancient and medieval Middle Eastern politics and commerce.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-03e05c621c24bf3d1e73a7ca94fea65b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2c0fbb619a916ac387c46ee41fe1af47&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-921eca26480da1b32897b0b58823f7d8</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210820</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ebla">
      <data key="d0">Ebla</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">The city-state of Ebla, located in northwestern Syria, competed with Akkadian Empire for dominance and adapted Sumerian cuneiform script.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-03e05c621c24bf3d1e73a7ca94fea65b</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210814</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Sumerian Cuneiform Script">
      <data key="d0">Sumerian Cuneiform Script</data>
      <data key="d1">source_or_artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">The Sumerian Cuneiform Script was an early writing system utilized by the Sumerians in Mesopotamia. This script was later adopted and adapted by the Ebla civilization for their language, expanding its use and influence beyond the original Sumerian culture.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-03e05c621c24bf3d1e73a7ca94fea65b</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210819</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Storm God Adad&quot;/&quot;Baal">
      <data key="d0">Storm God Adad"/"Baal</data>
      <data key="d1">cultural_or_religious_movement</data>
      <data key="d2">The storm god Adad, known as Baal among Semitic-speaking peoples like those of Ebla, was a significant deity in their religious practices and belief systems.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-03e05c621c24bf3d1e73a7ca94fea65b</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210815</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Baal">
      <data key="d0">Baal</data>
      <data key="d1">deity</data>
      <data key="d2">Same as above entities.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-03e05c621c24bf3d1e73a7ca94fea65b</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210815</data>
    </node>
    <node id="first empire era">
      <data key="d0">first empire era</data>
      <data key="d1">time_period</data>
      <data key="d2">Same as above entities.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-03e05c621c24bf3d1e73a7ca94fea65b</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210815</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Guti Tribes&quot;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Guti Tribes"</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;"historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;"The Guti tribes were a nomadic group that invaded Akkadian territories, leading to internal weaknesses within the empire. Modern scholarly perspectives suggest their incursion contributed to the decline of the Akkadian Empire without necessitating an external invasion as the primary cause."</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1a705813ff5affce703975dd4c0a231a</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210815</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Sargonic Empire&quot;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Sargonic Empire"</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;"civilization_or_empire</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;"The Sargonic Empire, founded by Sargon in the early 3rd millennium BCE, marked a period of significant conquest and centralized power. It unified Mesopotamia under its rule and is known for the Sargonid dynasty's expansionist policies."</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1a705813ff5affce703975dd4c0a231a</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210815</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Third Dynasty of Ur&quot;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Third Dynasty of Ur"</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;"civilization_or_empire</data>
      <data key="d2">The Third Dynasty of Ur, also known as the Ur III period (circa 2150 BCE - 2004 BCE), experienced a significant resurgence in Sumerian art and architecture. This era witnessed notable cultural and political unification under rulers such as Ur-Nammu, marked by major construction projects and temple building. The dynasty is also renowned for the creation of the Code of Ur-Nammu. However, despite these accomplishments, it eventually succumbed to invasions from Amorites and Elamites.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1a705813ff5affce703975dd4c0a231a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9ec6671f9959c328d4f5610f67e7c2fc</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210822</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Ur-Nammu&quot;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Ur-Nammu"</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;"historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;"Ur-Nammu was a prominent ruler during the Third Dynasty of Ur. He is noted for creating a law code, constructing a border wall for defense, and overseeing various building projects in Mesopotamia."</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1a705813ff5affce703975dd4c0a231a</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210816</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Sargon&quot;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Sargon"</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;"historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;"Sargon, founder of the Sargonic Empire, was responsible for significant expansionist policies. His conquests led to the unification of Sumerian regions and the eventual decline of indigenous Sumerian leadership."</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1a705813ff5affce703975dd4c0a231a</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210816</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Sumerian Civilization&quot;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Sumerian Civilization"</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;"civilization_or_empire</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;"The Sumerian civilization thrived in Southern Mesopotamia, known for its advanced agricultural techniques, literature like the Epic of Gilgamesh, and religious practices. It faced eventual decline around 2004 BCE."</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1a705813ff5affce703975dd4c0a231a</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210816</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Akadian Empire&quot;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Akadian Empire"</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;"civilization_or_empire</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;"The Akadian Empire, established by Sargon, was one of the earliest multi-ethnic empires in Mesopotamia. It experienced internal weakening and decline around 2150 BCE."</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1a705813ff5affce703975dd4c0a231a</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210816</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Epic of Gilgamesh&quot;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Epic of Gilgamesh"</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;"source_or_artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;"The Epic of Gilgamesh is an ancient Sumerian literary work, detailing the adventures and myths of King Gilgamesh. It provides insight into Sumerian culture and values."</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1a705813ff5affce703975dd4c0a231a</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210816</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Code of Hammurabi&quot;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Code of Hammurabi"</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;"source_or_artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;"The Code of Hammurabi is a set of laws inscribed on stone pillars, providing detailed guidance for governance and societal structure in ancient Mesopotamia. It followed the Sargonic and Ur dynasties."</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1a705813ff5affce703975dd4c0a231a</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210816</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Code of Hammurabi">
      <data key="d0">Code of Hammurabi</data>
      <data key="d1">textbook_structure</data>
      <data key="d2">The Code of Hammurabi is an ancient Babylonian legal code that introduced judicial concepts and served as a foundational legal framework in ancient Mesopotamia. This code outlines various laws, penalties, and social hierarchies within the context of a centralized state structure. The repetition and consistency across descriptions confirm its significance as one of the earliest and most comprehensive documented sets of laws from the ancient world.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-37a92110d64655a1b7585b3fb61d29b5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3ec6731194e5e13d1ca2bd169094d176</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210822</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hammurabi's death">
      <data key="d0">Hammurabi's death</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_event</data>
      <data key="d2">Hammurabi’s death marks the end of his rule, after which the Code continued to influence legal systems for centuries.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-37a92110d64655a1b7585b3fb61d29b5</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210817</data>
    </node>
