    </node>
    <node id="Pyramid">
      <data key="d0">Pyramid</data>
      <data key="d1">cultural_or_religious_movement</data>
      <data key="d2">The Pyramid was an ancient Egyptian religious monument constructed to house the remains of pharaohs. It symbolized the connection between the earthly world and the realm of the gods, embodying pharaonic grandeur and durability.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-262ce3f10c5cb95e159b65dfc03d5961</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210817</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pharaoh">
      <data key="d0">Pharaoh</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">Pharaoh was a ruler of ancient Egypt who combined divine and kingship in their persona, representing both the earthly power and the religious leadership. They were central to maintaining order (Ma'at) through religious ceremonies and governance.&lt;SEP&gt;Pharaohs were powerful leaders in ancient Egypt who ruled over this period during the Old Kingdom, known for their role in construction projects like pyramids.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3480f6387c4263e5b94d6ebc80dbe087&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-262ce3f10c5cb95e159b65dfc03d5961</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210817</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hieroglyphics">
      <data key="d0">Hieroglyphics</data>
      <data key="d1">cultural_or_religious_movement</data>
      <data key="d2">Hieroglyphics is a unique writing system developed by the Egyptians, known today as "sacred writings," which can also be referred to as medu-netjer, meaning "the god’s words." This ancient Egyptian writing system utilizes pictorial symbols that often represent sounds or ideas. Hieroglyphics played a crucial role in both written and religious expressions, functioning as a means of eternal communication within the society. These symbolic representations were integral to the cultural and spiritual heritage of Ancient Egypt.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-105af7da244f1f2b87a54f23919cd56d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-262ce3f10c5cb95e159b65dfc03d5961</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210823</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Utu (Shamash)">
      <data key="d0">Utu (Shamash)</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">Utu, known as Shamash among the Akkadians, is depicted in a limestone relief from the early ninth century BCE, symbolizing the balance of power between palace and temple.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f351e39bc01d0781d675dfb68a1bff58</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210818</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tablet of Shamash">
      <data key="d0">Tablet of Shamash</data>
      <data key="d1">source_or_artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">The Tablet of Shamash is a larger artifact which includes a limestone relief of Utu, providing insights into Mesopotamian religious beliefs and governance.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f351e39bc01d0781d675dfb68a1bff58</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210818</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Human sacrifice">
      <data key="d0">Human sacrifice</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">Human sacrifices to appease the gods through the offering of sheep and cattle in rituals are described as a commonly practiced tradition in Mesopotamia.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f351e39bc01d0781d675dfb68a1bff58</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210818</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Temples, hymns, and gifts">
      <data key="d0">Temples, hymns, and gifts</data>
      <data key="d1">cultural_or_religious_movement</data>
      <data key="d2">People built temples, sang hymns, and gave expensive gifts to honor their gods, indicating the extent of religious devotion in this civilization.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f351e39bc01d0781d675dfb68a1bff58</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210818</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Sahara Desert">
      <data key="d0">Sahara Desert</data>
      <data key="d1">geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">The Sahara Desert is a vast desert located in North Africa, which became increasingly arid around 6000 BCE as climatic changes transformed its environment from one more conducive to human settlement. Despite its inhospitable nature characterized by sparse rainfall and extreme temperature swings, the Sahara was vital to the prosperity of medieval West African kingdoms through the facilitation of trade and the establishment of trans-Saharan trade routes that connected distant regions. This transformation from a potentially habitable area to an arid landscape underscored its significant role in historical and economic exchanges across North Africa and beyond.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-038356ebaf9b5df91192c77bd51ac651&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fe1025f0b9f819b6ebc89df353b87d27&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ad97ea1ef8e2c731dddc24e3eecbe4f2</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210824</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Nile River Valley">
      <data key="d0">Nile River Valley</data>
      <data key="d1">geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">The region of fertile land along the Nile river, supporting early agricultural societies in Ancient Egypt and facilitating the growth of civilization through its regular flooding cycles, which provided rich soil for farming.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-038356ebaf9b5df91192c77bd51ac651</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210818</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Lower Egypt, Upper Egypt">
      <data key="d0">Lower Egypt, Upper Egypt</data>
      <data key="d1">civilization_or_empire</data>
      <data key="d2">("Two kingdoms that emerged during the Neolithic period, Lower Egypt corresponding to the Nile delta region while Upper Egypt was located upriver. They eventually unified around 3150 BCE.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-038356ebaf9b5df91192c77bd51ac651</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210818</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Narmer, Menes">
      <data key="d0">Narmer, Menes</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">The king believed to have united Upper and Lower Egypt into a single kingdom in about 3150 BCE; although the evidence is debated, he is often considered the first Pharaoh of Ancient Egypt.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-038356ebaf9b5df91192c77bd51ac651</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210818</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Fertile Crescent, Levant">
      <data key="d0">Fertile Crescent, Levant</data>
      <data key="d1">geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">("The region from which agricultural advancements such as wheat, barley, sheep, goats, and cattle might have been introduced to the Nile area during the Neolithic period, significantly impacting early Egyptian settlement patterns."</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-038356ebaf9b5df91192c77bd51ac651</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210818</data>
    </node>
    <node id="civilization_or_empire">
      <data key="d0">civilization_or_empire</data>
      <data key="d1">Ancient Egypt</data>
      <data key="d2">The civilization or empire known for developing complex hieroglyphics and other writing systems, flourishing from approximately 3100 BCE to 30 BCE, refers specifically to ancient Egyptian civilization. This description outlines a distinct historical entity that emerged around 3100 BCE and reached its peak during the New Kingdom period at around 1550-1069 BCE before gradually declining until it was incorporated into the Roman Empire in 30 BCE.

On the other hand, another usage of "civilization or empire" refers to territories unified under the rule of the Abbasid caliphs. This entity geographically spanned from present-day Turkey to North Africa and included regions such as Jordan and Egypt. The Abbasid Caliphate was established in 750 CE and lasted until its collapse in the 13th century, making this description applicable to a period much later than that of ancient Egypt.

Additionally, the term is also broadly used to describe various civilizations and empires that have existed or currently exist in Africa. This suggests a more general reference rather than a specific historical entity, encompassing diverse cultures and political entities across different regions of the African continent throughout history and into modern times.

Given these descriptions, it appears there are two distinct usages of "civilization or empire": one specifically referring to ancient Egypt and another to territories unified under the Abbasid caliphate. The latter definition is likely intended to distinguish from a broader sense describing multiple historical entities in Africa.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-105af7da244f1f2b87a54f23919cd56d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-82b28f56f2ef832a14237e5150ea2d0b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-395768da0b846b0a0ea31c551387d142</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210830</data>
    </node>
    <node id="The Ages of Egypt">
      <data key="d0">The Ages of Egypt</data>
      <data key="d1">textbook_structure</data>
      <data key="d2">The Ages of Egypt is a structured text that outlines different eras of ancient Egyptian history, developed by scholars in the nineteenth century.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-80195ddc16fc30dbc8c31afd00a15644</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210820</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Kingdom">
      <data key="d0">Kingdom</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">Kingdom refers to periods of high centralized state organization in ancient Egypt.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-80195ddc16fc30dbc8c31afd00a15644</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210820</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Intermediate">
      <data key="d0">Intermediate</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">Intermediate describes a time of weak centralized state organization in ancient Egypt.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-80195ddc16fc30dbc8c31afd00a15644</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210820</data>
    </node>
    <node id="19th Century Scholars">
      <data key="d0">19th Century Scholars</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">19th Century Scholars developed the labels for the different eras of ancient Egypt's history, influencing how we understand its chronology.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-80195ddc16fc30dbc8c31afd00a15644</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210820</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Egyptian Pyramids">
      <data key="d0">Egyptian Pyramids</data>
      <data key="d1">source_or_artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">The pyramids were tombs built for pharaohs in ancient Egypt, used for storing and preserving their bodies after death.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-80195ddc16fc30dbc8c31afd00a15644</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210820</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ka">
      <data key="d0">Ka</data>
      <data key="d1">cultural_or_religious_movement</data>
      <data key="d2">Ka is the spiritual double of a person in Egyptian religious beliefs, remaining in the tomb to be nourished with offerings.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-80195ddc16fc30dbc8c31afd00a15644</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210820</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ba">
      <data key="d0">Ba</data>
      <data key="d1">cultural_or_religious_movement</data>
      <data key="d2">Ba is a type of spiritual essence in Egyptian beliefs, separating from the body after death and traveling during the day and returning at night.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-80195ddc16fc30dbc8c31afd00a15644</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210820</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ahk">
      <data key="d0">Ahk</data>
      <data key="d1">cultural_or_religious_movement</data>
      <data key="d2">Ahk is another type of spirit in ancient Egyptian belief, whose duty was to travel to the underworld and the afterlife.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-80195ddc16fc30dbc8c31afd00a15644</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210820</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mummification">
      <data key="d0">Mummification</data>
      <data key="d1">textbook_structure</data>
      <data key="d2">The practice of mummification supports religious beliefs about preserving the physical body for the journey to the afterlife.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-80195ddc16fc30dbc8c31afd00a15644</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210820</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tombs">
      <data key="d0">Tombs</data>
      <data key="d1">source_or_artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">Tombs, including those made of mud-brick or stone, were crucial in ancient Egyptian religion and culture.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-80195ddc16fc30dbc8c31afd00a15644</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210820</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mud-Brick">
      <data key="d0">Mud-Brick</data>
      <data key="d1">textbook_structure</data>
      <data key="d2">Before the pyramids, tombs were often built of mud-brick, known as mastabas.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-80195ddc16fc30dbc8c31afd00a15644</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210820</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Djoser">
      <data key="d0">Djoser</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">Djoser was an early pharaoh of the 3rd dynasty in ancient Egypt, known best for commissioning the construction of his monumental Step Pyramid complex at Saqqara. This ambitious project significantly advanced Egyptian architecture and engineering and marked a pivotal moment in architectural history by constructing the first step pyramid.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-80195ddc16fc30dbc8c31afd00a15644&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c204cf87ddccb43d29862abf65e2506f</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210823</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Imhotep">
      <data key="d0">Imhotep</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">Imhotep was an Egyptian polymath who served as chancellor to the Pharaoh Djoser and is renowned for his role as an architect responsible for designing and building the first step pyramid for the Step Pyramid at Saqqara during the reign of Pharaoh Djoser. He also held multiple professions, including that of a physician, engineer, astrologer, and magician. Imhotep is widely recognized as the first named individual in history to have such diverse professional capabilities, contributing significantly to ancient Egyptian architecture and beyond.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-80195ddc16fc30dbc8c31afd00a15644&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d3e346a9ee7b8ed7d44126651c4f2dcb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c204cf87ddccb43d29862abf65e2506f</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210826</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Step Pyramid">
      <data key="d0">Step Pyramid</data>
      <data key="d1">source_or_artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">The Step Pyramid is an iconic ancient Egyptian monument that represents both a significant cultural and religious movement symbolizing the pharaoh's ascent to heaven. This architectural marvel, also considered a monumental shift in tomb construction techniques, was designed and constructed by the renowned architect Imhotep for King Djoser.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-80195ddc16fc30dbc8c31afd00a15644&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d3e346a9ee7b8ed7d44126651c4f2dcb</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210825</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Temples">
      <data key="d0">Temples</data>
      <data key="d1">source_or_artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">Imhotep also constructed a large complex of temples surrounding the step pyramid.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-80195ddc16fc30dbc8c31afd00a15644</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210822</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Old Kingdom">
      <data key="d0">Old Kingdom</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">The Old Kingdom refers to the period from 2613–2181 BCE when the power of pharaohs and state sophistication in Egypt allowed the construction of large-scale stone architecture, such as massive pyramids.&lt;SEP&gt;The Old Kingdom refers to the period from the 27th to the 4th dynasty (c. 2686–2181 BCE) when ancient Egypt experienced a significant cultural and economic boom.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3480f6387c4263e5b94d6ebc80dbe087&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-80195ddc16fc30dbc8c31afd00a15644</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210819</data>
    </node>
    <node id="First Intermediate Period">
      <data key="d0">First Intermediate Period</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">The First Intermediate Period of Egypt, spanning from approximately 2181 to 2055 BCE, was a time characterized by political instability and decentralization. During this period, which can also be described as a phase of disunity in ancient Egypt, local rulers in Thebes faced challenges from rival kingdoms and new ruling groups. This challenging period marked a shift away from centralized power, with various regional authorities contending for dominance.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-80195ddc16fc30dbc8c31afd00a15644&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-05c7011c59296ab43df76d1d813bcaed</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210827</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Second Intermediate Period">
      <data key="d0">Second Intermediate Period</data>
      <data key="d1">time_period</data>
      <data key="d2">During the Second Intermediate Period, which spanned from approximately 1650 to 1550 BCE, Egypt underwent significant changes as it was effectively divided into three kingdoms and experienced foreign occupation by the Hyksos in Lower Egypt while native Egyptian dynasties maintained their rule in the south. This period is also associated with the influx of Canaanites into the Nile delta, who took control of parts of Egypt. Some scholars have linked events during this time to those described in the biblical account of the Hebrews’ life in Egypt and their Exodus.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-80195ddc16fc30dbc8c31afd00a15644&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b8771c71bac2191eaaccfdac7bfbc0ea&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-01819cdf305281e0ef22ba7cb3469e6a</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210829</data>
    </node>
    <node id="New Kingdom">
      <data key="d0">New Kingdom</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">The New Kingdom is a historical period within ancient Egypt extending from the 18th to the 20th dynasty (c. 1550–1077 BCE). It encompasses some of Egypt's most renowned pharaohs including Hatshepsut, Thutmose III, and Ramses II. This era marked a significant shift in Egyptian history following the expulsion of the Hyksos by Pharaoh Ahmose, signaling a new chapter of greatness for the kingdom. During this period, there were notable advancements in various fields such as art, architecture, and military strength.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-80195ddc16fc30dbc8c31afd00a15644&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-01819cdf305281e0ef22ba7cb3469e6a</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210830</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Late Period">
      <data key="d0">Late Period</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">The Late Period covers the time after the end of the New Kingdom until the Ptolemaic era (c. 664–332 BCE), a period marked by foreign invasions and internal fragmentation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-80195ddc16fc30dbc8c31afd00a15644</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210824</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ptolemaic Era">
      <data key="d0">Ptolemaic Era</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">The Ptolemaic Era spans from 305 to 30 BC, during which Egypt was ruled first by the Macedonian-descended Ptolemies and then as part of the Roman Empire.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-80195ddc16fc30dbc8c31afd00a15644</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210824</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pyramids">
      <data key="d0">Pyramids</data>
      <data key="d1">source_or_artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">The pyramids were large monuments constructed in ancient Egypt primarily as tombs for pharaohs to preserve their bodies and support them in the afterlife, aligning with religious beliefs about the Ka, Ba, and Ahk.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-80195ddc16fc30dbc8c31afd00a15644</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210824</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mastaba">
      <data key="d0">Mastaba</data>
      <data key="d1">source_or_artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">Mastabas were early forms of pyramidal or rectilinear tombs built for Middle-Class Egyptians around 3050–2686 BCE, transitioning from simple mud-bricks to step pyramids during the Early Dynastic and Old Kingdom periods.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-80195ddc16fc30dbc8c31afd00a15644</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210824</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Great Pyramid">
      <data key="d0">Great Pyramid</data>
      <data key="d1">source_or_artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">The Great Pyramid, built by Pharaoh Khufu at Giza during the Fourth Dynasty, is the largest of all pyramids and one of the most famous examples of ancient Egyptian architecture.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-80195ddc16fc30dbc8c31afd00a15644</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210824</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Luxor Temple">
      <data key="d0">Luxor Temple</data>
      <data key="d1">source_or_artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">Located in Luxor, Upper Egypt, this temple has been expanded over many centuries but its main structures were built by Amenhotep III and Ramesses II as a place for important religious ceremonies centered around the cult of Amun.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-80195ddc16fc30dbc8c31afd00a15644</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210824</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Valley of the Kings">
      <data key="d0">Valley of the Kings</data>
      <data key="d1">source_or_artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">The Valley of the Kings is located in Luxor, Egypt, and served as ancient burial ground for kings during the New Kingdom period, many famous pharaohs such as Tutankhamun and Ramses II among its tombs.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-80195ddc16fc30dbc8c31afd00a15644</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210824</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Saqqara">
      <data key="d0">Saqqara</data>
      <data key="d1">source_or_artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">Saqqara is a vast cemetery site of historical pyramids, mastabas, and other monuments, located near Cairo. It served as an important necropolis during the Early Dynastic period and Old Kingdom era.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-80195ddc16fc30dbc8c31afd00a15644</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210824</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Giza Plateau">
      <data key="d0">Giza Plateau</data>
      <data key="d1">source_or_artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">The Giza Plateau hosts three large pyramids erected for Egyptian kings Khufu, Khafre, and Menkaure. It is one of the major sites in ancient Egypt known for monumental structures related to pharaonic beliefs about the afterlife.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-80195ddc16fc30dbc8c31afd00a15644</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210824</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Valley Temple">
      <data key="d0">Valley Temple</data>
      <data key="d1">source_or_artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">A Valley Temple was a structure built at the base of a pyramid complex near its entrance, serving mainly as approach point and part of mortuary temple complexes where offerings were made on behalf of the pharaoh. Many notable examples exist like those of Khafre's complex in Giza.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-80195ddc16fc30dbc8c31afd00a15644</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210824</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mortuary Temple">
      <data key="d0">Mortuary Temple</data>
      <data key="d1">source_or_artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">Mortuary Temples were built alongside pyramids as funerary shrines dedicated to a deceased king, housing statues and other items used for worship during the afterlife. Key examples include the Mortuary Temple of Amenhotep III in Luxor.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-80195ddc16fc30dbc8c31afd00a15644</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210824</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Nile River">
      <data key="d0">Nile River</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">The Nile River, a major river located in northeastern Africa, has been pivotal to the development and sustenance of ancient civilizations, particularly in Egypt. Sustaining life through its annual flooding, which was integral to agricultural practices and transportation, the Nile also played a significant role in spiritual beliefs, notably associated with creation myths involving figures such as Osiris and Isis. Unlike the Congo River, which is generally longer, the Nile forms part of distinct environmental and societal contexts within Africa.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-80195ddc16fc30dbc8c31afd00a15644&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-85139964461f2ef54605b30deb55201a</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210830</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Nile Delta">
      <data key="d0">Nile Delta</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">The Nile Delta is situated at the northernmost point where the Nile River branches off into several smaller rivers before emptying into the Mediterranean Sea. It's historically been an area of great agricultural fertility, supporting much of Ancient Egypt’s civilization from its early settled life through to complex urbanized and later times.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-80195ddc16fc30dbc8c31afd00a15644</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210824</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Osiris">
      <data key="d0">Osiris</data>
      <data key="d1">deity</data>
      <data key="d2">Osiris was a god in ancient Egyptian pantheon known for resurrection, underworld aspects &amp; regeneration/death cycles; central figure in myths including creation narratives such as Osirian cult surrounding his death/resurrection by brother Set into Osiris and wife Isis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-80195ddc16fc30dbc8c31afd00a15644</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210824</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Isis">
      <data key="d0">Isis</data>
      <data key="d1">deity</data>
      <data key="d2">Isis, one of the most prominent goddesses in ancient Egyptian religion, was known for her roles as a mother figure, healer, and magician, and was associated with powerful themes of resurrection and magic. According to mythological accounts, Isis played a crucial role in the story of her husband Osiris's death and his subsequent resurrection, seeking to bring him back from the underworld after he was killed by Set. Like other deities such as the Persian sun god Mithras, Isis was central to the formation of mystery religions that offered spiritual and mystical experiences to devotees.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-80195ddc16fc30dbc8c31afd00a15644&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-54ce354bc2591fc3936924cededa981a</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210832</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tutankhamun">
      <data key="d0">Tutankhamun</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">He was an Egyptian pharaoh of the 18th Dynasty during Egypt's Amarna Period and a prominent figure known for his brief reign (c. 1332–1323 BCE) where he reinstated traditional religious beliefs after his predecessor Akhenaten’s reforms, also famous for discovery of nearly intact tomb in modern age which revealed much about ancient Egypt.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-80195ddc16fc30dbc8c31afd00a15644</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210825</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ramesses II">
      <data key="d0">Ramesses II</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">Also known as Ramesses the Great, this Pharaoh ruled Egypt from 1279 to 1213 BCE and was one of its most powerful and prominent figures, notable for military campaigns, construction projects including temples like Abu Simbel and also being depicted in many artifacts showing his divine status.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-80195ddc16fc30dbc8c31afd00a15644</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210825</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Akhenaten">
      <data key="d0">Akhenaten</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">Akhenaten was a controversial pharaoh of the ancient Egyptian 18th Dynasty, known for his significant religious reforms that introduced theories about monotheism during his reign. He rejected the traditional polytheistic religion in favor of a worship centered solely on Aten, the sun disk, and established it as the state religion. His rule also saw the construction of various building projects and dedicated religious sites, notably the city of Akhetaten, which was built to celebrate and honor Aten as the sole deity.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-eade46978a4c4daa5b0a0ffa6887c985&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-80195ddc16fc30dbc8c31afd00a15644</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210832</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Figure 3.20 The Pyramid of Djoser">
      <data key="d0">Figure 3.20 The Pyramid of Djoser</data>
      <data key="d1">textbook_structure</data>
      <data key="d2">Figure 3.20 The Pyramid of Djoser illustrates the step pyramid, which was revolutionary for its time.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d3e346a9ee7b8ed7d44126651c4f2dcb</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210826</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pyramid of Djoser">
      <data key="d0">Pyramid of Djoser</data>
      <data key="d1">source_or_artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">The Pyramid of Djoser, sometimes called the step pyramid, is composed of six stone mastabasset atop each other, each slightly smaller than the one below. It was built by the architect Imhotep during the reign of Pharaoh Djoser.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d3e346a9ee7b8ed7d44126651c4f2dcb</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210826</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pharaoh Djoser">
      <data key="d0">Pharaoh Djoser</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">Pharaoh Djoser, also known as Zoser, is credited with commissioning the construction of the Pyramid of Djoser in ancient Egypt.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d3e346a9ee7b8ed7d44126651c4f2dcb</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210826</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Red Pyramid">
      <data key="d0">Red Pyramid</data>
      <data key="d1">source_or_artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">The Red Pyramid emerged during the reign of Snefru and was built from reddish limestone, originally covering a height of 344 feet over the surrounding landscape.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d3e346a9ee7b8ed7d44126651c4f2dcb</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210826</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Snefru">
      <data key="d0">Snefru</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">Snefru was a notable king of the 4th dynasty in ancient Egypt, playing a crucial role during his reign by successfully leading military campaigns to suppress rebellions and overseeing significant architectural projects. Specifically, Snefru is recognized for commissioning the construction of several pyramids, with key examples including the Bent Pyramid and the Red Pyramid. His contributions solidify his legacy as an important figure in ancient Egyptian history.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d3e346a9ee7b8ed7d44126651c4f2dcb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c204cf87ddccb43d29862abf65e2506f</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210832</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Great Pyramid at Giza">
      <data key="d0">Great Pyramid at Giza</data>
      <data key="d1">source_or_artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">The Great Pyramid at Giza was built by Snefru’s son Khufu and stands out more impressively compared to the earlier step pyramid of Djoser, symbolizing the organizational power of the Egyptian state.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d3e346a9ee7b8ed7d44126651c4f2dcb</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210827</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Khufu">
      <data key="d0">Khufu</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">King Khufu is responsible for constructing the Great Pyramid at Giza, succeeding his father Snefru and furthering Egypt's monumental construction endeavors.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d3e346a9ee7b8ed7d44126651c4f2dcb</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210827</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;img-0&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;img-0&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;img-0&gt; is a comprehensive collection of diverse images and maps that span various historical periods, regions, and topics. The entity includes depictions of ancient cityscapes such as Timbuktu and its surroundings on the Niger River, as well as representations of archaeological sites like Pompeii or Herculaneum covered in volcanic ash from the 18th century after excavation. It also features a Greco-Bactrian coin from the 6th century BCE and various historical artifacts, including traditional samurai helmets and katana swords symbolizing the rise of the samurai class during the Heian period.

The collection encompasses several significant events such as the Battle of Gaixia—a critical strategic meeting in ancient China's Chu Han Contention period—and historical trade activities in the Indian Ocean region during the Early Middle Ages. It also includes maps that illustrate climatic changes, like those during the Little Ice Age, and details of religious symbolism such as a depiction of Ahura Mazda with its symbolic attributes.

Additionally, &lt;img-0&gt; highlights regions from different continents, including North Africa (specifically Alexandria), historical sites related to events like the Black Death, and ancient palaeolithic sites. The collection may also contain images portraying a portrait of Qin Shi Huangdi, archaeological remains such as those associated with the Kennewick Man and his tools (Clovis points), and other geographical locations or historical sites from various periods.

Overall, &lt;img-0&gt; serves as an extensive visual resource encompassing a wide array of historical data, cultural artifacts, and geographic information.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b0b78a6a43531b2cc0eb37bc0ca299a2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-42a7884af18b1be9360e3134246f2ec8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-98e8a95a7016ab8987b814ff391981eb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7f9a47fe3a9c9c64cc2975619f1c44a3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-959d0e5d4763f5423c8c6e422ec0cdb2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1e09ce349d4a0e40bee9c4bf3685382c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2e05c323f2387cfb1c337fb65115e181&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d883170ec07b62302c41040a3bae56cd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7891c6c99c50753a0d022eafb7d7196a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3ae707c8e9f6cd312e0a53d74e93b4a9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-76d4974f6a32742560ccbc66f2b65d1b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9e0565ea301887f653d4d7d463873cc0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-44aa89ddb763022c44c3e2c4c5f3c057&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5eed38a4f29c432367bba95f27296bdf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-87ea0a32c0a6997da917b2e1b4f3cd69&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cfe63951f2b8d366e873d2eb166a6a43&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b8ad3f8265df80ff0cbd95a7914a6123&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a72dedb2114605de59f4327cf6088215&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5010e0f708f284a5e25e323f470ad4d6</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210794</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Giza pyramid complex">
      <data key="d0">Giza pyramid complex</data>
      <data key="d1">textbook_structure</data>
      <data key="d2">The Giza pyramid complex, located just outside modern Cairo, includes three large pyramids and many other monuments and minor pyramids.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d3e346a9ee7b8ed7d44126651c4f2dcb</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210827</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Giza-pyramids&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Giza-pyramids&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;geographical_location&gt;</data>
      <data key="d2">The Great Pyramids at Giza were built during the Old Kingdom and are aligned to the position of Sirius in the summer months, indicating significant astronomical knowledge. They are a testament to ancient Egyptian engineering and construction.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-166229fe54e2881fb1a257f7f577fc20</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210827</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Herodotus&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Herodotus&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;historical_figure&gt;</data>
      <data key="d2">Greek historian who reported that the construction of Khufu's pyramid took 100,000 workers over twenty years, though modern archaeologists estimate this was more likely around 20,000.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-166229fe54e2881fb1a257f7f577fc20</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210827</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Khufu&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Khufu&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;historical_figure&gt;</data>
      <data key="d2">The pharaoh who commissioned and oversaw the construction of the Great Pyramid at Giza during the Fourth Dynasty of Egypt's Old Kingdom period.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-166229fe54e2881fb1a257f7f577fc20</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210827</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Old_Kingdom&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Old_Kingdom&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;time_period&gt;</data>
      <data key="d2">Refers to the period in ancient Egyptian history from approximately 2686 BCE to 2181 BCE, characterized by centralized power and significant architectural achievements such as the construction of pyramids.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-166229fe54e2881fb1a257f7f577fc20</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210827</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;First_Intermediate_Period&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;First_Intermediate_Period&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;time_period&gt;</data>
      <data key="d2">A transitional period in ancient Egypt from around 2181 BCE to 2055 BCE, marked by a decline in central power, with wealth being redirected towards regional governors or nomarchs.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-166229fe54e2881fb1a257f7f577fc20</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210827</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Middle_Kingdom&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Middle_Kingdom&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;time_period&gt;</data>
      <data key="d2">Refers to the period in Egyptian history following the First Intermediate Period and seeing renewed centralization, starting around 2055 BCE and ending around 1650 BCE, which saw the construction of grand temples dedicated to gods like Amon-Re.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-166229fe54e2881fb1a257f7f577fc20</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210827</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Amenemhat&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Amenemhat&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;historical_figure&gt;</data>
      <data key="d2">The founder of the First Middle Kingdom dynasty who led Egypt into a period of political stability.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-166229fe54e2881fb1a257f7f577fc20</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210827</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Karnak_temple_complex&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Karnak_temple_complex&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;cultural_or_religious_movement&gt;</data>
      <data key="d2">Situated in Thebes, it is a complex of religious buildings dating from the Middle Kingdom onwards and dedicated to Amon-Re, with significant construction during the New Kingdom.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-166229fe54e2881fb1a257f7f577fc20</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210827</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Thebes&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Thebes&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;geographical_location&gt;</data>
      <data key="d2">A city located on the east bank of the Nile that became an important center for worship and administration during the Middle Kingdom.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-166229fe54e2881fb1a257f7f577fc20</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210827</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Middle Kingdom Egypt">
      <data key="d0">Middle Kingdom Egypt</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_period</data>
      <data key="d2">The Middle Kingdom of Egypt reached its height in the 1870s and 1860s BCE during the reign of Senusret III.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-58fa5bf2acfbf424dd2563814c0f0472</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210827</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Senusret III">
      <data key="d0">Senusret III</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">He was a powerful warrior pharaoh and capable administrator who greatly expanded Egypt's territorial control and increased its wealth through military campaigns into Kush and Levant.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-58fa5bf2acfbf424dd2563814c0f0472</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210827</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Temple of Amon-Re">
      <data key="d0">Temple of Amon-Re</data>
      <data key="d1">source_or_artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">The Temple of Amon-Re, built around 2055 BCE, was originally a significant religious site that has undergone plundering and is now in ruins, with some ceremonial gates left standing.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-58fa5bf2acfbf424dd2563814c0f0472</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210827</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Karnak Temple Complex at Luxor, Egypt">
      <data key="d0">Karnak Temple Complex at Luxor, Egypt</data>
      <data key="d1">source_or_artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">A modification of this work by Daniel Csörföly provides a reference for the temple structure mentioned, sourced from Wikimedia Commons under Public Domain.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-58fa5bf2acfbf424dd2563814c0f0472</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210827</data>
    </node>
    <node id="centralized state">
      <data key="d0">centralized state</data>
      <data key="d1">concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">The term refers to the consolidation of power and reduction in authority of nomarchs during Senusret III's reign..</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-58fa5bf2acfbf424dd2563814c0f0472</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210827</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Treaty Power">
      <data key="d0">Treaty Power</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">A concept that characterizes the extent of control and influence over other regions, as Senusret III expanded Egypt's territorial holdings.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-58fa5bf2acfbf424dd2563814c0f0472</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210827</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Nomarchs">
      <data key="d0">Nomarchs</data>
      <data key="d1">role</data>
      <data key="d2">These were local rulers or officials under Senusret III whose authority was diminished to strengthen centralized state power.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-58fa5bf2acfbf424dd2563814c0f0472</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210827</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cultural flourishing">
      <data key="d0">Cultural flourishing</data>
      <data key="d1">concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">A period of enhanced artistic, architectural, and literary refinement that occurred during the reign of Senusret III.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-58fa5bf2acfbf424dd2563814c0f0472</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210827</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Ming Dynasty&quot;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Ming Dynasty"</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">"The Ming Dynasty (1368–1644) was a period of significant political and cultural development in Chinese history. It followed the fall of the Yuan Dynasty, ushered in by Zhu Yuanzhang, who established a new imperial family known as the Hongwu Emperor. The Ming era is noted for its advancements in technology, art, architecture, and maritime exploration. Notable achievements include the construction of the Great Wall of China, the development of blue-and-white ceramics, and the establishment of the Wanli (or Zhengde) era of prosperity. However, towards the end of the dynasty, it faced internal strife and external threats that gradually weakened its power until its fall to the Manchu-led Qing Dynasty in 1644."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-be37fc10d5448a07bdf21b06248f3471</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210827</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Qing Dynasty&quot;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Qing Dynasty"</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">"The Qing Dynasty (1644–1912) was the last imperial dynasty of China, established by the Jurchen people after defeating the Ming Dynasty in 1644. During its rule, it expanded significantly into Central Asia and North Korea, and made significant cultural, administrative, and technological advances. The Qing era is marked by the Kangxi, Qianlong, and Yongzheng Emperors, who were known for their military conquests and administrative reforms. However, towards the late 19th century, the dynasty showed signs of decline due to internal corruption, economic distress, and external pressures from Western powers."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-be37fc10d5448a07bdf21b06248f3471</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210827</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Indus Valley Culture">
      <data key="d0">Indus Valley Culture</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">Indus Valley Culture refers to the ancient civilization that was spread over parts of Pakistan and India by 2000 BCE, known for its high level of urbanization with several large cities and many smaller urban areas.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-33b80661398bbb8712dfa331df14ca97</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210827</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Harappa">
      <data key="d0">Harappa</data>
      <data key="d1">geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">Harappa is an important archaeological site located in the northern part of the Indus Valley, often used as a synonym for the entire Indus valley civilization. Situated near Pakistan's Ravi River, Harappa serves as a significant symbol of early urbanization and cultural exchange in South Asia. The site was discovered during early nineteenth-century British excavations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-96a9973262a04f62e37d1ec6cfe70c42&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-33b80661398bbb8712dfa331df14ca97</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210832</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mohenjo-Daro">
      <data key="d0">Mohenjo-Daro</data>
      <data key="d1">geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">Mohenjo-Daro is a significant archaeological site located in the south of the Indus Valley that has received extensive study. Known for its well-laid-out city streets and similar structural organization to Harappa, Mohenjo-Daro was an important ancient city in the region. Some passages in religious texts like the Rigveda suggest that it might have been attacked by Aryans.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-eda2368f7c0ef00cbce121f87fbbdaa1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-33b80661398bbb8712dfa331df14ca97</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210835</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Archaeological Sites">
      <data key="d0">Archaeological Sites</data>
      <data key="d1">source_or_artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">The archaeological sites of Harappa and Mohenjo-Daro are sources of historical artifacts and insights into the urban planning and societal structure of the Indus Valley Civilization.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-33b80661398bbb8712dfa331df14ca97</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210830</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Political Organization">
      <data key="d0">Political Organization</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">The political organization of the Indus Valley Civilization remains imperfectly understood, with theories ranging from independent city-states to a centralized authority or regional capital system.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-33b80661398bbb8712dfa331df14ca97</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210830</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Central Authority">
      <data key="d0">Central Authority</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">The concept of central authority in the Indus Valley Civilization suggests a form of governance that might have existed at the larger cities, potentially controlling smaller surrounding settlements.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-33b80661398bbb8712dfa331df14ca97</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210830</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Citadel">
      <data key="d0">Citadel</data>
      <data key="d1">textbook_structure</data>
      <data key="d2">Citing from the text, a citadel is described as divided into two sections: a lower residential area and an upper walled-off section for ceremonial and elite activities. It likely served as a center for ritual activities and the residence of ruling elites.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-33b80661398bbb8712dfa331df14ca97</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210830</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mud-Brick Wall">
      <data key="d0">Mud-Brick Wall</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">The mud-brick wall encircling Harappa's citadel, which was forty-two feet thick at its base and nearly fifty feet high, is a physical manifestation of the urban planning and engineering capabilities of the Indus Valley Civilization.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-33b80661398bbb8712dfa331df14ca97</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210830</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Structural Organization">
      <data key="d0">Structural Organization</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">The similar structural organization, including the well-laid-out city streets and platforms for ritual activities, across different sites like Harappa and Mohenjo-Daro supports theories about central authority governance in the Indus Valley Civilization.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-33b80661398bbb8712dfa331df14ca97</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210830</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Shang Dynasty&quot;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Shang Dynasty"</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">The Shang Dynasty was a powerful and influential Bronze Age civilization that existed in China from approximately 1600 BCE to 1046 BCE, based primarily in the Yellow River valley. This dynasty is renowned for its advanced bronze work, sophisticated socio-political structures, the development of characters in writing, urban planning, and human sacrifice practices. The Shang Dynasty played a significant role during ancient Chinese history by contributing not only to technological advancements but also to cultural and societal developments through its oracle bone script, reflecting both its achievements and unique traditions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-17c7ceed9ec0b5e033838e24607d90cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dff0e11d470077fc40e0d05ff82a3124&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a8006bb84efc385e264f6921f9b06e3d</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210836</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Yellow River&quot;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Yellow River"</data>
      <data key="d1">geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">"The Yellow River, also known as Huang He, is a major river in China that runs through several provinces and is considered sacred by some Chinese religions. It has been crucial for agriculture throughout its history."&lt;&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-dff0e11d470077fc40e0d05ff82a3124</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210830</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Qin Dynasty&quot;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Qin Dynasty"</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">The Qin Dynasty (221–206 BCE) was a significant Chinese dynasty that unified the country and established centralised governance, marking an important period in Chinese history. It introduced numerous standardized systems that endured for centuries, including the standardization of weights and measures, currency, writing system, and cartographic grid system. Emperor Qin Shi Huang, one of its prominent leaders, played a crucial role in these transformations.

The dynasty's unification efforts led to the establishment of a centralized bureaucratic state and facilitated major military conquests. These changes laid the foundational structures for subsequent Chinese states and had lasting impacts on various aspects of Chinese society, culture, and administration.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b7494d5cadc1afd15065737504e9cd58&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dff0e11d470077fc40e0d05ff82a3124&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cb1fcf0230e00461dcf4e472b596b4c2</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210837</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hagia Sophia">
      <data key="d0">Hagia Sophia</data>
      <data key="d1">source_or_artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">Hagia Sophia, originally known as the Church of Holy Wisdom and now located in Istanbul, is a significant historical landmark that spans multiple eras. Initially constructed during the reign of Emperor Justinian around 532-537 AD, Hagia Sophia was built next to the imperial residence and near the hippodrome. It served as an iconic symbol of Orthodox Christianity during Byzantine rule, intended to showcase the grandeur and power of the Byzantine Empire.

The structure has undergone several transformations throughout history, most notably after it was severely damaged by an earthquake, leading to a reconstruction that preserved many original architectural elements from its initial construction under Justinian. In 1453, following the Ottoman conquest of Constantinople led by Sultan Mehmed II, Hagia Sophia transitioned into a mosque, where it underwent further modifications to accommodate Islamic prayer practices while maintaining key Byzantine features.

Currently operated as a museum, Hagia Sophia continues to stand as a remarkable testament to the architectural and artistic integration of Greek Orthodox and Islamic traditions. Its most notable feature is its large central dome, which has become emblematic of its rich cultural heritage spanning centuries. This historical landmark not only serves as a powerful representation of Byzantine engineering and artistic achievements but also exemplifies cross-cultural architectural fusion, offering invaluable insights into both Christian and Islamic civilizations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-622311a2741b1845b3a8c00c6e5d7b93&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50e25b5ca5c2528a7011ec84bc476f76&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-72d7bb40fa0aa36bf5b0bb66bf7975fe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aef10b168281ffac76d2b994317417e1</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210799</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Confucius&quot;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Confucius"</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;"historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">Confucius, a Chinese philosopher born in 551 BCE and passing away in 479 BCE during the late Spring and Autumn period (770-476 BCE), is renowned for his profound influence on ethical and moral philosophies both within China and in various parts of East Asia. His teachings form the foundation of Confucianism, emphasizing social harmony, ethical behavior, and moral governance. These principles have significantly shaped Chinese and Asian societies and continue to be studied and appreciated globally.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a8006bb84efc385e264f6921f9b06e3d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dff0e11d470077fc40e0d05ff82a3124&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-35781a3da9b896d7300657dd50bc7ddd</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210836</data>
    </node>
