      <data key="d0">FIGURE 3.26 Indus Valley Trade with Mesopotamia</data>
      <data key="d1">textbook_structure</data>
      <data key="d2">Figure 3.26 illustrates significant trade between the Indus River valley and Mesopotamia based on archaeological evidence.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-756f0b26d168efc2bc1d5ec8a2aa2219</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210832</data>
    </node>
    <node id="FIGURE 3.27 An Indus Valley Seal">
      <data key="d0">FIGURE 3.27 An Indus Valley Seal</data>
      <data key="d1">textbook_structure</data>
      <data key="d2">Figure 3.27 depicts a seal from the Indus valley civilization and its modern clay impression, highlighting unique animal depictions and undeciphered script.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-756f0b26d168efc2bc1d5ec8a2aa2219</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210832</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Indus River valley">
      <data key="d0">Indus River valley</data>
      <data key="d1">geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">The Indus River valley refers to the geographical area where the Indus Valley Civilization thrived, engaging in significant trade with Mesopotamia.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-756f0b26d168efc2bc1d5ec8a2aa2219</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210832</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Indus Valley Civilization">
      <data key="d0">Indus Valley Civilization</data>
      <data key="d1">civilization_or_empire</data>
      <data key="d2">The Indus Valley Civilization was an ancient cultural and historical entity that thrived in the region of the Indus River. Known for its advanced urban planning and extensive trade, this civilization engaged in significant commercial activities with Mesopotamia, showcasing sophisticated societal structures and economic networks. The flourishing period of the Indus Valley Civilization lasted until around 1500 BCE when it began a gradual decline, ultimately collapsing by approximately 1500-1400 BCE.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-756f0b26d168efc2bc1d5ec8a2aa2219&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eda2368f7c0ef00cbce121f87fbbdaa1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9d648c8e307c0c6dacb2622d4d8e1c24</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210838</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Trade">
      <data key="d0">Trade</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">Trade between the Indus River valley and Mesopotamia is a significant historical concept representing economic exchange and cultural interaction between two ancient civilizations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-756f0b26d168efc2bc1d5ec8a2aa2219</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210832</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Seals">
      <data key="d0">Seals</data>
      <data key="d1">source_or_artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">Seals are artifacts used for marking goods with inscriptions, providing evidence of trade and cultural practices in the Indus Valley Civilization and their interactions with Mesopotamia.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-756f0b26d168efc2bc1d5ec8a2aa2219</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210832</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Indus Valley Religious Imagery">
      <data key="d0">Indus Valley Religious Imagery</data>
      <data key="d1">concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">The religious imagery of the Indus Valley is noted for its possible influence on later Indian cultures.")&lt;)(((/entity)) ("entity</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e2b97f3e59f7a42ed6560944da478375</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210832</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mohenjo-Daro Dancer">
      <data key="d0">Mohenjo-Daro Dancer</data>
      <data key="d1">source_or_artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">A four-inch-tall bronze statue of a young woman discovered at Mohenjo-Daro and dated to circa 2300–1750 BCE, potentially depicting a goddess or an elite class female dancer. It suggests the incorporation of music and dance into social and religious rituals.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f0769d59b8ab636e14cdfc06142060d5</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
