    <node id="Sargon_of_Akkad">
      <data key="d0">Sargon_of_Akkad</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">Sargon of Akkad was one of the most famous empires in ancient Mesopotamia. He founded the Akkadian Empire and is credited with unifying Mesopotamia under a single rule, expanding his empire into parts of modern-day Iran, Iraq, Syria, Israel, Lebanon, and Jordan.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c204cf87ddccb43d29862abf65e2506f</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210833</data>
    </node>
    <node id="nomarchs">
      <data key="d0">nomarchs</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">Nomarchs were regional administrators or governors in ancient Egypt who acted as representatives of the Pharaohs, managing local affairs and taxes. They played a critical role in ensuring stability and prosperity within their regions during various periods.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c204cf87ddccb43d29862abf65e2506f</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210833</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hyksos">
      <data key="d0">Hyksos</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">The Hyksos were a group that influenced parts of ancient Egypt, specifically during the Second Intermediate Period from approximately 1650 BCE to 1535 BCE. Originating from the Levant, these rulers brought significant cultural and political changes to Egyptian society while also introducing advanced military tactics and technologies to the region.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c204cf87ddccb43d29862abf65e2506f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-01819cdf305281e0ef22ba7cb3469e6a</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210838</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Second_Intermediate_Period_of_Egypt">
      <data key="d0">Second_Intermediate_Period_of_Egypt</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">The Second Intermediate Period of Egypt was a phase in the history of ancient Egypt, lasting from about 1650 to 1538 BCE. During this period, centralized rule declined as nomarchs gained more autonomy and eventually established their own dynasties.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c204cf87ddccb43d29862abf65e2506f</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210833</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Amenemhet_I">
      <data key="d0">Amenemhet_I</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">Amenemhet I was the first king of the 12th dynasty in ancient Egypt. He unified Upper and Lower Egypt, extended Egyptian influence into Nubia, and established a lasting government structure that lasted for several centuries.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c204cf87ddccb43d29862abf65e2506f</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210833</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Reign of Sobekneferu">
      <data key="d0">Reign of Sobekneferu</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">"Significant events during Sobekneferu's rule, including her status as both pharaoh and priest, and whether she was the last pharaoh of the Middle Kingdom.")&lt;|</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9d648c8e307c0c6dacb2622d4d8e1c24</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210833</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Nomadic Life Transition">
      <data key="d0">Nomadic Life Transition</data>
      <data key="d1">time_period</data>
      <data key="d2">Nomadic Life Transition refers to the period when a society shifts from a nomadic way of life to a settled one.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3ec6731194e5e13d1ca2bd169094d176</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210833</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Settled Life">
      <data key="d0">Settled Life</data>
      <data key="d1">time_period</data>
      <data key="d2">Settled Life represents the period when societies transitioned to a sedentary existence with permanent dwellings and agricultural practices, leading to more complex societal structures.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3ec6731194e5e13d1ca2bd169094d176</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210833</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Agricultural Surplus in Neolithic Towns">
      <data key="d0">Agricultural Surplus in Neolithic Towns</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">The emergence of agricultural surplus in towns like Jericho and Çatalhöyük marked a critical step towards settled life and the development of more complex societies through enhanced food production and storage.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3ec6731194e5e13d1ca2bd169094d176</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210833</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Independent City-States in Mesopotamia">
      <data key="d0">Independent City-States in Mesopotamia</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">The era of independent city-states in Mesopotamia refers to a period characterized by autonomous city-republics before the unification under the Akkadian Empire, showcasing the diverse and decentralized nature of governance.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3ec6731194e5e13d1ca2bd169094d176</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210833</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mesopotamian Gods and Worldview">
      <data key="d0">Mesopotamian Gods and Worldview</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">The Mesopotamian gods and their actions are depicted as capricious and often aligned with a pessimistic worldview that influenced social, religious, and political practices in ancient societies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3ec6731194e5e13d1ca2bd169094d176</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210833</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Chronological Organization of Egyptian History">
      <data key="d0">Chronological Organization of Egyptian History</data>
      <data key="d1">textbook_structure</data>
      <data key="d2">19th-century scholars developed the chronological framework for ancient Egyptian history through named periods (Old Kingdom, Middle Kingdom, etc.), providing a structured timeline for understanding the pharaonic era's evolution.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3ec6731194e5e13d1ca2bd169094d176</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210833</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Unification of Mesopotamia vs. Egypt">
      <data key="d0">Unification of Mesopotamia vs. Egypt</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">The unification processes in Mesopotamia and ancient Egypt differ, reflecting distinct political histories and the timing and manner in which central authority was established or consolidated in these regions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3ec6731194e5e13d1ca2bd169094d176</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210834</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Trade between Indus Valley and Mesopotamia">
      <data key="d0">Trade between Indus Valley and Mesopotamia</data>
      <data key="d1">civilization_or_empire</data>
      <data key="d2">The trade relations between the Indus Valley civilization and Mesopotamia highlight intercultural exchanges that significantly influenced both economies and societies during prehistoric times.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3ec6731194e5e13d1ca2bd169094d176</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210834</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Elements of Indus Valley Culture">
      <data key="d0">Elements of Indus Valley Culture</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">("Indus Valley elements", "include advanced urban planning, standardized weights and measures, writing systems, hydraulic engineering, and a complex social hierarchy.")</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3ec6731194e5e13d1ca2bd169094d176</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210834</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Modern Cities">
      <data key="d0">Modern Cities</data>
      <data key="d1">textbook_structure</data>
      <data key="d2">The modern city can be used as a comparative model to evaluate the development of ancient urban centers like those in Mesopotamia and the Indus Valley.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3ec6731194e5e13d1ca2bd169094d176</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210834</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Iron Age">
      <data key="d0">Iron Age</data>
      <data key="d1">time_period</data>
      <data key="d2">The Iron Age is a significant period marked by technological advancements in iron metallurgy and corresponding social changes leading to more complex societies, following the decline of major cultures such as Mycenae around 1100 BCE. Spanning from approximately 1200 BCE onwards, this age witnessed its emergence across multiple regions including Mesopotamia, Central Asia Minor, Israel, Egypt, and the broader Mediterranean area. Key characteristics include the widespread use of iron tools and weapons alongside sustained trade networks, further shaping the socio-economic landscapes of these regions during this transformative era.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fe793fb21f15c20ae7e7d7a78a23923e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-23d1e6b89ed34f781b195088854edaee</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210840</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Assyrian Empire">
      <data key="d0">Assyrian Empire</data>
      <data key="d1">civilization_or_empire</data>
      <data key="d2">Comprehensive description of the Assyrian Empire, a powerful kingdom that rose to prominence in the Neo-Assyrian Period (911–609 BCE), known for its advanced military technology and extensive conquests.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-23d1e6b89ed34f781b195088854edaee</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210834</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ashurbanipal">
      <data key="d0">Ashurbanipal</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">Comprehensive description of Ashurbanipal, the last great ruler of the Assyrian Empire in the 7th century BCE. Known for his extensive empire-building and cultural pursuits such as compiling cuneiform texts like "Hammurabi's code."</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-23d1e6b89ed34f781b195088854edaee</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210834</data>
    </node>
