    </node>
    <node id="&quot;484 BCE&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"484 BCE"</data>
      <data key="d1">time_period</data>
      <data key="d2">A point in time marked by the presence and activities of historical figure Herodotus, a Greek historian, whose life is exemplified by a marble bust in the Metropolitan Museum of Art.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b8d6d7a01f04863f017d0ad8ee1b4e77</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210834</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Persepolis">
      <data key="d0">Persepolis</data>
      <data key="d1">cultural_or_religious_movement</data>
      <data key="d2">Persepolis was the majestic capital city of both the Achaemenid and Persian Empires, renowned for its stunning palaces, impressive public buildings, and exquisite artwork that served as a grand showcase of Persian power and culture. Built at the foot of Mount Damaškion, this significant architectural landmark featured a substantial palatial complex that symbolized the rich heritage and advancements of the Persian period.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-61d32311e91cb2bbe252985a76162eeb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b8d6d7a01f04863f017d0ad8ee1b4e77</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210839</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Old Babylonian Empire&quot;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Old Babylonian Empire"</data>
      <data key="d1">civilization_or_empire</data>
      <data key="d2">"The Old Babylonian Empire was a significant period of Mesopotamian history from approximately 2004 BCE to 1595 BCE, led by King Hammurabi. This empire is known for the Code of Hammurabi, which established legal and social norms that influenced various societies. Hammurabi extended his rule over several city-states, making Babylon a major center of trade and culture."&lt;&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9ec6671f9959c328d4f5610f67e7c2fc</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210835</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Sargon of Akkad&quot;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Sargon of Akkad"</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">"Sargon of Akkad is noted as one of the founders of Mesopotamia's first empire around 2300 BCE. He played a crucial role in unifying different city-states and establishing a centralized administrative system that was influential for future empires."&lt;&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9ec6671f9959c328d4f5610f67e7c2fc</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210835</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Amorites&quot;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Amorites"</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">"The Amorites were nomadic people who migrated to the region during this period, bringing significant cultural shifts. They played a key role in influencing language and urbanization but also posed a threat to established city-states such as Ur."&lt;&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9ec6671f9959c328d4f5610f67e7c2fc</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210835</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Elamites&quot;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Elamites"</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">"The Elamites came from the eastern regions of Mesopotamia, playing a significant role in undermining Sumerian and Akkadian hegemony and contributing elements of their culture to the region."&lt;&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9ec6671f9959c328d4f5610f67e7c2fc</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210835</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Hammurabi&quot;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Hammurabi"</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">"Hammurabi was a King of Babylon who ruled from 1764 - 1750 BCE. He is most famous for his_code, which established a set of laws and norms that were intended to maintain social order and provide justice for all citizens."&lt;&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9ec6671f9959c328d4f5610f67e7c2fc</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210835</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Babylonian Empires Administrative Center&quot;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Babylonian Empires Administrative Center"</data>
      <data key="d1">source_or_artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">"This refers to the administrative core where Hammurabi's empire was centered. It was responsible for overseeing the legal, economic, and social functioning of the state through a complex bureaucratic system that ensured compliance with the Code of Hammurabi."&lt;&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9ec6671f9959c328d4f5610f67e7c2fc</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210835</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Imperial Expansion and Administration&quot;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Imperial Expansion and Administration"</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">"This concept refers to the process by which empires like Akkad and Babylon extended their influence over neighboring regions, establishing governance structures in the conquered territories. It includes aspects such as military conquests, administrative reforms, and cultural assimilation."&lt;&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9ec6671f9959c328d4f5610f67e7c2fc</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210835</data>
    </node>
    <node id="1755 BCE">
      <data key="d0">1755 BCE</data>
      <data key="d1">time_period</data>
      <data key="d2">This is the time period when Hammurabi had transformed his small kingdom into a rival Mesopotamian empire.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0a81a35fe50587e39b82df8bed7f241f</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210835</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Babylonian Empire">
      <data key="d0">Babylonian Empire</data>
      <data key="d1">civilization_or_empire</data>
      <data key="d2">The Babylonian Empire refers to the region controlled by Hammurabi’s rule, stretching from north to south in Mesopotamia, and it is noted for having reached its zenith during Hammurabi's reign around 1894 BCE. Historical records indicate that at its peak, the empire extended as far as the Persian Gulf. This period was also characterized by significant contributions to mathematics, science, culture, and is often recognized for the formulation of one of the earliest comprehensive legal codes in history. The empire's influence lasted until around 1531 BCE when it began to decline.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a3a4ace18f781f226af8fab221b9ba4c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0a81a35fe50587e39b82df8bed7f241f</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210842</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hammurabi">
      <data key="d0">Hammurabi</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">("Hammurabi was a ruler who transformed his small kingdom into a powerful empire, known for establishing a set of laws and for receiving royal insignia from a god. He is credited with the development and codification of the legal system, which left a lasting legacy in legal traditions.")</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0a81a35fe50587e39b82df8bed7f241f</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210836</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Kingdom of Hammurabi's Inheritance">
      <data key="d0">Kingdom of Hammurabi's Inheritance</data>
      <data key="d1">geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">("This refers to the initial small kingdom that Hammurabi inherited, situated in Mesopotamia from which he later expanded and formed the Babylonian Empire.")</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0a81a35fe50587e39b82df8bed7f241f</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210836</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Assyria">
      <data key="d0">Assyria</data>
      <data key="d1">civilization_or_empire</data>
      <data key="d2">Assyria was a major Mesopotamian civilization that ruled large parts of Western Asia from around 2500 BCE to approximately 612 BCE, recognized both for its powerful military and intricate bureaucratic organization. During this period, Assyria was frequently referenced as a significant power and adversary against which Hammurabi sent his armies, illustrating its pivotal role in the politics and stability of the region. The kingdom was renowned for its mighty military strength and opulent palaces, solidifying its status as a formidable force in ancient Mesopotamia.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-140c5ae98caac7c02297295e6317e72f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0a81a35fe50587e39b82df8bed7f241f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c1d02d55a0d3b389107528715e89a23a</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210841</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Zagros Mountains">
      <data key="d0">Zagros Mountains</data>
      <data key="d1">geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">("The Zagros Mountains are significant geographical regions where resistance to the Babylonian Empire occurred under Samsu-iluna's reign. This mountain range posed a significant challenge for the expansion and consolidation of the empire’s control.")</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0a81a35fe50587e39b82df8bed7f241f</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210836</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Persian Gulf">
      <data key="d0">Persian Gulf</data>
      <data key="d1">geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">("The Persian Gulf is mentioned as part of the southern border during the height of Hammurabi’s reign, suggesting that his empire extended to the shoreline of this geographical feature.")</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0a81a35fe50587e39b82df8bed7f241f</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210836</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Kassites">
      <data key="d0">Kassites</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">("The Kassites are a group that caused resistance to Babylonian rule under Samsu-iluna's reign. Their actions represented one of many challenges faced by the empire as it sought to maintain control over its territories.")</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0a81a35fe50587e39b82df8bed7f241f</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210836</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Sealand Kingdom">
      <data key="d0">Sealand Kingdom</data>
      <data key="d1">civilization_or_empire</data>
      <data key="d2">("Sealand is a newly formed kingdom that emerged in the marshy region near the Persian Gulf, posing resistance to the Babylonian Empire under Samsu-iluna’s rule. Its existence and independence challenged Hammurabi’s efforts to consolidate power.")</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0a81a35fe50587e39b82df8bed7f241f</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210836</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hittites">
      <data key="d0">Hittites</data>
      <data key="d1">civilization_or_empire</data>
      <data key="d2">The Hittites were an Indo-European-speaking power that emerged as a dominant force in Anatolia around 1600 BCE, distinct from the Semitic-speaking Babylonians. This emergence was marked by their significant geopolitical impact, particularly highlighted by the sacking of Babylon and signaling the end of Hammurabi's dynasty. These actions underscored a major shift in the political landscape of the region during this period.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6c7a4a569c62f9e0a3d17c9150fbdd9d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0a81a35fe50587e39b82df8bed7f241f</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210842</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hammurabis Realm">
      <data key="d0">Hammurabis Realm</data>
      <data key="d1">geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">"Hammurabi's Realm refers to the geographical area under Hammurabi's control during his reign. It is shown in green on the map.")</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6c7a4a569c62f9e0a3d17c9150fbdd9d</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210837</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Babylonians">
      <data key="d0">Babylonians</data>
      <data key="d1">civilization_or_empire</data>
      <data key="d2">The Babylonians were a civilization led by Hammurabi, who expanded to conquer much of Mesopotamia and established the first empire in the region. Additionally, they successfully managed to defeat the Assyrians and gained control over parts of the former Neo-Assyrian Empire. These achievements highlight the significant military prowess and territorial expansion under Babylonian leadership.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6c7a4a569c62f9e0a3d17c9150fbdd9d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-260b1cd2746e3353aa446913dc7c88d2</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210842</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Anatolia">
      <data key="d0">Anatolia</data>
      <data key="d1">geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">Anatolia, also known as ancient Asia Minor, is a significant geographical and historical region located in the western part of modern-day Turkey. Anatolia played a crucial role in various historical events and military campaigns, such as during Mehmed II's conquest where vassals from the region were summoned to provide soldiers for his army. The Hittites originated in this area, setting it apart from Mesopotamia. Additionally, many important historical events have occurred in Anatolia, including the defeat of Bayezid I by Timur during his invasion.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6c7a4a569c62f9e0a3d17c9150fbdd9d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-81aaa6bf9b333d06305b8e02f056d212&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-325c2f087ce183f26e739c5d7d7a119e</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210845</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mursilis">
      <data key="d0">Mursilis</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">Mursilis was the ruler of the Hittite Empire who expanded its borders and took Babylon, but whose power weakened after his absence.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6c7a4a569c62f9e0a3d17c9150fbdd9d</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210838</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Battle of Qadesh">
      <data key="d0">Battle of Qadesh</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">The Battle of Qadesh, fought near Qades in approximately 1274 BCE, was a significant conflict between the Hittite Empire and the Egyptian New Kingdom under Pharaoh Ramses II. This battle is renowned for showcasing Ramses II's exceptional bravery and strategic abilities. The epic engagement aimed to secure control over the eastern Mediterranean region.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6c7a4a569c62f9e0a3d17c9150fbdd9d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-81ce12ad3d24ade27cd421454056d695</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210845</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hittite Empire">
      <data key="d0">Hittite Empire</data>
      <data key="d1">civilization_or_empire</data>
      <data key="d2">"The Hittite Empire is a historical civilization that extended over parts of modern-day Turkey, Syria, and other territories at its peak in the thirteenth century BCE."&lt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5d1179d415100f7415f3b8329f3acfa2</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210839</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Egypto-Hittite Peace Treaty">
      <data key="d0">Egypto-Hittite Peace Treaty</data>
      <data key="d1">source_or_artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">"The Egypto-Hittite Peace Treaty is a historical artifact from 1258 BCE which established peace between ancient Egypt and the Hittite Empire.")&lt;|</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a3a4ace18f781f226af8fab221b9ba4c</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210839</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mythology">
      <data key="d0">Mythology</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">Mythology encompasses the traditional beliefs, legends, and narratives of a culture, providing foundational stories that explain natural phenomena, human origins, societal norms, and values, widely practiced in ancient Mesopotamian societies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a3a4ace18f781f226af8fab221b9ba4c</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210839</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Figure 4.10 Receiving Tribute&quot;&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Figure 4.10 Receiving Tribute"&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;"geographical_location"&gt;</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;"Assyria"&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d5d48d6e025626040d94a22696735414</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210840</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Babylonian period&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Babylonian period&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">time_period</data>
      <data key="d2">Comprehensive description of the Babylonian period encompassing the history and civilizations that flourished in Mesopotamia during this era, including early Sumerian culture, Old Babylonian Kingdom, and Assyrian influence.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fa4fdd673b3c8b55420298adc9d54361</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210840</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Mesopotamia&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Mesopotamia&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">Mesopotamia is a historical region located in Western Asia that spans parts of modern-day Iraq, Kuwait, eastern Syria, parts of Libya, and northwest Iran. This influential area, historically known as the land "between rivers" due to its position between the Tigris and Euphrates Rivers (which also extends into present-day western Iran, northeastern Syria, and southeastern Turkey), was home to ancient Mesopotamian civilizations that had a significant impact on art and iconography, particularly influencing the depiction of divine figures like Ahura Mazda. These early civilizations played a crucial role in shaping cultural traditions and providing artistic inspiration to other regions including Egypt.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2e05c323f2387cfb1c337fb65115e181&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fd503ba9613920462a3a6dc53ae595a8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa4fdd673b3c8b55420298adc9d54361</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210847</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Babylonia, Assyria&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Babylonia, Assyria&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">civilization_or_empire</data>
      <data key="d2">Refers specifically to the kingdoms of Babylonia and Assyria, which were prominent in the ancient Near East. Babylonia was located south of Assyria, known for its rich culture, legal codes, and religion, while Assyria was an empire characterized by its military prowess.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fa4fdd673b3c8b55420298adc9d54361</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210841</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;the elevation of Marduk to the highest tier of the pantheon during Hammurabis dynasty, New Year Festival&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;the elevation of Marduk to the highest tier of the pantheon during Hammurabis dynasty, New Year Festival&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">During Hammurabi's reign in Babylon, Marduk was raised to the highest rank among gods and the festival celebrating this event, known as Akitu or the New Year Festival, solidified Marduk's role as the chief deity.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fa4fdd673b3c8b55420298adc9d54361</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210841</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Hammurabi&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Hammurabi&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">The sixth king of Babylon’s First Dynasty, Hammurabi is best known for his extensive legal code and reforms that aimed to establish a just social order in Babylonia. During his reign, Marduk's status was elevated.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fa4fdd673b3c8b55420298adc9d54361</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210842</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Enki (Ea), Sin (Nanna), Shamash (Utu)&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Enki (Ea), Sin (Nanna), Shamash (Utu)&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">Key deities of Babylonian religion: Enki (also known as Ea) is the god of water and intelligence; Sin (also known as Nanna) is the moon god, associated with wisdom and knowledge; Shamash (also known as Utu) is the sun god, associated with law and justice.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fa4fdd673b3c8b55420298adc9d54361</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210842</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Babylonian religion, Mesopotamian religious practices&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Babylonian religion, Mesopotamian religious practices&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">cultural_or_religious_movement</data>
      <data key="d2">The complex set of beliefs and rituals practiced by ancient civilizations in Mesopotamia, including worship rites dedicated to various gods, temple ceremonies involving offerings, and community festivals.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fa4fdd673b3c8b55420298adc9d54361</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210842</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Adda Seal, British Museum&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Adda Seal, British Museum&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">source_or_artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">An artifact from the British Museum that features a detail depicting Enki (Ea), one of the prominent deities in Mesopotamian mythology, which is estimated to be from around 2300 BCE.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fa4fdd673b3c8b55420298adc9d54361</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210842</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Chapter 4.11&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Chapter 4.11&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">textbook_structure</data>
      <data key="d2">Section of a textbook discussing particular aspects of ancient Babylonia and its religious and cultural practices.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fa4fdd673b3c8b55420298adc9d54361</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210842</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Neo-Assyrian Empire">
      <data key="d0">Neo-Assyrian Empire</data>
      <data key="d1">time_period,civilization_or_empire</data>
      <data key="d2">The Neo-Assyrian Empire was a period of extensive military conquests characterized by a highly organized standing army where service was expected from Assyrians. The king during this time was considered a viceroy of the gods, reflecting a strong religious and political framework. This empire emerged in the late 8th century BCE, rising to power and expanding into Canaan. Consequently, it led Israel and Judah into an era of foreign domination within the broader region under Assyrian control.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-34166fd0e8c3a3a12c955d75e396404c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8eda6b23d003c8e57a8bc25015984894</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210847</data>
    </node>
    <node id="deportation of entire populations">
      <data key="d0">deportation of entire populations</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_event</data>
      <data key="d2">"Deportation of entire populations refers to the forced relocation of large groups of people to other parts of the empire for various reasons, including cultural assimilation and control.")</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8ebd4501968f47aeb33713529271f56c</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210842</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Deportation of entire populations">
      <data key="d0">Deportation of entire populations</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_event</data>
      <data key="d2">Deportations involved the forced relocation of large groups of people for reasons such as cultural assimilation and control.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8ebd4501968f47aeb33713529271f56c</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210842</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Neo-Assyrian War Machine">
      <data key="d0">Neo-Assyrian War Machine</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">The Neo-Assyrian War Machine refers to a highly efficient military organization that was one of the most modern and effective in ancient times.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8ebd4501968f47aeb33713529271f56c</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210842</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Inscriptions, art, Bible">
      <data key="d0">Inscriptions, art, Bible</data>
      <data key="d1">source_or_artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">Inscriptions, art, and even the Bible are primary sources providing evidence about the practices and advancements of the Neo-Assyrian army.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8ebd4501968f47aeb33713529271f56c</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210842</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Neo-Assyrian military">
