    <node id="&lt;Amenhotep I&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Amenhotep I&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">Amenhotep I was the seventh pharaoh of the 18th dynasty of Egypt. He succeeded his father Ahmose, reigning from around 1526 to 1506 BCE. Amenhotep I's reign is marked by limited military campaigns and political stability.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fd503ba9613920462a3a6dc53ae595a8</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210843</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Thutmose I&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Thutmose I&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">Thutmose I was the second pharaoh of the 18th dynasty of Egypt, ruling from around 1504 to 1492 BCE. He expanded Egyptian holdings into Syria during his reign and built the Temple of Karnak.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fd503ba9613920462a3a6dc53ae595a8</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210843</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Thutmose II&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Thutmose II&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">Thutmose II was the third pharaoh of the 18th dynasty of Egypt, reigning from around 1493 to 1480 BCE. He continued his father's military campaigns and is known for his expedition into Nubia.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fd503ba9613920462a3a6dc53ae595a8</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210843</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Thutmose III&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Thutmose III&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">Thutmose III was the sixth pharaoh of the 18th dynasty of Egypt, ruling from around 1479 to 1425 BCE. He is one of the most powerful and successful military leaders in ancient history, known for his extensive campaigns into Syria.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fd503ba9613920462a3a6dc53ae595a8</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210843</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Hatshepsut&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Hatshepsut&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">Hatshepsut was the second historical female pharaoh of Egypt, ruling from around 1479 to 1458 BCE. She ruled during a time of peace and prosperity, known for her ambitious building projects, including the Temple of Deir el-Bahri.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fd503ba9613920462a3a6dc53ae595a8</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210843</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Nubia&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Nubia&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">Nubia is an ancient cultural region in northeastern Africa, located to the south of Egypt. It was part of the historical regions between the First and Second Cataracts of the Nile River. Nubia played a significant role in Ancient Egyptian trade.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fd503ba9613920462a3a6dc53ae595a8</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210843</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Syria&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Syria&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">Syria is an ancient land and one of the earliest centers of civilization within the Fertile Crescent. It is the region where many early cultures developed including the Euphrates Civilization, which influenced Egyptian expeditions through its strategic trade position.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fd503ba9613920462a3a6dc53ae595a8</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210843</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Succession_of_pharaohs&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Succession_of_pharaohs&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">The succession of pharaohs refers to the process through which Egyptian kings passed power from one ruler to another. This period is marked by complex family relationships and alliances, religious customs, military campaigns, and political strategies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fd503ba9613920462a3a6dc53ae595a8</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210843</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;time_period&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;time_period&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">time_period</data>
      <data key="d2">The time_period entity encompasses various distinct historical intervals, each marked by significant developments and changes:

- The 1400s (Late Middle Ages or Renaissance) saw major transformations and advancements across Europe.
- The early Abbasid period highlighted a crucial phase characterized by extensive scholarly and translational efforts.
- A narrower timeframe from the Third Century CE (201 to 300 AD), marked by significant cultural and political progress.
- Another specific interval, from 36 BCE to 31 BCE, saw critical historical events such as:
  - The crushing of the optimates and the division of Rome.
  - Important battles, including the naval battle of Actium.

These descriptions collectively outline a broad timeline ranging from the early Abbasid era through multiple centuries, highlighting different periods of significant cultural, scholarly, political, and military developments.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5914a1bc816edf3338d898be6d68146b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-89576c0d4225b9ce6218c684fd4f58b7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-48ba3692af8d78d86183a9d177cdb16d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b8ad3f8265df80ff0cbd95a7914a6123&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4809fc11398ec2c07172fc5d5bf5447f</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210816</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Regency&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Regency&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">A regency in ancient Egypt referred to a form of government where power was temporarily held by someone other than the legitimate ruler due to his youth or absence. Hatshepsut played a significant role as regent before fully assuming the throne.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fd503ba9613920462a3a6dc53ae595a8</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210843</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Military_campaigns&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Military_campaigns&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">Military campaigns under Egyptian pharaohs, especially during the 18th dynasty, involved extensive expeditions to Syria and Nubia. These campaigns were crucial for expansion of territory, control over trade routes, and demonstrating divine authority.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fd503ba9613920462a3a6dc53ae595a8</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210843</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Divine_right_to_rule&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Divine_right_to_rule&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">The concept of *divine right to rule* asserts that the pharaohs of ancient Egypt derived their legitimacy from their supposed direct connection with deities. This belief was used to explain and legitimize their rule, as well as to control power.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fd503ba9613920462a3a6dc53ae595a8</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210843</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;15th Dynasty of Egypt&quot;|time_period)">
      <data key="d0">"15th Dynasty of Egypt"|time_period)</data>
      <data key="d1">Hatshepsut’s reign"|historical_concept_or_event)</data>
      <data key="d2">"Punt"|geographical_location)</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ea1d66e7e1ca6fb29c879b644db4aab6</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210843</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hatshepsuts reign">
      <data key="d0">Hatshepsuts reign</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">The period of Hatshepsut's rule as pharaoh during the 18th Dynasty, characterized by significant trade expeditions and building projects.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ea1d66e7e1ca6fb29c879b644db4aab6</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210844</data>
    </node>
    <node id="15th Dynasty of Egypt">
      <data key="d0">15th Dynasty of Egypt</data>
      <data key="d1">civilization_or_empire</data>
      <data key="d2">Historically inaccurately named; it actually refers to the New Kingdom during Hatshepsut's reign (c. 1479-1458 BCE).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ea1d66e7e1ca6fb29c879b644db4aab6</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210844</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Deir el-Bahri">
      <data key="d0">Deir el-Bahri</data>
      <data key="d1">geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">A site in upper Egypt where the Temple of Hatshepsut is located, near Luxor.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ea1d66e7e1ca6fb29c879b644db4aab6</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
