    <node id="&lt;&quot;Abu Simbel Temple&quot;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Abu Simbel Temple"</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;&gt;The Abu Simbel Temple is an ancient Egyptian monument constructed during the reign of Ramesses II. It consists of two monumental rock temples located in southern Egypt, near the Sudan border. The temple features four massive statues of Ramesses II and serves as a symbol of his glory and achievements.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-90a0cd35a7a0cf833524887a685de2a2</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210845</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Aswan Dam&quot;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Aswan Dam"</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;engineering_project</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;&gt;The Aswan Dam is a large dam on the Nile River in southern Egypt. Its construction in the 1960s led to the need for relocating the Abu Simbel Temple to protect it from flooding.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-90a0cd35a7a0cf833524887a685de2a2</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210845</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Thutmose III&quot;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Thutmose III"</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;&gt;Thutmose III was an Egyptian Pharaoh who ruled during the New Kingdom period. Known as "the Napoleon of Egypt," he was renowned for his military conquests and diplomatic efforts to maintain Egyptian dominance in the region.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-90a0cd35a7a0cf833524887a685de2a2</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210845</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Seti I&quot;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Seti I"</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;&gt;Seti I was a pharaoh of the 19th dynasty who ruled during the 13th century BCE. He continued the policies initiated by Thutmose III to secure loyalty in Canaan and weaken Mitanni.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-90a0cd35a7a0cf833524887a685de2a2</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210845</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Ramesses II&quot;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Ramesses II"</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;&gt;Ramesses II, also known as Ramses or Rameses, was a pharaoh of Egypt who reigned during the 19th dynasty. He commissioned the construction of Abu Simbel Temple and maintained his grandfather's policies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-90a0cd35a7a0cf833524887a685de2a2</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210845</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Canaan&quot;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Canaan"</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">Canaan is an entity deeply rooted in biblical history as the land where Abraham traveled and became associated with according to the Bible. Historically, Canaan corresponds roughly to parts of present-day Israel, Palestine, Lebanon, Jordan, and parts of Syria. This region played a significant role during the New Kingdom period of ancient Egypt, being crucial for both diplomatic and military engagements.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-90a0cd35a7a0cf833524887a685de2a2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-39ae02e5e637fac459bc83d51db22137</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210850</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Mitanni&quot;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Mitanni"</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;civilization_or_empire</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;&gt;Mitanni was an ancient Hurrian-speaking kingdom that flourished in eastern Anatolia and northwestern Syria between about 1500 BCE to 1300 BCE. It had a complex relationship with Egypt, involving both conflict and diplomacy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-90a0cd35a7a0cf833524887a685de2a2</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210845</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Nubia&quot;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Nubia"</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">Nubia is a geographical region located south of the second cataract of the Nile River, encompassing parts of modern-day Sudan and southern Egypt. This historic area has been home to multiple Nubian states, including Kerma, and has experienced significant interaction with Egyptian pharaohs who conducted campaigns there for wealth acquisition and territorial expansion. The region boasts a rich historical legacy marked by cultural exchanges and political sovereignty.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-90a0cd35a7a0cf833524887a685de2a2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d7edb5c90c1347b6f59e1607916d8804</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210851</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Eastern Mediterranean">
      <data key="d0">Eastern Mediterranean</data>
      <data key="d1">geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">The Eastern Mediterranean, which encompasses regions including Canaan and Syria, played a crucial role in ancient trade networks by serving as vital connecting routes between Mesopotamia and Egypt as well as other parts of the world. This region was also significant for its religious importance, particularly to the Christian Church, which managed to thrive despite facing challenges due to wars on the eastern frontier. Additionally, the Eastern Mediterranean became a focal point in Byzantine history, characterizing interactions and migrations among various states within this geopolitical area.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7c6e6bce081c423fde8b3531385165b5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-921eca26480da1b32897b0b58823f7d8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f82fcaeaf8ded7ccd594a70788c5c5e9</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210852</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Canaan">
      <data key="d0">Canaan</data>
      <data key="d1">geographical_location historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">Canaan is a historic region situated roughly between the Sinai Peninsula and northern Mesopotamia, known for being home to various civilizations and empires throughout history. This strategic location also positions Canaan as a crucial trade hub in the Eastern Mediterranean, serving as an essential connection point between Mesopotamian trade routes and major centers such as Egypt or other regions. Canaan played significant roles in facilitating trade, particularly in transporting goods like papyrus from Byblos to Egypt, underlining its importance in ancient economic systems.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-722b57dcb585512b05d668236596f71a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-921eca26480da1b32897b0b58823f7d8</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210853</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Nubia">
      <data key="d0">Nubia</data>
      <data key="d1">geographical_location historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">Nubia is a significant geographic and historical region that stretches from northern Sudan to southern Egypt. Known for its rich natural resources such as ivory, ebony, leopard skins, incense, and gold, Nubia served as an essential hub in the trade networks linking ancient Egypt with other parts of the Eastern Mediterranean. This area was not only economically important due to its resource exports but also historically significant as the homeland of the Kingdom of Kush, which originated and expanded within this region.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fd4afb589af42f7e98b4b08ee50decdd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-921eca26480da1b32897b0b58823f7d8</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210854</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Sinai">
      <data key="d0">Sinai</data>
      <data key="d1">geographical_location historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">Sinai produces essential resources like turquoise, alabaster, and quartzite for the broader Eastern Mediterranean trade network. These were significant components in various trades conducted within this region.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-921eca26480da1b32897b0b58823f7d8</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210849</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hittite Kingdom">
      <data key="d0">Hittite Kingdom</data>
      <data key="d1">civilization_or_empire</data>
      <data key="d2">The Hittite Kingdom was one of the important realms in maintaining control over eastern Mediterranean trade routes, often competing with Egypt for dominance.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-921eca26480da1b32897b0b58823f7d8</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210849</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mitanni Kingdom">
      <data key="d0">Mitanni Kingdom</data>
      <data key="d1">civilization_or_empire</data>
      <data key="d2">Mitanni Kingdom is another powerful empire engaging in trading activities and attempting to thwart Egyptian influence on Eastern Mediterranean trade routes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-921eca26480da1b32897b0b58823f7d8</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210849</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tin">
      <data key="d0">Tin</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">Tin was a key trading good obtained through Mesopotamian trade routes, often transported via Canaan. It played a significant role in various trade networks within the Eastern Mediterranean region.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-921eca26480da1b32897b0b58823f7d8</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210849</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Copper">
      <data key="d0">Copper</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">Copper is another crucial trading resource coming from Anatolia and transported to Egypt or other parts of the Eastern Mediterranean through various trade routes, such as those connecting Mesopotamia with Canaan.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-921eca26480da1b32897b0b58823f7d8</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210849</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Papyrus">
      <data key="d0">Papyrus</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">Papyrus was a significant trading item originating from the Phoenician port city Byblos. It was valued for constructing Egyptian ships and served as an essential resource in trade networks.&lt;SEP&gt;Papyrus was a writing material like paper used in ancient Egypt, made from fibers of a reed plant growing along the banks of the Nile, often rolled into scrolls for storage and preservation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3480f6387c4263e5b94d6ebc80dbe087&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-921eca26480da1b32897b0b58823f7d8</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210819</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Byblos">
      <data key="d0">Byblos</data>
      <data key="d1">geographical_location cultural_or_religious_movement</data>
      <data key="d2">The Phoenician port city of Byblos is a key hub for papyrus and other goods, contributing to trade with Egypt and the broader Eastern Mediterranean. Not only does it have commercial significance, but it also provides a cultural connection as the origin of the English word 'Bible'.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-921eca26480da1b32897b0b58823f7d8</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210849</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Taxes">
      <data key="d0">Taxes</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">Taxes or tolls likely applied on trading goods within various trade networks, facilitating state control and revenue. This concept is associated with managing these economic activities.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-921eca26480da1b32897b0b58823f7d8</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210849</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Late Bronze Age Collapse">
      <data key="d0">Late Bronze Age Collapse</data>
      <data key="d1">time_period</data>
      <data key="d2">A period of significant disruption in various regions including Egypt, Mycenaean kingdoms, Hittite kingdom, Syria, Canaan, and others during the 12th century BCE.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-165d93bf9daeb1d5f3c05cf618b21e7b</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210849</data>
    </node>
    <node id="New Kingdom Egypt">
      <data key="d0">New Kingdom Egypt</data>
      <data key="d1">civilization_or_empire</data>
      <data key="d2">The height of ancient Egyptian civilization from approximately 1570–1085 BCE, known for its strong economy and extensive trade networks.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-165d93bf9daeb1d5f3c05cf618b21e7b</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210849</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Sea Peoples">
      <data key="d0">Sea Peoples</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">A collective term used to describe various groups presumed responsible for attacking the Levant and Egypt during the Late Bronze Age Collapse.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-165d93bf9daeb1d5f3c05cf618b21e7b</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210849</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ramesses III">
      <data key="d0">Ramesses III</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">The last great Pharaoh of the 20th dynasty, fought against the Sea Peoples, and is known from his battles and temple inscriptions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-165d93bf9daeb1d5f3c05cf618b21e7b</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210849</data>
    </node>
