    <node id="&lt;&quot;Babylonia and Media&quot;&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Babylonia and Media"&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;historical_figure&gt;</data>
      <data key="d2">Babylonia and Media were historical geopolitical regions that frequently referred to a combined state or alliance during the Achaemenid period. This political union or alliance typically involved the merging of Babylonia and the Median empire into a single entity during the first millennium BCE, reflecting their collaborative efforts in governance and administration within the broader context of the Achaemenid Empire.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b7685a6f4e6e873aa232bfe92f1fbd33</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210856</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Zoroastrianism&quot;&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Zoroastrianism"&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;cultural_or_religious_movement&gt;</data>
      <data key="d2">Zoroastrianism is an ancient Persian religious tradition founded by Zoroaster in the 6th century BCE. This religion emphasizes dualism, the eternal struggle between good and evil, and the importance of moral choices. It has significantly influenced both Persian religion and culture. Prior to the Arab-Muslim conquests, Zoroastrianism was the dominant faith in Persia; however, its influence gradually waned among the common people during this period.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-927613aa4aec81a8719529ae83d4bfd6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b7685a6f4e6e873aa232bfe92f1fbd33</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210857</data>
    </node>
