    <node id="&lt;&quot;Ancient Egypt&quot;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Ancient Egypt"</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;civilization_or_empire</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;"Ancient Egypt is an historical term used to refer to the period of pharaonic civilisation in Northeast Africa encompassing various periods, from the rise of the first dynasty around 3100 BC until the conquest by Alexander the Great in 332 BC. Its civilization left a profound cultural and architectural legacy and influenced many aspects including religion, politics, art, technology, and literature."</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-defab705e0941333d4e1516cced12575</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210851</data>
    </node>
    <node id="time_period">
      <data key="d0">time_period</data>
      <data key="d1">time_period</data>
      <data key="d2">During several distinct time_periods, significant historical developments occurred across various contexts. The Tang Empire Period refers to a crucial era when the Tang Dynasty reached its peak, marking great prosperity both domestically and externally. However, internal pressures and external threats began to emerge during this era, eventually contributing to the empire's decline.

Another pivotal period is associated with notable scientific and technological advancements. This could refer to any epoch renowned for such innovations, though specific details are not provided here.

The Hellenistic Period marks the time following Alexander the Great’s conquests, when Greek culture spread extensively across a vast territory, including parts of Africa, Asia, and Europe. This era was characterized by significant political, social, and religious developments as various Greek cities were influenced by emerging Hellenic kingdoms.

Lastly, the period during which detailed maps of Africa were created highlights the importance of geographic mapping in historical education and documentation. While serving educational purposes, these maps reflect broader efforts to understand and document African landscapes and civilizations.

In summary, these time_periods are marked not only by achievements and growth but also by challenges that shaped their legacies—such as the zenith of the Tang Empire, pivotal discoveries, cultural expansion during the Hellenistic era, and mapping endeavors in Africa.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-82b28f56f2ef832a14237e5150ea2d0b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c7fc526d4d7b88eb3c5457b58cd983ec&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c0ec7ff4392fec904160cf53b1ab7318&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-66bf9f3cc9430a0eef2cdadfeaad7643&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-91a847d29a702803fcc6217de2a69c95</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210805</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Pharaohs&quot;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Pharaohs"</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;"A title representing the ancient Egyptian kings who were believed to have divine legitimacy and were seen as intermediaries between gods and people. They ruled from a variety of dynasties throughout Ancient Egypt’s history, with famous figures like Ramses II and Tutankhamun."</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-defab705e0941333d4e1516cced12575</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210852</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Pyramids&quot;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Pyramids"</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;source_or_artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;"Ancient pyramidal structures built primarily by ancient Egyptians as tombs for their pharaohs, often believed to be symbolic representations of the mastaba, an earlier form of tombs in ancient Mesopotamia. They were a significant architectural and engineering feat showcasing Egypt's technological advancements."</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-defab705e0941333d4e1516cced12575</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210852</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Persian Empire under Darius I">
      <data key="d0">Persian Empire under Darius I</data>
      <data key="d1">geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">"The Persian Empire under Darius I extended from India in the east to Libya in the west. It was managed through a system of satrapies and a sophisticated communication network.")</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bc5aadc970171edc2b4b7ab0c901e46e</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210852</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Satraps">
      <data key="d0">Satraps</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">Satraps were regional governors under the Persian Empire during Darius I's reign. They managed local governance in satrapies but ultimately reported to the king for accountability. Their primary responsibilities included overseeing tax collection, military affairs, and maintaining order.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bc5aadc970171edc2b4b7ab0c901e46e</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210852</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Royal Officials">
      <data key="d0">Royal Officials</data>
      <data key="d1">royal_officials</data>
      <data key="d2">Royal officials, known as 'the eyes and ears of the great king,' conducted unannounced audits of satraps across the empire to ensure proper governance. They relayed information directly back to the king for review and decision-making.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bc5aadc970171edc2b4b7ab0c901e46e</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210852</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Garrisons">
      <data key="d0">Garrisons</data>
      <data key="d1">cultural_or_religious_movement</data>
      <data key="d2">Garrisons enforced laws and maintained security within satrapies, directly reporting any issues or actions for review by the central government and the king. They were positioned strategically across the empire to ensure rapid response capabilities.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bc5aadc970171edc2b4b7ab0c901e46e</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210852</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Royal Roads">
      <data key="d0">Royal Roads</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">The Persian Empire's sophisticated communication network included a series of roads known as 'royal roads.' These roads were used for rapid and secure transmission of information across vast distances, ensuring efficient administrative operations during Darius I’s reign.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bc5aadc970171edc2b4b7ab0c901e46e</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210852</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Message Delivery System">
      <data key="d0">Message Delivery System</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">The system for delivering messages involved army messengers traveling along the royal road network to inns and waystations for fast horse changes. It ensured rapid communication over long distances within a short timeframe.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bc5aadc970171edc2b4b7ab0c901e46e</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210852</data>
    </node>
    <node id="First Station Travel">
      <data key="d0">First Station Travel</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">Army messengers would travel about 20 miles to the first station, change horses, and continue onward. This process ensured timely message delivery throughout the empire’s vast territories.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bc5aadc970171edc2b4b7ab0c901e46e</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210852</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Persian Empire">
      <data key="d0">Persian Empire</data>
      <data key="d1">civilization_or_empire</data>
      <data key="d2">The Persian Empire was one of the largest empires in antiquity and held sway over a vast area encompassing parts of Asia, Africa, Europe, Central Asia, the Caucasus, Eastern Europe, North Africa, and South Asia during its height. It was renowned for its sophisticated communications network and excellent administration skills under leaders such as Darius I and Xerxes I. The empire is often recognized as one of the largest of its time, stretching to the farthest corners of Western Asia, Central Asia, parts of Eastern Europe, North Africa, and South Asia, with significant influence under the rule of notable figures like Darius I and Xerxes I.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9cd8db275ef55c04230f868e23c45f8c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b0c96eda8ec75ff7b7bb50a03f72ca9a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-17ddaf0fb532418dfd517db22df102e9</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210859</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Eighth Avenue Post Office">
      <data key="d0">Eighth Avenue Post Office</data>
      <data key="d1">geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">The Eighth Avenue Post Office is a post office located in New York City that features an inscription inspired by the ancient Persian courier system. The inscription is displayed at this location, serving as a reminder of the historical significance and efficiency of ancient communication methods.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9cd8db275ef55c04230f868e23c45f8c</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210852</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Herodotus Histories">
      <data key="d0">Herodotus Histories</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">The Histories are a historical account written by Herodotus that provide detailed information about various civilizations, including the Persian Empire. In his work, Herodotus described the efficient courier system used by the Persians known as angareion.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9cd8db275ef55c04230f868e23c45f8c</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210852</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Persian Courier System (angareion)">
      <data key="d0">Persian Courier System (angareion)</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">The angareion was a sophisticated and rapid communication network implemented during the reign of the Persian Empire. It allowed for the swift transmission of messages over vast distances, serving both administrative and military purposes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9cd8db275ef55c04230f868e23c45f8c</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210852</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Persian courier system">
      <data key="d0">Persian courier system</data>
      <data key="d1">source_or_artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">The Persian courier system, an efficient communication network used by the empire to relay information swiftly.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-23c4f3285e56a84c6b2d0c9af99400d8</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210852</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Qanats for Irrigation">
      <data key="d0">Qanats for Irrigation</data>
      <data key="d1">source_or_artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">Qanats built by Darius were used to carry fresh water over long distances into populated areas or to irrigate land for farming.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-128d8329b0d933db6104dc96fcde9a02</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210852</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Ahura Mazda&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Ahura Mazda&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">concept_or_deity</data>
      <data key="d2">The supreme deity in Zoroastrianism, symbolizing the force of good and wisdom. Associated with light and truth, often depicted in art with a crown or rays of light.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2e05c323f2387cfb1c337fb65115e181</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210852</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Zoroastrianism&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Zoroastrianism&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">religion</data>
      <data key="d2">A religious movement centered around the teachings of Zoroaster and the core concepts of good versus evil, particularly focusing on the opposition between Ahura Mazda (good) and Angra Mainu (evil).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2e05c323f2387cfb1c337fb65115e181</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210852</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Zoroaster&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Zoroaster&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">Founder of Zoroastrianism, believed to have lived between 1400 and 900 BCE, who promoted the belief in a single god, Ahura Mazda.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2e05c323f2387cfb1c337fb65115e181</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210852</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Angra Mainu&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Angra Mainu&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">concept_or_deity</data>
      <data key="d2">A destructive spirit representing the forces of wickedness and chaos in Zoroastrianism. Opposed to Ahura Mazda, symbolizing all that is opposed to good.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2e05c323f2387cfb1c337fb65115e181</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210852</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Persia&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Persia&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">The historical region where Zoroastrianism thrived, associated with Ahura Mazda's depictions and Persepolis as a cultural and religious center during the Achaemenid dynasty.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2e05c323f2387cfb1c337fb65115e181</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210852</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Indo-European speakers&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Indo-European speakers&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">cultural_or_religious_movement</data>
      <data key="d2">Migration group that brought polytheistic traditions to India similar to those of the Persians, influencing early Zoroastrian beliefs.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2e05c323f2387cfb1c337fb65115e181</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210852</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Persepolis&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Persepolis&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">Ancient Persian city where Ahura Mazda's image was prominently displayed, located in modern Iran and a symbol of Achaemenid power and religious significance.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2e05c323f2387cfb1c337fb65115e181</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210852</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Zarathustra&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Zarathustra&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">The original name or title for Zoroaster, likely the founder of Zoroastrianism who promoted religious reforms and teachings about a single god, Ahura Mazda.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2e05c323f2387cfb1c337fb65115e181</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210853</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Vedic traditions&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Vedic traditions&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">civilization_or_empire</data>
      <data key="d2">Religious practices associated with ancient Indo-European speakers that influenced early Zoroastrian beliefs. These include polytheistic worship and ritual practices carried over from earlier cultures.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2e05c323f2387cfb1c337fb65115e181</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210853</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Zoroastrianism&quot;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Zoroastrianism"</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;"cultural_or_religious_movement</data>
      <data key="d2">Zoroastrianism is an ancient religious tradition originating in Persia that emphasizes the conflict between good and evil, featuring a belief in a final judgment. This faith played a significant role in the governance and religious practices of the Persian Empire and had a profound influence on other religions, shaping their concepts of moral order and divine justice.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ffae3d9ce72ea12c539d4ad3129f19b5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e206803b8187f4934c68619b5564e588</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210858</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Judaism&quot;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Judaism"</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;"cultural_or_religious_movement</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;"Judaism is one of the oldest monotheistic religions, originated among the ancient Jewish people. It encompasses religious practices, beliefs, and laws derived from the Tanakh or Hebrew Bible, including concepts of a single God, moral values, and ethical conduct."</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ffae3d9ce72ea12c539d4ad3129f19b5</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210853</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Christianity&quot;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Christianity"</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;"cultural_or_religious_movement</data>
      <data key="d2">Christianity is a monotheistic religion that emerged from first-century Judaism, focusing on the life and teachings of Jesus Christ, who is revered as the Son of God. Central to Christian belief are concepts such as salvation, redemption, and moral living. This religious movement spread extensively throughout both the eastern and western halves of the empire, often encountering conflicts with pagan beliefs and practices.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ffae3d9ce72ea12c539d4ad3129f19b5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c2afe1adc2d651dcc1a06b89f50c8933</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210859</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Persian Empire&quot;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Persian Empire"</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;"geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">The Persian Empire refers to a series of ancient empires that spanned a vast area including parts of modern-day Iran, Iraq, Turkey, Egypt, and Central Asia. It significantly influenced the region's culture, religion, and politics through its extensive territorial reach and powerful administrative systems. The empire was particularly known for its religious practices under Zoroastrianism, which emphasized the dichotomy between good and evil, a doctrine that left a profound impact on Judaism and the Hebrew people.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ffae3d9ce72ea12c539d4ad3129f19b5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e206803b8187f4934c68619b5564e588</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Judeans&quot;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Judeans"</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;"civilization_or_empire</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;"Judean refers to the people or the inhabitants of the ancient Kingdom of Judah in what is now modern-day Israel. This term can also denote a religious community adhering to Judaism, often referred to as Jews."</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ffae3d9ce72ea12c539d4ad3129f19b5</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210855</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Tanakh&quot;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Tanakh"</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;"source_or_artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;"The Tanakh (also known as Hebrew Bible</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ffae3d9ce72ea12c539d4ad3129f19b5</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210855</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Old Testament&quot;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Old Testament"</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;"source_or_artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;"The Old Testament in Christian tradition consists of the same texts as the Hebrew Bible or Tanakh but includes additional books not found in the Jewish canon."</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ffae3d9ce72ea12c539d4ad3129f19b5</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210855</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Bible&quot;&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Bible"&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;"source_or_artifact"&gt;</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;"The Bible is a collection of texts considered sacred by Christians and Jews. It contains the stories of the Hebrews, including those of Abraham and his family, as well as teachings and instructions for believers."</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-39ae02e5e637fac459bc83d51db22137</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210855</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Mesopotamia&quot;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Mesopotamia"</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;"geographical_location"&gt;</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;"The ancient region known as Mesopotamia includes parts of present-day Iraq, where significant historical and archaeological sites like Ur are located."</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-39ae02e5e637fac459bc83d51db22137</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210855</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Ur&quot;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Ur"</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;"geographical_location"&gt;</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;"Ur is a city in southern Mesopotamia mentioned as Abraham's birthplace in the Bible and an important ancient city with significant archaeological remains."</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-39ae02e5e637fac459bc83d51db22137</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210855</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Hebrew Bible&quot;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Hebrew Bible"</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;"source_or_artifact"&gt;</data>
      <data key="d2">The Hebrew Bible is a collection of texts that serve as a crucial source for both historical narratives and religious practices related to the Hebrew people. It includes stories detailing the journey of Abraham and his family from Mesopotamia to Canaan, forming the foundation of their historical legacy. The Hebrew Bible provides significant insights into the religious beliefs, traditions, and conflicts experienced by the Hebrews throughout their history.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e206803b8187f4934c68619b5564e588&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-39ae02e5e637fac459bc83d51db22137</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210861</data>
    </node>
