    <node id="Ravi River">
      <data key="d0">Ravi River</data>
      <data key="d1">geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">The Ravi River, flowing through Pakistan, serves as the geographical location where ancient cities like Harappa were established, facilitating trade and communication with neighboring regions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-96a9973262a04f62e37d1ec6cfe70c42</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210858</data>
    </node>
    <node id="India">
      <data key="d0">India</data>
      <data key="d1">civilization_or_empire</data>
      <data key="d2">The civilization centered in present-day India is marked as a major cultural pole during ancient times, known for its city planning, standardized practices, and religious innovations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-96a9973262a04f62e37d1ec6cfe70c42</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210858</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Steppes">
      <data key="d0">Steppes</data>
      <data key="d1">geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">The Steppes refer to the vast plain regions between Asia's mountain ranges that served as conduits for nomadic trade and migration throughout ancient history.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-96a9973262a04f62e37d1ec6cfe70c42</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210858</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Koreans">
      <data key="d0">Koreans</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">The historical figure of Koreans refers to a group that, through migration and trade, developed their own distinct civilization influencing neighboring Asian regions like China and India.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-96a9973262a04f62e37d1ec6cfe70c42</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210858</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Japanese">
      <data key="d0">Japanese</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">The historical figure of Japanese people played a role in developing civilizations in Asia through migrations and trade connections with other regions, significantly impacting the broader cultural landscape.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-96a9973262a04f62e37d1ec6cfe70c42</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210858</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Southeast Asia">
      <data key="d0">Southeast Asia</data>
      <data key="d1">geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">Southeast Asia, a region including countries such as Indonesia, Malaysia, Myanmar, Thailand, Vietnam, and others, served as a significant area where ancient civilizations emerged through trade and migration from China and India. This exchange profoundly impacted local social structures and practices in the region, contributing to its rich cultural heritage.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7933b681356fd848600cd95d60b6d201&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-96a9973262a04f62e37d1ec6cfe70c42</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210862</data>
    </node>
    <node id="traveling monks">
      <data key="d0">traveling monks</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">Traveling monks were significant in spreading religious beliefs and knowledge across the vast regions of Asia, contributing to cultural exchanges between different civilizations during ancient times.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-96a9973262a04f62e37d1ec6cfe70c42</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210858</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ancient China">
      <data key="d0">Ancient China</data>
      <data key="d1">civilization_or_empire</data>
      <data key="d2">Ancient China represents one of the dominant civilizational poles in Asia, alongside India, characterized by impressive cities, kingdoms, and empires that significantly influenced regional history and culture for millennia. This term specifically refers to ancient times in the geographical area now known as China.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-96a9973262a04f62e37d1ec6cfe70c42&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-793114b9aad7a4e7d97590998af0342f</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210863</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Ancient Greece&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Ancient Greece&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;geographical_location&gt;</data>
      <data key="d2">Ancient Greece was a civilization in southeastern Europe during classical antiquity. It is known for its contributions to Western philosophy, literature, and political thought.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d074051902a427319c33ea918573bbf9</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210858</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Plato&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Plato&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;historical_figure&gt;</data>
      <data key="d2">Plato was an ancient Greek philosopher and student of Socrates who founded the Academy of Athens and made significant contributions to various fields, including metaphysics, ethics, and politics.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d074051902a427319c33ea918573bbf9</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210858</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Academy of Plato&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Academy of Plato&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;cultural_or_religious_movement&gt;</data>
      <data key="d2">The Academy of Plato was a philosophical institution in Classical Athens associated with the teachings and works of the philosopher Plato. It served as an intellectual center for studying philosophy, mathematics, and science.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d074051902a427319c33ea918573bbf9</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210858</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Egypt&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Egypt&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">Egypt was an ancient civilization based in northeastern North Africa, primarily along the banks of the Nile river. The culture reached its peak from approximately 3150 BCE to 30 BCE when it came under Roman rule. Known for significant achievements such as the construction of pyramids and temples, Ancient Egypt also boasted advanced agricultural techniques including irrigation methods. This civilization featured a complex social hierarchy with notable figures like pharaohs and mummies, contributing to its lasting legacy in regional politics throughout the Late Bronze Age and into the Iron Age.

Geographically, it was bounded by the Mediterranean Sea to the north, the Red Sea and Sudan to the east, Nubia and Libya to the south, and included the shorelines of Lebanon and Syria. This expansive territory underscored Ancient Egypt's political stability and sophisticated governance systems.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-31f3de74d24f9f5ce534259a76865d74&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b17e8c11e35dce286053409ecc49bab9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fd503ba9613920462a3a6dc53ae595a8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2b5f6c1ce60c5a963cfeed75216a1324</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210850</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Phaedo&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Phaedo&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;textbook_structure&gt;</data>
      <data key="d2">Phaedo is a dialogue written by Plato in which Socrates defends philosophy during his final day before execution. It focuses on the nature of immortality and knowledge.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d074051902a427319c33ea918573bbf9</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210858</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Early Dynasties">
      <data key="d0">Early Dynasties</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">Refers to one of the first political organizations that developed in ancient China, such as Xia and Shang dynasties.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-793114b9aad7a4e7d97590998af0342f</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210858</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Warring States Period">
      <data key="d0">Warring States Period</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">A period characterized by regional warfare between small Chinese states, which significantly influenced Chinese politics and culture during the 5th to 3rd centuries BC.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-793114b9aad7a4e7d97590998af0342f</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210858</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Terracotta Army">
      <data key="d0">Terracotta Army</data>
      <data key="d1">source_or_artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">The Terracotta Army is a collection of ancient terracotta sculptures depicting the armies that served the first Emperor of China, Qin Shi Huang. It was created in the 3rd century BCE to protect the emperor in his afterlife.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-793114b9aad7a4e7d97590998af0342f</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210858</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Great Wall">
      <data key="d0">Great Wall</data>
      <data key="d1">source_or_artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">An extensive defensive structure built over several centuries to protect imperial territories from invasions. The Great Wall as it is known today was mainly constructed during the Ming Dynasty, but parts of its earlier versions were built even much earlier.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-793114b9aad7a4e7d97590998af0342f</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210858</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Qin Empire">
      <data key="d0">Qin Empire</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">The Qin dynasty (221-206 BC) unified China for the first time and initiated monumental projects such as the Terracotta Army and construction of parts of the Great Wall. It laid the foundation for future Chinese dynasties.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-793114b9aad7a4e7d97590998af0342f</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210859</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Peking Man">
      <data key="d0">Peking Man</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">Peking Man, also known as Homo erectus pekinensis, is a species of the genus Homo that lived in the region now comprising northern China and southern Mongolia between 600,000 and 250,000 years ago.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-793114b9aad7a4e7d97590998af0342f</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210859</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Homo sapiens">
      <data key="d0">Homo sapiens</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">The species Homo sapiens emerged around 100,000 years ago in Africa and later spread to Asia. In China, the earliest known remains of modern humans date back about 80,000-60,000 years.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-793114b9aad7a4e7d97590998af0342f</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210859</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Paleolithic and Neolithic China">
      <data key="d0">Paleolithic and Neolithic China</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">Describes early prehistoric periods in China characterized by distinctive cultural complexes such as the Yangshao culture in the Yellow River valley and Hemudu culture in the lower reaches of Yangtze River. These cultures are marked by unique social structures, religious beliefs, and technological developments.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-793114b9aad7a4e7d97590998af0342f</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210859</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Prehistory">
      <data key="d0">Prehistory</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">This term encompasses all human history prior to written records, including archaeological evidence such as tools, fossils, and inscriptions. In the context of China, prehistory extends from the first emergence of humans in the region until around 3000 BCE when writing was invented.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-793114b9aad7a4e7d97590998af0342f</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210859</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Paleolithic period">
      <data key="d0">Paleolithic period</data>
      <data key="d1">time_period</data>
      <data key="d2">The Paleolithic period spans roughly 100,000 to 10,000 BCE and is characterized by early human development in China.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-990942779714eb1b55832fae0d8365e4</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210859</data>
    </node>
    <node id="rivers">
      <data key="d0">rivers</data>
      <data key="d1">geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">China's many rivers are important geographical features that influenced the way of life for prehistoric groups.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-990942779714eb1b55832fae0d8365e4</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210859</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mountains">
      <data key="d0">Mountains</data>
      <data key="d1">geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">Eastern and western parts of ancient China are characterized by mountain ranges.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-990942779714eb1b55832fae0d8365e4</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210859</data>
    </node>
    <node id="deserts">
      <data key="d0">deserts</data>
      <data key="d1">geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">Desert regions exist within ancient China's diverse geography.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-990942779714eb1b55832fae0d8365e4</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210859</data>
    </node>
    <node id="grasslands">
      <data key="d0">grasslands</data>
      <data key="d1">geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">Grasslands were part of the varied topography in different regions of ancient China.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-990942779714eb1b55832fae0d8365e4</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210859</data>
    </node>
    <node id="high plateaus">
      <data key="d0">high plateaus</data>
      <data key="d1">geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">High plateaus contributed to regional variations among early cultures in ancient China.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-990942779714eb1b55832fae0d8365e4</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210859</data>
    </node>
    <node id="jungles">
      <data key="d0">jungles</data>
      <data key="d1">geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2"> Dense jungles were also a part of the varied geography in parts of ancient China.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-990942779714eb1b55832fae0d8365e4</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210859</data>
    </node>
    <node id="region of Harbin">
      <data key="d0">region of Harbin</data>
      <data key="d1">geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">The region surrounding Harbin is known for its frozen environments, contrasting with southern regions like Hong Kong.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-990942779714eb1b55832fae0d8365e4</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210859</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hong Kong">
      <data key="d0">Hong Kong</data>
      <data key="d1">geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">Southern regions of ancient China have a subtropical climate distinct from northern regions with frozen environs.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-990942779714eb1b55832fae0d8365e4</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210859</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Egypt">
      <data key="d0">Egypt</data>
      <data key="d1">geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">Alexander the Great conquered Egypt during his extensive conquests in the late 4th century BCE, transforming it into a significant geographical location and administrative entity ruled by pharaohs. Historically, Egypt was crucial as a primary source of grain for the Roman Empire, often referred to as the "breadbasket" that supplied essential food needs.

This country maintained control over vital trading routes connecting Mesopotamia with regions such as Canaan, Syria, Nubia, Sinai, Byblos, and Phoenicia, making it a key strategic point in ancient commerce. Egypt exerted considerable cultural influence on Rome through its religious beliefs, art, architecture, and practices like embalming, leaving an enduring legacy that extends to both the Greek and Roman periods.

Egypt's importance transcends this period, functioning as both a primary supplier of grain for the Roman Empire and a significant geographical location with extensive administrative reach under pharaonic rule. This comprehensive description reflects its multifaceted roles in ancient history and highlights Egypt’s strategic and cultural significance within the broader context of the Eastern Mediterranean.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cdc7bbe967c34e7d69b34931a2e372ce&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-54ce354bc2591fc3936924cededa981a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-26ccbf3441b6b8fad246e6cd56535b53&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-921eca26480da1b32897b0b58823f7d8</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210856</data>
    </node>
    <node id="majority ethnic group in China">
      <data key="d0">majority ethnic group in China</data>
      <data key="d1">civilization_or_empire</data>
      <data key="d2">Han Chinese constitutes the majority ethnic group within modern-day China, particularly in densely populated areas along the Yellow and Yangtze Rivers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-990942779714eb1b55832fae0d8365e4</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210859</data>
    </node>
    <node id="regional variations">
      <data key="d0">regional variations</data>
      <data key="d1">cultural_or_religious_movement</data>
      <data key="d2">Ancient China's diverse geography led to varied cultural practices, spiritual beliefs, and independent development of agriculture among early societies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-990942779714eb1b55832fae0d8365e4</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210859</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;outlying areas'&lt;">
      <data key="d0">"outlying areas'&lt;</data>
      <data key="d1">geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">Outlying areas outside the core provinces along major river systems contain unique religious and ethnic minorities such as Mongolians, Tibetans, Uyghurs, and Manchu.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-990942779714eb1b55832fae0d8365e4</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210859</data>
    </node>
    <node id="symbolic language">
      <data key="d0">symbolic language</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">Development of symbolic language during the Paleolithic period aided in the evolution of abstract ideas like kinship and afterlife, contributing to shared culture and society.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-990942779714eb1b55832fae0d8365e4</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210859</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;figure 5.4'&lt;">
      <data key="d0">"figure 5.4'&lt;</data>
      <data key="d1">textbook_structure</data>
      <data key="d2">Figure 5.4 is a map depicting the varied topography of ancient China, showing fertile plains, river valleys, mountain ranges, deserts, and coastlines on the eastern edge.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-990942779714eb1b55832fae0d8365e4</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210859</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;outlying areas'&lt;&lt;">
      <data key="d0">"outlying areas'&lt;&lt;</data>
      <data key="d1">geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">Outlying areas in ancient China included regions beyond major river systems and mountain ranges. These areas often had more isolated or unique cultures compared to central regions, influenced by environmental factors such as deserts, jungles, and plateaus.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-990942779714eb1b55832fae0d8365e4</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210859</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;entity_name&gt;&quot;The Shang civilization&quot;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;entity_name&gt;"The Shang civilization"</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;entity_type&gt;"civilization_or_empire</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;entity_description&gt;"The Shang civilization was an ancient Chinese empire that existed from approximately 1600 to 1046 BCE. It is the first dynasty for which written records are available in China and is known for its advanced organizational capacity, military prowess, and introduction of writing in the form of a logographic script. The Shang built one of the world’s first large territorial states controlled by a noble warrior class and were characterized by their religious practices centered around ancestor worship and divination. They also made significant advancements in metallurgy, producing large, heavy bronze vessels such as cups, goblets, and cauldrons."</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3a02c137a8c6f13a1e5fca2e09969f0e</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210859</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;entity_name&gt;&quot;logographic script&quot;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;entity_name&gt;"logographic script"</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;entity_type&gt;"historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;entity_description&gt;"The logographic script used by the Shang civilization was a key method of writing in ancient China. This type of script consists of complex symbols representing complete words or concepts rather than individual sounds, which required extensive memorization for literacy. It became crucial for the transmission and preservation of Chinese ideas, values, and spiritual beliefs across dynasties."</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3a02c137a8c6f13a1e5fca2e09969f0e</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210859</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;entity_name&gt;&quot;Henan, Anhui, Shandong, Hebei, Shanxi provinces&quot;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;entity_name&gt;"Henan, Anhui, Shandong, Hebei, Shanxi provinces"</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;entity_type&gt;"geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;entity_description&gt;"These provinces are historically significant for the Shang civilization. They form part of the central and eastern regions of China where the early Shang rulers built their capital cities and expanded their control through military conquests."</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3a02c137a8c6f13a1e5fca2e09969f0e</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210859</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;entity_name&gt;&quot;continuity of Chinese cultural heritage&quot;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;entity_name&gt;"continuity of Chinese cultural heritage"</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;entity_type&gt;"historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;entity_description&gt;"This concept refers to the transmission and preservation of ideas, values, and spiritual beliefs from one dynasty to another through the use of logographic script. The continuity suggests a lasting influence on later Chinese civilizations despite changes in political power."</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3a02c137a8c6f13a1e5fca2e09969f0e</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210859</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;entity_name&gt;&quot;historical_concept_or_event of written records&quot;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;entity_name&gt;"historical_concept_or_event of written records"</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;entity_type&gt;"historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;entity_description&gt;"This refers to the written documents that provide historical accounts and evidence about past events. For the Shang civilization, these records are significant because they offer direct insights into ancient China’s socio-political structure, technological advancements, and cultural practices."</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3a02c137a8c6f13a1e5fca2e09969f0e</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210859</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;entity_name&gt;&quot;source_entity_of writings&quot;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;entity_name&gt;"source_entity_of writings"</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;entity_type&gt;"historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;entity_description&gt;"This entity is not directly mentioned in the text but refers to where written records originate from, such as official scribes or other record-keepers of the Shang civilization who compiled and preserved these historical documents."</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3a02c137a8c6f13a1e5fca2e09969f0e</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210859</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Neolithic cultures of northern China&quot;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Neolithic cultures of northern China"</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;cultural_or_religious_movement</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;"The Neolithic era in northern China was marked by the development of early agricultural societies, including practices such as farming, pottery making, and the construction of settlements. These cultures laid the foundation for later dynasties."|</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-17c7ceed9ec0b5e033838e24607d90cc</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210860</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Di&quot;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Di"</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;"The term 'Di' refers to various groups or events that appeared in early Chinese history. It is often used in a historical context, but its exact nature can vary depending on the source."|</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-17c7ceed9ec0b5e033838e24607d90cc</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210860</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Qiang&quot;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Qiang"</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;"Qiang refers to proto-Tibetan tribes and potentially specific individuals or groups from ancient China, known for their military prowess and resistance against other dynasties."|</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-17c7ceed9ec0b5e033838e24607d90cc</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210860</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Zhou Dynasty&quot;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Zhou Dynasty"</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;civilization_or_empire</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;"The Zhou Dynasty ruled from approximately 1046 BCE to 256 BCE. It is divided into two periods: the Western Zhou and Eastern Zhou, known for significant political and cultural changes, including the introduction of the concept of heavenly mandate."|</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-17c7ceed9ec0b5e033838e24607d90cc</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210860</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Shaanxi province in north-central China&quot;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Shaanxi province in north-central China"</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;"Shaanxi Province is located in central western China. It has been an important historical region, serving as a significant capital for dynasties like the Zhou and Han."|</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-17c7ceed9ec0b5e033838e24607d90cc</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210860</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Shang kings&quot;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Shang kings"</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;"The Shang kings ruled the Shang Dynasty from around 1600 to 1046 BCE. They were central figures in early Chinese history, known for their military prowess and involvement in human sacrifice rituals."|</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-17c7ceed9ec0b5e033838e24607d90cc</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210860</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Qiang, a proto-Tibetan tribe&quot;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Qiang, a proto-Tibetan tribe"</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;"The Qiang represented a group of tribal peoples who inhabited parts of northern China during the prehistoric period. They are often mentioned as opponents or allies to various dynasties."|</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-17c7ceed9ec0b5e033838e24607d90cc</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210860</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Bo kofDocuments, records of dozens of speeches and announcements attributed to historical leaders from the ancient world directly to future generations.&quot;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Bo kofDocuments, records of dozens of speeches and announcements attributed to historical leaders from the ancient world directly to future generations."</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;source_or_artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;"The Bo Kǒu refers to written records or tablets that document important announcements and declarations made by ancient Chinese kings or other influential figures. These serve as primary sources for understanding early Chinese history."|</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-17c7ceed9ec0b5e033838e24607d90cc</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210860</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Table 5.1 Chinas Dynasties&quot;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Table 5.1 Chinas Dynasties"</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;textbook_structure</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;"This is a chart of dynasties in Chinese history, often found in textbooks and used to provide an overview of the periods and events that shaped ancient Chinese civilizations."|</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-17c7ceed9ec0b5e033838e24607d90cc</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210860</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;heaven&quot;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"heaven"</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;cultural_or_religious_movement</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;"Heaven plays a central role in Chinese religious and philosophical thought as a guiding force and moral authority. It is often associated with concepts such as celestial mandate and divine will."|</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-17c7ceed9ec0b5e033838e24607d90cc</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210860</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;cosmos&quot;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"cosmos"</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;"Cosmos refers to the overarching universe or natural order believed to govern the cosmos in Chinese antiquity, impacting the legitimacy of rule through various cosmological practices."|</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-17c7ceed9ec0b5e033838e24607d90cc</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210860</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Qin dynasty in 1912&quot;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Qin dynasty in 1912"</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;time_period</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;"The term 'Qin dynasty in 1912' is somewhat anachronistic as it refers to China after the fall of the Qing Dynasty, specifically a period when discussions or references to the ancient Qin dynasty were made during this time."|</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-17c7ceed9ec0b5e033838e24607d90cc</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210860</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Shang Dynasty">
      <data key="d0">Shang Dynasty</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">The Shang Dynasty refers to one of China's early dynastic periods, noted for its despotic rulers whose reign ruined the lives of their subjects and squandered resources.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3f1b435017b04eb71afccb13dbfc34c9</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210860</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Zhou Dynasty">
      <data key="d0">Zhou Dynasty</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">The Zhou Dynasty marks a significant shift in Chinese history characterized by political ideology spread promoting shared cultural identity and the concept of the Mandate of Heaven, justifying the overthrow of bad governments.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3f1b435017b04eb71afccb13dbfc34c9</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210860</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mandate of Heaven">
      <data key="d0">Mandate of Heaven</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Mandate of Heaven is a historical and conceptual framework deeply rooted in ancient Chinese governance, which justified the legitimacy of rule through favor from the natural order or divine authority. This principle was particularly significant for the Song dynasty, as it claimed to derive its right to govern from this belief. The Mandate of Heaven asserted that rulers were granted authority not only by birth but also based on their moral leadership and commitment to societal welfare. This concept played a crucial role in justifying the legitimacy of rule and provided a justification for the overthrow of corrupt rulers, influencing numerous dynastic cycles throughout Chinese history.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3f1b435017b04eb71afccb13dbfc34c9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-65567519161cbb48515c5592fd7dfa04&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dfd43568e4817f7de7fa5fd0bbb3f2e0</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210865</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Qing Dynasty">
      <data key="d0">Qing Dynasty</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">The Qing Dynasty was China's last imperial dynasty which fell in 1912, ending the long period of rule by various Chinese dynasties.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3f1b435017b04eb71afccb13dbfc34c9</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210860</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Chinese Civilization">
      <data key="d0">Chinese Civilization</data>
      <data key="d1">civilization_or_empire</data>
      <data key="d2">Chinese Civilization spans multiple dynasties and encompasses a shared cultural identity fostered through ideological shifts and historical concepts like the Mandate of Heaven.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3f1b435017b04eb71afccb13dbfc34c9</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210860</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Physical Borders">
      <data key="d0">Physical Borders</data>
      <data key="d1">geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">The physical borders of Chinese civilization fluctuated between different dynasties, indicating varying territorial extents during different periods.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3f1b435017b04eb71afccb13dbfc34c9</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210860</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Shang Rulers Despotism">
      <data key="d0">Shang Rulers Despotism</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">Despotism under Shang rulers refers to their oppressive and mismanaged governance leading to social upheaval and the rise of the Zhou dynasty.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3f1b435017b04eb71afccb13dbfc34c9</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210860</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Western Zhou&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Western Zhou&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;time_period&gt;</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;"The period from around 1046 to 771 BC where the Zhou dynasty established early forms of a more decentralized governance system, marked by territorial expansion eastward over the Yellow River through military campaigns."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8fed1d6dd15981d033c4b2d57d29efbc</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210860</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Yellow River&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Yellow River&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;geographical_location&gt;</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;"A key geographical feature that serves as a boundary for territorial expansion during the Western Zhou period and played a significant role in defining and expanding the reach of the early Zhou dynasty."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8fed1d6dd15981d033c4b2d57d29efbc</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210860</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Mandate of Heaven&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Mandate of Heaven&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;historical_concept_or_event&gt;</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;"The belief that justifies imperial rule and unites China under a supreme sovereign, allowing for dissent and revolution against unpopular rulers. Adopted by the Zhou rulers to legitimize their rule."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8fed1d6dd15981d033c4b2d57d29efbc</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210860</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Zhou Rulers&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Zhou Rulers&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;historical_figure&gt;</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;"Leaders who initiated the early Zhou dynasty’s territorial expansion through military campaigns and established systems of decentralized governance."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8fed1d6dd15981d033c4b2d57d29efbc</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210860</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Zhongguo (China)">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Zhongguo (China)</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;civilization_or_empire&gt;</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;"A term used to denote the core region where Chinese civilization was centered, defined by shared cultural traditions including farming, writing, and metalworking."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8fed1d6dd15981d033c4b2d57d29efbc</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210860</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Huaxia (Chinese)">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Huaxia (Chinese)</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;cultural_or_religious_movement&gt;</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;"An identity or group of people within the Zhou dynasty, characterized as descendants of mythical figures and common ancestors who share cultural traditions."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8fed1d6dd15981d033c4b2d57d29efbc</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210860</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Mandate of Heaven Conceptual Framework&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Mandate of Heaven Conceptual Framework&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;source_or_artifact&gt;</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;"A key conceptual framework used by the Zhou and their successors to justify and maintain imperial rule, emphasizing divine mandate for governance."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8fed1d6dd15981d033c4b2d57d29efbc</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210860</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Historical Division into Periods&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Historical Division into Periods&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;textbook_structure&gt;</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;"The categorization of the early Zhou period (Western Zhou) as a stage in the development of governance and territorial control, highlighting key stages."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8fed1d6dd15981d033c4b2d57d29efbc</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210860</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Eastern Zhou Dynasty&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Eastern Zhou Dynasty&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">civilization_or_empire</data>
      <data key="d2">The Eastern Zhou dynasty (771–256 BCE) spanned two distinct periods: the Spring and Autumn period and the Warring States period. During this time, the Zhou kings' authority gradually eroded, giving rise to rival states engaging in political intrigue, military competition, and cultural flourishing.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8d279577288a6b48e916b34e9cf14a35</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Spring and Autumn Annals&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Spring and Autumn Annals&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">textbook_structure</data>
      <data key="d2">"Spring and Autumn Annals" (Chunqiu) chronicles the gradual erosion of Zhou kings' power from around 771 BCE. It is a historical chronicle that details events and political changes during the Spring and Autumn period.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8d279577288a6b48e916b34e9cf14a35</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Confucius&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Confucius&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">Confucius, who lived from approximately 551 to 479 BCE in the state of Lu, was a significant philosopher and educator. He is known for his teachings on moral conduct and societal order, which are encapsulated in texts like the "Analects".</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8d279577288a6b48e916b34e9cf14a35</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Mozi&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Mozi&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">Mozi, who lived during the Warring States period (475–221 BCE), was a philosopher known for his teachings on universal love and non-aggression. He founded Mohism and wrote influential texts that sought to challenge and expand upon Confucian ideals.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8d279577288a6b48e916b34e9cf14a35</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Sun Wu">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Sun Wu</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">Sun Wu, also known as Sun Tzu, was an author who lived during the Eastern Zhou dynasty (770–256 BCE) and is best known for his strategic military treatise "The Art of War".</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8d279577288a6b48e916b34e9cf14a35</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;The Art of War&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;The Art of War&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">textbook_structure</data>
      <data key="d2">"The Art of War" is a military treatise written by Sun Wu during the Eastern Zhou dynasty. It offers insights into strategic thought and remains one of the most influential works on warfare in history.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8d279577288a6b48e916b34e9cf14a35</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Mohism&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Mohism&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">philosophy</data>
      <data key="d2">Mohism is a philosophical school founded by Mozi, which emphasized universal love, non-aggression, and practical benefits. Its teachings and influence are studied in contrast to Confucian thought.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8d279577288a6b48e916b34e9cf14a35</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Warring States period&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Warring States period&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">philosophical_period</data>
      <data key="d2">The Warring States period (475–221 BCE) saw a flourishing of philosophy as various schools competed for influence, leading to the formation of classical Chinese thought.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8d279577288a6b48e916b34e9cf14a35</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;The Analects of Confucius&quot;&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"The Analects of Confucius"&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;textbook_structure&gt;</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;"Comprises 20 books with thematic quotes focusing on ethical behavior and social order through the concept of junzi."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d49b5280ff80378cd7267e9b7173e02e</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Daoism&quot;&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Daoism"&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;cultural_or_religious_movement&gt;</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;"An indigenous religion venerated nature, influenced by various ideological systems. Emerged in significant periods post-Zhou, particularly gaining influence during the later Han dynasty with key figures like Laozi and Zhuangzi"&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d49b5280ff80378cd7267e9b7173e02e</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Zhou Era&quot;&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Zhou Era"&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;time_period&gt;</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;"A significant period in Chinese history where Daoism gained notable influence due to teachings by Laozi and Zhuangzi, contributing to texts and ideas that would later shape Confucian ethics."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d49b5280ff80378cd7267e9b7173e02e</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Confucius&quot;&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Confucius"&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;historical_figure&gt;</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;"The founder of Confucianism who emphasized the importance of feeling shame for personal integrity and moral duty, promoting ethical leadership through virtue."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d49b5280ff80378cd7267e9b7173e02e</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Laozi&quot;&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Laozi"&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;historical_figure&gt;</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;"Attributed author of Tao Te Ching, a key figure in Daoist philosophy, advocating living in harmony with nature and natural laws."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d49b5280ff80378cd7267e9b7173e02e</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Zhuang Zhou&quot;&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Zhuang Zhou"&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;historical_figure&gt;</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;"A central figure in Daoism who wrote the Zhuangzi text, known for his philosophical dialogue and critique of materialistic pursuits."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d49b5280ff80378cd7267e9b7173e02e</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Mandate of Heaven&quot;&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Mandate of Heaven"&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;historical_concept_or_event&gt;</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;"A concept suggesting that rulers receive divine approval or guidance from a higher cosmic order to rule ethically. Both Confucianism and Daoism use this concept to justify moral governance."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d49b5280ff80378cd7267e9b7173e02e</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Eastern Zhou period&quot;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Eastern Zhou period"</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;time_period</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;"The Eastern Zhou period (771–256 BCE</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-37f074b28c1e83e9d33b610392b3d2cb</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="China">
      <data key="d0">China</data>
      <data key="d1">geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">Ancient China served as one of the dominant civilizational poles alongside India, significantly influencing regional history and culture for millennia through impressive cities, kingdoms, and empires. This period encompassed various historical events involving diplomatic relations such as those with Goguryeo and Baekje, architectural projects, and conflicts with neighbors including Silla’s surrounding states Goguryeo and Baekje. Based on the descriptions provided, Ancient China is noted for being the origin of important cultural artifacts like the Diamond Sutra, an early form of Buddhism that emerged in this period. Additionally, it played a central role in trade routes by receiving goods from southern Africa, including gold, ivory, and rare animals, illustrating its vast network and influence in ancient Asia. The descriptions collectively highlight China's pivotal role in shaping not only regional but global interactions during this era.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2ffc1f0ad0cbf2629423c1e50ba8c211&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e2224dbd0185a7c0eadc02caea19f029&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-96a9973262a04f62e37d1ec6cfe70c42&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e0e7cff15604f2aa13915047df90d715&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ac97c61bc79f0fafcdc0133b3f827bda</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210865</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Qin Empire&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Qin Empire&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">civilization_or_empire</data>
      <data key="d2">Established by Emperor Qin Shi Huang in 221 BCE, conquered numerous states and unified China under a centralized state. Notable for its Legalist principles, expansionist policies, and large-scale infrastructure projects like the Great Wall of China. However, it collapsed shortly after the emperor's death due to social instability and harsh rule.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e282907e1ce23dfd30fb1de511b8e168</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;time_period&gt;&quot;Qin's rise and fall&quot;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;time_period&gt;"Qin's rise and fall"</data>
      <data key="d1">time_period</data>
      <data key="d2">Refers to the historical period during which the Qin Empire was established under Emperor Qin Shi Huang, reaching its zenith before rapidly declining. This spanned from 221 BCE when the empire was unified until shortly after Qin Shi Huang's death in 207 BCE.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e282907e1ce23dfd30fb1de511b8e168</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Qin's mausoleum&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Qin's mausoleum&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">Located in Xi'an, Shaanxi Province, China, this is a vast archaeological site encompassing Emperor Qin Shi Huang's tomb and the Terracotta Army. Designated as a UNESCO World Heritage Site in 1987.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e282907e1ce23dfd30fb1de511b8e168</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;UNESCO World Heritage site designation&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;UNESCO World Heritage site designation&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">The recognition by UNESCO of outstanding cultural or natural heritage significance, including Qin's mausoleum and the Terracotta Army, which have been preserved as an enduring symbol of one of the world's earliest centralized states.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e282907e1ce23dfd30fb1de511b8e168</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;collapse of the Qin Empire&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;collapse of the Qin Empire&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">The sudden end of the Qin Dynasty following Emperor Qin Shi Huangdi’s death in 210 BCE. This event marked by internal strife, economic pressure, and widespread rebellion against his authoritarian regime.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e282907e1ce23dfd30fb1de511b8e168</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Shihuangdi&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Shihuangdi&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">The first emperor of a unified China (221–210 BCE), known for implementing Legalist philosophy, constructing the Great Wall, standardizing laws, weights and measures, and establishing cultural unification. His death led to a rapid fall of his empire.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e282907e1ce23dfd30fb1de511b8e168</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Prince Fusu&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Prince Fusu&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">Eldest son and right heir of Emperor Qin Shi Huang, advocating for more benevolent governance with respect to the populace. Being denied by Shihuangdi, he later became associated with rebellion efforts and ultimately perished during the fall of Qin.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e282907e1ce23dfd30fb1de511b8e168</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;General Wang Jian&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;General Wang Jian&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">A military general who played a significant role in the unification and expansion of the Qin state. Appointed as a governor after Qin's conquests, he was later executed for opposing Shihuangdi’s policies leading to his own execution.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e282907e1ce23dfd30fb1de511b8e168</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Chancellor Li Si&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Chancellor Li Si&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">Advisor and prime minister to Emperor Qin Shi Huang responsible for implementing centralization of China. His influence in promoting Legalist ideology contributed to both the unification and the eventual downfall due to his harsh governance practices.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e282907e1ce23dfd30fb1de511b8e168</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Legalist philosophy and practices&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Legalist philosophy and practices&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">cultural_or_religious_movement</data>
      <data key="d2">A political and philosophical school emphasizing social control through strict laws, severe punishments, centralization of authority, and economic control. Its impact was felt during the Qin era but faced criticism in succeeding dynasties.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e282907e1ce23dfd30fb1de511b8e168</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;UNESCO website&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;UNESCO website&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">source_or_artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">The official platform from UNESCO mentioning the inclusion of "Qin's Mausoleum" as a World Heritage Site, highlighting its universal value and significance to world heritage.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e282907e1ce23dfd30fb1de511b8e168</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Xiongnu Empire&quot;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Xiongnu Empire"</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;"civilization_or_empire</data>
      <data key="d2">The Xiongnu Empire was a confederation of nomadic tribes that controlled extensive territories north of China during much of the 1st century BCE. This empire engaged in recurrent military conflicts with the Han Dynasty, primarily over the control of trade routes and valuable resources. The Xiongnu's dominance over vast steppe territories had significant implications for neighboring powers, including the Han Dynasty, prompting strategic responses such as the expansion of the Silk Roads to circumvent or establish new trade connections in the region.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f690328b61b90fc6d55ed10ca3e0322b</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210865</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Han Empire&quot;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Han Empire"</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;"civilization_or_empire</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;"The Han Empire, or Western Han, was a major empire that ruled China from 206 BCE to 9 CE. It is known for its expansion into Central Asia through the Silk Roads and its interactions with nomadic groups like the Xiongnu."&lt;|</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f690328b61b90fc6d55ed10ca3e0322b</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Silk Roads&quot;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Silk Roads"</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;"historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;"The Silk Roads were a network of trade routes that connected China to the Mediterranean world, facilitating cultural and economic exchanges between the East and West. They were pivotal during the Han period."&lt;|</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f690328b61b90fc6d55ed10ca3e0322b</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210862</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Tributary relations with China&quot;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Tributary relations with China"</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;"historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;"Tributary systems were diplomatic and cultural exchanges between China and other states, often involving the presentation of gifts and submission to Chinese authority. The Xianbei and various nomadic tribes maintained such relationships."&lt;|</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f690328b61b90fc6d55ed10ca3e0322b</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210862</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Cultural exchanges between Inner Asian Steppe and China&quot;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Cultural exchanges between Inner Asian Steppe and China"</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;"historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;"Cultural exchanges between the Inner Asian steppe and China involved not only trade but also technological, religious, and artistic influences. This was facilitated by the Silk Roads."&lt;|</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f690328b61b90fc6d55ed10ca3e0322b</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210862</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Han Dynasty and its successor states (220-589 CE)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Han Dynasty and its successor states (220-589 CE)"</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;"historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;"The Han Dynasty's rule ended in 220 AD with the fall of Eastern Han, marking the beginning of the Three Kingdoms period. Its successor states included the Wei, Shu, and Wu."&lt;|</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f690328b61b90fc6d55ed10ca3e0322b</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210862</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Six Dynasties Period&quot;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Six Dynasties Period"</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;"historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;"The Six Dynasties period refers to a series of three northern and three southern dynasties from 220 AD to 589 AD. It followed the short-lived Three Kingdoms period."&lt;|</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f690328b61b90fc6d55ed10ca3e0322b</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210862</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Stirrups and heavy cavalry&quot;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Stirrups and heavy cavalry"</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;"historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;"The innovation of stirrups allowed for more effective use of heavy cavalry, which had a significant impact on military strategies across Eurasia, including during the battles between nomadic tribes and settled agrarian states."&lt;|</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f690328b61b90fc6d55ed10ca3e0322b</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210862</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Northern Wei Dynasty">
      <data key="d0">Northern Wei Dynasty</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">The Northern Wei Dynasty, which lasted from 386 to 534 CE, was a pivotal period marked by cultural exchange and assimilation between Xianbei and Han Chinese nobles.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7933b681356fd848600cd95d60b6d201</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210862</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Xiaowen's reign">
      <data key="d0">Xiaowen's reign</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">Xiaowen, who ruled the Northern Wei Dynasty from 423 to 499 CE, is known for his reforms that accelerated cultural integration between Xianbei and Han Chinese elites.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7933b681356fd848600cd95d60b6d201</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210862</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Khitan people">
      <data key="d0">Khitan people</data>
      <data key="d1">civilization_or_empire</data>
      <data key="d2">The Khitan, a confederation of nomadic tribes, rose to prominence in the steppe regions from the 4th century onwards, eventually founding the Liao dynasty (907–1125 CE).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7933b681356fd848600cd95d60b6d201</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210862</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Liao Dynasty">
      <data key="d0">Liao Dynasty</data>
      <data key="d1">civilization_or_empire</data>
      <data key="d2">The Liao dynasty, established by the Khitan people, ruled a vast territory spanning from modern-day Mongolia to parts of northeastern China.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7933b681356fd848600cd95d60b6d201</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210862</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mongols">
      <data key="d0">Mongols</data>
      <data key="d1">cultural_or_religious_movement</data>
      <data key="d2">The Mongol Empire, founded in the 13th century, expanded to cover much of Eurasia and even conquered all of China, establishing a unified political entity. This empire was known for its significant influence on the political landscapes across Eurasia and had historical ties with the Ottoman Empire through various interactions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7933b681356fd848600cd95d60b6d201&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-72d7bb40fa0aa36bf5b0bb66bf7975fe</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210865</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Jurchen">
      <data key="d0">Jurchen</data>
      <data key="d1">cultural_or_religious_movement</data>
      <data key="d2">The Jurchen people established the Jin dynasty (1115–1234 CE), which controlled significant parts of northern China before being invaded by the Mongols.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7933b681356fd848600cd95d60b6d201</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210862</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Korea">
      <data key="d0">Korea</data>
      <data key="d1">geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">Korea, specifically referring to the Korean Peninsula, served as a significant hub for cultural, religious, and commercial exchanges during the early medieval period, acting as a crossroad linking East Asia with regions beyond. This strategic location facilitated influences from both China and Sogdiana through trade routes such as the Silk Roads. Additionally, Korea's bureaucratic system demonstrated similarities to that of Tang China, indicating shared administrative practices and possibly cultural or political influences in this period.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7933b681356fd848600cd95d60b6d201&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e0e7cff15604f2aa13915047df90d715&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ff2834c0656da75835dd726c9d9fbb32</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210867</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Japan">
      <data key="d0">Japan</data>
      <data key="d1">geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">In the Early Middle Ages, Japan, a border state with connections to other regions, was part of extensive interactions that facilitated significant cultural exchanges. This engagement involved considerable influence from both Korea and China through trade routes such as the Silk Roads, leading to the development of unique Japanese traditions including insei (the practice of imperial succession within the imperial family), samurai (warriors who served nobles), and shogun (military dictators). Despite these external influences, Japan evolved distinct indigenous systems that contributed to its rich and complex cultural heritage.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7933b681356fd848600cd95d60b6d201&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e0e7cff15604f2aa13915047df90d715&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ff2834c0656da75835dd726c9d9fbb32</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210868</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Inner Asian Steppe peoples">
      <data key="d0">Inner Asian Steppe peoples</data>
      <data key="d1">geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">Inner Asian Steppe peoples refer to groups living on the Inner Asian steppes and their practices such as levirate marriage.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9dbf312c249001353625959992df9d78</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210863</data>
    </node>
    <node id="early Korean ruling families">
      <data key="d0">early Korean ruling families</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">Early Korean ruling families adopted various practices, including levirate marriage, from Inner Asian Steppe peoples.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9dbf312c249001353625959992df9d78</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210863</data>
    </node>
    <node id="levirate marriage">
      <data key="d0">levirate marriage</data>
      <data key="d1">cultural_or_religious_movement</data>
      <data key="d2">Levirate marriage is a practice where a young male marries his elder brother’s widow.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9dbf312c249001353625959992df9d78</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210863</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Chinese power decline in the fourth century">
      <data key="d0">Chinese power decline in the fourth century</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">The decline of Chinese power in the fourth century unleashed refugees and initiated state-building processes in Korea.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9dbf312c249001353625959992df9d78</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210863</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Three Kingdoms (313–668 CE)">
      <data key="d0">Three Kingdoms (313–668 CE)</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">The Three Kingdoms era, spanning from 313 to 668 CE, is a pivotal period in Korean history involving states of Goguryeo, Baekje, and Silla.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9dbf312c249001353625959992df9d78</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210863</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Goguryeo">
      <data key="d0">Goguryeo</data>
      <data key="d1">civilization_or_empire</data>
      <data key="d2">Goguryeo, one of the Three Kingdoms of Korea alongside Silla and Baekje, was known for its expansionist policies and the construction of distinctive mounded tombs. Originating under Han Chinese rule, Goguryeo regained independence in 313 CE. It engaged in significant military competition with Silla and eventually fell to a combined attack by Silla and Tang forces. Over time, Goguryeo managed to absorb Silla, leading to its ultimate unification.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-464eb2e1546acfe2e39b57d49cd04088&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2ffc1f0ad0cbf2629423c1e50ba8c211&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9dbf312c249001353625959992df9d78</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210869</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Baekje">
      <data key="d0">Baekje</data>
      <data key="d1">civilization_or_empire</data>
      <data key="d2">Baekje was one of the three major kingdoms during the Three Kingdoms era in Korea, alongside Goguryeo and Silla. Initially, Baekje had formed an alliance against Goguryeo but later faced conflict with both Goguryeo and its rival kingdom, Silla. Ultimately, Baekje's power diminished, leading to its fall in 660 CE when it was defeated by a combined military force from Silla and Tang China.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-464eb2e1546acfe2e39b57d49cd04088&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2ffc1f0ad0cbf2629423c1e50ba8c211&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9dbf312c249001353625959992df9d78</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210871</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Silla">
      <data key="d0">Silla</data>
      <data key="d1">civilization_or_empire</data>
      <data key="d2">Silla was a significant kingdom in historical Korean politics that unified much of today's Korea and played a crucial role in shaping its history. Known as one of the Three Kingdoms in ancient Korea, Silla initially became a vassal state under Goguryeo by the early fifth century but later managed to absorb the Gaya confederacy and forge alliances with the Tang dynasty. It eventually rose to become the dominant force among the Three Kingdoms and played a pivotal role in unifying Korea.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-464eb2e1546acfe2e39b57d49cd04088&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2ffc1f0ad0cbf2629423c1e50ba8c211&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9dbf312c249001353625959992df9d78</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210872</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Khitan and Xianbei groups">
      <data key="d0">Khitan and Xianbei groups</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">The Khitan and Xianbei were northern nomadic tribes that posed threats to Korean kingdoms like Baekje and Silla, and influenced Goguryeo's expansionary policies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9dbf312c249001353625959992df9d78</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210865</data>
    </node>
    <node id="King Gwanggaeto (375-413 AD)">
      <data key="d0">King Gwanggaeto (375-413 AD)</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">King Gwanggaeto led Goguryeo in a series of expansionist wars against Baekje and its Japanese allies during the late fourth to early fifth century.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9dbf312c249001353625959992df9d78</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210865</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Wa (Japanese kingdom, now Japan) people">
      <data key="d0">Wa (Japanese kingdom, now Japan) people</data>
      <data key="d1">cultural_or_religious_movement</data>
      <data key="d2">Wa people provided support to Baekje as allies, influencing Korean-Korean conflicts in the age of Three Kingdoms.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9dbf312c249001353625959992df9d78</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210865</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Three pivotal kingdoms-Goguryeo, Baekje, and Silla">
      <data key="d0">Three pivotal kingdoms-Goguryeo, Baekje, and Silla</data>
      <data key="d1">civilization_or_empire</data>
      <data key="d2">These three kingdoms dominated trade and cultural exchange with China and Japan and vied for unifying Korea during a specific period.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-57575be45b7d19b2434b047e8858a767</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210865</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Three Kingdoms era">
      <data key="d0">Three Kingdoms era</data>
      <data key="d1">time_period</data>
      <data key="d2">The Three Kingdoms era refers to a period in Korean history where several states were vying for control.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f40f7804851b12abfcd5e873e7ef3e1f</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210865</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Three Kingdoms, Korea">
      <data key="d0">Three Kingdoms, Korea</data>
      <data key="d1">geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">The Three Kingdoms of Korea encompassed regions that are today part of North and South Korea. It was a center for significant cultural and political interactions during the period.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f40f7804851b12abfcd5e873e7ef3e1f</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210865</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Unified Rule">
      <data key="d0">Unified Rule</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">The concept of Unified Rule refers to the period when Silla ruled over most of the Korean peninsula until 892 CE.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2ffc1f0ad0cbf2629423c1e50ba8c211</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210865</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Queen Seondeok">
      <data key="d0">Queen Seondeok</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">Queen Seondeok ruled Silla during the 640s, a period marked by significant female rule and cultural development. She played a crucial role in Silla's rise through her adept use of diplomacy and military tactics. Her reign was pivotal in laying the groundwork for future queens in Silla and saw the emergence of new art forms, including celadon ceramics, which contributed to the rich cultural heritage of the kingdom.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c4306b2ba6966f8e780acc5a0a8ddd2f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2ffc1f0ad0cbf2629423c1e50ba8c211</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210872</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Queen Seondeoks reign">
      <data key="d0">Queen Seondeoks reign</data>
      <data key="d1">time_period</data>
      <data key="d2">Queen Seondeok's reign refers to a period of unprecedented female rule during which new art forms emerged and laid the groundwork for future queens in Silla.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c4306b2ba6966f8e780acc5a0a8ddd2f</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210867</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Goguryeo Tombs Murals&quot;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Goguryeo Tombs Murals"</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;"source_or_artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;"The murals from Anak Tomb No. 3 in Goguryeo tombs, dating back to the era of the Goguryeo kingdom (37 BCE – 668 CE). Depict scenes such as processions, feasts, and hunting, indicating elite values like status symbols and the connection to nature, with some showing Chinese-influenced decorative elements."&gt;&lt;|</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-977f854853b555ebe9aa7924aa9d1777</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210867</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Anak Tomb No. 3&quot;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Anak Tomb No. 3"</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;"geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;"A specific tomb site within the Goguryeo royal or elite burial grounds, containing murals that provide insights into the lives and values of the ruling class during the Goguryeo period."&gt;&lt;|</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-977f854853b555ebe9aa7924aa9d1777</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210868</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Chinese-style decorative elements&quot;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Chinese-style decorative elements"</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;"cultural_or_religious_movement</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;"Decorative motifs and styles influenced by Chinese culture that appear in some Goguryeo murals, indicating possible exchanges or influences between Goguryeo and neighboring states."&gt;&lt;|</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-977f854853b555ebe9aa7924aa9d1777</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210868</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Early Waves of Foragers and Hunters">
      <data key="d0">Early Waves of Foragers and Hunters</data>
      <data key="d1">time_period</data>
      <data key="d2">The period during which early groups of foragers and hunters made their way to Japan through overland routes or across the Tsushima Strait, descended from Ural-Altaic, Chinese, and Austro-Asiatic peoples.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-eb95afa387a7f6959f4e43eac7aed572</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210868</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Climate Shift">
      <data key="d0">Climate Shift</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">A significant climate shift around 12,000 years ago that isolated Japan from the Asian continent by severing land connections, leading to changes in the environment such as the disappearance of grasslands and smaller wolves.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-eb95afa387a7f6959f4e43eac7aed572</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210868</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Jōmon Pottery">
      <data key="d0">Jōmon Pottery</data>
      <data key="d1">source_or_artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">Pottery developed during the Jōmon period characterized by elaborate handles and distinct cord patterns around rims, first used for storing vegetables and boiling sea water to make salt, later serving ritualistic and functional purposes such as promoting unity and cooperation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-eb95afa387a7f6959f4e43eac7aed572</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210868</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Dogu Figurines">
      <data key="d0">Dogu Figurines</data>
      <data key="d1">textbook_structure</data>
      <data key="d2">Earthenware figurines believed to represent female gods, possibly serving as sympathetic magic tools for healing illness and aiding childbirth, significant cultural symbols during the Jōmon period in Japan.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-eb95afa387a7f6959f4e43eac7aed572</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210868</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Settlements with Shared Spaces">
      <data key="d0">Settlements with Shared Spaces</data>
      <data key="d1">cultural_or_religious_movement</data>
      <data key="d2">Communities where early inhabitants of Japan lived, featuring shared spaces for burials, food storage, and elaborate ceremonies, indicative of a more settled lifestyle and complex cultural practices.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-eb95afa387a7f6959f4e43eac7aed572</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210868</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Brisbane Dick, Louis V. Bell Funds, Joseph Pulitzer Bequest, The Annenberg Fund Inc. Gift, 1975&quot;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Brisbane Dick, Louis V. Bell Funds, Joseph Pulitzer Bequest, The Annenberg Fund Inc. Gift, 1975"</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;"source_or_artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;"The Jōmon people's skeletal remains and artifacts gifted to the Metropolitan Museum of Art in 1975."</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2837c43f553cbb300fb213aeacfbd742</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210868</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Jōmon people&quot;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Jōmon people"</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;"civilization_or_empire"\|"historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;"A prehistoric culture that thrived in Japan from approximately 14,000 BCE to 300 BCE. Known for their unique pottery and diverse diet including fruits, nuts, fish, and clams."</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2837c43f553cbb300fb213aeacfbd742</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210868</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Yayoi people&quot;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Yayoi people"</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;"civilization_or_empire"\|"historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;"The group that arrived in southern Japan around 300 BCE, bringing agriculture and changing the landscape of ancient Japan with a more stratified society."</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2837c43f553cbb300fb213aeacfbd742</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210868</data>
    </node>
