    </node>
    <node id="First Century CE">
      <data key="d0">First Century CE</data>
      <data key="d1">time_period</data>
      <data key="d2">The First Century CE refers to a specific period in history starting from AD 1, often regarded as a continuation of the Roman Republic, and ending with the fall of Jerusalem in 70 CE. During this era, significant historical events such as the construction of Alexander's mosaic in Pompeii, the life and death of Jesus Christ, and the early development of Christianity took place. Additionally, it was a time when Jewish culture and community within the Roman Empire experienced a notable flourishing period.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a1680636fd5c9fd858957b6ed8564896&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-754df6979479251d63e61b06ae37d304</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210875</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pompeii, Italy">
      <data key="d0">Pompeii, Italy</data>
      <data key="d1">geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">("A city in southern Italy known for its well-preserved ruins from the Roman era, including historical art such as mosaics. It serves as a key archaeological site that illustrates the cultural and technological advancements of the period.")</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a1680636fd5c9fd858957b6ed8564896</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210871</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Alexander the Great">
      <data key="d0">Alexander the Great</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">Alexander III of Macedon, also known as Alexander the Great, lived from 356 BCE to 323 BCE. He was an influential military leader famous for his extensive conquests, notably the decisive Battle of Issus where he defeated Darius III in 333 BCE. His reign significantly expanded the ancient Greek kingdom and fundamentally shaped history by establishing one of the largest and most powerful empires of its time. Alexander's legacy profoundly impacted Hellenistic culture and politics.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a1680636fd5c9fd858957b6ed8564896&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b0c96eda8ec75ff7b7bb50a03f72ca9a</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210875</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Battle of Issus (333 BCE)">
      <data key="d0">Battle of Issus (333 BCE)</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_event</data>
      <data key="d2">("A key event where Alexander the Great decisively defeated the Persian Empire, marking a turning point in military history and an event that later influenced mosaic representations in Pompeii."</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a1680636fd5c9fd858957b6ed8564896</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210871</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mediterranean Sea">
      <data key="d0">Mediterranean Sea</data>
      <data key="d1">geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">("The vast body of water that surrounds many ancient civilizations, facilitating trade, cultural exchange, and transportation networks among various regions like Greece and Italy.")</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a1680636fd5c9fd858957b6ed8564896</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210871</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ancient Greece">
      <data key="d0">Ancient Greece</data>
      <data key="d1">cultural_or_religious_movement</data>
      <data key="d2">Ancient Greece refers to a significant historical period in Greek history that encompassed major contributions in politics, philosophy, art, and athletics, which had profound impacts on regions such as Italy during the Roman Empire era. This civilization notably contributed to Western philosophy, literature, and art, with important periods including the Archaic (8th-6th century BCE) and Classical (5th-4th century BCE) eras. Additionally, Ancient Greece is known for being the historical context from which early Olympic Games originated and flourished, serving as a crucial foundation for various athletic events in the Western world.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7e152daf4ec320b12f5eed36d7fbef7a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a1680636fd5c9fd858957b6ed8564896&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3097b7631833d13406561b217f399efa</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210879</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hellenistic Era">
      <data key="d0">Hellenistic Era</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_period</data>
      <data key="d2">("The time following Alexander's conquests when a blend of Greek and Near Eastern cultures emerged, spreading ideas like Hellenism across the Mediterranean.")</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a1680636fd5c9fd858957b6ed8564896</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210872</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Roman Republic">
      <data key="d0">Roman Republic</data>
      <data key="d1">civilization_or_empire</data>
      <data key="d2">The Roman Republic was a significant political structure in ancient Rome, existing before the city-state transformed into an empire and ruled over regions including Italy, which encompassed places like Pompeii. It emerged as one of the principal powers, notably becoming the main rival to Carthage by the third century BCE. This period saw the consolidation of power and influence of the Roman Republic throughout key territories in the Italian peninsula and Mediterranean region.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-102a3de218fabdb69815ca7d8d632b0a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a1680636fd5c9fd858957b6ed8564896</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210878</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Augustus">
      <data key="d0">Augustus</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">Augustus was the first emperor of the Roman Empire and the founder of the Pax Romana, a period marked by stability and cultural flourishing (27 BC - 14 AD). As a ruler, Augustus implemented significant reforms in governance, religion, and military, which contributed to the empire's稳固和文化的繁荣。此外，他还授予了河对岸城市中的犹太人特权，显示了罗马与犹太人在他统治时期积极互动的良好关系。</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9e131b6d032e817cf8fce3b119011788&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a1680636fd5c9fd858957b6ed8564896&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c5228e6c35a7a85bef70ff1dbbea5597</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210879</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mosaic">
      <data key="d0">Mosaic</data>
      <data key="d1">source_or_artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">("A decorative technique using small pieces to create images, such as those found in Pompeii that depict historical events like the Battle of Issus. These mosaics were significant in preserving history through art.")</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a1680636fd5c9fd858957b6ed8564896</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210874</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Common Culture Development">
      <data key="d0">Common Culture Development</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">"This common culture developed over time.")(Figure 6.2 Timeline: Mediterranean Peoples)(credit “2000 BCE”: modification of work “Figurine of the Snake Goddess. Archaeological Museum of Herakleion” by O.Mustafin/Wikimedia Commons, CC0 1.0)(776 BCE: modification of work “Discobolus side 2” by Ricky Bennison/Wikimedia Commons, CC0 1.0)(490 BCE, 480–479 BCE: modification of work “Persian Warrior (Left) and Greek Warrior (Right) in a Duel” by National Museums Scotland/Wikimedia Commons, Public Domain)(334 BCE: modification of work “Alexander and Bucephalus Battle of Issus mosaic” by Museo Archeologico Nazionale – Naples/Wikimedia Commons, Public Domain)(264 BCE: modification of work “Heinrich Leutemann Hannibals Übergang über die Alpen (cropped)” by Musée Dauphinois Grenoble/Wikimeida Commons, PublicDomain</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e7947de4c39b1a3f1c2374df9010eff9</data>
