    </node>
    <node id="Mediterranean Peoples Timeline">
      <data key="d0">Mediterranean Peoples Timeline</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">The Mediterranean Peoples Timeline represents a periodization of key events in the history of the Mediterranean civilizations, highlighting significant developments from 2000 BCE to 334 BCE.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e7947de4c39b1a3f1c2374df9010eff9</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210874</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Phoenicians">
      <data key="d0">Phoenicians</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">The Phoenicians were a maritime civilization that flourished from approximately 1550 to 300 BCE, renowned for their sophisticated navigation methods and extensive trade networks. They played a significant role in advancing human communication by developing innovative technologies and contributing to the creation of alphabetic writing systems, which the Greeks later borrowed and adapted, leading to the development of their own unique writing system. Additionally, the Phoenicians are credited with introducing advanced technologies and engaging in trade across the Sahara Desert, facilitating interactions with North African societies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5d119d4dbb5f5c92b4539e982ee90fae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fe793fb21f15c20ae7e7d7a78a23923e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-176c01702975d9f9a613b9f917b023dd</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210882</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mediterranean Peoples">
      <data key="d0">Mediterranean Peoples</data>
      <data key="d1">geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">Mediterranean Peoples refers to the diverse ethnic groups that lived or interacted within the lands surrounding the Mediterranean Sea during the ancient periods.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fe793fb21f15c20ae7e7d7a78a23923e</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210875</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Late Bronze Age World">
      <data key="d0">Late Bronze Age World</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">The Late Bronze Age World encompasses the period from c. 1600–1200 BCE characterized by significant advancements in trade, technology, and culture across regions including Greece, Crete, Egypt, Anatolia, Mesopotamia, southern Iran, etc.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fe793fb21f15c20ae7e7d7a78a23923e</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210875</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Minoan Civilization">
      <data key="d0">Minoan Civilization</data>
      <data key="d1">civilization_or_empire</data>
      <data key="d2">The Minoan Civilization was a highly advanced society that flourished on the island of Crete during the Late Bronze Age from c. 2700–1450 BCE, known for its urban planning, art, and script called Linear A.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fe793fb21f15c20ae7e7d7a78a23923e</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210875</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mycenaean Civilization">
      <data key="d0">Mycenaean Civilization</data>
      <data key="d1">civilization_or_empire</data>
      <data key="d2">The Mycenaean Civilization represents one of the main Hellenic cultures in Greece during the Late Bronze Age from c. 1600–1100 BCE, known for advanced technologies and the Linear B script which provided evidence of early Greek language.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fe793fb21f15c20ae7e7d7a78a23923e</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210875</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Minoan Period&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Minoan Period&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">time_period</data>
      <data key="d2">The Minoan Period is a time period in history that spans from approximately 3000 to 1400 BCE. It is named after the legendary King Minos of Crete and encompasses the rise, peak, and fall of the Minoan civilization on the island of Crete.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b17e8c11e35dce286053409ecc49bab9</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210875</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Silk Roads">
      <data key="d0">Silk Roads</data>
      <data key="d1">geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">The Silk Roads refer to a series of ancient trade networks connecting East Asia, particularly China, with other significant regions such as Central Asia, India, parts of the Middle East, and eventually Europe during the early medieval period. These interconnected routes were primarily used for extensive commercial interactions between Roman and Chinese markets, facilitating both economic activities and substantial cultural exchanges. The network played a crucial role in spreading religions like Buddhism to various parts of Asia. Additionally, these trade routes served as strategic locations for the Mongols during their conquests, further emphasizing their multifaceted importance beyond commerce alone.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7933b681356fd848600cd95d60b6d201&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ff2834c0656da75835dd726c9d9fbb32&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-60db003690e75609ab22675a54f60648&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-582dc813b509020dd77ed6ee9db0e981&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dfd43568e4817f7de7fa5fd0bbb3f2e0</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210869</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Late Bronze Age&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Late Bronze Age&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">time_period</data>
      <data key="d2">The Late Bronze Age is a period in ancient history that spans roughly from 1500 to 1200 BCE. It is characterized by significant cultural, economic, and political interactions among various civilizations across the Near East and Mediterranean region.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b17e8c11e35dce286053409ecc49bab9</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210875</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Near East and Mediterranean in 2000 BCE&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Near East and Mediterranean in 2000 BCE&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">textbook_structure</data>
      <data key="d2">This refers to a geographical location centered around the Near East and the eastern portion of the Mediterranean region during the early second millennium BCE.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b17e8c11e35dce286053409ecc49bab9</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210875</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Mycenaean Greece&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Mycenaean Greece&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">civilization_or_empire</data>
      <data key="d2">Mycenaean Greece refers to a civilization that flourished in the Greek region during the Late Bronze Age, known for its powerful city-states and advanced cultural exchanges.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b17e8c11e35dce286053409ecc49bab9</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210875</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Alphabet&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Alphabet&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">textbook_structure</data>
      <data key="d2">The alphabet is a system of written symbols used for writing language, first appearing independently in Egypt (in hieroglyphs), but later notably evolving among the Phoenicians and Greeks.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b17e8c11e35dce286053409ecc49bab9</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210875</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;King Minos&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;King Minos&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">textbook_structure</data>
      <data key="d2">According to myth, King Minos of Crete was a powerful ruler who was said to have divine authority and the practice of hosting the Minotaur in his labyrinth on Crete. He is often associated with the Minoan civilization.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b17e8c11e35dce286053409ecc49bab9</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210875</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Palace of Knossos">
      <data key="d0">Palace of Knossos</data>
      <data key="d1">geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">Palace of Knossos was a significant palace on Crete where many cultural and religious practices took place.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2c44b9a5dc565a22076d920d391b10a2</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210875</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Labrys">
      <data key="d0">Labrys</data>
      <data key="d1">textbook_structure</data>
      <data key="d2">Labrys, or double ax, was a common weapon and symbol in the palaces of Crete, from which the word 'labyrinth' originated.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2c44b9a5dc565a22076d920d391b10a2</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210875</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bullfights">
      <data key="d0">Bullfights</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_event</data>
      <data key="d2">Young men and women participated in bullfights in the courtyards of Crete that may be related to the myth of the Minotaur.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2c44b9a5dc565a22076d920d391b10a2</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210875</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Minotaur">
      <data key="d0">Minotaur</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">The Minotaur, a mythical creature often associated with bullfights, was likely based on real events or practices in Minoan culture.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2c44b9a5dc565a22076d920d391b10a2</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210875</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Frescoes">
      <data key="d0">Frescoes</data>
      <data key="d1">source_or_artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">Frescoes depicting bullfights and other natural scenes were found in the palaces of Crete, providing insights into Minoan life and traditions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2c44b9a5dc565a22076d920d391b10a2</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210875</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Minoan Religion">
      <data key="d0">Minoan Religion</data>
      <data key="d1">cultural_or_religious_movement</data>
      <data key="d2">The Minoan religion revered bulls and a goddess associated with snakes, nature, and fertility, and was practiced through various rituals in small shrines and sacred places.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2c44b9a5dc565a22076d920d391b10a2</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210875</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Snakes and Women Figurines">
      <data key="d0">Snakes and Women Figurines</data>
      <data key="d1">source_or_artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">Figurines of snake-wielding female deities were abundant, suggesting women may have enjoyed high social status in Minoan society.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2c44b9a5dc565a22076d920d391b10a2</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210876</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Linear B">
      <data key="d0">Linear B</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">Linear B was a script used by scribes after the destruction of Cretan palaces around 1500 BCE and is considered an early form of Greek.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2c44b9a5dc565a22076d920d391b10a2</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210876</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology Website">
      <data key="d0">Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology Website</data>
      <data key="d1">source_or_artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">The link to Dartmouth University’s Aegean Prehistoric Archaeology website provides additional information on the archaeology of the region.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2c44b9a5dc565a22076d920d391b10a2</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210876</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mycenae">
      <data key="d0">Mycenae</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">Mycenaean is a term used for the Bronze Age culture that emerged after Cretan palaces were destroyed, centered around Mycenae, a city in Greece.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2c44b9a5dc565a22076d920d391b10a2</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210876</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Linear A">
      <data key="d0">Linear A</data>
      <data key="d1">textbook_structure</data>
      <data key="d2">Linear A was an earlier script possibly derived by Linear B scribes and found on Crete.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2c44b9a5dc565a22076d920d391b10a2</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210876</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Greeks from the Mainland">
      <data key="d0">Greeks from the Mainland</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">Historians concluded that Greeks from the mainland conquered Crete, leading to the rebuild of Knossos and the adoption of new scripts like Linear B.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2c44b9a5dc565a22076d920d391b10a2</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210876</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Balkans Migration">
      <data key="d0">Balkans Migration</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_event</data>
      <data key="d2">Around 2000 BCE, Bronze Age Greeks may have migrated into mainland Greece from the Balkans, adopting Minoan civilization features.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2c44b9a5dc565a22076d920d391b10a2</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210876</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bullfighting">
      <data key="d0">Bullfighting</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_event</data>
      <data key="d2">Young men and women participated in bullfights in the courtyards of Crete that may be related to the myth of the Minotaur.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2c44b9a5dc565a22076d920d391b10a2</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210876</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Transition Period">
      <data key="d0">Transition Period</data>
      <data key="d1">time_period</data>
      <data key="d2">This period marks the transition from Minoan to Mycenaean culture in the region, involving significant cultural and political changes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2c44b9a5dc565a22076d920d391b10a2</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210876</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Troy&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Troy&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">Trojan city located on the northwest coast of Anatolia, modern-day Turkey; known for its historical significance and involvement in the Trojan War</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-31f3de74d24f9f5ce534259a76865d74</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210876</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;westernTurkey&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;westernTurkey&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">Region along the Aegean coast of Anatolia, where Troy is situated</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-31f3de74d24f9f5ce534259a76865d74</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210876</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;TrojanWar&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;TrojanWar&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">Legendary conflict between the Mycenaeans and Trojans lasting for nine years, traditionally dated to around 1200 BCE</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-31f3de74d24f9f5ce534259a76865d74</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210876</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;KingAgamemnonofMycenae&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;KingAgamemnonofMycenae&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">Leading Mycenaean king who allegedly led the Achaean forces in the Trojan War; central figure in Greek mythology and literature</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-31f3de74d24f9f5ce534259a76865d74</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210876</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;LinearBtablets&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;LinearBtablets&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">source_or_artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">Script used during the Mycenaean era, providing textual evidence of the society; Linear B has been deciphered to reveal Mycenaean records</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-31f3de74d24f9f5ce534259a76865d74</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210876</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;TheCollapseoftheBronzeAgeWorld&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;TheCollapseoftheBronzeAgeWorld&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">Period marked by significant social and economic changes in several Mediterranean civilizations, including the fall of major centers like Troy</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-31f3de74d24f9f5ce534259a76865d74</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210876</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;MycenaeanGreece&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;MycenaeanGreece&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">civilization_or_empire</data>
      <data key="d2">Advanced Greek civilization that ruled parts of Greece during the Bronze Age, also referred to as Mycenaean Greece from around 1600 to 1100 BCE</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-31f3de74d24f9f5ce534259a76865d74</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210876</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;DarkAgeinGreece&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;DarkAgeinGreece&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">Period marked by cultural decline and reduced complexity following Mycenaean civilization's collapse; lasting approximately until the formation of city-states in 8th century BCE</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-31f3de74d24f9f5ce534259a76865d74</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210876</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;AegeanRegion&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;AegeanRegion&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">Geographic area surrounding the Aegean Sea, comprising islands such as Crete, Rhodes, and others; center of Mycenaean civilization</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-31f3de74d24f9f5ce534259a76865d74</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210876</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;GreeceandCrete&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;GreeceandCrete&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">Territory encompassing mainland Greece and the island of Crete during the Late Bronze Age to Iron Age</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-31f3de74d24f9f5ce534259a76865d74</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210876</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;NorthAfrica&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;NorthAfrica&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">Region located to the north of the Sahara Desert, home to various ancient civilizations that interacted with Mediterranean ones</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-31f3de74d24f9f5ce534259a76865d74</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210876</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Cyprus&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Cyprus&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">Island nation in the eastern Mediterranean Sea, involved in trade and interactions with other Bronze Age civilizations</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-31f3de74d24f9f5ce534259a76865d74</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210876</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;theLevant&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;theLevant&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">Geographic region bordering the eastern shore of the Mediterranean Sea, including parts of modern-day Lebanon, Israel, Palestine, Jordan, Syria, and Cyprus; area affected by the collapse of Late Bronze Age civilizations</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-31f3de74d24f9f5ce534259a76865d74</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210876</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;SeaPeoples&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;SeaPeoples&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">cultural_or_religious_movement</data>
      <data key="d2">Term used for various groups known to have invaded lands during the late 13th century BCE, including the Philistines, Sherden, Lukka, etc.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-31f3de74d24f9f5ce534259a76865d74</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210876</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Libyans&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Libyans&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_civilization_or_empire</data>
      <data key="d2">Libyans inhabited the North African coastal region west of Egypt and invaded the northern Nile River valley, settling there. The Sea Peoples' attacks along with Libyans contributed to the collapse of Egypt’s central governments after 1100 BCE, ending the New Kingdom period.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bb886d541386cb6fe2ba6006b55bcddc</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210876</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Phrygians&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Phrygians&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_civilization_or_empire</data>
      <data key="d2">The Phrygians from southeast Europe migrated into Asia Minor (Turkey).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bb886d541386cb6fe2ba6006b55bcddc</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210876</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Aramaeans&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Aramaeans&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_civilization_or_empire</data>
      <data key="d2">The Aramaeans, nomadic tribes speaking a Semitic language in the Arabian Desert, migrated into Syria and Mesopotamia.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bb886d541386cb6fe2ba6006b55bcddc</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210876</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Phoenicians&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Phoenicians&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_civilization_or_empire</data>
      <data key="d2">The Phoenicians emerged from Bronze Age Canaanites and established trading posts across the Mediterranean in cities like Sidon and Tyre. They were great sailors and traders, inventing the world's first known alphabet by strung consonant symbols to represent words, making literacy more accessible to a larger population. Their commercial success was attributed partly to their improved record-keeping system using this new script.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bb886d541386cb6fe2ba6006b55bcddc</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210876</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Bronze AgeCollapse&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Bronze AgeCollapse&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_event</data>
      <data key="d2">During the disruptions of the Late Bronze Age Collapse, artisans experimented with iron ore due to a lack of tin for bronze production. They developed techniques like steeling, quenching, and tempering to produce superior iron-based metals by around 900 BCE.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bb886d541386cb6fe2ba6006b55bcddc</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210877</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;IronAge&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;IronAge&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">time_period</data>
      <data key="d2">The period following the Bronze Age during which iron became prevalent in metalworking.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bb886d541386cb6fe2ba6006b55bcddc</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210877</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;PhoenicianAlphabet&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;PhoenicianAlphabet&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">Around 1100 BCE, Phoenicians expanded their trade and influence through the development of the alphabet. By the eighth century BCE, the Greeks adopted and adapted the Phoenician alphabet for writing their language while other cultures like Aramaean peoples and Israelites also incorporated this new script into their languages.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bb886d541386cb6fe2ba6006b55bcddc</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210877</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Carthage&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Carthage&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_civilization_or_empire</data>
      <data key="d2">Carthage was an important city-state within the Phoenician Empire, located in modern-day Tunisia. As the Phoenician kingdoms came under the influence of Iron Age western Asian empires such as the Assyrians, Chaldeans, and Persians, they continued to flourish due to their renowned artists, craftwork, and naval capabilities. By 700 BCE, many Phoenicians migrated to Carthage, significantly contributing to its size, wealth, and dominant position in the western Mediterranean by the fifth century BCE.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bb886d541386cb6fe2ba6006b55bcddc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-556925683aa4f212797f42e3798eb53b</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210883</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;6.2 Ancient Greece&quot;&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"6.2 Ancient Greece"&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;time_period&gt;</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;"The period from approximately 1100 BCE to 323 BCE during which significant developments in Greek civilization occurred, including the rise of city-states and major contributions in philosophy, literature, and art."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-deeeaa267faae20e29663a365d84657c</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210877</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Greek city-state development&quot;&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Greek city-state development"&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;historical_event&gt;</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;"The process through which independent city-state (polis) systems evolved politically, economically, and socially during the Iron Age of Ancient Greece."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-deeeaa267faae20e29663a365d84657c</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210877</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Mycenaean kingdoms collapse&quot;&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Mycenaean kingdoms collapse"&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;historical_event&gt;</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;"The collapse of Mycenaean civilization around 1100 BCE, marking the end of the Bronze Age and ushering in a period of cultural darkness known as the Greek Dark Ages."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-deeeaa267faae20e29663a365d84657c</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210877</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Innovative consensual governments&quot;&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Innovative consensual governments"&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;political_system&gt;</data>
      <data key="d2">Innovative consensual governments emerged during the evolution of Greek city-states, developing diverse forms of governance that went beyond simple monarchies. This period saw the development and refinement of democratic systems alongside other governance structures, reflecting a complex interplay of power and consent among citizens within these early political entities.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-deeeaa267faae20e29663a365d84657c</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210881</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Athenian and Spartan governance styles&quot;&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Athenian and Spartan governance styles"&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;geopolitical_event&gt;</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;"The contrasting approaches to governance seen in Athens (democracy) and Sparta (oligarchy), which significantly shaped life and culture during the Classical period."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-deeeaa267faae20e29663a365d84657c</data>
