    <node id="2021 Games of the XXXII Olympiad">
      <data key="d0">2021 Games of the XXXII Olympiad</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">The 2021 Olympics included over 300 events across thirty-three sports and were postponed due to the COVID-19 pandemic.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7e152daf4ec320b12f5eed36d7fbef7a</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210877</data>
    </node>
    <node id="International Olympic Committee">
      <data key="d0">International Olympic Committee</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">The International Olympic Committee was established in 1896 to oversee modern Olympic games.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7e152daf4ec320b12f5eed36d7fbef7a</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210877</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Olympia">
      <data key="d0">Olympia</data>
      <data key="d1">geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">Olympia is the site of the original stadium and serves as a sanctuary dedicated to Zeus, known for hosting the ancient Olympic Games during Ancient Greece. This location was of immense religious significance due to its worship of Zeus and the important role it played in the cultural and sporting life of Ancient Greece through the tradition of the Olympic Games.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7e152daf4ec320b12f5eed36d7fbef7a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f530a5f4d471fe3bf47b9e7f89b1521f</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210883</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Delphi and Nemea">
      <data key="d0">Delphi and Nemea</data>
      <data key="d1">geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">Delphi and Nemea are sanctuaries, locations for athletic contests near Argos during ancient times.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7e152daf4ec320b12f5eed36d7fbef7a</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210878</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Homos Iliad">
      <data key="d0">Homos Iliad</data>
      <data key="d1">textbook_structure</data>
      <data key="d2">The Iliad of Homer is a source text in which athletic contests were held to honor soldiers who had died, and where prizes, or athla (Athlete), are given out as honors.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7e152daf4ec320b12f5eed36d7fbef7a</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210878</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Olympic Games">
      <data key="d0">Olympic Games</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">The Olympic Games were ancient Greek athletic contests focused on skills useful for war, including running, jumping, throwing, wrestling, and early chariot races. </data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7e152daf4ec320b12f5eed36d7fbef7a</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210878</data>
    </node>
    <node id="stadion">
      <data key="d0">stadion</data>
      <data key="d1">cultural_or_religious_movement</data>
      <data key="d2">The stadion was a 200-yard sprint and the central event of the ancient Olympic Games.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7e152daf4ec320b12f5eed36d7fbef7a</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210878</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Archaic period">
      <data key="d0">Archaic period</data>
      <data key="d1">time_period</data>
      <data key="d2">The Archaic period refers to an early phase in ancient Greek history, particularly relevant for both the development of arts and trades. This era is notable for its increased sophistication and specialization, setting the stage for later advancements in artistic expression that would include the works by renowned artists like Myron and Praxiteles. Additionally, it saw enhanced interactions between Greek culture and neighboring regions such as Phoenicia and Egypt, which influenced the artistic output of the time.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d1b923a222fa396828cfac46f7a026c9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f530a5f4d471fe3bf47b9e7f89b1521f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d24eeb8bc264770c94a560ba5506d6b9</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210884</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ancient Olympic Games">
      <data key="d0">Ancient Olympic Games</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">The Ancient Olympic Games were a series of athletic competitions held in Olympia for Greek men; free admission and celebration of victors through poetry were key features.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f530a5f4d471fe3bf47b9e7f89b1521f</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210879</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Modern Olympic Games">
      <data key="d0">Modern Olympic Games</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">The Modern Olympic Games, inspired by the ancient version, are an international multi-sport event featuring athletes from various countries competing in various sports.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f530a5f4d471fe3bf47b9e7f89b1521f</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210879</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pindar">
      <data key="d0">Pindar</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">Pindar was a renowned Greek poet in the early fifth century BCE known for composing odes to Olympic victors.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f530a5f4d471fe3bf47b9e7f89b1521f</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210879</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Theodosius I">
      <data key="d0">Theodosius I</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">Theodosius I, Roman Emperor from 379-395 CE, is associated with the halt of the Olympics due to his Christian reign in 393 CE.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f530a5f4d471fe3bf47b9e7f89b1521f</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210879</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Phoenician alphabet">
      <data key="d0">Phoenician alphabet</data>
      <data key="d1">source_or_artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">The Phoenician alphabet was adapted by Greeks during the Archaic period, featuring vowels (a, e, i, o, u) which marked a significant innovation for literacy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f530a5f4d471fe3bf47b9e7f89b1521f</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210879</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Greek civilization">
      <data key="d0">Greek civilization</data>
      <data key="d1">cultural_or_religious_movement</data>
      <data key="d2">Greek society, influenced by contact with Phoenicians, saw a return to literacy and adaptation of technologies such as the alphabet.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f530a5f4d471fe3bf47b9e7f89b1521f</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210879</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Athenian silver coin&quot; from Figure 6.11&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Athenian silver coin" from Figure 6.11&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">source_or_artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">"An ancient Athenian silver coin used as a form of currency during the Archaic period, often inscribed with political and religious symbols."&lt;|</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e45be2e323006ed08ffd5a87585ef51d</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210879</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Pisistratus&quot;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Pisistratus"</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;"historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;"Historical figure who ruled Athens during his tyranny from 546 to 510 BCE. Pisistratus is known for establishing a form of benevolent despotism and introducing social changes in Athens, particularly land redistribution efforts for farmers to alleviate poverty and unrest. His rule laid the groundwork for future political developments in Athens."</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a0e8d0356f28f7848ea4b63b26a9e3d1</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210879</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Solon&quot;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Solon"</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;"historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;"Solon was an Athenian statesman who lived from approximately 638 to 558 BCE and later returned during his second phase around 594-592 BCE. He is famous for implementing comprehensive reforms, including debt relief measures, promoting social harmony, and initiating political restructuring based on wealth."</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a0e8d0356f28f7848ea4b63b26a9e3d1</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210879</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Council of Four Hundred&quot;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Council of Four Hundred"</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;"historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;"A new governmental body established during the time period 508-509 BCE in Athens. The council aimed to provide a more inclusive governance structure, allowing for greater civic participation and oversight by non-aristocrats."</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a0e8d0356f28f7848ea4b63b26a9e3d1</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210879</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Jury Courts&quot;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Jury Courts"</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;"historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;"Public legal institutions where citizens served as jurors to participate in the judicial process. Introduced during Solon's period, these courts represented a shift towards democratic elements in Athenian governance by involving common people in decision-making."</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a0e8d0356f28f7848ea4b63b26a9e3d1</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210879</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Wealth-Based Hierarchy&quot;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Wealth-Based Hierarchy"</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;"historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;"A political system where positions and roles within the government were determined based on an individual's economic status. This structured approach aimed to ensure that those with greater financial capacity held more influential positions in society."</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a0e8d0356f28f7848ea4b63b26a9e3d1</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210880</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tang Dynasty">
      <data key="d0">Tang Dynasty</data>
      <data key="d1">civilization_or_empire</data>
      <data key="d2">The Tang Dynasty, a powerful empire in China that ruled from 618 CE to 907 CE, was a pivotal period in Chinese history and one of the golden ages in terms of economic and cultural achievements. Known for its monumental constructions such as the Grand Canal, the dynasty significantly contributed to trade networks and exerted substantial influence over much of East Asia and beyond through both religious and political means.

The Tang Dynasty provided critical resources, engaged in trade relations, and later extended military support to Silla, a kingdom situated on the southern part of the Korean Peninsula. This period was marked by extensive economic growth and cultural advancements, alongside significant developments in science, literature, and art. Although the dynasty's influence and achievements were profound, internal financial difficulties ultimately led to its decline and fragmentation into chaos.

In summary, the Tang Dynasty played a crucial role not only within China but also internationally through support for Silla, while leaving behind enduring legacies through its monumental constructions and cultural achievements.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-42369c47f2f60a9ab4ac526953bf6b40&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2ffc1f0ad0cbf2629423c1e50ba8c211&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-65567519161cbb48515c5592fd7dfa04&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-68886ae6bf0868f51bdd288cf9f2091f</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210876</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Pisistratus's Benevolent Despotism&quot;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Pisistratus's Benevolent Despotism"</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;"historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;"A form of rule characterized by Pisistratus, which combined elements of tyranny with a focus on social welfare and popular support. This system aimed to pacify the populace through economic policies and public works."</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a0e8d0356f28f7848ea4b63b26a9e3d1</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210880</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Cleisthenes&quot;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Cleisthenes"</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;"historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;"Aristocratic statesman who played a crucial role in reorganizing Athenian democracy, notably introducing the territorial demotic system. His involvement marked a significant political shift from aristocracy to more egalitarian governance."</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a0e8d0356f28f7848ea4b63b26a9e3d1</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210880</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Spartan Intervention&quot;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Spartan Intervention"</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;"historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;"An external influence in the internal politics of Athens during Cleisthenes' reforms. The intervention by Spartan allies underscored the complex nature of Athenian political development and the impact of outside forces on its governance."</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a0e8d0356f28f7848ea4b63b26a9e3d1</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210880</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Archaic period&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Archaic period&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;time_period&gt;</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;The Archaic period of Ancient Greece spans the 8th to the end of the 6th century BCE, characterized by the development of early Greek culture, including literature, philosophy, and the beginnings of democratic institutions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3c1c88f98fc438aa2819347849f24dd4</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210880</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Spartans&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Spartans&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;historical_figure&gt;</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;The Spartans were a powerful ancient Greek city-state known for their militaristic lifestyle and the strict social hierarchy that placed the military elite above all else in society.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3c1c88f98fc438aa2819347849f24dd4</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210880</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Cleisthenes&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Cleisthenes&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;historical_figure&gt;</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;Cleisthenes was an Athenian statesman who reformed the system of government and is credited with establishing the foundations of democracy in Athens during the 6th century BCE.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3c1c88f98fc438aa2819347849f24dd4</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210880</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Solons system of government&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Solons system of government&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;historical_concept_or_event&gt;</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;Solon's system of government established fundamental political reforms in ancient Athens, laying the groundwork for later democratic institutions by introducing a council of four hundred and dividing citizens into socioeconomic classes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3c1c88f98fc438aa2819347849f24dd4</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210880</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;democracy&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;democracy&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;historical_concept_or_event&gt;</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;Democracy in its broadest sense, as practiced in ancient Athens during the 5th century BCE, involved citizen participation in decision-making processes through assembly meetings and juries.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3c1c88f98fc438aa2819347849f24dd4</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210880</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Council of Five Hundred&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Council of Five Hundred&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;source_or_artifact&gt;</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;The Council of Five Hundred was an important administrative body established by Cleisthenes that consisted of lot-selected members to manage daily affairs in Athens.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3c1c88f98fc438aa2819347849f24dd4</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210880</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;ten new tribes&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;ten new tribes&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;source_or_artifact&gt;</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;Ten new tribes were created by Cleisthenes to replace the four traditional tribes, promoting greater political equality and mixing citizens from different parts of Attica.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3c1c88f98fc438aa2819347849f24dd4</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210880</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Athenian Assembly&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Athenian Assembly&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;source_or_artifact&gt;</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;The Athenian Assembly was a key institution in ancient Athens' democratic system, where citizens gathered to discuss and decide on significant issues such as laws and wars.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3c1c88f98fc438aa2819347849f24dd4</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210880</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;judiciary&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;judiciary&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;textbook_structure&gt;</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;In the context of ancient Athenian democracy, the judiciary involved citizens serving on juries to interpret and apply laws and make judgments in legal cases.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3c1c88f98fc438aa2819347849f24dd4</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210880</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;Ancient Rome&quot;|">
      <data key="d0">"Ancient Rome"|</data>
      <data key="d1">civilization_or_empire"|</data>
      <data key="d2">A powerful ancient civilization centered in the Italian Peninsula, which grew to rule a significant portion of Europe and the Mediterranean. It had various governmental forms during its long history, including monarchy, republic, and empire. The society was structured according to a complex hierarchy with citizens, freedmen, slaves, and various social roles</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-629b8eb2a542c32ccebdf708239587e0</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210880</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;Julius Caesar&quot;|">
      <data key="d0">"Julius Caesar"|</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_figure"|</data>
      <data key="d2">A Roman general and statesman of the late Roman Republic, known for his military genius and political reforms. He played a critical role in the transformation from republic to empire, leading to his assassination.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-629b8eb2a542c32ccebdf708239587e0</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210880</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;Battle of Alexandria&quot;|">
      <data key="d0">"Battle of Alexandria"|</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event"|</data>
      <data key="d2">The final battle fought by Mark Antony and Cleopatra against Octavian’s forces, marking the end of Roman Civil Wars and the beginning of the Roman Empire. It was a naval battle where both sides suffered significant losses.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-629b8eb2a542c32ccebdf708239587e0</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210880</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;Pax Romana&quot;|">
      <data key="d0">"Pax Romana"|</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event"|</data>
      <data key="d2">A period of relative peace in the history of ancient Rome, lasting from approximately 27 AD to 180 AD. It was marked by general political stability and decreasing violence, leading to economic prosperity.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-629b8eb2a542c32ccebdf708239587e0</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210880</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;Rome&quot;|">
      <data key="d0">"Rome"|</data>
      <data key="d1">geographical_location"|</data>
      <data key="d2">The capital city of Italy which served as the center of power for both ancient and modern Rome. The name is also used as a general term that encompasses the entire Roman civilization.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-629b8eb2a542c32ccebdf708239587e0</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210880</data>
    </node>
