    <node id="431BCE">
      <data key="d0">431BCE</data>
      <data key="d1">time_period</data>
      <data key="d2">431 BCE marks the beginning of the Peloponnesian War with the invasion of Attica by Sparta and its allies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7f10b984844e9f3bf6227734f9ce29f8</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210881</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Peloponnesian League">
      <data key="d0">Peloponnesian League</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">The Peloponnesian League was an alliance that led to the start of the Peloponnesian War in 431 BCE.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7f10b984844e9f3bf6227734f9ce29f8</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210881</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Sparta">
      <data key="d0">Sparta</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">Sparta was an ancient Greek city-state renowned for its militaristic culture, emphasizing physical training and military discipline. It played a significant role during the Peloponnesian War against Athens, which involved substantial conflicts between their respective alliances. Sparta, as a leading member of the Peloponnesian League, took the initiative by invading Attica and initiating the Peloponnesian War. These actions were part of a broader conflict that shaped ancient Greek history through its intense military confrontations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5d119d4dbb5f5c92b4539e982ee90fae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7f10b984844e9f3bf6227734f9ce29f8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3097b7631833d13406561b217f399efa</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210887</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Athenian Aggression">
      <data key="d0">Athenian Aggression</data>
      <data key="d1">cultural_or_religious_movement</data>
      <data key="d2">Athens' actions that were perceived as aggressive by Sparta led to the outbreak of the Peloponnesian War.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7f10b984844e9f3bf6227734f9ce29f8</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210881</data>
    </node>
    <node id="421BCE">
      <data key="d0">421BCE</data>
      <data key="d1">time_period</data>
      <data key="d2">421 BCE marks the end of a significant phase in the Peloponnesian War when the Peace of Nicias was agreed upon and maintained for some time, keeping the Athenian empire intact.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7f10b984844e9f3bf6227734f9ce29f8</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210881</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Peace of Nicias">
      <data key="d0">Peace of Nicias</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">The Peace of Nicias in 421 BCE temporarily halted hostilities between Athens and Sparta during the Peloponnesian War.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7f10b984844e9f3bf6227734f9ce29f8</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210881</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Plague of 426BCE">
      <data key="d0">Plague of 426BCE</data>
      <data key="d1">source_or_artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">The Plague of 426 BCE, documented by Thucydides, was a significant event that affected Athens heavily, leading to many deaths including Pericles.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7f10b984844e9f3bf6227734f9ce29f8</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210881</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pericles">
      <data key="d0">Pericles</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">Pericles was a prominent ancient Greek statesman, general, and orator who played a crucial role in developing democratic institutions during his time as an Athenian statesman. He is renowned for leading Athens during its golden age in the 5th century BC and initiating significant building projects, notably those seen today at Athens' Acropolis. Serving his city-state not only through strategic leadership but also in periods of conflict like the Peloponnesian War, Pericles advised Athenians on how to respond to Spartans' aggression. His tenure was marked by personal tragedy as he faced the Plague of 426 BCE, an event that likely had a profound impact on both his life and his work for Athens.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7f10b984844e9f3bf6227734f9ce29f8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7a1e917dfafdb038f16895e854ca80d3</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210888</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Alcibiades">
      <data key="d0">Alcibiades</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">Alcibiades, a young Athenian politician from Pericles' kinsmen, was involved in planning an invasion of Sicily which diverted resources and attention away from the ongoing conflict with Sparta.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7f10b984844e9f3bf6227734f9ce29f8</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210882</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Figure 6.17 The Death of Socrates">
      <data key="d0">Figure 6.17 The Death of Socrates</data>
      <data key="d1">textbook_structure</data>
      <data key="d2">Figure 6.17 The Death of Socrates is a large oil painting from 1787 by Jacques-Louis David, depicting the final moments of Socrates drinking hemlock as described in historical accounts.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c8a46da47c7b3c4b8e674ef868767514</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210882</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Socrates">
      <data key="d0">Socrates</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">Socrates is portrayed as a central figure in philosophical dialogue and practice, known for his method of questioning others to explore ethical and moral questions. He was forced to drink poison by the state during the period 399-360 BCE.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c8a46da47c7b3c4b8e674ef868767514</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210882</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Plato">
      <data key="d0">Plato</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">Plato was a classical Greek philosopher who founded the Academy in Athens around 387 BCE. Known for incorporating mathematical thought as superior knowledge according to Neoplatonists like Nicholas Cusanus, Plato's work extensively covered various areas of study including ethics, physics, psychology, and epistemology. He is recognized for writing dialogues that featured Socrates and contributed significantly to the philosophical system through his Academy in Athens.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c8a46da47c7b3c4b8e674ef868767514&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6472411bb2f04035fe16270eda293c74</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210887</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Aristotle">
      <data key="d0">Aristotle</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">Aristotle was a prominent Greek philosopher who made significant contributions to logic, science, and ethics during the Classical period of philosophy. He was initially a distinguished pupil under Plato but later disagreed with his teacher on various philosophical questions. In 335 BCE, Aristotle went on to found his own school at the Lyceum in Athens, where he focused extensively on natural philosophy and biology. His work established foundational principles that influenced subsequent philosophical thought.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c8a46da47c7b3c4b8e674ef868767514&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-54ce354bc2591fc3936924cededa981a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6472411bb2f04035fe16270eda293c74</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210889</data>
    </node>
    <node id="The Academy">
      <data key="d0">The Academy</data>
      <data key="d1">cultural_or_religious_movement</data>
      <data key="d2">The Academy refers to a specific institution established by Plato in ancient Greece, which served as a center for philosophical study and discussion in the Western tradition.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c8a46da47c7b3c4b8e674ef868767514</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210883</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Lyceum">
      <data key="d0">Lyceum</data>
      <data key="d1">cultural_or_religious_movement</data>
      <data key="d2">The Lyceum was Aristotle's own school founded in Athens around 335 BCE, emphasizing empirical studies of nature and the natural world.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c8a46da47c7b3c4b8e674ef868767514</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210883</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Golden Age of Athens">
      <data key="d0">Golden Age of Athens</data>
      <data key="d1">time_period</data>
      <data key="d2">The period known as the Golden Age of Athens refers to the flourishing of arts, culture, theater, and philosophy around the fifth century BCE. It is marked by significant contributions from figures like Socrates, Plato, and Sophocles.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c8a46da47c7b3c4b8e674ef868767514</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210883</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Sophocles">
      <data key="d0">Sophocles</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">Sophocles was a renowned playwright who wrote tragedies that included elements of music and dance, among other features, in the fourth century BCE. His works such as 'Antigone' continue to reflect philosophical themes relevant even today.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c8a46da47c7b3c4b8e674ef868767514</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210883</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Euripides">
      <data key="d0">Euripides</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">Euripides was another prominent playwright from ancient Greece known for his provocative and innovative tragedies which shared similar characteristics with the works of Sophocles.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c8a46da47c7b3c4b8e674ef868767514</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210883</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Greek Theatre">
      <data key="d0">Greek Theatre</data>
      <data key="d1">cultural_or_religious_movement</data>
      <data key="d2">Greek Theatre is a form of performance, marked by its use of masks, singing, and plot-driven drama, evolving from ancient traditions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d1b923a222fa396828cfac46f7a026c9</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210883</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Dionysus">
      <data key="d0">Dionysus</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">Dionysus is the god of wine in Greek mythology, depicted as a theatrical mask on a terracotta figurine from the first or second century BCE.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d1b923a222fa396828cfac46f7a026c9</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210883</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Terracotta figurine">
      <data key="d0">Terracotta figurine</data>
      <data key="d1">source_or_artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">A terracotta figurine depicting Dionysus, used in ancient Greek theatre as a representation of a theatrical mask worn by actors.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d1b923a222fa396828cfac46f7a026c9</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210883</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Golden Age">
      <data key="d0">Golden Age</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">The Golden Age refers to the period of significant cultural and intellectual development in ancient Greece, encompassing history, theatre, and art.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d1b923a222fa396828cfac46f7a026c9</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
