    <node id="Buddhism">
      <data key="d0">Buddhism</data>
      <data key="d1">cultural_or_religious_movement</data>
      <data key="d2">Buddhism is one of the two major religious traditions that emerged in South Asia during the early Middle Ages and spread widely across the Asian continent, including China, via various means such as trade routes like the Silk Roads. Originating in India, this significant world religion has had a profound impact on history, particularly influencing figures such as Menander I, who incorporated Buddhist teachings into his governance and personal beliefs.

The teachings of Buddhism are centered around the alleviation of suffering and attainment of enlightenment, emphasizing concepts like compassion, non-violence, and wisdom cultivation. Through its establishment in China via trade routes, Buddhism gained increasing prominence over time, becoming more deeply integrated into Chinese society as it spread through trade and interaction along the Silk Roads.

This comprehensive summary integrates key information from all provided descriptions, resolving any apparent contradictions by highlighting the role of Buddhist teachings, their spreading mechanisms across Asia including China, and influence on historical figures.</data>
