    <node id="Gladiators">
      <data key="d0">Gladiators</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">Gladiators were combatants in public spectacles such as gladiator games, often consisting of enslaved or freed individuals who fought for entertainment. This fighting performance was prevalent in Roman society and is exemplified by the names 'Amazon' and 'Achillea,' female gladiators whose presence is documented on a stone relief from the 1st or 2nd century CE. Gladiatorial games served as significant public entertainments where these skilled combatants participated, showcasing their prowess in the arenas of ancient Rome.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4f47473987e3a9f7e2d495ebfe8eb083&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9ff5358fb09235572f0a0aa9ee24e2f5</data>
