    <node id="&lt;&quot;Column Base of Antoninus Pius&quot;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Column Base of Antoninus Pius"</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;"source_or_artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;"The Column Base of Antoninus Pius is a significant artifact that visually represents the apotheosis and deification of Emperor Antoninus Pius and his wife Empress Faustina. This base is located in Rome, providing a tangible representation of the imperial cult practices during this period."</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-035528ca825e083d04a2873ad0606500</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210907</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Imperial Cult Period&quot;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Imperial Cult Period"</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;"time_period</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;"The Imperial Cult Period refers to the historical time when the Roman Empire adopted and expanded the practice of deifying its emperors after their deaths. This period signifies a transformation in religious practices, emphasizing the divine nature of the ruling class."</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-035528ca825e083d04a2873ad0606500</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210907</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Roman Religion&quot;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Roman Religion"</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;"cultural_or_religious_movement</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;"Roman Religion encompasses the complex and evolving belief systems, rituals, and practices that were prevalent in ancient Rome. It includes the worship of gods, heroes, and later emperors, reflecting both local traditions and Hellenistic influences."</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-035528ca825e083d04a2873ad0606500</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210907</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Apotheosis&quot;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Apotheosis"</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;"historical_event</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;"Apotheosis is a historical concept where an individual, often a deceased emperor or prominent figure, was elevated to divine status through a declaration of divinity. This process marked their transition from the mortal realm to the divine in Roman religious practices."</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-035528ca825e083d04a2873ad0606500</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210907</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Cult of Deified Emperors&quot;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Cult of Deified Emperors"</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;"civilization_or_empire</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;"The Cult of Deified Emperors refers to the worship of deified Roman emperors. This practice was institutionalized during the Imperial Cult Period and played a significant role in the political and religious life of the Roman Empire."</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-035528ca825e083d04a2873ad0606500</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210907</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Rome&quot;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Rome"</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;"geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">Rome is the capital city of Italy, known for its historical significance as the center of the Roman Empire, where the imperial cult practices were established and flourished during the Imperial Cult Period. In 536, it was occupied by Belisarius, leading to significant advancements in reclaiming cities across Italy until the early 540s.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0e7c06e4e57d374415a1c78f71508589&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-035528ca825e083d04a2873ad0606500</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210912</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Caligula&quot;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Caligula"</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;"historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">Gaius Julius Caesar Augustus Germanicus, known as Caligula, was a Roman emperor who ruled from 37 to 41 CE. Reputed for his erratic and often cruel behavior during his tenure, Caligula asserted deification while he was still alive. His actions also included persecution of the Jewish population, which resulted in riots breaking out in Alexandria. Notably, Caligula responded to these riots by moving images off synagogues as a gesture of conciliation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-035528ca825e083d04a2873ad0606500&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a2d1db24c99f5fef7c3bc0aa3497e439</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210915</data>
    </node>
    <node id="204 BCE">
      <data key="d0">204 BCE</data>
      <data key="d1">time_period</data>
      <data key="d2">204 BCE marks a significant historical event where the goddess Magna Mater was brought to Rome during Hannibal’s invasion of Italy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1ad4001529d6aa235590af8279266d7d</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210908</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Temple of Vesta">
      <data key="d0">Temple of Vesta</data>
      <data key="d1">geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">The Temple of Vesta, dedicated to the goddess Vesta, served as the location for the priestly office of vestal virgins.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1ad4001529d6aa235590af8279266d7d</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210908</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Vestal Virgins">
      <data key="d0">Vestal Virgins</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">The Vestal Virgins were ancient Roman priestesses chosen at an early age to serve for ten to thirty years in the Temple of Vesta. These six women, upon taking a vow of chastity, were responsible for maintaining the sacred fire in Rome, ensuring its continuous and pure state within the temple.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f4adb7fe027038f7279ff275f67a969f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1ad4001529d6aa235590af8279266d7d</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210914</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Magna Mater">
      <data key="d0">Magna Mater</data>
      <data key="d1">cultural_or_religious_movement</data>
      <data key="d2">The cult of Magna Mater, the goddess brought from Asia Minor, symbolizes a religious movement that influenced Roman beliefs and practices.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1ad4001529d6aa235590af8279266d7d</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210909</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ostia">
      <data key="d0">Ostia</data>
      <data key="d1">geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">Ostia was the port city where the ship carrying the statue of Magna Mater was grounded during Hannibal’s invasion.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1ad4001529d6aa235590af8279266d7d</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210909</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Claudia Quinta">
      <data key="d0">Claudia Quinta</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">Claudia Quinta is a historical figure whose actions demonstrated her virginity and played a significant role in the miraculous event at Ostia.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1ad4001529d6aa235590af8279266d7d</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210909</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hannibal's Invasion of Italy">
      <data key="d0">Hannibal's Invasion of Italy</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">The invasion by Hannibal into Italy, dating back to 204 BCE, triggered significant historical changes including the transport of the goddess Magna Mater to Rome.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1ad4001529d6aa235590af8279266d7d</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210909</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ship Burial Ritual">
      <data key="d0">Ship Burial Ritual</data>
      <data key="d1">source_or_artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">The concept of burying a ship in stone as a sacrifice or ritual act symbolizes the religious practices and beliefs prevalent during the ancient Roman era.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1ad4001529d6aa235590af8279266d7d</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210909</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Roman Historian Herodian of Antioch">
      <data key="d0">Roman Historian Herodian of Antioch</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">Herodian of Antioch, a source of historical information about Claudia Quinta’s miraculous event, provides insight into the sanctity of the vestal virgins and their influence in Rome.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1ad4001529d6aa235590af8279266d7d</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210909</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Claudia Quintas rescue of the ship carrying Magna Mater to Rome">
      <data key="d0">Claudia Quintas rescue of the ship carrying Magna Mater to Rome</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_event</data>
      <data key="d2">Cláudia Quinta's rescue of the ship carrying Magna Mätr is an historical event inspired by Lambert Lombard, which demonstrates the enduring inspiration of the story of a vestal virgin long after the fall of the empire.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1aadcf58877c3051f5fe46d47a39f00b</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
