    <node id="Carthage">
      <data key="d0">Carthage</data>
      <data key="d1">civilization_or_empire</data>
      <data key="d2">Carthage was a powerful city-state located in North Africa, originally a Phoenician civilization turned empire known for its significant naval power and involvement in conflicts with Rome during the Punic Wars, which took place throughout the third and second centuries BCE. It rivaled Rome for dominance in the western Mediterranean region, leading to periods of intense competition and marking shifts in power dynamics.

Despite facing challenges such as Roman persecution from the third century, including dramatic stories about martyrs like Perpetua, Carthage maintained a thriving early Christian community. Eventually, after falling under Vandal control, it was recaptured by the Byzantines under Justinian's command. This historical overview unites various aspects of Carthage’s complex history, from its early strength as an ancient Phoenician empire to its conflicts with Rome and its later capture by the Byzantines.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-102a3de218fabdb69815ca7d8d632b0a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7595511c6280259b348ea8ca3413a6d3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5874c3ff9fc6f9f51a9a1072e47f5bed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-622311a2741b1845b3a8c00c6e5d7b93&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-094b316ebd1084db7394e7e033e7e84b</data>
