    <node id="&lt;historical_concept_or_event&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;historical_concept_or_event&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">The entity in focus is a historical concept or event involving multiple significant episodes, which include the famines that affected rural areas around the Nile delta during a specific period. These famines led to a decrease in agriculture and prompted people to move to urban centers for survival opportunities such as unskilled wage labor. Concurrently, the Mamluk elite fled their citadels during a plague era, impacting governance and stability within the region by leaving behind those who stayed to protect their establishments.

This historical concept also encompasses broader events like the crushing of optimates, the division of the Roman Empire among Octavian, Lepidus, and Antony marking the transition from the Roman Republic to the Roman Empire. The period includes civil war, leading up to the decisive naval battle of Actium won by Octavian in 31 BCE.

Additionally, the term covers the Silk Roads, a network of ancient trade routes that originated in Han China (modern-day Xinjiang) and extended as far west as the Mediterranean Sea, connecting East, Central, South Asia with European civilizations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5010e0f708f284a5e25e323f470ad4d6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b8ad3f8265df80ff0cbd95a7914a6123&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5914a1bc816edf3338d898be6d68146b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2ae7648f16b65be16cc6ebc5e6c4363b</data>
