    <node id="Last Glaciation Period">
      <data key="d0">Last Glaciation Period</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">The Last Glaciation Period occurred from around 126,000 years ago to about 11,700 years ago, during which sea levels were lower and allowed a land bridge between Asia and North America.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-97ab4cac183886ee18d87b065b2e0ad5</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210914</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Rome">
      <data key="d0">Rome</data>
      <data key="d1">geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">Rome refers to both the geographical area of ancient Rome, including its extensive empire centered around it and historical political authorities such as the Roman population. Rome has played a pivotal role in world history, significantly influencing military, political, social, religious, and architectural landscapes within its territory and beyond, particularly through its strategic actions during conflicts such as the Second Punic War with Hannibal, where the Battle of Zama established its dominance.

In terms of governance and urban development, Rome shaped local settlements and coastal regions in North Africa through extensive building projects. Additionally, Roman influence extended to religious matters, notably affecting bishops of Rome who were influenced by the Cluniac reform program to implement changes within their church, such as ending simony (illicit financial transactions) and ensuring clerical celibacy.

Throughout various historical periods—from facing Hannibal in the Second Punic War to exerting influence during the Hellenistic period—Rome's legacy has been profound and far-reaching. The term "Rome" encapsulates both the ancient city-state and its extensive empire, encompassing strategic military victories, governance innovations, and cultural legacies that continue to shape our understanding of history and civilization.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7595511c6280259b348ea8ca3413a6d3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6988ac1b561abfb0681a94305ea53594&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-774c1aebb34d8cee02655e76bf182dd9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cdc7bbe967c34e7d69b34931a2e372ce&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-094b316ebd1084db7394e7e033e7e84b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-65da8baf696cc67fe70482eefac175f5</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210901</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Continental United States">
      <data key="d0">Continental United States</data>
      <data key="d1">geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">The Continental United States refers to the region of the United States excluding Alaska and Hawaii, extending from the Great Lakes to Central America in the east and west respectively.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-97ab4cac183886ee18d87b065b2e0ad5</data>
