    <node id="Obsidian, salt, cotton, cacao, marine shells">
      <data key="d0">Obsidian, salt, cotton, cacao, marine shells</data>
      <data key="d1">source_or_artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">Primary goods traded by Teotihuacán in southern regions of Mexico during its period of significant influence.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-03a15d067f74707fd348b5bcb5e978e7</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210918</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;geographical_location&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;geographical_location&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">The geographical locations mentioned include Adulis, Aksum, Eritrea, Gulf of Suez, Red Sea, Sinai Peninsula, and Yemeni area of the Arabian Peninsula. These locations are predominantly situated in northeastern Africa and the southern Arabian Peninsula.

Adulis is an ancient port city located on the southern shore of the Red Sea in what is now Eritrea, known for its role in trade during the 2nd and 3rd centuries AD. Aksum, a historical capital city of the Kingdom of Aksum, is situated in the modern-day Tigray Region of Ethiopia and was significant as a center of trade and culture from the 1st to the 8th centuries AD.

Eritrea, a country bordering the Red Sea with coastlines along the Horn of Africa, has territories that influenced trade, agriculture, and population dynamics during historical periods. The Red Sea, running between various countries including Eritrea, Sudan, Djibouti, Yemen, Saudi Arabia, Israel, Jordan, Egypt, and the Gaza Strip, was pivotal in ancient trade routes.

The Sinai Peninsula, located in northeastern Egypt with a coastline along the Gulf of Suez on its northern part, serves as a strategic geographical feature. The Yemeni area, situated in south-central Yemen, played a significant role in trade during ancient times, especially due to the influence of the Sabaeans and their impact on the Arabian Peninsula.

These locations served not only as centers of trade but also experienced the significant impacts of historical events such as the Black Death, particularly affecting densely populated cities.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b8ad3f8265df80ff0cbd95a7914a6123&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5914a1bc816edf3338d898be6d68146b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-89576c0d4225b9ce6218c684fd4f58b7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2ae7648f16b65be16cc6ebc5e6c4363b</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210904</data>
    </node>
    <node id="400 BCE to 300 CE">
      <data key="d0">400 BCE to 300 CE</data>
      <data key="d1">time_period</data>
      <data key="d2">The timeframe when major construction and cultural significance of Teotihuacán emerged, exerting considerable influence on Mesoamerican societies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-03a15d067f74707fd348b5bcb5e978e7</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210918</data>
    </node>
    <node id="100,000 people">
      <data key="d0">100,000 people</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">Estimated population count for Teotihuacán in the 3rd century CE, signifying its size and cultural importance as one of the largest cities in pre-Columbian America.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-03a15d067f74707fd348b5bcb5e978e7</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210918</data>
    </node>
    <node id="6th and 7th centuries CE">
      <data key="d0">6th and 7th centuries CE</data>
      <data key="d1">time_period</data>
      <data key="d2">The period during which Teotihuacán declined and its influence waned with the rise of other civilizations in Mesoamerica.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-03a15d067f74707fd348b5bcb5e978e7</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210918</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Teotihuacán&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Teotihuacán&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;geographical_location&gt;</data>
      <data key="d2">Teotihuacán is an ancient city located northeast of Mexico City, known for its impressive architectural structures such as the Pyramid of the Sun and the Pyramid of the Moon. It played a significant role in Mesoamerican history through its trade networks, religious practices, and interactions with other civilizations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4083e7ff876dd54b2e91d37d47639816</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210918</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Pyramid_of_the_Sun&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Pyramid_of_the_Sun&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;source_or_artifact&gt;</data>
      <data key="d2">It is a monumental pyramid structure located in Teotihuacán that stands 197 feet tall. It serves as an important landmark and a testament to the city's architectural prowess and religious practices, particularly centered around deities like Quetzalcoatl.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4083e7ff876dd54b2e91d37d47639816</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210918</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Pyramid_of_the_Moon&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Pyramid_of_the_Moon&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;source_or_artifact&gt;</data>
      <data key="d2">It is another significant pyramid structure found in Teotihuacán that stands 141 feet tall. Like the Pyramid of the Sun, it plays a crucial role in understanding the city's architectural and religious heritage.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4083e7ff876dd54b2e91d37d47639816</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210918</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Temple_of_Quetzalcoatl&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Temple_of_Quetzalcoatl&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;source_or_artifact&gt;</data>
      <data key="d2">This temple is one of the key ceremonial structures within Teotihuacán, dedicated to Quetzalcoatl, the feathered serpent deity. It underscores the city's religious and cultural significance through its design and location.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4083e7ff876dd54b2e91d37d47639816</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210918</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Quetzalcoatl&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Quetzalcoatl&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;cultural_or_religious_movement&gt;</data>
      <data key="d2">Quetzalcoatl is a prominent deity in Mesoamerican religion, associated with knowledge, wind, and rain. His worship is reflected in the architectural features of Teotihuacán, such as the Temple of Quetzalcoatl, highlighting its religious importance.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4083e7ff876dd54b2e91d37d47639816</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210918</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Elite_Military_Leaders&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Elite_Military_Leaders&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;historical_figure&gt;</data>
      <data key="d2">These individuals were key figures responsible for constructing significant architectural works like the Pyramids of Teotihuacán and likely occupied prominent positions within the city's social hierarchy. Their roles encompassed both military leadership and urban development.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4083e7ff876dd54b2e91d37d47639816</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210918</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Maya city-states&quot;&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Maya city-states"&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;"civilization_or_empire"&gt;</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;"The Maya city-states were dominant during the Early Classic period (250-600 CE</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-61bac3803539bced81376c7bf783eb5d</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210918</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Maya Calendar System">
      <data key="d0">Maya Calendar System</data>
      <data key="d1">textbook_structure</data>
      <data key="d2">"The Maya Calendar System refers to the two interlocking calendars that reveal important dates years in advance.")&lt;|</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a3469f37ac0d52cc0b4327a6357a2e63</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210918</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Maya Great Cycle">
      <data key="d0">Maya Great Cycle</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">Maya Great Cycle is a period starting in 2012 CE, marking the beginning of a new cycle rather than an end of the world according to scholars.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a3469f37ac0d52cc0b4327a6357a2e63</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210918</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Classic Period">
      <data key="d0">Classic Period</data>
      <data key="d1">time_period</data>
      <data key="d2">The Classic Period started around 250 CE and lasted until about 900, during which urbanization in the Maya world expanded extensively with numerous city-states emerging.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a3469f37ac0d52cc0b4327a6357a2e63</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210918</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tikal, Calakmul, Palenque, Copan, Yaxchilan, Piedras Negras">
      <data key="d0">Tikal, Calakmul, Palenque, Copan, Yaxchilan, Piedras Negras</data>
      <data key="d1">civilization_or_empire</data>
      <data key="d2">These are some of the most powerful and influential city-states during the Classic Period, known for their rulers referred to as 'divine lords'.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a3469f37ac0d52cc0b4327a6357a2e63</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210918</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Divine Lords">
      <data key="d0">Divine Lords</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">Divine Lords were the rulers of Maya city-states who controlled religious rituals, ceremonies, and wars against other city-states.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a3469f37ac0d52cc0b4327a6357a2e63</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210918</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Urbanization">
      <data key="d0">Urbanization</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">Urbanization during the Classic Period in the Maya region involved significant growth in population density and infrastructure development within urban centers. This phenomenon transformed both social and economic structures within the region. After the Black Death, a similar pattern of urbanization was observed, where people migrated from rural areas to cities in search of safety, further illustrating the transformative impact of this process on society.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d85eeeedc638b2dc40a70eec24196c37&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a3469f37ac0d52cc0b4327a6357a2e63</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210922</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tribute System">
      <data key="d0">Tribute System</data>
      <data key="d1">cultural_or_religious_movement</data>
      <data key="d2">A system where defeated enemies provided animal products, salt, textiles, artwork, and agricultural goods like cacao and maize as tribute to victorious city-states.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a3469f37ac0d52cc0b4327a6357a2e63</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210918</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Maya World">
      <data key="d0">Maya World</data>
      <data key="d1">geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">At its height, the Maya civilization included as many as forty city-states, and today includes ruins such as Palenque.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d9cbdf3b7df94a4de04479b6873b5dfd</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210918</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Maya Civilization">
      <data key="d0">Maya Civilization</data>
      <data key="d1">civilization_or_empire</data>
      <data key="d2">The primary civilization discussed is the Maya, which reached its peak in the seventh century with vibrant temples and rituals.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d9cbdf3b7df94a4de04479b6873b5dfd</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210918</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Family Ancestors">
      <data key="d0">Family Ancestors</data>
      <data key="d1">cultural_or_religious_movement</data>
      <data key="d2">Ancestral veneration was central to Maya religious practices, involving shrines, bloodletting, and interments under floors. Temple structures served as grand versions of family shrines with tombs for deceased kings.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d9cbdf3b7df94a4de04479b6873b5dfd</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210918</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Maya Calendar">
      <data key="d0">Maya Calendar</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">The calendar was integral to Maya rituals, influencing the timing of religious practices and divine interactions according to seasonal markers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d9cbdf3b7df94a4de04479b6873b5dfd</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210918</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ritual Bloodletting">
      <data key="d0">Ritual Bloodletting</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">Bloodletting rituals were practiced to communicate with ancestors; releases sacred essence 'chu’ulel' through blood.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d9cbdf3b7df94a4de04479b6873b5dfd</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210918</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Human Sacrifice">
      <data key="d0">Human Sacrifice</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Human Sacrifice within the Aztec society was a significant ritual practice deeply embedded in their religious life and ceremonies. Captured individuals, especially rival leaders, were sometimes sacrificed during ball games and other important rituals such as those held in the Templo Mayor and other temples. This practice can be traced back to earlier cultures, indicating its long-standing significance in Mesoamerican spiritual traditions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d9cbdf3b7df94a4de04479b6873b5dfd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-833156afc8ed2d210a0859d7cb784483</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210923</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Olmec Ritual Ball Game">
      <data key="d0">Olmec Ritual Ball Game</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">Information about Olmec rituals is limited but suggests a similar game was played with protective padding and stone rings for the ball.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d9cbdf3b7df94a4de04479b6873b5dfd</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210918</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ritual Ball Game">
      <data key="d0">Ritual Ball Game</data>
      <data key="d1">cultural_or_religious_movement</data>
      <data key="d2">Maya ritual ball games held significant religious practice, reenacting mythological aspects and ending in potential sacrifice if losing.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d9cbdf3b7df94a4de04479b6873b5dfd</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210919</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Maya Ball Court">
      <data key="d0">Maya Ball Court</data>
      <data key="d1">source_or_artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">"Maya ball court designs varied from city to city but all had a common I-shaped layout."&lt;&lt;image:https://example.com/image1.jpg&gt;&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-86b8b74f4921d905c9deab7414c00e0c</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210919</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tikal Ballcourt">
      <data key="d0">Tikal Ballcourt</data>
      <data key="d1">specific_example_of_artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">"A specific example of a Maya ball court, located in Tikal."&lt;&lt;image:https://example.com/tikal_ballcourt_credit_gary_todd_flickr_public_domain.jpg&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-86b8b74f4921d905c9deab7414c00e0c</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210919</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Maya Writing System">
      <data key="d0">Maya Writing System</data>
      <data key="d1">cultural_or_religious_movement</data>
      <data key="d2">The Maya Writing System refers to a phonetically based writing system that was eventually decoded after years of research, making it one of the most complex and advanced writing systems in Mesoamerica. This writing system is exemplified by stucco characters from Palenque, which were created between the fifth and eighth centuries CE. Scholars have succeeded in decoding this intricate system, revealing its sophistication and significance within the cultural context of ancient Maya civilization.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-733f0173d74970851253f094a6529a69&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-86b8b74f4921d905c9deab7414c00e0c</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210924</data>
    </node>
    <node id="City-States of Maya Civilization">
      <data key="d0">City-States of Maya Civilization</data>
      <data key="d1">geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">"Maya civilization consisted of numerous city-states, each with its own distinct ball court."&lt;&lt;image:https://example.com/image3.jpg&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-86b8b74f4921d905c9deab7414c00e0c</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210919</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Decipherment of Maya Writing">
      <data key="d0">Decipherment of Maya Writing</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">"The decipherment of the Maya writing system by scholars is a significant event in Mesoamerican historical research."&lt;&lt;image:https://example.com/image4.jpg&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-86b8b74f4921d905c9deab7414c00e0c</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210919</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Classic Maya Civilization">
      <data key="d0">Classic Maya Civilization</data>
      <data key="d1">civilization_or_empire</data>
      <data key="d2">Classic Maya civilization, which entered a period of decline in the ninth century CE, experienced rapid deterioration over about a century due to complex socio-environmental issues such as deforestation, soil erosion, population growth, and climatic variations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-733f0173d74970851253f094a6529a69</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210919</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Collapse of Classic Maya Civilization">
      <data key="d0">Collapse of Classic Maya Civilization</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_event</data>
      <data key="d2">The collapse of the Classic Maya civilization by 900 CE is marked by decreased luxury goods production, breakdown of alliances, common conflicts, and the depopulation of urban centers, with suggested causes including environmental destruction, population growth problems, and climate variability.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-733f0173d74970851253f094a6529a69</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210919</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Yucatán Peninsula">
      <data key="d0">Yucatán Peninsula</data>
      <data key="d1">geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">The Yucatán Peninsula is mentioned as a region north of old Maya power centers where Maya civilization experienced a renaissance from the sixteenth century onwards following the decline of Classic Maya cities.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-733f0173d74970851253f094a6529a69</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210919</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Norte Chico">
      <data key="d0">Norte Chico</data>
      <data key="d1">civilization_or_empire</data>
      <data key="d2">Norte Chico was an early complex society that emerged by 3000 BCE in today’s Peru, representing one of the earliest known civilizations outside of Mesopotamia and Egypt.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-733f0173d74970851253f094a6529a69</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210919</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Neolithic Settlements in South America">
      <data key="d0">Neolithic Settlements in South America</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">Early Neolithic settlements such as Norte Chico, which existed by 3000 BCE, along with later developments in Northern Highlands from subsequent centuries, indicate independent cultural and civilizational evolution south of Mesoamerica.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-733f0173d74970851253f094a6529a69</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210919</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Huaricoto, Galgada, and Kotosh&quot;&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Huaricoto, Galgada, and Kotosh"&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;"geographical_location"&gt;</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;"Huaricoto, Galgada, and Kotosh were likely religious centers for offering sacrifices."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2c6c1373577de3083ed79abe6dcb559a</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210919</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Sechin Alto&quot;&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Sechin Alto"&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;"geographical_location"&gt;</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;"Sechin Alto was built along the desert coast after 2000 BCE."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2c6c1373577de3083ed79abe6dcb559a</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210919</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Southern Highlands area around Lake Titicaca&quot;&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Southern Highlands area around Lake Titicaca"&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;"geographical_location"&gt;</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;"The Southern Highlands area around Lake Titicaca is located on the border between Peru and Bolivia, where groups began growing in size after adopting agricultural practices and constructing a large sunken court around 1000 BCE."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2c6c1373577de3083ed79abe6dcb559a</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210919</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Andes region&quot;&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Andes region"&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;"geographical_location"&gt;</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;"The Andes region underwent a transformation around 900 BCE when the Chavín culture expanded across the area, marking the Early Horizon or Formative period."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2c6c1373577de3083ed79abe6dcb559a</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210919</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Chavín culture&quot;&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Chavín culture"&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;"civilization_or_empire"&gt;</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;"Chavín culture is known for its distinctive pottery style and spread of sophisticated rituals throughout the entire region. It includes Chavín de Huántar, El Lanzón, Norte Chico settlements, religious centers, agricultural practices, sunken court, temple complex, tunnels, sculptures, deity, Andes Mountains, Lake Titicaca, Peru, Bolivia."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2c6c1373577de3083ed79abe6dcb559a</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210919</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Early Horizon (or Formative period)&quot;&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Early Horizon (or Formative period)"&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;"historical_concept_or_event"&gt;</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;"The Early Horizon or Formative period began around 900 BCE when the Chavín culture expanded across the entire Andes region."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2c6c1373577de3083ed79abe6dcb559a</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210919</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Chavín de Huántar&quot;&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Chavín de Huántar"&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;"geographical_location"&gt;</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;"Chavín de Huántar is a prominent religious center that served the Chavín culture and features a large temple complex, tunnels, sculptures."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2c6c1373577de3083ed79abe6dcb559a</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210919</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Chavín de Huántar">
      <data key="d0">Chavín de Huántar</data>
      <data key="d1">geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">Chavín de Huántar was an important site around 200 BCE and earlier, known for its role in religious or ceremonial functions, not as a major population center.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c77ae3b4926fdf54e2d53803e968a1d6</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210919</data>
    </node>
    <node id="400 BCE">
      <data key="d0">400 BCE</data>
      <data key="d1">time_period</data>
      <data key="d2">By 400 BCE, the Chavín religion and culture had spread across much of the Andes region, indicating cultural exchange through means such as trade or warfare.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c77ae3b4926fdf54e2d53803e968a1d6</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210919</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Chavín Religion and Culture">
      <data key="d0">Chavín Religion and Culture</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">Chavín religion and culture represented a widespread influence by 400 BCE, affecting architectural and artistic styles, burial practices, textile production, and metalworking across the Andes region.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c77ae3b4926fdf54e2d53803e968a1d6</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210919</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Trade or Warfare">
      <data key="d0">Trade or Warfare</data>
      <data key="d1">cultural_or_religious_movement</data>
      <data key="d2">The transmission of Chavín influences is unclear; it could be through trade or warfare, but either way, it spread cultural elements widely.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c77ae3b4926fdf54e2d53803e968a1d6</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210919</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Textile Production and Metalworking">
      <data key="d0">Textile Production and Metalworking</data>
      <data key="d1">textbook_structure</data>
      <data key="d2">Innovations in textile production and metalworking developed around the time of Chavín's influence, showcasing advancements in craftsmanship across multiple communities.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c77ae3b4926fdf54e2d53803e968a1d6</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210919</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Moche Culture">
      <data key="d0">Moche Culture</data>
      <data key="d1">civilization_or_empire</data>
      <data key="d2">By 200 BCE, following a decline in Chavín cultural dominance, the Moche culture emerged as one of several regional civilizations that grew from earlier influences.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c77ae3b4926fdf54e2d53803e968a1d6</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210919</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Nazca and Tiwanaku Civilizations">
      <data key="d0">Nazca and Tiwanaku Civilizations</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">The Nazca and Tiwanaku cultures developed independently but arose in the wake of Chavín's decline, becoming full-fledged civilizations with their own distinct identities.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c77ae3b4926fdf54e2d53803e968a1d6</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210920</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Content Keywords">
      <data key="d0">Content Keywords</data>
      <data key="d1">content_keywords</data>
      <data key="d2">Chavín culture, religious influence, trade/warfare, innovations, civilizational succession</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c77ae3b4926fdf54e2d53803e968a1d6</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210920</data>
    </node>
    <node id="200 BCE - 100 CE">
      <data key="d0">200 BCE - 100 CE</data>
      <data key="d1">time_period</data>
      <data key="d2">"The Moche and Nazca civilizations emerged around 200 BCE, while the Tiwanaku civilization's major building period started around 100 CE.")&lt;|</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-71f7f5f2f0b6e70e490afffdd36ed2fe</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210920</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Moche civilization">
      <data key="d0">Moche civilization</data>
      <data key="d1">cultural_or_religious_movement</data>
      <data key="d2">The Moche civilization emerged in northern Peru and thrived from approximately 100 BCE to 600 CE. It was characterized by advanced agricultural practices, skilled metallurgy, intricate art works, sophisticated administrative structures, and a hierarchical social system with divine kings and families of nobles ruling from atop large pyramids.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-71f7f5f2f0b6e70e490afffdd36ed2fe</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210920</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Nazca culture">
      <data key="d0">Nazca culture</data>
      <data key="d1">cultural_or_religious_movement</data>
      <data key="d2">The Nazca civilization emerged around 200 BCE in the southern part of Peru. Known for their distinctive line drawings etched into the desert floor, they also constructed underground aqueduct networks and are believed to engage in maritime trade. Their art frequently depicts animals, geometric shapes, and religious symbols.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-71f7f5f2f0b6e70e490afffdd36ed2fe</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210920</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tiwanaku civilization">
      <data key="d0">Tiwanaku civilization</data>
      <data key="d1">civilization_or_empire</data>
      <data key="d2">The Tiwanaku civilization thrived high in the mountains near Lake Titicaca, centered primarily in western Bolivia but exerting extensive influence over parts of Peru. Known for its remarkable architectural achievements, innovative agricultural practices, and sophisticated political organization, the Tiwanaku civilization experienced a period of significant building around 100 CE. This era marked a pivotal phase in the civilization's development, contributing notably to the region before the rise of the Inca Empire.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-71f7f5f2f0b6e70e490afffdd36ed2fe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3ab2455895eb8921de59c456fa3781d0</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210925</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Chavín area">
      <data key="d0">Chavín area</data>
      <data key="d1">geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">The Chavín area is located in the central Andes of Peru. It served as an important cultural hearth that influenced neighboring civilizations such as Moche and Nazca with its religious practices, artistic styles, and possibly administrative or political structures.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-71f7f5f2f0b6e70e490afffdd36ed2fe</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210920</data>
    </node>
