    <node id="&lt;&quot;The Aztec Empire&quot;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"The Aztec Empire"</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;"civilization_or_empire</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;"The Aztec Empire was a powerful Mesoamerican civilization that rose to prominence in central Mexico during the 13th through early 16th centuries. The empire is known for its complex social structure, hierarchical governance, and advanced city-planning, including famous cities like Tenochtitlan. It was eventually conquered by Spanish forces led by Hernán Cortés."</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a363d0ffc56298883492bdff98828737</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210923</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Aztec Society&quot;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Aztec Society"</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;"historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;"Aztec Society refers to the social structure and organization of Aztec civilization during its golden age, which included a complex system of classes, genders, and roles. The society was divided into various social tiers such as nobility, priests, commoners, and slaves."</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a363d0ffc56298883492bdff98828737</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210923</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Aztec Women's Roles&quot;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Aztec Women's Roles"</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;"historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;"The roles of women in Aztec society were diverse and significant, ranging from domestic duties to economic activities and spiritual practices. They played crucial roles as traders, healers, and even as warriors in times of need."</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a363d0ffc56298883492bdff98828737</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210923</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Social Tiers in Aztec Civilization&quot;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Social Tiers in Aztec Civilization"</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;"historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;"Social tiers in Aztec civilization were divided into nobility, commoners, slaves, artisans, merchants, and scribes. The top tier was the nobility, followed by the priests and warriors, and then the lower classes including peasants."</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a363d0ffc56298883492bdff98828737</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210923</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Council of Four&quot;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Council of Four"</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;"historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;"The Council of Four in Aztec society included the Hueyi Tlatoani and three other high-ranking officials. They were responsible for selecting a new ruler when the current one died or was deposed."</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a363d0ffc56298883492bdff98828737</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210923</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Aztec Emperor (Huey Tlatoani)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Aztec Emperor (Huey Tlatoani)"</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;"historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;"The Aztec Emperor, also known as Hueyi Tlatoani, was the highest authority in the Aztec Empire. The role involved significant political and religious duties. The selection process occurred via the Council of Four."</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a363d0ffc56298883492bdff98828737</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210923</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Expansion and Corruption of Aztec Authority&quot;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Expansion and Corruption of Aztec Authority"</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;"historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;"The expansion of the Aztec Empire led to overextension, which resulted in corruption and inefficiency within the empire. This ultimately contributed to their downfall by the Spanish conquistadors."</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a363d0ffc56298883492bdff98828737</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210923</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Migration">
      <data key="d0">Migration</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">Migration refers to the movement of people prompted by changes in climate, which may have influenced the founding of Cuzco. This phenomenon is also associated with the expansion of cultural corridors enabled by ironworking in Africa, suggesting that migration played a significant role in both South American and African contexts through different mechanisms.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9262b57773b73a3ec93d925625b303fc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e0b75181e879a7cb5f46e925d8f88288</data>
