      <data key="d0">Labor System in Incan Empire</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">"Subjects were required to contribute labor for infrastructure projects like road construction and military service."("&lt;figure 8.34 Inca Soldiers facing right, figure 8.35 Administrative Regions of the Incas")&lt;|</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-81ff6d082654c526c4224665019c04f1</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210924</data>
    </node>
    <node id="The Inca Empire">
      <data key="d0">The Inca Empire</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">At its height in the early sixteenth century, the Inca Empire controlled an enormous area that reached from modern Columbia down into modern Chile and Argentina.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3ff58dc29260ce90c12894688e1e2ac5</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210924</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Chinchansuyu, Antisuyu, Cuntinsuyu, Collasuyu">
      <data key="d0">Chinchansuyu, Antisuyu, Cuntinsuyu, Collasuyu</data>
      <data key="d1">geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">These were the four administrative regions of the Inca Empire, representing a vast territorial control.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3ff58dc29260ce90c12894688e1e2ac5</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210924</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Quipu">
      <data key="d0">Quipu</data>
      <data key="d1">source_or_artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">The quipu was an ingenious recordkeeping and communication system made of knotted strings used by the Inca.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3ff58dc29260ce90c12894688e1e2ac5</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210924</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Inca Empire writing systems">
      <data key="d0">Inca Empire writing systems</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">Unlike the Maya, the Inca did not have a writing system that could be inked into a codex or carved in stone. Instead, they used quipus for record-keeping and communication.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3ff58dc29260ce90c12894688e1e2ac5</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210924</data>
    </node>
    <node id="At its height in the early sixteenth century">
      <data key="d0">At its height in the early sixteenth century</data>
      <data key="d1">time_period</data>
      <data key="d2">This phrase refers to a specific period in history when the Inca Empire reached its peak in terms of territorial control and power.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3ff58dc29260ce90c12894688e1e2ac5</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210924</data>
    </node>
    <node id="resource management">
      <data key="d0">resource management</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">Resource management in each of the four sunyas (Chinchansuyu, Antisuyu, Cuntinsuyu, Collasuyu) was a key aspect of Inca governance and ensured efficient use of resources across the empire.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3ff58dc29260ce90c12894688e1e2ac5</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210924</data>
    </node>
    <node id="governance systems">
      <data key="d0">governance systems</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">The Inca Empire's advanced governance systems encompassed administrative regions, record-keeping through quipus, and intricate resource management to maintain control over their vast territory.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3ff58dc29260ce90c12894688e1e2ac5</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210924</data>
    </node>
    <node id="resource distribution">
      <data key="d0">resource distribution</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">Efficient resource distribution was a key aspect of Inca administrative practices, ensuring that resources from each suyu (Chinchansuyu, Antisuyu, Cuntinsuyu, Collasuyu) were utilized effectively to support the empire's needs.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3ff58dc29260ce90c12894688e1e2ac5</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210924</data>
    </node>
    <node id="chuncho plants">
      <data key="d0">chuncho plants</data>
      <data key="d1">source_or_artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">While not mentioned in the text directly, chuncho plants (quinoa and other grains) played an important role in Inca agriculture. The cultivation of these plants was essential for supporting the empire's population.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3ff58dc29260ce90c12894688e1e2ac5</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210924</data>
    </node>
    <node id="1530s">
      <data key="d0">1530s</data>
      <data key="d1">time_period</data>
      <data key="d2">"The 1530s refer to the decade of the 1530s, which saw significant events in the history of South America."|</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a0eb56c52113d1fc2efc13b6051efae7</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210924</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Diseases brought by Europeans">
      <data key="d0">Diseases brought by Europeans</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_event</data>
      <data key="d2">"The spread of diseases such as smallpox among native populations due to European contact, leading to significant demographic changes in South America."|</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a0eb56c52113d1fc2efc13b6051efae7</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210924</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Emperor Wayna Qhapaq">
      <data key="d0">Emperor Wayna Qhapaq</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">"Wayna Qhapaq was the emperor of the Inca Empire around 1528 and his untimely death occurred just before major changes in the region due to European contact."|</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a0eb56c52113d1fc2efc13b6051efae7</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210924</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Francisco Pizarro">
      <data key="d0">Francisco Pizarro</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">"Spanish conquistador Francisco Pizarro who reached Ecuador in the early 1530s, initiating conflict within the Inca Empire."|</data>
