    <node id="Ecuador">
      <data key="d0">Ecuador</data>
      <data key="d1">geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">"The geographical location of Ecuador where Francisco Pizarro's army first made contact with potential allies and initiated conflict within the Inca Empire."|</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a0eb56c52113d1fc2efc13b6051efae7</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210924</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Minor civil war over ascension">
      <data key="d0">Minor civil war over ascension</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_event</data>
      <data key="d2">"A political struggle for the throne within the Inca Empire around 1532 that coincided with Francisco Pizarro's entry into the conflict."|</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a0eb56c52113d1fc2efc13b6051efae7</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210924</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Spanish conquest of the Inca Empire">
      <data key="d0">Spanish conquest of the Inca Empire</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_event</data>
      <data key="d2">"The military victory of Spanish forces led by Francisco Pizarro over the Inca Empire, marking a turning point in South American history and leading to significant social and cultural changes."|</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a0eb56c52113d1fc2efc13b6051efae7</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210924</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mississippian period">
      <data key="d0">Mississippian period</data>
      <data key="d1">time_period</data>
      <data key="d2">Mississippian period refers to a time when complex societies developed in the Eastern Woodlands of North America, particularly around Cahokia.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cd9c7f6e58d53fe2d7895baaf718081c</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210924</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cahokia site">
      <data key="d0">Cahokia site</data>
      <data key="d1">geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">Cahokia is an archaeological site near modern St. Louis where a large urban center flourished from about 1050 to 1250 AD.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cd9c7f6e58d53fe2d7895baaf718081c</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210924</data>
    </node>
    <node id="St. Louis">
      <data key="d0">St. Louis</data>
      <data key="d1">geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">St. Louis is the city in Missouri, USA, close to which the Cahokia site was located.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cd9c7f6e58d53fe2d7895baaf718081c</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210924</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Large urban center at Cahokia">
      <data key="d0">Large urban center at Cahokia</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">The large urban center of Cahokia is an example of complex societal development during the Mississippian period.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cd9c7f6e58d53fe2d7895baaf718081c</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210924</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Monks Mound">
      <data key="d0">Monks Mound</data>
      <data key="d1">source_or_artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">Monks Mound, built around 1100, is a large earthen structure at Cahokia and the largest in North America.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cd9c7f6e58d53fe2d7895baaf718081c</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210924</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Temple mound">
      <data key="d0">Temple mound</data>
      <data key="d1">source_or_artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">The temple mound was a key part of Monks Mound, with a wooden structure on top and a staircase leading up from the central plaza.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cd9c7f6e58d53fe2d7895baaf718081c</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210924</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Defensive wall around Cahokia">
      <data key="d0">Defensive wall around Cahokia</data>
      <data key="d1">source_or_artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">A defensive wall, which included watchtowers, surrounded the central complex at Cahokia, indicating organized societal structures.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cd9c7f6e58d53fe2d7895baaf718081c</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210925</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Burial mounds of Cahokia">
      <data key="d0">Burial mounds of Cahokia</data>
      <data key="d1">source_or_artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">These mounds served as burial sites and were used for significant burials like that of the ‘birdman’ found underneath one mound.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cd9c7f6e58d53fe2d7895baaf718081c</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210925</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Chunkey game">
      <data key="d0">Chunkey game</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">Chunkey, a ritual ball game played at Cahokia, was likely important in Mississippian culture similar to how rituals were practiced in Mesoamerica for rubber ball games.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cd9c7f6e58d53fe2d7895baaf718081c</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210925</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mound 72 at Cahokia">
      <data key="d0">Mound 72 at Cahokia</data>
      <data key="d1">source_or_artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">One of the excavated mounds at Cahokia contained remains of several dozen sacrificed individuals, including a ‘birdman’ with significant grave offerings.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cd9c7f6e58d53fe2d7895baaf718081c</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210925</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Defensive wall around Cahokia site">
      <data key="d0">Defensive wall around Cahokia site</data>
      <data key="d1">source_or_artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">A defensive wall, which included watchtowers, surrounded the central complex at Cahokia, indicating organized societal structures.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cd9c7f6e58d53fe2d7895baaf718081c</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210925</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cahokia">
      <data key="d0">Cahokia</data>
      <data key="d1">geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">Cahokia refers to a significant pre-Columbian settlement and civilization located in North Dakota.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ceba2c93f185573d710c16b29e1b461f</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210925</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mississippian settlements">
      <data key="d0">Mississippian settlements</data>
      <data key="d1">civilization_or_empire</data>
      <data key="d2">Mississippian settlements were large, organized chiefdoms characterized by complex social structures, often centered around major ceremonial sites such as Cahokia.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ceba2c93f185573d710c16b29e1b461f</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210925</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cahokia Mounds Museum Society website">
      <data key="d0">Cahokia Mounds Museum Society website</data>
      <data key="d1">source_or_artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">A source where one can learn more about Cahokia and its archaeological discoveries. The URL is provided as [https://openstax.org/l/77Cahokia].</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ceba2c93f185573d710c16b29e1b461f</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210925</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Moundville in Alabama">
      <data key="d0">Moundville in Alabama</data>
      <data key="d1">geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">Moundville is another significant Mississippian archaeological site located in Alabama, similar to Cahokia.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ceba2c93f185573d710c16b29e1b461f</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210925</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Etowah in Georgia">
      <data key="d0">Etowah in Georgia</data>
      <data key="d1">geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">Etowah is a major archaeological site representing a significant Mississippian chiefdom located in Georgia.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ceba2c93f185573d710c16b29e1b461f</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210925</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Nobility">
      <data key="d0">Nobility</data>
      <data key="d1">social_class</data>
      <data key="d2">Male members of the nobility in Cahokia formed the core fighting forces due to their ability to afford horses and uphold traditional warrior roles. This group held a privileged status within the societal structure, characterized by access to luxury goods and surplus resources. The nobility played crucial roles both as warriors and symbolically through their status and possessions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ceba2c93f185573d710c16b29e1b461f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-46291ecb41bc06fae9280ac62fe3751e</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210929</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tribute system">
      <data key="d0">Tribute system</data>
      <data key="d1">economic_system</data>
      <data key="d2">Cahokia's power was solidified by its tribute system, which enabled the control and exploitation of resources from surrounding communities.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ceba2c93f185573d710c16b29e1b461f</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210925</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Long-distance trade">
      <data key="d0">Long-distance trade</data>
      <data key="d1">economic_activity</data>
      <data key="d2">Trade played a vital role in sustaining and expanding Cahokia's economy by bringing exotic items from distant regions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ceba2c93f185573d710c16b29e1b461f</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210925</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Third Millennium BCE">
      <data key="d0">Third Millennium BCE</data>
      <data key="d1">time_period</data>
      <data key="d2">Agriculture was incorporated into a largely hunter-gatherer existence in the third millennium BCE.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c30d8b2342ab7e0a7d4403aa83d331e1</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210925</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pacific Northwest">
      <data key="d0">Pacific Northwest</data>
      <data key="d1">geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">"Pacific Northwest is an area located on the northwestern coast of North America, including parts of western Canada and the U.S.",</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a5b49369a7f1e01725e4809d8498a27f</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210925</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Chasing bison with horses">
      <data key="d0">Chasing bison with horses</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">Chasing bison with horses is a historical event that describes a specific way of hunting involving Native American tribes, primarily used in the Pacific Northwest region.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a5b49369a7f1e01725e4809d8498a27f</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210925</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Olmec civilization">
      <data key="d0">Olmec civilization</data>
      <data key="d1">civilization_or_empire</data>
      <data key="d2">The Olmec civilization was one of the earliest Mesoamerican civilizations, often referred to as a 'mother culture' because it influenced other cultures in the region. It is known for its large stone heads and advanced calendar systems.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a5b49369a7f1e01725e4809d8498a27f</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210925</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Maya city-states">
      <data key="d0">Maya city-states</data>
      <data key="d1">civilization_or_empire</data>
      <data key="d2">The Maya were an ancient Mesoamerican civilization known for their hieroglyphic writing, monumental architecture, and complex calendar systems. The larger political organization of the Maya world was a collection of independent city-states.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a5b49369a7f1e01725e4809d8498a27f</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210925</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hopewell tradition">
      <data key="d0">Hopewell tradition</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">The Hopewell tradition is an archaeological term that refers to a pre-Columbian indigenous culture in North America, characterized by the construction of large earthen mounds and extensive trade networks. It was contemporaneous with cultures such as the Olmec and Zapotec but did not directly influence them.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a5b49369a7f1e01725e4809d8498a27f</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210925</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mesoamerica">
      <data key="d0">Mesoamerica</data>
      <data key="d1">geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">Mesoamerica is a region consisting of much of Mexico, all of Belize, Guatemala, El Salvador, Honduras, and parts of Nicaragua. It was home to several Mesoamerican cultures including the Maya and the Olmec.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a5b49369a7f1e01725e4809d8498a27f</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210925</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Moche">
      <data key="d0">Moche</data>
      <data key="d1">civilization_or_empire</data>
      <data key="d2">The Moche were an ancient culture that thrived in what is now Peru between the 1st century AD and 800 AD. Known for their intricate metalwork, ceramics, and vivid wall paintings depicting everyday life and religious iconography.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a5b49369a7f1e01725e4809d8498a27f</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210925</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tiwanaku">
      <data key="d0">Tiwanaku</data>
      <data key="d1">civilization_or_empire</data>
      <data key="d2">Tiwanaku was a pre-Columbian indigenous culture in the Andean region that influenced much of the Andean world. It rose to prominence around 500 AD and had a significant impact on subsequent South American cultures.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a5b49369a7f1e01725e4809d8498a27f</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210925</data>
