    <node id="Trade Networks">
      <data key="d0">Trade Networks</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">Trade Networks refer to a complex web of economic and cultural exchange between different regions, including those within and outside Southeast Asia. These networks involved the flow of goods such as copper, quartz, mica, marine shells, and obsidian, alongside the sharing of knowledge and ideas through various trade routes. Notably, entities like Mapungubwe engaged in extensive local and long-distance trade, interacting with regional elites and coastal regions. The expansion and decline of trade networks, particularly across regions such as Bure and Awdaghost, are highlighted as crucial elements shaping the economic power dynamics in the region. Overall, these trade networks played a significant role in the exchange and dissemination of resources, knowledge, and cultural practices among diverse communities.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d32d7ef2fac445505a9bd6f9b878a6f4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a860eedfd18381766279d84087f3bdb1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-862492df2845838c3baa08785c6e8963&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ef5829225aa0fe5157b728e0142f7c68</data>
