    <node id="Hunter-Gatherer Societies">
      <data key="d0">Hunter-Gatherer Societies</data>
      <data key="d1">civilization_or_empire</data>
      <data key="d2">Hunter-Gatherer Societies were nomadic groups in early African history who relied on hunting and foraging for survival.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-49e2501bde8bdce818ca3bb48333fd46</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210927</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Savannas">
      <data key="d0">Savannas</data>
      <data key="d1">geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">Savanna regions are critical ecosystems characterized by vast grasslands that historically supported settled human communities and provided fertile ground for agriculture in Africa. These areas are home to a diverse array of wildlife such as gazelles, wildebeests, elephants, and zebras.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0ce3eaaba21272a94462cd9692507262&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-49e2501bde8bdce818ca3bb48333fd46</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210931</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Deserts">
      <data key="d0">Deserts</data>
      <data key="d1">geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">Deserts in Africa, being more arid regions, were suitable for nomadism and herding practices due to their poor agricultural suitability.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-49e2501bde8bdce818ca3bb48333fd46</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210927</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Rainforests">
      <data key="d0">Rainforests</data>
      <data key="d1">geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">Rainforests provided resources such as wildlife and other natural assets that supported human populations in tropical areas of Africa.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-49e2501bde8bdce818ca3bb48333fd46</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210927</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Grasslands">
      <data key="d0">Grasslands</data>
      <data key="d1">geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">Grasslands were essential in terms of resource availability, supporting diverse communities that engaged in both agriculture and herding practices.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-49e2501bde8bdce818ca3bb48333fd46</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210927</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Wet, tropical land">
      <data key="d0">Wet, tropical land</data>
      <data key="d1">geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">Wet, tropical land refers to a specific region in Africa characterized by heavy rainfall and dense rainforest coverage.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-85139964461f2ef54605b30deb55201a</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210927</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Equatorial Rainforest">
      <data key="d0">Equatorial Rainforest</data>
      <data key="d1">geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">Equatorial Rainforest is the deep canopy and undergrowth of Africa’s equatorial area, particularly centered on the Congo River Basin.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-85139964461f2ef54605b30deb55201a</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210927</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Democratic Republic of Congo">
      <data key="d0">Democratic Republic of Congo</data>
      <data key="d1">geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">This term refers to a central state where the rainforest is densest, known for its vast tree coverage and minimal sunlight reaching the forest floor.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-85139964461f2ef54605b30deb55201a</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210927</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Congo Rainforest">
      <data key="d0">Congo Rainforest</data>
      <data key="d1">geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">Covers approximately 386,000 square miles in extent, second largest of its kind in the world after the Amazon, with a yearly rainfall of between sixty-three and seventy-eight inches.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-85139964461f2ef54605b30deb55201a</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210927</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tropical Rainforests">
      <data key="d0">Tropical Rainforests</data>
      <data key="d1">cultural_or_religious_movement</data>
      <data key="d2">This term symbolizes regions within an ecological zone known for dense forests where cultures developed societies without historical records but relied on oral stories passed down through generations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-85139964461f2ef54605b30deb55201a</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210927</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Egyptian Civilization">
      <data key="d0">Egyptian Civilization</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">Refers specifically to the civilization that developed along the Nile, known for monumental architecture and well-documented history through hieroglyphs.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-85139964461f2ef54605b30deb55201a</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210927</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Savannas and Deserts">
      <data key="d0">Savannas and Deserts</data>
      <data key="d1">geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">These regions are identified alongside tropical rainforests as part of the diverse landscapes found across Africa.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-85139964461f2ef54605b30deb55201a</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210927</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Oral Stories">
      <data key="d0">Oral Stories</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">Oral traditions and transmission forms a significant cultural practice in societies developed near the Congo River, lacking written records to preserve historical events.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-85139964461f2ef54605b30deb55201a</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210927</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Sahel">
      <data key="d0">Sahel</data>
      <data key="d1">geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">The Sahel is a transitional region that extends from the southern edge of the Sahara Desert, gradually giving way to grassland savanna in the south. This area has historically served as a crucial grazing and migration zone for nomadic pastoralists.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b4fa88ad0236b22ad9d8b538ceba22b1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-68be3898587a9f6e093512ef1a1060f3</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210932</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Savanna">
      <data key="d0">Savanna</data>
      <data key="d1">ecological_system</data>
      <data key="d2">Savanna refers to an ecosystem characterized by short grasses and scattered trees, found in southern parts of the Sahel and northern Tanzania.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b4fa88ad0236b22ad9d8b538ceba22b1</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210928</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Serengeti">
      <data key="d0">Serengeti</data>
      <data key="d1">geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">Serengeti is a renowned tropical savanna located in northern Tanzania, known for its diverse ecosystem and large animal migrations, particularly notable for the migration of wildebeest. This vast grassland plain serves as an important ecological zone where multiple species undertake seasonal migrations, making it a significant area for wildlife conservation and ecological study.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a95d702249640b70cee3c922166a0f96&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b4fa88ad0236b22ad9d8b538ceba22b1</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210933</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Wildebeests Migration">
      <data key="d0">Wildebeests Migration</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_event_or_concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Migration involving wildebeests, zebras, gazelles, and other animals in the Serengeti ecosystem, spanning from January to November annually.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b4fa88ad0236b22ad9d8b538ceba22b1</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210929</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Masai Mara Reserve">
      <data key="d0">Masai Mara Reserve</data>
      <data key="d1">geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">Masai Mara Reserve is a wildlife sanctuary in southern Kenya, part of the migratory path for wildebeests and other animals.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b4fa88ad0236b22ad9d8b538ceba22b1</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210929</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ngorongoro Conservation Area">
      <data key="d0">Ngorongoro Conservation Area</data>
      <data key="d1">geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">Ngorongoro Conservation Area is located at the southern extreme of the Serengeti in Tanzania and is a starting point for many animal migrations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b4fa88ad0236b22ad9d8b538ceba22b1</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210929</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tanzania">
      <data key="d0">Tanzania</data>
      <data key="d1">geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">Tanzania is a country where the vast majority of the Serengeti ecosystem and Ngorongoro Conservation Area are located.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b4fa88ad0236b22ad9d8b538ceba22b1</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210929</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Kenya">
      <data key="d0">Kenya</data>
      <data key="d1">geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">("The Republic of Kenya, a nation east of Tanzania, also participates in wildebeests' migration. Masai Mara Reserve is one of its major wildlife sanctuaries.")</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b4fa88ad0236b22ad9d8b538ceba22b1</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210929</data>
    </node>
    <node id="River Crossings">
      <data key="d0">River Crossings</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_event_or_concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Wildlife crossings over rivers along migration routes, facing dangers of hippopotamuses and crocodiles.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b4fa88ad0236b22ad9d8b538ceba22b1</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210929</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Predators">
      <data key="d0">Predators</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_figure_or_concept</data>
      <data key="d2">("Natural predators encountered by migrating animals on land during their journey.")</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b4fa88ad0236b22ad9d8b538ceba22b1</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210929</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Calves Birth">
      <data key="d0">Calves Birth</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">("Annual birthing season of wildebeests in Kenya, where around half a million calves are born during the dry season.")</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b4fa88ad0236b22ad9d8b538ceba22b1</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
