    </node>
    <node id="Savanna Grasslands">
      <data key="d0">Savanna Grasslands</data>
      <data key="d1">ecological_system</data>
      <data key="d2">("Grassland savannas providing grazing areas for migrating animals throughout their journey.")</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b4fa88ad0236b22ad9d8b538ceba22b1</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210929</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Oases">
      <data key="d0">Oases</data>
      <data key="d1">cultural_or_religious_movement</data>
      <data key="d2">Oases are green dots of civilization formed around underground water sources that reach the surface via wells or springs. They occupy an area of approximately eight hundred square miles, making up about 0.02 percent of the total desert landmass. These oases played a vital role in supporting seminomadic settlements and served as key points facilitating the point-to-point trading system across the Sahara, contributing to early trade networks.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fe1025f0b9f819b6ebc89df353b87d27&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-176c01702975d9f9a613b9f917b023dd</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210934</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Nomads">
      <data key="d0">Nomads</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">The population includes nomadic peoples such as the Tuareg and Teda who are involved in economic activities like farming and herding but on a severely restricted basis. Their pasturage exists only in marginal areas, governing their lifestyle.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fe1025f0b9f819b6ebc89df353b87d27</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210929</data>
    </node>
    <node id="African Horn Desert Region">
      <data key="d0">African Horn Desert Region</data>
      <data key="d1">geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">The African Horn is also a desert region corresponding to Somalia, Ethiopia, and northern Kenya</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fe1025f0b9f819b6ebc89df353b87d27</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210929</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Namib Desert">
      <data key="d0">Namib Desert</data>
      <data key="d1">geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">Namib Desert is part of the continent's arid regions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fe1025f0b9f819b6ebc89df353b87d27</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210929</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Lake Victoria">
      <data key="d0">Lake Victoria</data>
      <data key="d1">textbook_structure</data>
      <data key="d2">Lake Victoria is a central geographical feature in East Africa, surrounded by countries like Uganda, Kenya, and Tanzania, with detailed descriptions about its climatic conditions and wildlife.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0ce3eaaba21272a94462cd9692507262</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210929</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Highlands and Mountains">
      <data key="d0">Highlands and Mountains</data>
      <data key="d1">geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">The highlands and mountains of East Africa host cooler climates compared to the plains and coastlines, showcasing diverse microclimates within the region.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0ce3eaaba21272a94462cd9692507262</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210929</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ethiopian Highlands">
      <data key="d0">Ethiopian Highlands</data>
      <data key="d1">geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">Ethiopia's highlands form a drier climate region due to the influence of eastern deserts, adding to the diversity in African climates.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0ce3eaaba21272a94462cd9692507262</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210929</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Annual Migration">
      <data key="d0">Annual Migration</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_event</data>
      <data key="d2">Wildebeest migrations across East African savannas highlight the seasonal movements of these animals influenced by climatic conditions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0ce3eaaba21272a94462cd9692507262</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210929</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Giraffes, Elephants, Hippopotamuses, Rhinoceroses">
      <data key="d0">Giraffes, Elephants, Hippopotamuses, Rhinoceroses</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">These large mammals are significant figures within African ecosystems and have adapted to various climates across different regions like sub-Saharan savannas and inland areas of Africa such as Botswana, Zimbabwe, and Zambia.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0ce3eaaba21272a94462cd9692507262</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210929</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Microclimates">
      <data key="d0">Microclimates</data>
      <data key="d1">concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">Local variations in climate significantly impact the diverse ecosystems seen throughout Africa, reflecting both adaptation strategies by humans and natural landscapes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0ce3eaaba21272a94462cd9692507262</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210929</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;9.2 The Emergence of Farming and the Bantu Migrations&quot;&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"9.2 The Emergence of Farming and the Bantu Migrations"&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">The section focuses on the emergence and spread of agriculture, as well as the migration patterns of the Bantu people in Africa. This includes topics such as the introduction of farming techniques, the impact of ironworking technology, and the geographic extent of the Bantu migrations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-77b80d611a0770d6a510e247aaf56af0</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210929</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;San civilization_or_empire&quot;&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"San civilization_or_empire"&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">cultural_or_religious_movement</data>
      <data key="d2">A group of peoples living in Southern Africa, characterized by their rock art and hunter-gatherer lifestyle. They are not an empire or civilization but a cultural movement that has left lasting archaeological evidence.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-77b80d611a0770d6a510e247aaf56af0</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210929</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Settlements time_period&quot;&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Settlements time_period"&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">time_period</data>
      <data key="d2">The period when early human groups started to establish permanent living structures, which is closely associated with the onset of farming and agricultural practices in Africa.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-77b80d611a0770d6a510e247aaf56af0</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210929</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Neolithic Revolution time_period&quot;&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Neolithic Revolution time_period"&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">time_period</data>
      <data key="d2">A major shift in human technological development, characterized by the transition from foraging and hunting to agriculture and animal husbandry, occurring around 10,000-8,000 BCE in various parts of the world.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-77b80d611a0770d6a510e247aaf56af0</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210930</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Fertile Crescent geographical_location&quot;&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Fertile Crescent geographical_location"&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">A region in the Middle East where early agricultural civilizations developed, stretching approximately from modern-day southern Turkey to northern Egypt and Iraq. It is considered one of the cradles of civilization.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-77b80d611a0770d6a510e247aaf56af0</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210930</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Nile River geographical_location&quot;&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Nile River geographical_location"&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">The longest river in Africa, running northward through eastern Africa.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-77b80d611a0770d6a510e247aaf56af0</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210930</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Eastern Sahara geographical_location&quot;&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Eastern Sahara geographical_location"&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">An arid region located between the Nile Valley and the Red Sea, home to ancient cultures that developed agricultural practices.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-77b80d611a0770d6a510e247aaf56af0</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
