      <data key="d5">1783210930</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Red Sea hills geographical_location&quot;&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Red Sea hills geographical_location"&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">A mountainous region near the Red Sea. It is geographically significant as it offers strategic points for trade routes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-77b80d611a0770d6a510e247aaf56af0</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210930</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Nilo-Saharan civilization_or_empire&quot;&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Nilo-Saharan civilization_or_empire"&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">civilization_or_empire</data>
      <data key="d2">An ancient culture located in the Nile Valley and eastern Africa, characterized by early farming practices and distinct cultural developments.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-77b80d611a0770d6a510e247aaf56af0</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210930</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;cultural_or_religious_movement&quot;&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"cultural_or_religious_movement"&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">cultural_or_religious_movement</data>
      <data key="d2">Refers to significant societal changes such as beliefs, customs, and traditions. In this context, it covers the spread of agricultural practices among various African cultures.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-77b80d611a0770d6a510e247aaf56af0</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210930</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;source_or_artifact&quot;&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"source_or_artifact"&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">source_or_artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">Anything that provides information or evidence for study. In archaeological contexts, this includes artifacts like tools and settlements.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-77b80d611a0770d6a510e247aaf56af0</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210930</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;textbook_structure&quot;&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"textbook_structure"&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">textbook_structure</data>
      <data key="d2">The organization of the educational material into sections to facilitate learning and understanding.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-77b80d611a0770d6a510e247aaf56af0</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210930</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Congo River Basin&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Congo River Basin&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">The Congo River Basin is a significant geographical area characterized by its rich ancient climate and diverse geography. It includes the vast region drained by the Congo River, which was an important focal point for early human settlements and civilizations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-41f85aebcdbec291afd82019b0842d65</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210930</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Introduction of Bronze Technology&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Introduction of Bronze Technology&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">The introduction of bronze to farming populations significantly increased their agricultural productivity through its harder materials as compared to previous tools. This event marked a shift in technological advancement, enabling more efficient land management practices.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-41f85aebcdbec291afd82019b0842d65</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210930</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Ironwork Mastery&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Ironwork Mastery&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">By 900 BCE, Near Eastern blacksmiths had mastered iron processing techniques, producing tools superior to those made from bronze. This development underscored the transition away from Bronze Age technology towards the Iron Age.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-41f85aebcdbec291afd82019b0842d65</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210930</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Central Africa (1000 BCE)&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Central Africa (1000 BCE)&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">Refers to a geographical region around 1000 BCE including parts of modern Chad, Central African Republic, and South Sudan. At this time, evidence suggests early ironworking practices were prominent in the area.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-41f85aebcdbec291afd82019b0842d65</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210930</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Nok culture&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Nok culture&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">civilization_or_empire</data>
      <data key="d2">The Nok civilization initially used iron for jewelry but later adopted it for farming tools and weapons around present-day Nigeria, marking a significant phase in their development. Their practice of using iron demonstrates the spread and adaptation of metalworking techniques.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-41f85aebcdbec291afd82019b0842d65</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210930</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Iron Tools for Farming&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Iron Tools for Farming&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">Iron tools were essential for Bantu migrants who utilized them to clear vegetation and improve irrigation systems, establishing advanced agricultural methods. This reflects the practical application of iron metallurgy in enhancing farming efficiency.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-41f85aebcdbec291afd82019b0842d65</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210930</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Ironsmelting Origin&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Ironsmelting Origin&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">Recent evidence challenges traditional beliefs about ironworking’s origins, instead suggesting it may have begun in Central Africa around 1500 BCE. This finding alters the understanding of metallurgical history.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-41f85aebcdbec291afd82019b0842d65</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210930</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Iron Smelting Precedence (pre-1000 BCE)&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Iron Smelting Precedence (pre-1000 BCE)&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">Scholars now agree that iron smelting likely predates its introduction to Egypt by a few centuries, indicating an independent development in sub-Saharan Africa. This highlights the region's early technological achievements.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-41f85aebcdbec291afd82019b0842d65</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210930</data>
    </node>
    <node id="1000 BCE">
      <data key="d0">1000 BCE</data>
      <data key="d1">time_period</data>
      <data key="d2">1000 BCE marks the earliest evidence of ironworking in Africa.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e0b75181e879a7cb5f46e925d8f88288</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210930</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ironworking">
      <data key="d0">Ironworking</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">Ironworking revolutionized human civilization in Africa, helping make large-scale agriculture possible through stronger farming tools and enabling migration.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e0b75181e879a7cb5f46e925d8f88288</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210930</data>
    </node>
    <node id="African Rainforest">
      <data key="d0">African Rainforest</data>
      <data key="d1">geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">The African Rainforest is a location where iron axes and knives were used to clear paths for settlement and cultural diffusion.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e0b75181e879a7cb5f46e925d8f88288</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210930</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Common Cultural Foundation">
      <data key="d0">Common Cultural Foundation</data>
      <data key="d1">textbook_structure</data>
      <data key="d2">The Common Cultural Foundation represents the shared advancements derived from iron metallurgy that connected various African societies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e0b75181e879a7cb5f46e925d8f88288</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210930</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Proto-Bantu">
      <data key="d0">Proto-Bantu</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">Proto-Bantu is an ancient parent language from which many Bantu languages derive, playing a significant role in linguistic history and cultural diffusion across southern Africa.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e0b75181e879a7cb5f46e925d8f88288</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210930</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bantu Migrations">
      <data key="d0">Bantu Migrations</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">Bantu Migrations refer to the expansion of Bantu speakers beyond their original region and across other parts of sub-Saharan Africa, starting around 3000-2000 BCE.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-171b3e11780c09f4c082d1fcb308f43d</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210930</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Proto-Bantu Speakers">
      <data key="d0">Proto-Bantu Speakers</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">Proto-Bantu Speakers are the earliest known Bantu people who emerged between modern-day Nigeria and Cameroon in West and Central Africa.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-171b3e11780c09f4c082d1fcb308f43d</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210930</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Sangha River Interval">
      <data key="d0">Sangha River Interval</data>
      <data key="d1">geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">Sangha River Interval is a 200-mile-wide grassland corridor running north-south between southeastern Cameroon, southern Central African Republic, and northern Republic of Congo, which facilitated the southward movement of Bantu speakers from tropical rainforest areas.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-171b3e11780c09f4c082d1fcb308f43d</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210931</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Neolithic People">
      <data key="d0">Neolithic People</data>
      <data key="d1">civilization_or_empire</data>
      <data key="d2">Neolithic People refers to the proto-Bantu, who were farmers practicing agriculture in the region with cultivation of pearl millet and yams, and extraction of oil from palm and bush candle trees.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-171b3e11780c09f4c082d1fcb308f43d</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210931</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bantu Subfamily">
      <data key="d0">Bantu Subfamily</data>
      <data key="d1">cultural_or_religious_movement</data>
      <data key="d2">Bantu Subfamily is part of the Niger-Congo family and extends from western Africa to the far south within the broader Bantu language group distribution.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-171b3e11780c09f4c082d1fcb308f43d</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210931</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Niger-Congo Family">
      <data key="d0">Niger-Congo Family</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">Niger-Congo Family is a large family of languages found in sub-Saharan Africa, including the Bantu subfamily.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-171b3e11780c09f4c082d1fcb308f43d</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210931</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pearl Millet and Yam Cultivation">
      <data key="d0">Pearl Millet and Yam Cultivation</data>
      <data key="d1">textbook_structure</data>
      <data key="d2">Pearl Millet and Yam Cultivation refers to agricultural practices of proto-Bantu people, who cultivated these crops for sustenance.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-171b3e11780c09f4c082d1fcb308f43d</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210931</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Weather Patterns Shifts">
      <data key="d0">Weather Patterns Shifts</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">Weather Patterns Shifts involve changes in climate that caused the rainforests to recede, leading to the formation of open savanna patches and facilitating Bantu movement southward.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-171b3e11780c09f4c082d1fcb308f43d</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210931</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Figure 9.13 The Bantu Migrations&quot;&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Figure 9.13 The Bantu Migrations"&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">Describes the geographical locations and periods of various Bantu migrations and cultural expansions from around 5000 BCE to the 6th century CE, including eastward, southward, westward, and expansion into the Great Lakes region.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-429595d2f64e355e1d384d5c5f93ecff</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210931</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Bantu homeland in West and Central Africa&quot;&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Bantu homeland in West and Central Africa"&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">Origin location of Bantu-speaking peoples before their migrations, encompassing present-day Nigeria, Cameroon, and surrounding countries.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-429595d2f64e355e1d384d5c5f93ecff</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210931</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Eastward migration (early split)&quot;&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Eastward migration (early split)"&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">time_period</data>
      <data key="d2">Refers to the initial dispersal of some Bantu groups eastwards from West and Central Africa around 1500 BCE, marking a significant bifurcation in their movement patterns.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-429595d2f64e355e1d384d5c5f93ecff</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210931</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Southward western expansion&quot;&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Southward western expansion"&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">time_period</data>
      <data key="d2">Describes the spread of Bantu-speaking peoples southwards through the western regions of Africa during the same approximate period as the eastward migration around 1500 BCE.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-429595d2f64e355e1d384d5c5f93ecff</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210931</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Great Lakes region of East Africa&quot;&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Great Lakes region of East Africa"&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">Region where diverse Bantu cultures and archaeological artifacts flourished, including parts of Uganda, Tanzania, Rwanda, Burundi, and Democratic Republic of Congo.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-429595d2f64e355e1d384d5c5f93ecff</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210931</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Urewe culture&quot;&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Urewe culture"&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">A cultural phenomenon characterized by early farming communities, ironworking, and pottery production in the Great Lakes region around the 2nd to 5th centuries CE.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-429595d2f64e355e1d384d5c5f93ecff</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210931</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Urewe ware pottery&quot;&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Urewe ware pottery"&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">source_or_artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">Handmade pottery artifacts produced during the Urewe culture period (2nd to 5th centuries CE) with distinctive dimple-based designs, indicative of early Bantu craftsmanship.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-429595d2f64e355e1d384d5c5f93ecff</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210931</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Kwale ware pottery&quot;&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Kwale ware pottery"&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">source_or_artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">Pottery from a later period (3rd century CE), representing developments in ceramic technology among Bantu-speaking peoples, closely related to Urewe culture but with variations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-429595d2f64e355e1d384d5c5f93ecff</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210931</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Swahili speakers of the East African coast&quot;&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Swahili speakers of the East African coast"&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">civilization_or_empire</data>
      <data key="d2">The coastal inhabitants along the eastern part of the African continent who spoke a form of Swahili and were part of various trade networks extending from Somalia to South Africa, known for their maritime and commercial activities during the early medieval period.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-429595d2f64e355e1d384d5c5f93ecff</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210931</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Early Iron Age farm settlements in southwestern Congo&quot;&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Early Iron Age farm settlements in southwestern Congo"&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">Establishment of farming communities and the use of iron tools in the region around 2nd century CE, linked to the broader Bantu expansion into central Africa.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-429595d2f64e355e1d384d5c5f93ecff</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210931</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Copper and iron smelting evidence point (Lake Kale)&quot;&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Copper and iron smelting evidence point (Lake Kale)"&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">source_or_artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">Archeological site dating to the 2nd to 5th centuries CE where artifacts indicate copper and iron smelting activities, aligning with Bantu technological advancements.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-429595d2f64e355e1d384d5c5f93ecff</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210931</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Kalundu and Dambwa pottery, Early Iron Age styles (Zambezi valley)&quot;&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Kalundu and Dambwa pottery, Early Iron Age styles (Zambezi valley)"&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">source_or_artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">Pottery styles from early Iron Age settlements in the Zambezi valley area, indicative of westward Bantu expansion and related cultural diffusion.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-429595d2f64e355e1d384d5c5f93ecff</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210931</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Bantu expansion&quot;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Bantu expansion"</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;"historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;"The Bantu expansion refers to a series of movements by populations speaking Bantu languages from West and Central Africa into Southern and Eastern Africa, bringing with them agricultural techniques, metalworking skills, livestock rearing, and various cultural practices. This movement significantly influenced the linguistic and cultural landscape of sub-Saharan Africa over several centuries."</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8533180218df9d22e37ddeaf3cd68674</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210931</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Bantu_people&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Bantu_people&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;civilization_or_empire&gt;</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;entity_description&gt; The Bantu people are an ethnic group that expanded across southern Africa during the Iron Age, known for their advancements in agriculture and metalworking. They interacted with various indigenous groups, including Twa, Khoekhoe, and Khoisan peoples, influencing each other through cultural exchanges.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0a0ad2ef18acb3858c06b8757cf7250b</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210931</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Twa&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Twa&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;civilization_or_empire&gt;</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;entity_description&gt; The Twa people are an indigenous group of hunter-gatherers residing in southern Africa. They were one of the earliest inhabitants of the region and had a close relationship with Bantu groups, sharing knowledge about adapting to the rainforest environment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0a0ad2ef18acb3858c06b8757cf7250b</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210931</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Khoekhoe_and_Khoisan&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Khoekhoe_and_Khoisan&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;civilization_or_empire&gt;</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;entity_description&gt; The Khoekhoe and Khoisan are indigenous pastoralist groups that inhabited southern Africa before the arrival of the Bantu. They were known for their cattle-herding practices, which influenced the Bantu people who later learned these skills.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0a0ad2ef18acb3858c06b8757cf7250b</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210931</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Kush&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Kush&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;civilization_or_empire&gt;</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;entity_description&gt; The Kingdom of Kush, centered in Nubia south of the first cataract on the Nile, was a powerful and influential state that flourished from the 10th century BC to 350 AD. It had close ties with Ancient Egypt but developed its own unique culture.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0a0ad2ef18acb3858c06b8757cf7250b</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210931</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Ancient_Egypt&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Ancient_Egypt&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;civilization_or_empire&gt;</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;entity_description&gt; Ancient Egypt is a civilization located in northeastern Africa, known for its advanced architecture, hieroglyphic writing system, and complex religious practices. The Egyptians had significant interactions with the Kingdom of Kush, influencing each other culturally.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0a0ad2ef18acb3858c06b8757cf7250b</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210931</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Origin of the Kingdom of Kush">
      <data key="d0">Origin of the Kingdom of Kush</data>
      <data key="d1">time_period</data>
      <data key="d2">The origins of the Kingdom of Kush date back almost two thousand years before its impressive period.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fd4afb589af42f7e98b4b08ee50decdd</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210931</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Kushite kings">
      <data key="d0">Kushite kings</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">Kushite kings refer to rulers who governed the Nubian Kingdom of Kush during its history, with some even ascending to rule Egypt in the eighth century BCE.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fd4afb589af42f7e98b4b08ee50decdd</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210931</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Nile delta">
      <data key="d0">Nile delta</data>
      <data key="d1">geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">The Nile delta is mentioned as part of the territory covered by the Kingdom of Kush, from north to south.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fd4afb589af42f7e98b4b08ee50decdd</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210932</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Khartoum">
      <data key="d0">Khartoum</data>
      <data key="d1">geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">Khartoum is identified as the capital of present-day northern Sudan and part of the Kingdom of Kush's southern boundary in later centuries.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fd4afb589af42f7e98b4b08ee50decdd</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210932</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Trade with Egypt">
      <data key="d0">Trade with Egypt</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">The engagement between Nubia (Kingdom of Kush) and ancient Egypt in trade, primarily involving goods like ostrich feathers, ivory, ebony, incense, and gold, is highlighted.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fd4afb589af42f7e98b4b08ee50decdd</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210932</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ostrich feathers">
      <data key="d0">Ostrich feathers</data>
      <data key="d1">source_or_artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">Ostrich feathers are mentioned as one of the key trading goods between Nubia and Egypt's Old Kingdom.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fd4afb589af42f7e98b4b08ee50decdd</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210932</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pharaonic ritual and ceremony">
      <data key="d0">Pharaonic ritual and ceremony</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">Gold, a commodity from Nubia, was significant in pharaonic rituals, playing a role in crafting sarcophagus masks like that of Tutankhamun.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fd4afb589af42f7e98b4b08ee50decdd</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210932</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Sarcophagus mask of Tutankhamun">
      <data key="d0">Sarcophagus mask of Tutankhamun</data>
      <data key="d1">textbook_structure</data>
      <data key="d2">The mask of Tutankhamun is used as an example to illustrate the importance of Nubian gold in Egyptian rituals and ceremonies from the Old Kingdom period.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fd4afb589af42f7e98b4b08ee50decdd</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210932</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Kerma&quot;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Kerma"</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;"geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;"Kerma is a Nubian state that arose around 2400 BCE and was located in northern Sudan. At its height, Kerma contained a large population and urban center featuring structures like the Western Deffufa temple."</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d7edb5c90c1347b6f59e1607916d8804</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210932</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Western Deffufa&quot;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Western Deffufa"</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;"source_or_artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;"The Western Deffufa is a large three-story mud-brick temple, forty-five meters tall, that served as part of Kerma’s urban center and is one of three known deffufsas in the region."</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d7edb5c90c1347b6f59e1607916d8804</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210932</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Kush&quot;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Kush"</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;"civilization_or_empire</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;"Kush is a Nubian city-state known to Egyptians since the Middle Kingdom. Its population was significant, and it had substantial wealth during around the eighteenth century BCE before integrating elements of Egyptian culture into its practices."</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d7edb5c90c1347b6f59e1607916d8804</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210932</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Middle Kingdom&quot;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Middle Kingdom"</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;"historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;"The Middle Kingdom in Egypt (2040–1782 BCE) is characterized by an era of expansion southward, including interactions with Nubian states like Kerma which were integral to broader economic and cultural exchanges."</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d7edb5c90c1347b6f59e1607916d8804</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210932</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Second Intermediate Period&quot;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Second Intermediate Period"</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;"historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;"The Second Intermediate Period (1782–1570 BCE) is a period where trade and contact with Kush declined, leading Egyptian fortresses to become more self-sufficient in response."</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d7edb5c90c1347b6f59e1607916d8804</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210932</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Kingdom of Kush&quot;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Kingdom of Kush"</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;"civilization_or_empire</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;"After the decline of the Middle Kingdom, the Kingdom of Kush emerged as an empire that absorbed the fortress communities, integrating aspects of Egyptian culture into its own practices."</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d7edb5c90c1347b6f59e1607916d8804</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210932</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Hyksos&quot;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Hyksos"</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;"historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;"The Hyksos are a group that controlled Lower Egypt during the Second Intermediate Period and facilitated interactions with Kush to weaken native Egyptian power centered in Thebes."</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d7edb5c90c1347b6f59e1607916d8804</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210932</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Trade with Kush&quot;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Trade with Kush"</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;"cultural_or_religious_movement</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;"Trade in goods and wealth between Egypt and Nubia's Kerma state during the rise of its middle kingdom, reflecting cultural exchange over time."</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d7edb5c90c1347b6f59e1607916d8804</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210932</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Ancestor Worship&quot;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Ancestor Worship"</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;"historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;"Ceremonies associated with ancestor worship may have been performed at Western Deffufa in Kerma, though specific details are not well-documented."</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d7edb5c90c1347b6f59e1607916d8804</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210932</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Fortresses&quot;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Fortresses"</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;"source_or_artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;"Fortress communities established by Egypt were designed to consolidate trade control and protect southward expansion into Nubia / Kush territories."</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d7edb5c90c1347b6f59e1607916d8804</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210932</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;736 BCE - 656 BCE&quot;&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"736 BCE - 656 BCE"&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;time_period&gt;</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;"This period corresponds to the early part of what is often referred to as the Kushite dominance in Nubia and Egypt. During this time, several kings from the Kushite kingdom asserted their power over various parts of the Nile Valley, including Upper Egypt and eventually all of Egypt."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b13ca3c5039a592c7efe58cadf289401</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210932</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Nile Valley&quot;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Nile Valley"</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;geographical_location&gt;</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;"A vital region that spans both present-day Sudan and Egypt along the course of the Nile River. It was central to economic activities, agriculture, and political power during this era."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b13ca3c5039a592c7efe58cadf289401</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210932</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Napata city&quot;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Napata city"</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;geographical_location&gt;</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;"The capital city of the ancient Kushite kingdom, Napata served as a cultural and administrative center in the early part of the period discussed. It was located in Sudan near modern-day Karima."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b13ca3c5039a592c7efe58cadf289401</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210932</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Kushite kingdom&quot;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Kushite kingdom"</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;civilization_or_empire&gt;</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;"A powerful Nubian/Ethiopian empire that dominated parts of Eastern Africa, including what is now northeastern Sudan and southern Egypt during this period. The Kushites are known for their military prowess, religious innovations, and interactions with the neighboring Egyptian kingdoms."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b13ca3c5039a592c7efe58cadf289401</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210932</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;installation of Piye's sister as high priestess in Thebes&quot;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"installation of Piye's sister as high priestess in Thebes"</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;historical_concept_or_event&gt;</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;"This event marked a significant political move by King Piye to establish his dominance over Upper Egypt. By installing his sister as a prominent religious figure, he ensured both cultural and spiritual control over key Egyptian cities."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b13ca3c5039a592c7efe58cadf289401</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210932</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Kushite conquest of Egypt&quot;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Kushite conquest of Egypt"</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;historical_concept_or_event&gt;</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;"A series of military campaigns led by Kushite kings that eventually resulted in the annexation or significant control over northern regions of ancient Egypt, marking the beginning of the 25th Dynasty or Ethiopian dynasty."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b13ca3c5039a592c7efe58cadf289401</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210932</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;start of the Twenty-Fifth Dynasty or Ethiopian dynasty&quot;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"start of the Twenty-Fifth Dynasty or Ethiopian dynasty"</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;historical_concept_or_event&gt;</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;"This refers to the period when the Kushite kingdom established a full reign over all of ancient Egypt, with its capital at Thebes. This was marked by changes in governance, religious practices, and cultural expressions."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b13ca3c5039a592c7efe58cadf289401</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210932</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Piye, king of Kush&quot;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Piye, king of Kush"</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;historical_figure&gt;</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;"The first known major ruler of the Kushite kingdom during this period, Piye is noted for his military campaigns that expanded the realm and his strategic appointments like installing his sister as high priestess in Thebes."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b13ca3c5039a592c7efe58cadf289401</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210932</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Egyptianization of Nubian culture and traditions; promotion of Egyptian religious practices; wearing traditional double crown; hieroglyphic inscriptions on temples&quot;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Egyptianization of Nubian culture and traditions; promotion of Egyptian religious practices; wearing traditional double crown; hieroglyphic inscriptions on temples"</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;cultural_or_religious_movement&gt;</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;"During the Kushite reign over parts of Egypt, cultural practices such as those associated with Egyptian religion were adapted by the Kushites. This included adopting symbols like the pharaonic double crown and engraving temple walls with hieroglyphic inscriptions to legitimize their rule."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b13ca3c5039a592c7efe58cadf289401</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210932</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;stone remnant displaying the name of Pharaoh Chabaka&quot;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"stone remnant displaying the name of Pharaoh Chabaka"</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;source_or_artifact&gt;</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;"This artifact, likely a fragment from a larger monument or inscription, bears the title and possibly an image of Pharaoh Chabaka. It is significant as it provides evidence for the activities and governance during this period."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b13ca3c5039a592c7efe58cadf289401</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210932</data>
    </node>
