    <node id="France">
      <data key="d0">France</data>
      <data key="d1">geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">(country in Europe) - Provided support to the American colonists during the Revolutionary War.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-593059281b5741efb3ebfe791c839b26</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210933</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Spain">
      <data key="d0">Spain</data>
      <data key="d1">geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">Spain, a country located in Europe, has had significant historical involvement in various conflicts. During the Second Punic War (circa 218-201 BCE), Spain was notably contested by both Rome and Carthage, as Roman conquests aimed to reduce Carthaginian influence on the Iberian Peninsula, which constituted a crucial strategic move during this period. While these events in the 3rd century BCE do not directly fall under Spanish history, they provide broader context for understanding Spain's role in Roman-Carthaginian rivalries.

In more recent times, Spain supported the American cause by participating in conflicts against British forces. This involvement underscores Spain's historical commitment to supporting allied nations during moments of conflict.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-102a3de218fabdb69815ca7d8d632b0a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-593059281b5741efb3ebfe791c839b26</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210937</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Treaty of Paris (1783)">
      <data key="d0">Treaty of Paris (1783)</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">The agreement officially ending the American Revolutionary War, recognizing the independence of the United States and setting boundaries for the new nation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-593059281b5741efb3ebfe791c839b26</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210933</data>
    </node>
    <node id="River Rubicon">
      <data key="d0">River Rubicon</data>
      <data key="d1">geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">The River Rubicon served as a political and geographical boundary, with Rome consolidating control over its south until the early 3rd century BCE.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-102a3de218fabdb69815ca7d8d632b0a</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210933</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Magna Graecia">
      <data key="d0">Magna Graecia</data>
      <data key="d1">cultural_or_religious_movement</data>
      <data key="d2">Refers to southern Italy colonized by Greek culture during the Archaic period, known for significant Greek influence well before the rise of Rome.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-102a3de218fabdb69815ca7d8d632b0a</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210933</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pyrrhic War">
      <data key="d0">Pyrrhic War</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">An inconclusive conflict that marked a pivotal development in Roman history, ending with the Romans gaining full control over southern Italy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-102a3de218fabdb69815ca7d8d632b0a</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210933</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Punic Wars">
      <data key="d0">Punic Wars</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">A series of long and drawn out conflicts between Rome and Carthage that had a profound impact on both empires, particularly through the actions of Hannibal Barca.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-102a3de218fabdb69815ca7d8d632b0a</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210933</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Second Punic or Hannibalic War">
      <data key="d0">Second Punic or Hannibalic War</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">Noted for its scale and intensity; this war saw the invasion of Italy by Carthaginian forces under Hannibal Barca, leading to significant Roman defeats and uprisings against their own government.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-102a3de218fabdb69815ca7d8d632b0a</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210933</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hannibal Barca">
      <data key="d0">Hannibal Barca</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">A renowned Carthaginian general who invaded Italy with his army and elephants during the Second Punic War, achieving notable victories such as those at Trebia, Lake Trasimene, and Cannae.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-102a3de218fabdb69815ca7d8d632b0a</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210933</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Battle of Trebia">
      <data key="d0">Battle of Trebia</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">One of several key battles in the Second Punic War where Hannibal Barca defeated Roman forces, setting the stage for further confrontations, notably at Lake Trasimene and especially at Cannae.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-102a3de218fabdb69815ca7d8d632b0a</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210933</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Lake Trasimene">
      <data key="d0">Lake Trasimene</data>
      <data key="d1">geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">A lake in central Italy where a significant battle during the Second Punic War occurred, resulting in a major victory for Hannibal Barca over Roman forces.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-102a3de218fabdb69815ca7d8d632b0a</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210933</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Battle of Cannae">
      <data key="d0">Battle of Cannae</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">The most infamous and destructive day of battle ever fought; on this day, Hannibal Barca annihilated the bulk of the Roman army after outmaneuvering them to secure a decisive victory in 216 BCE.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-102a3de218fabdb69815ca7d8d632b0a</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210933</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Publius Cornelius Scipio">
      <data key="d0">Publius Cornelius Scipio</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">A Roman general instrumental in finalizing Rome's defeat of Carthage during the Second Punic War, later returning as a hero who earned his honorary title Africanus following his triumph at Zama.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-102a3de218fabdb69815ca7d8d632b0a</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210933</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cato the Censor">
      <data key="d0">Cato the Censor</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">A leading conservative Roman politician who influenced the decision to wage the Third Punic War, known for his strict adherence to traditional values and political principles.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-102a3de218fabdb69815ca7d8d632b0a</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210933</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Third Punic War">
      <data key="d0">Third Punic War</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">The final confrontation between Rome and Carthage, resulting in the destruction of both cities and Carthage's replacement by a Roman province centered around Tunis.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-102a3de218fabdb69815ca7d8d632b0a</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210933</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tunis">
      <data key="d0">Tunis</data>
      <data key="d1">geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">Modern-day capital city in Tunisia that became the new seat of power for Rome's African Province after the destruction of Carthage.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-102a3de218fabdb69815ca7d8d632b0a</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210933</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cannae">
      <data key="d0">Cannae</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">("The infamous Battle of Cannae where Hannibal Barca defeated a massive Roman army under Varro. This battle defined the strategies and leadership capabilities necessary for Rome to eventually win the Second Punic War."</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-102a3de218fabdb69815ca7d8d632b0a</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210933</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Sicily">
      <data key="d0">Sicily</data>
      <data key="d1">geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">("Island off Italy's southern coast that changed hands several times amidst the ongoing conflicts between Rome and Carthage, critical for control of regional naval power and resources. Not directly part of 3rd century BCE but provides historical context."</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-102a3de218fabdb69815ca7d8d632b0a</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210933</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Gauls">
      <data key="d0">Gauls</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">("Not under direct discussion during third and early second centuries BCE, but their interactions with Romans became more prominent as Rome expanded into Gaul (modern-day France), setting the stage for future conflicts beyond 3rd century period."</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-102a3de218fabdb69815ca7d8d632b0a</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210933</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Alexandria&quot;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Alexandria"</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">Founded by Alexander the Great; became a major commercial hub in Hellenistic Egypt; role in spreading Greek culture and settlement in North Africa</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-23874d9b9ec9ab088432d4c9da9bd149</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210933</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Carthage&quot;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Carthage"</data>
      <data key="d1">civilization_or_empire</data>
      <data key="d2">Major city-state in ancient North Africa; rival of Rome during Punic Wars; limited interaction with hinterland due to food supply issues within city boundaries</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-23874d9b9ec9ab088432d4c9da9bd149</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210933</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Hannibal&quot;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Hannibal"</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">Commander for Carthage during the Second Punic War; led forces across the Alps into Italy; significant in Roman conquest of North Africa and agricultural production intensification</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-23874d9b9ec9ab088432d4c9da9bd149</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210934</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Limes&quot;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Limes"</data>
      <data key="d1">cultural_or_religious_movement</data>
      <data key="d2">\-Territorial limits of Roman occupation established to manage human flow during intensive farming periods, functioning in protecting crops from livestock</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-23874d9b9ec9ab088432d4c9da9bd149</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210934</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Roman Empire&quot;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Roman Empire"</data>
      <data key="d1">civilization_or_empire</data>
      <data key="d2">The Roman Empire was a dominant power that encompassed much of the Mediterranean basin, including Egypt, known for its complex military and administrative structures. This vast empire was governed by emperors such as Augustus, who faced significant challenges from influential figures like Cleopatra. The empire expanded into North Africa to meet growing food demands, which led to extensive agricultural expansions and the establishment of limes (frontiers) to protect these new territories.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-784822cf8d18c093216a7caab55b8730&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-23874d9b9ec9ab088432d4c9da9bd149</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210937</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Phoenician coastal cities&quot;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Phoenician coastal cities"</data>
      <data key="d1">geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">\-Commercial centers for shipping produce and livestock; growth due to increased economic activities from agricultural production</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-23874d9b9ec9ab088432d4c9da9bd149</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210934</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Ptolemaic dynasty&quot;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Ptolemaic dynasty"</data>
      <data key="d1">cultural_or_religious_movement</data>
      <data key="d2">Hellenistic succession of Egyptian pharaohs (305–30 BCE); rule after Alexandrian conquest, cultural fusion between Greek and Egyptian traditions in governance</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-23874d9b9ec9ab088432d4c9da9bd149</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210934</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Cleopatra VII&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Cleopatra VII&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;historical_figure&gt;</data>
      <data key="d2">Cleopatra VII (69–30 BCE) was the last ruler of the Ptolemaic dynasty in Egypt and a prominent historical figure during the Hellenistic period. She was highly politically ambitious, fluent in many languages, and maintained significant influence despite the growing Roman dominance over the Mediterranean region.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-58144bcbba7c9ca8dda4b4bb3d8ab8b6</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210934</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Julius Caesar&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Julius Caesar&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;historical_figure&gt;</data>
      <data key="d2">Julius Caesar (100–44 BCE) was a Roman general and statesman who played a crucial role in the transformation of Rome from a republic to an empire. He was involved in numerous military campaigns, political conflicts, and personal relationships, including his affair with Cleopatra VII.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-58144bcbba7c9ca8dda4b4bb3d8ab8b6</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210934</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Marc Antony&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Marc Antony&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;historical_figure&gt;</data>
      <data key="d2">Marc Antony (83–30 BCE) was a Roman politician and general who became one of the most important leaders during the later years of the Roman Republic. He formed close alliances, notably with Octavian and Cleopatra VII, leading to significant military conflicts that ultimately resulted in his defeat at the Battle of Actium.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-58144bcbba7c9ca8dda4b4bb3d8ab8b6</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210934</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Octavian&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Octavian&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;historical_figure&gt;</data>
      <data key="d2">Octavian (63 BCE–14 CE), also known as Augustus, was the first Roman emperor. He defeated Marc Antony and Cleopatra VII at the Battle of Actium in 31 BCE, ending the Ptolemaic dynasty's rule over Egypt.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-58144bcbba7c9ca8dda4b4bb3d8ab8b6</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210934</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Battle of Actium (31 BCE)&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Battle of Actium (31 BCE)&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;historical_event&gt;</data>
      <data key="d2">The Battle of Actium (31 BCE), a naval battle fought near the Ionian coast of Greece, was the decisive confrontation between the opposing forces of Octavian and Marc Antony, leading to Octavian's victory that set the stage for his assumption of power as emperor.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-58144bcbba7c9ca8dda4b4bb3d8ab8b6</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210934</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Caesarion&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Caesarion&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;historical_figure&gt;</data>
      <data key="d2">Caesarion (62/61–30 BCE) was Cleopatra VII’s son by Julius Caesar. He was named Pharaoh and became a legitimate heir to the Egyptian throne, but his rule was short-lived due to the political upheaval caused by Octavian.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-58144bcbba7c9ca8dda4b4bb3d8ab8b6</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210934</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Cleopatra&quot;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Cleopatra"</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;"historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;"A queen of Egypt; known for her remarkable beauty, strategic and diplomatic influence, and interactions with Roman figures such as Julius Caesar and Mark Antony. She played significant roles in the shifting political landscape during the late Roman Republic and early Roman Empire."&lt;&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-784822cf8d18c093216a7caab55b8730</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210934</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Julius Caesar&quot;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Julius Caesar"</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;"historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;"A leader of the Roman Republic, known for his political and military accomplishments, including conquests in Gaul. He died in a civil war that saw him in conflict with Mark Antony before the eventual rise of Augustus."&lt;&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-784822cf8d18c093216a7caab55b8730</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210934</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Mark Antony&quot;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Mark Antony"</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;"historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;"A general and politician who aligned with Cleopatra against Octavian (Augustus), leading to several significant military conflicts, most notably at Actium."&lt;&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-784822cf8d18c093216a7caab55b8730</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210934</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Octavian (Augustus)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Octavian (Augustus)"</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;"historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;"The first Roman Emperor who defeated Mark Antony in the civil war and effectively ended the Roman Republic. Augustus managed Egypt through Cleopatra's successor, Ptolemy XIII."&lt;&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-784822cf8d18c093216a7caab55b8730</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210934</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Ptolemy XIII&quot;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Ptolemy XIII"</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;"historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;"The last known ruler of Egypt from the Ptolemaic dynasty before becoming a figure in the power struggles after Mark Antony and Octavian subdued Cleopatra’s forces."&lt;&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-784822cf8d18c093216a7caab55b8730</data>
