    <node id="Maghreb">
      <data key="d0">Maghreb</data>
      <data key="d1">geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">The Maghreb is a region located in northwest Africa, extending from modern-day Morocco to Libya. Historically, this area served as a key route for the spread of Christianity westward during the second and third centuries. Additionally, the northern parts of the Maghreb, particularly those areas north of the Sahara, were characterized by better grazing grounds. As a result, nomadic tribes often migrated to these regions during harsh seasons in search of more favorable living conditions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5874c3ff9fc6f9f51a9a1072e47f5bed&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-68be3898587a9f6e093512ef1a1060f3</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210943</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Haratin">
      <data key="d0">Haratin</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">The Haratin were small groups that lived in oases, harvesting dates and digging salt but often subordinate to nomads.</data>
