    </node>
    <node id="Western Frontier">
      <data key="d0">Western Frontier</data>
      <data key="d1">geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">The western borders of the Roman Empire are mentioned, drawing attention to threats from groups such as Germanic kingdoms in this region.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f82fcaeaf8ded7ccd594a70788c5c5e9</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210941</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Palmyra">
      <data key="d0">Palmyra</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">Palmyra is noted for its significant political and economic role as a state on Rome's eastern frontier during the 3rd century CE. Additionally, Palmyra was an important oasis town that formed part of the broader Silk Road network, facilitating trade and cultural exchange along this ancient route.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-582dc813b509020dd77ed6ee9db0e981&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f82fcaeaf8ded7ccd594a70788c5c5e9</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210945</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Late Antique World">
      <data key="d0">Late Antique World</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">This overarching term encapsulates the complex state interactions and cultural dynamics occurring from a broad Eastern European perspective into the Near and Middle East, reflecting changing power structures and interdependencies across these regions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f82fcaeaf8ded7ccd594a70788c5c5e9</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210941</data>
    </node>
    <node id="3rd Century CE">
      <data key="d0">3rd Century CE</data>
      <data key="d1">time_period</data>
      <data key="d2">The 3rd century CE is a period in history that spans from 200 to 284 CE. Notably, within this timeframe, the significant event of 330 CE marked the formal dedication of Constantinople as the new capital of the Roman Empire. Both descriptions highlight the importance of these two specific events during this historical span, focusing on the administrative and geographical changes in the Roman Empire.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1ca8bf4d9560cf713649b9941df5971f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f82fcaeaf8ded7ccd594a70788c5c5e9</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210947</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;Empires of Faith">
      <data key="d0">"Empires of Faith</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">Empires of Faith is a concept relating to religiously motivated empires that have historically existed, often intertwined with political and cultural power structures.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1901aa513d6edb893106964e1ffc5369</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210941</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;Naqsh Rustam. Shapour">
      <data key="d0">"Naqsh Rustam. Shapour</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">Naqsh Rustam. Shapour is a significant event in 260 CE under Shapur I, the second Sassanian Shahanshah of Persia.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1901aa513d6edb893106964e1ffc5369</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210941</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;267 CE">
      <data key="d0">"267 CE</data>
      <data key="d1">time_period</data>
      <data key="d2">267 CE represents the period during which Zenobia tried to establish her own empire in the Roman province of Syria.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1901aa513d6edb893106964e1ffc5369</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210941</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;Zenobia">
      <data key="d0">"Zenobia</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">Zenobia was a queen regnant (ruling in her own right) who established an independent Palmyrene realm, often referred to as the Queen of Palmyra or Bostra.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1901aa513d6edb893106964e1ffc5369</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210942</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;284 CE">
      <data key="d0">"284 CE</data>
      <data key="d1">time_period</data>
      <data key="d2">284 CE marks the start of the Tetrarchy and the rule of Diocletian, a period that saw significant administrative reforms in the Roman Empire.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1901aa513d6edb893106964e1ffc5369</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210942</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;Portrait of the Four Tetrarchs">
      <data key="d0">"Portrait of the Four Tetrarchs</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">The Portrait of the Four Tetrarchs is associated with 284 CE, a time when Diocletian established the system of tetrarchy in Rome.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1901aa513d6edb893106964e1ffc5369</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210942</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;330 CE">
      <data key="d0">"330 CE</data>
      <data key="d1">time_period</data>
      <data key="d2">330 CE marks the founding of Constantinople by Emperor Constantine the Great as the new capital of the Roman Empire and later the Byzantine Empire.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1901aa513d6edb893106964e1ffc5369</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210942</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;Hagia Sophia, Istanbul, 1857 Lithography">
      <data key="d0">"Hagia Sophia, Istanbul, 1857 Lithography</data>
      <data key="d1">source_or_artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">The Hagia Sophia, a significant structure in Constantinople, has had various religious and cultural roles since the Byzantine era.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1901aa513d6edb893106964e1ffc5369</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210942</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;410 CE">
      <data key="d0">"410 CE</data>
      <data key="d1">time_period</data>
      <data key="d2">410 CE marks the event of the Sack of Rome by the Visigoths, marking the beginning of the fall of the Western Roman Empire.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1901aa513d6edb893106964e1ffc5369</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210942</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;Visigoths and Sack of Rome">
      <data key="d0">"Visigoths and Sack of Rome</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">This event refers to the Visigoth-led raid on Rome in 410 CE, which symbolizes a critical turning point.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1901aa513d6edb893106964e1ffc5369</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210942</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Third Through Seventh Centuries CE">
      <data key="d0">Third Through Seventh Centuries CE</data>
      <data key="d1">time_period</data>
      <data key="d2">The time period from the third through the seventh centuries CE saw profound and fundamental cultural changes in the Roman Empire.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f86511eceaa5d73b4eea4557e54d1a17</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210942</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Late Antiquity">
      <data key="d0">Late Antiquity</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">Late Antiquity is a historical timeframe transitioning from classical antiquity to the Middle Ages, roughly spanning from the 3rd century CE to the Early Medieval Period (c. 600 CE). This period is characterized by significant changes including the decline of the Western Roman Empire and the rise of Christianity as the dominant religion. Additionally, major transformations such as Christianization and Germanic invasions also define this era, alongside notable shifts in political, social, and religious structures. The timeline encompasses from the 3rd to the 7th century CE and is recognized for its pivotal role in bridging ancient and medieval worlds.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-64b6a2af1c3cece75a3cbd9b707b8713&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-754df6979479251d63e61b06ae37d304&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f86511eceaa5d73b4eea4557e54d1a17</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210950</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Roman Empire Shift Eastward">
      <data key="d0">Roman Empire Shift Eastward</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">The shift of the Roman Empire's focus eastward during Late Antiquity marked significant political and cultural changes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f86511eceaa5d73b4eea4557e54d1a17</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210943</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Christianization of Rome">
      <data key="d0">Christianization of Rome</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_event</data>
      <data key="d2">The adoption of Christianity as the official religion of the Roman Empire in the fourth century had a profound impact on Mediterranean society.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f86511eceaa5d73b4eea4557e54d1a17</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210943</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Germanic Migrations and Invasions">
      <data key="d0">Germanic Migrations and Invasions</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Germanic invasions played a significant role in the collapse of Roman authority in the West during Late Antiquity.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f86511eceaa5d73b4eea4557e54d1a17</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210944</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Byzantine Empire Rise">
      <data key="d0">Byzantine Empire Rise</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">The rise of the Byzantine Empire from the remnants of the Roman state is noted as an important historical concept or event.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f86511eceaa5d73b4eea4557e54d1a17</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210944</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Constantinople Becoming Capital">
      <data key="d0">Constantinople Becoming Capital</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">The establishment of Constantinople as the new capital in the eastern Mediterranean marks a significant change for the Roman and later Byzantine Empire.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f86511eceaa5d73b4eea4557e54d1a17</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210944</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Byzantine Christian Orthodoxy">
      <data key="d0">Byzantine Christian Orthodoxy</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Maintaining Christian orthodoxy became a key focus of Byzantine society, emphasizing the role of religious identity in state affairs.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f86511eceaa5d73b4eea4557e54d1a17</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210944</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Roman Empire's Cultural Changes">
      <data key="d0">Roman Empire's Cultural Changes</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">The Roman Empire experienced substantial cultural changes during Late Antiquity, influenced by its classical heritage and the emergence of new religious identities. There were also significant internal transformations within the empire, which included both political decentralization and cultural hybridization. These complex shifts reflect a period of profound transition in Roman society and governance.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f86511eceaa5d73b4eea4557e54d1a17</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210949</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Shift Towards Medieval World">
      <data key="d0">Shift Towards Medieval World</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Late Antiquity is seen as a period transitioning towards medieval times with changing regional and cultural dynamics.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f86511eceaa5d73b4eea4557e54d1a17</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210944</data>
    </node>
    <node id="third century">
      <data key="d0">third century</data>
      <data key="d1">time_period</data>
      <data key="d2">The third century refers to the period from 235 to 284 AD during which the Roman Empire experienced significant upheaval and change.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4b61ce25cf795d5df68cfe86411e23d4</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210944</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Crisis of the Third Century">
      <data key="d0">Crisis of the Third Century</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">Crisis of the Third Century is a term used to describe the political, economic, and social instability of the Roman Empire from 235 to 284 AD.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4b61ce25cf795d5df68cfe86411e23d4</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210944</data>
    </node>
    <node id="twenty-six different claimants">
      <data key="d0">twenty-six different claimants</data>
      <data key="d1">number_of_emperors</data>
      <data key="d2">From 235 to 284 AD, there were approximately twenty-six distinct rulers who claimed the imperial throne.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4b61ce25cf795d5df68cfe86411e23d4</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210944</data>
    </node>
    <node id="devaluation of currency">
      <data key="d0">devaluation of currency</data>
      <data key="d1">economic_problem</data>
      <data key="d2">The devaluation of currency refers to the diminishing value of coins issued by the Roman government after the third century.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4b61ce25cf795d5df68cfe86411e23d4</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210944</data>
    </node>
    <node id="price of goods">
      <data key="d0">price of goods</data>
      <data key="d1">economy</data>
      <data key="d2">As a result of the devaluation of currency, the price of goods rose significantly in the Roman Empire.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4b61ce25cf795d5df68cfe86411e23d4</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210944</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Diocletian">
      <data key="d0">Diocletian</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">Diocletian, an Illyrian military official, rose to become emperor of Rome in 284 AD. He is recognized as one of the pivotal historical figures who initiated the Tetrarchy, a system that divided imperial powers among four rulers to stabilize the empire. Following his significant reforms that left a lasting impact on the Roman Empire, Diocletian chose to abdicate and retire peacefully.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-76ce6c5d8fc29b6728dc6d89f3cbb2c1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4b61ce25cf795d5df68cfe86411e23d4</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210950</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Illyria">
      <data key="d0">Illyria</data>
      <data key="d1">geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">Illyria is a region in the Balkans from which Diocletian hailed before becoming emperor.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4b61ce25cf795d5df68cfe86411e23d4</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210944</data>
    </node>
    <node id="tetrarchy">
      <data key="d0">tetrarchy</data>
      <data key="d1">government_structure</data>
      <data key="d2">Tetrarchy refers to the rule of four emperors, each with their own sphere of influence, that emerged under Diocletian to address the instability and strengthen governance.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4b61ce25cf795d5df68cfe86411e23d4</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210944</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Edict on Maximum Prices">
      <data key="d0">Edict on Maximum Prices</data>
      <data key="d1">source_or_artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">The Edict on Maximum Prices was an edict issued by Diocletian in 301 AD aimed at addressing inflation through price controls for certain goods.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4b61ce25cf795d5df68cfe86411e23d4</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210944</data>
    </node>
    <node id="St. Marks Basilica, Venice">
      <data key="d0">St. Marks Basilica, Venice</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">Figure 10.4 refers to the sculptural portrait of the Roman tetrarchs located in St. Mark’s Basilica in Venice, Italy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4b61ce25cf795d5df68cfe86411e23d4</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210944</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tetrarchy">
      <data key="d0">Tetrarchy</data>
      <data key="d1">government_structure</data>
      <data key="d2">Tetrarchy refers to the system of four co-rulers or emperors each with their own sphere of influence that emerged under Diocletian in an attempt to stabilize and strengthen governance. This arrangement represented a significant shift in how power was distributed within the Roman Empire, marking a pivotal restructuring of imperial rule.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-76ce6c5d8fc29b6728dc6d89f3cbb2c1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4b61ce25cf795d5df68cfe86411e23d4</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210951</data>
    </node>
    <node id="300 CE">
      <data key="d0">300 CE</data>
      <data key="d1">time_period</data>
      <data key="d2">300 CE marks the period when the Four Tetrarchs were carved, characterized by a transition in artistic style and political structure in Roman society.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-76ce6c5d8fc29b6728dc6d89f3cbb2c1</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210945</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Maximian">
      <data key="d0">Maximian</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">Maximian is another important figure associated with the Tetrarchy, serving as a co-emperor until formally leaving office in 305 CE.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-76ce6c5d8fc29b6728dc6d89f3cbb2c1</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210945</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Battle of Milvian Bridge">
      <data key="d0">Battle of Milvian Bridge</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">The Battle of Milvian Bridge was a key conflict that significantly contributed to Constantine's rise to imperial power, marking his victory over Maxentius in 312 CE.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-76ce6c5d8fc29b6728dc6d89f3cbb2c1</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210946</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Constantines Bio by Eusebius">
      <data key="d0">Constantines Bio by Eusebius</data>
      <data key="d1">textbook_structure</data>
      <data key="d2">Eusebius' biography of Constantine provides an important source for understanding the historical and religious context of Constantine's rule, including his claims about divine intervention.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-76ce6c5d8fc29b6728dc6d89f3cbb2c1</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210946</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Arch of Constantine in Rome">
      <data key="d0">Arch of Constantine in Rome</data>
      <data key="d1">source_or_artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">The Arch of Constantine is a significant source of archaeological evidence that illustrates the blending of Roman tradition with early Christian symbolism during Constantine’s reign, as indicated by its design and inscription.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-76ce6c5d8fc29b6728dc6d89f3cbb2c1</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
