    <node id="&lt;Serpent Column&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Serpent Column&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">source_or_artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">An artifact dating back to antiquity, symbolizing the historical and cultural context of Constantine’s rule and its transfer from one location to another.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fc15c8190bd017249609745764d34f78</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210947</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Constantinople&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Constantinople&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">The capital city during the reigns of Constantine's successors where significant Christian monuments and policies were developed, serving as a central hub for imperial Christian governance.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fc15c8190bd017249609745764d34f78</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210947</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Edict of Milan&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Edict of Milan&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">A decree that promoted religious freedom for Christians within the empire by legally recognizing their rights, indirectly promoting Christianity while not making it the official state religion, marking a significant step in the early spread of Christianity under Constantine.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fc15c8190bd017249609745764d34f78</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210947</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Christianity&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Christianity&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">cultural_or_religious_movement</data>
      <data key="d2">Christianity is a monotheistic religion based on the teachings of Jesus Christ, founded in 1st-century Palestine. It comprises diverse denominations that adhere to Jesus' teachings as recorded in the New Testament and believe him to be God's son and savior, while also sharing core beliefs such as original sin, salvation by faith through grace, resurrection, and eternal life after death. The religion experienced significant growth under Constantine, gaining substantial support and status within the Roman Empire, which ultimately led to its dominant position and integration into governmental matters.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2d6af87b20cd5c5556a01a3b05d06cc0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fc15c8190bd017249609745764d34f78</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210954</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Constantinian Era&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Constantinian Era&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">time_period</data>
      <data key="d2">The period marked by the reign of Constantine and his successors, characterized by Christian governance and imperial policies in support of Christianity, establishing it as a powerful institution within the state and society.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fc15c8190bd017249609745764d34f78</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210947</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Ctesiphon&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Ctesiphon&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">The Persian capital where Julian carried out his final military campaign that led to his death, symbolizing the brief resurgence of paganism against Christian dominance, highlighting the conflict between polytheistic and monotheistic cultures.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fc15c8190bd017249609745764d34f78</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210947</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Rule of Roman Christianity&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Rule of Roman Christianity&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">The period following Constantine's reign where Christian governance and institutions flourished under emperors who controlled church policy through ecumenical councils and legislation, reinforcing the central role of Christianity in imperial affairs.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fc15c8190bd017249609745764d34f78</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210947</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Ecumenical Councils&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Ecumenical Councils&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">textbook_structure</data>
      <data key="d2">Meetings of bishops organized by Emperors to discuss matters of religious orthodoxy, shaping the direction of Christianity within the Roman Empire, facilitating formalized theological discussions and decisions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fc15c8190bd017249609745764d34f78</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210947</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Sasanians&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Sasanians&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">civilization_or_empire</data>
      <data key="d2">The Persian empire against which Julian waged war, representing adversaries in the context of the brief resurgence of paganism, emphasizing the geopolitical tensions between Christian Rome and its Eastern enemies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fc15c8190bd017249609745764d34f78</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210947</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Mortal Wound&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Mortal Wound&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">The fatal injury sustained by Julian during his campaign that effectively ended any hope for a renewed polytheist empire, underscoring the transition from pagan to Christian dominance in the region.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fc15c8190bd017249609745764d34f78</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210947</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Christian Governance&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Christian Governance&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">cultural_or_religious_movement</data>
      <data key="d2">A period defined by the increasing dominance and regulation of Christian affairs by Roman emperors, establishing Christianity as a powerful institution within the state and society, marking the consolidation of Christian power under imperial support.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fc15c8190bd017249609745764d34f78</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210947</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Pagan Practice&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Pagan Practice&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">cultural_or_religious_movement</data>
      <data key="d2">The religious practices associated with pre-Christian religions that were increasingly marginalized under Constantine's successors, reflecting the decline in popularity and influence of traditional paganism.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fc15c8190bd017249609745764d34f78</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210947</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Theodosius_II">
      <data key="d0">Theodosius_II</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">Theodosius II ruled as Emperor of the Eastern Roman Empire from 408 until his death, issuing the Theodosian Code which codified laws and solidified Christianity's role in Roman society.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fa8b96093ad85d351d71a70ae72eb655</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210947</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Theodosian_Code">
      <data key="d0">Theodosian_Code</data>
      <data key="d1">source_or_artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">The Theodosian Code is a legal document established by Emperor Theodosius II that unified and codified Roman law from 312 CE, emphasizing Christian values in the empire.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fa8b96093ad85d351d71a70ae72eb655</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210947</data>
    </node>
    <node id="emperors">
      <data key="d0">emperors</data>
      <data key="d1">civilization_or_empire</data>
      <data key="d2">Refers to leaders of the Roman Empire who used councils for religious and political control, as demonstrated by Constantine's convening and Theodosius II's legal reforms.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fa8b96093ad85d351d71a70ae72eb655</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210947</data>
    </node>
    <node id="bishops">
      <data key="d0">bishops</data>
      <data key="d1">cultural_or_religious_movement</data>
      <data key="d2">Bishops were key figures in early Christianity, addressing theological issues at council meetings like the First Council of Nicaea and playing roles in legal and political decisions throughout Roman history.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fa8b96093ad85d351d71a70ae72eb655</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210947</data>
    </node>
    <node id="empire">
      <data key="d0">empire</data>
      <data key="d1">geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">The historical name for the state ruled by emperors at its height from 27 BC to 476 AD, including present-day Italy, Greece, Turkey, parts of Western Europe, North Africa, and the Near East.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fa8b96093ad85d351d71a70ae72eb655</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210947</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Council_of_Nicaea">
      <data key="d0">Council_of_Nicaea</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">The First Council of Nicaea was a pivotal meeting in 325 AD where bishops and emperor addressed theological disputes like Arianism and established doctrines such as the Nicene Creed to strengthen Christian orthodoxy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fa8b96093ad85d351d71a70ae72eb655</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210947</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Arians">
      <data key="d0">Arians</data>
      <data key="d1">cultural_or_religious_movement</data>
      <data key="d2">Arianism is characterized by its belief that Jesus Christ was not fully divine, distinct from other early church teachings. It was addressed at the Council of Nicaea and had sympathizers who persisted throughout subsequent councils like Constantinople.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fa8b96093ad85d351d71a70ae72eb655</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210947</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Nicene_Creed">
      <data key="d0">Nicene_Creed</data>
      <data key="d1">textbook_structure</data>
      <data key="d2">The Nicene Creed established by the First Council of Nicaea in 325 AD defined orthodox Christian beliefs, particularly regarding Christ's divinity, and became a core tenet of early Christianity.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fa8b96093ad85d351d71a70ae72eb655</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210947</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Arius">
      <data key="d0">Arius</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">Arius, a priest from Alexandria, proposed Arianism and was excommunicated but his teachings remained influential during the Council of Nicaea and beyond.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fa8b96093ad85d351d71a70ae72eb655</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210948</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Constantinople&quot;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Constantinople"</data>
      <data key="d1">geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">Capital of the Eastern Roman Empire, located on the Bosphorus Strait, strategic location controlling trade routes and military movements.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c2afe1adc2d651dcc1a06b89f50c8933</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210948</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Ravenna&quot;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Ravenna"</data>
      <data key="d1">geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">Capital of the Western Roman Empire from 402 to 476 AD, located on the Adriatic Sea in Italy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c2afe1adc2d651dcc1a06b89f50c8933</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210948</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Danube River&quot;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Danube River"</data>
      <data key="d1">geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">Border river between the Eastern and Western Empires, used for trade and defense against invasions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c2afe1adc2d651dcc1a06b89f50c8933</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210948</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Rhine River&quot;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Rhine River"</data>
      <data key="d1">geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">River bordering the Roman Empire, often crossed by Germanic tribes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c2afe1adc2d651dcc1a06b89f50c8933</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210948</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;The Fall of the Roman West&quot;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"The Fall of the Roman West"</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_event</data>
      <data key="d2">Event marking the end of Roman rule in the Western provinces around 476 AD, signaling the beginning of the Middle Ages.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c2afe1adc2d651dcc1a06b89f50c8933</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210948</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Theodosius&quot;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Theodosius"</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">Last emperor to rule over a united empire; died in 395, after which the empire divided into East and West.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c2afe1adc2d651dcc1a06b89f50c8933</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210948</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Arcadius&quot;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Arcadius"</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">Eastern Roman Emperor from 395 to 408 AD; ruled from Constantinople.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c2afe1adc2d651dcc1a06b89f50c8933</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210948</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Honorius&quot;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Honorius"</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">Western Roman Emperor from 395 to 423 AD; controlled the Western half of the empire from Ravenna.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c2afe1adc2d651dcc1a06b89f50c8933</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210948</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Paganism&quot;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Paganism"</data>
      <data key="d1">cultural_or_religious_movement</data>
      <data key="d2">Opposing religious movement to Christianity during this period; beliefs and practices were still prevalent among common people. </data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c2afe1adc2d651dcc1a06b89f50c8933</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210948</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;A Winged Victory by Antonio Canova&quot;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"A Winged Victory by Antonio Canova"</data>
      <data key="d1">source_or_artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">Statue representing Victory, originally pagan, later transformed into Christian symbolism.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c2afe1adc2d651dcc1a06b89f50c8933</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210948</data>
    </node>
    <node id="526 CE">
      <data key="d0">526 CE</data>
      <data key="d1">time_period</data>
      <data key="d2">526 CE marks the period when a map of Germanic kingdoms was created, showing their control over various regions such as Italy, Gaul (modern-day France and Belgium), the Iberian Peninsula, and North Africa.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e8b93582db5d7a873cf44814a77997d9</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210948</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Germanic Kingdoms">
      <data key="d0">Germanic Kingdoms</data>
      <data key="d1">cultural_or_religious_movement</data>
      <data key="d2">Germanic Kingdoms are the political entities formed by Germanic peoples in the western Roman Empire during this period of increasing conflict and fragmentation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e8b93582db5d7a873cf44814a77997d9</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
