      <data key="d0">Roman Empire|</data>
      <data key="d1">civilization_or_empire|</data>
      <data key="d2">The Roman Empire, a vast and influential polity that controlled Palmyra, providing cultural impact and imperial support through its policies and practices.|</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a4164713b8d188f57453f06a2a3003ed</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210952</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Palmyra|">
      <data key="d0">Palmyra|</data>
      <data key="d1">geographical_location|</data>
      <data key="d2">An ancient city known for its significance as an important crossroads between Rome and the East, Palmyra experienced periods of Roman colony status, independence under Zenobia, and eventual annexation by Rome.|</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a4164713b8d188f57453f06a2a3003ed</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210952</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Septimius Odaenathus|">
      <data key="d0">Septimius Odaenathus|</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_figure|</data>
      <data key="d2">A notable historical figure who led the Palmyrene Kingdom into closer alliances with Rome while dealing with Parthian threats, ultimately laying the groundwork for Zenobia's expansionist policies.|</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a4164713b8d188f57453f06a2a3003ed</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210952</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Zenobia|">
      <data key="d0">Zenobia|</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_figure|</data>
      <data key="d2">Queen of the Palmyrene Kingdom during its period of de facto independence and expansion, Zenobia’s rule was marked by territorial gains but ended in defeat against Roman imperial authority under Aurelian.|</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a4164713b8d188f57453f06a2a3003ed</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210952</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Baal worship|">
      <data key="d0">Baal worship|</data>
      <data key="d1">cultural_or_religious_movement|</data>
      <data key="d2">The religious practice centered around the Mesopotamian deity Baal, which played a crucial role in Palmyrene cultural and religious life, reflecting indigenous beliefs intertwined with influences from neighboring regions.|</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a4164713b8d188f57453f06a2a3003ed</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210952</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Byzantine Empire and Persia conflict&quot;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Byzantine Empire and Persia conflict"</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;"historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;"The prolonged military and political conflict between the Byzantine Empire and the Sasanian Empire, primarily during the 6th and early 7th centuries. This conflict involved numerous battles and campaigns, impacting both empires' territories and resources."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8380859d289f759870d05deb1753edc3</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210952</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;historical_concept_or_event&quot;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"historical_concept_or_event"</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;"barbarian_invasions</data>
      <data key="d2">The historical concept or event in question pertains to a significant series of military conflicts and territorial changes that took place during the 5th century involving various entities such as Vandals, Romans, Huns, and Germanic tribes. Specifically, it refers to the invasion led by Genseric, a key leader of the Vandals, which profoundly affected trade and governance in North African regions such as Carthage.

This event is part of broader invasions by nomadic and tribal groups into Roman territories during that period. It involved an initial phase where the Vandals were granted territories including Mauretania and Numidia through a formal agreement following their military conquest. Additionally, it encompasses the migration and invasion of various Germanic tribes across Europe, leading to Pannonian residence and subsequent invasions into North Africa.

The concept culminates with the reconquest led by Byzantine general Belisarius to reclaim territories from the Vandals for Byzantine rule, marking a return to imperial control after the initial Vandal invasion. This series of events together forms part of the complex historical development, though not directly linked to the Ming dynasty period as mentioned in one description.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a8af48913c15cba43ba646a75127490d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e1de9bd935bc75f9ff86b8e4ed9342c0</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210941</data>
    </node>
