    <node id="&lt;&quot;Rashidun Caliphate&quot;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Rashidun Caliphate"</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;"civilization_or_empire</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;Entity_description: The Rashidun Caliphate was established after Muhammad’s death in 632 CE, spanning from his immediate successors until its final split due to succession disputes by the mid-7th century. It marked a period of significant expansion and consolidation within early Islamic governance, characterized by the rule of the "Rightly Guided" or Rashidun Caliphs: Abu Bakr, Umar, Uthman, and Ali."</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-eca248d7090f58e7e1c63a9bfb407080</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210963</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Rashidun Caliph&quot;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Rashidun Caliph"</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;"historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;Entity_description: The leadership role within early Islamic governance during the time period of 632-656 CE. Specifically refers to Abu Bakr, Umar, Uthman, and Ali who governed under the title of caliphs in succession after Muhammad’s death.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-eca248d7090f58e7e1c63a9bfb407080</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
