    </node>
    <node id="Byzantine Gold Solidus">
      <data key="d0">Byzantine Gold Solidus</data>
      <data key="d1">source_or_artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">The Byzantine Gold Solidus is an artifact from 608 CE, featuring inscriptions in Greek, images of Emperor Heraclius and his son, and a Christian cross on the reverse. It serves as a reference for understanding pre-Islamic influences.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8515eeaece70ecd2cb37ce5d05f004bd</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210964</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Umayyad family">
      <data key="d0">Umayyad family</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">The Umayyad family refers to a dynasty that played a crucial role in Islamic history through their reforms and transformations of governance.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8515eeaece70ecd2cb37ce5d05f004bd</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210964</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Arabic script on coins and buildings">
      <data key="d0">Arabic script on coins and buildings</data>
      <data key="d1">source_or_artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">Arabic script appearing on coins and buildings during the later part of the seventh century marks a significant shift from earlier scripts used by Byzantine and Persian rulers, providing historical sources for understanding Islamic art and architecture.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8515eeaece70ecd2cb37ce5d05f004bd</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210964</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Byzantine empire">
      <data key="d0">Byzantine empire</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">The Byzantine Empire is described as one of the cultural influences on early Islamic art and architecture. It includes its rulers, their coin designs, and architectural styles.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8515eeaece70ecd2cb37ce5d05f004bd</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210964</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Persian empire">
      <data key="d0">Persian empire</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">The Persian Empire influenced early Islamic art and architecture through similar patterns in coin design and architectural motifs.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8515eeaece70ecd2cb37ce5d05f004bd</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210964</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Christian crosses">
      <data key="d0">Christian crosses</data>
      <data key="d1">cultural_or_religious_movement</data>
      <data key="d2">Christian crosses, particularly on coins, were removed by the Umayyad caliphs as part of their cultural and religious reforms, indicating a shift from earlier influences.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8515eeaece70ecd2cb37ce5d05f004bd</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210964</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Arabic language">
      <data key="d0">Arabic language</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">The introduction of Arabic as an official language reflects significant linguistic and cultural changes during the early Islamic period.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8515eeaece70ecd2cb37ce5d05f004bd</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210964</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Umayyad Gold Dinar">
      <data key="d0">Umayyad Gold Dinar</data>
      <data key="d1">source_or_artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">Umayyad Gold Dinar demonstrates how the Umayyad rulers replaced earlier designs with their own, highlighting shifts in artistic and governmental practices within the Islamic Empire.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8515eeaece70ecd2cb37ce5d05f004bd</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210964</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Dome of the Rock&quot;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Dome of the Rock"</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;"geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;"A significant building in Jerusalem that exemplifies early Islamic architecture and design, featuring extensive mosaics and Arabic inscriptions from the Quran. It serves as an important historical artifact reflecting cultural exchanges and religious beliefs during the Umayyad period."</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-acb0a07de6465f61f482a035df463ed8</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210964</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Islam">
      <data key="d0">Islam</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_figures</data>
      <data key="d2">Islam is a religious tradition founded upon the teachings of the Prophet Muhammad and his followers, which significantly expanded across various regions during critical periods such as those governed by the Almohad and Almoravid Empires. Its influence can be seen in diverse aspects including legal systems, architecture, education, and social structures within territories they controlled.

The religion also entered South Asia, particularly modern-day Pakistan and Bangladesh, and parts of China via trade routes and diplomatic missions during the early Tang period. Additionally, Islam spread across North Africa through military conquests orchestrated by Islamic leaders. These historical events contextualize the broader timeline, beginning with the establishment of Judaism and Christianity, indicating its role in shaping cultural developments from an early date into the 7th century CE.

While not explicitly depicted on a specific map, these events illustrate how Islam played a pivotal role in the socio-cultural landscape of regions it influenced.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-42369c47f2f60a9ab4ac526953bf6b40&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3047d60403fb89082b52beb809b2b4c2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5f105692039d53c800b15e8de2086313&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-70d03803311b31d7e11f7ace07e0a57f</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210967</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;晚7世纪">
      <data key="d0">&lt;晚7世纪</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;"time_period</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;"A specific time frame in history during which various significant events and changes took place, such as the rise of the Umayyads and the changing dynamics of coin design."</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-acb0a07de6465f61f482a035df463ed8</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210964</data>
    </node>
