    </node>
    <node id="750 CE">
      <data key="d0">750 CE</data>
      <data key="d1">time_period</data>
      <data key="d2">The year 750 CE marks a significant transition in which the Abbasid dynasty took over from the Umayyads and solidified Islam's place in the region and world history.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e1c8266d2e41b1fb97811d14df71d790</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210964</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Abbasids">
      <data key="d0">Abbasids</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">The Abbasid Caliphate succeeded the Umayyad Caliphate, emerging as a significant power that expanded from Iraq across northern Africa into Central Asia by the latter part of the 8th century CE. Ruling from 750 CE onwards, the Abbasids established themselves as the dominant state in the Middle East and played a crucial role in the spread of Islam. This dynasty is also noted for its profound impact on world history due to its enthusiastic support and patronage of the arts and sciences during their reign.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e1c8266d2e41b1fb97811d14df71d790&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-68886ae6bf0868f51bdd288cf9f2091f</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210968</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Islamic Community Splits">
      <data key="d0">Islamic Community Splits</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">During the Abbasid rule, there was a split within the Islamic community forming distinct sects with different theological beliefs and goals.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e1c8266d2e41b1fb97811d14df71d790</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210964</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Khurasan">
      <data key="d0">Khurasan</data>
      <data key="d1">geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">Khurasan province played a crucial role in the Abbasid's rise to power as it was a source of revolutionary activity against Umayyad rule.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e1c8266d2e41b1fb97811d14df71d790</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210964</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ancient Knowledge Transmission">
      <data key="d0">Ancient Knowledge Transmission</data>
      <data key="d1">source_or_artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">Under the Abbasids, there was an emphasis on preserving and building upon ancient knowledge that had been previously lost or unseen.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e1c8266d2e41b1fb97811d14df71d790</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210964</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Umayyad Dynasty">
      <data key="d0">Umayyad Dynasty</data>
      <data key="d1">civilization_or_empire</data>
      <data key="d2">The Umayyad Dynasty ruled parts of the Islamic Caliphate before it was taken over by the Abbasids in 750 CE.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e1c8266d2e41b1fb97811d14df71d790</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210964</data>
    </node>
    <node id="767-913 CE">
      <data key="d0">767-913 CE</data>
      <data key="d1">time_period</data>
      <data key="d2">The period from 767 to 913 CE during which Baghdad was at its zenith under Abbasid rule.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b90cdacdb1358ddf475df36a8406c224</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210964</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Baghdad">
      <data key="d0">Baghdad</data>
      <data key="d1">geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">Baghdad, identified as the capital of the Abbasid Caliphate, was designed to be one of the most prominent cities in its region, characterized by its circular layout and holding significant prestige for those residing near the caliph’s palace. It is renowned for its intellectual achievements and its role as a center for both trade and cultural exchanges, serving as a major hub for scholarly activities during the golden age of the Caliphate.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-70a588c1e9025f193747e47553aa2ca6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-58a412da38bd50aef179fd14786b22f7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b90cdacdb1358ddf475df36a8406c224</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210969</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Shift in Politics and Economics">
      <data key="d0">Shift in Politics and Economics</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">A major change in regional dynamics where political power moved further eastwards, signaling a shift in economic centers from traditional Mediterranean cities to Baghdad along with increased connections through the Silk Roads.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b90cdacdb1358ddf475df36a8406c224</data>
