    <node id="Middle Ages">
      <data key="d0">Middle Ages</data>
      <data key="d1">time_period</data>
      <data key="d2">The Middle Ages refers to a significant period in European history characterized by social obligations and ties between serfs and their lords. This era also witnessed important changes in the role of the caliph, especially under the Abbasid caliphs. The Middle Ages mark the transition from the medieval period to early modern times, ending with the rise of the Ottoman Empire.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a3cce5ba44faddd8f9fd27e8b4f71bef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-72d7bb40fa0aa36bf5b0bb66bf7975fe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-997b1992dbcfe1fe016997656821d078</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210970</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Abbasid Period">
      <data key="d0">Abbasid Period</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">The Abbasid Period refers to a specific era within the Islamic world characterized by shifts in power dynamics and the rise of the ulama class.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a3cce5ba44faddd8f9fd27e8b4f71bef</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210966</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ulamā">
      <data key="d0">Ulamā</data>
      <data key="d1">cultural_or_religious_movement</data>
      <data key="d2">The Ulamā is a religious scholarly class that gained prominence during the Abbasid period, playing an increasingly important role as interpreters of Islamic law in Sunni Islam.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a3cce5ba44faddd8f9fd27e8b4f71bef</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210966</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Caliph">
      <data key="d0">Caliph</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">The Caliph is a historical figure who served as both the leader and spiritual head in the early Islamic world, initially holding comprehensive authority over both secular and religious matters under the Umayyad dynasty. This role was crucial for military and political decisions during the initial spread of Islam across various regions. Over time, however, his influence gradually diminished, leading to changes in the governance structure within the Islamic community.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a3cce5ba44faddd8f9fd27e8b4f71bef&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-70d03803311b31d7e11f7ace07e0a57f</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210971</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Abbasid Caliphs">
      <data key="d0">Abbasid Caliphs</data>
      <data key="d1">civilization_or_empire</data>
      <data key="d2">Abbasid Caliphs refers to the rulers of the Abbasid Empire during a period of transformation that saw changes in their authority and role.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a3cce5ba44faddd8f9fd27e8b4f71bef</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210967</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Islamic World">
      <data key="d0">Islamic World</data>
      <data key="d1">geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">The Islamic World encompasses the regions where Islamic rule was established, including areas ruled by Abbasids and other dynasties.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a3cce5ba44faddd8f9fd27e8b4f71bef</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210967</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Shia Conception of Imamate">
      <data key="d0">Shia Conception of Imamate</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">The Shia Conception of Imamate is a religious concept that challenged the authority of caliphs within the Shia movement and contributed to changes in power dynamics during the Abbasid period.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a3cce5ba44faddd8f9fd27e8b4f71bef</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210967</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Umayyad Period">
      <data key="d0">Umayyad Period</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">The Umayyad Period precedes the Abbasid Period, setting the stage for subsequent changes in Islamic rule and leadership roles.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a3cce5ba44faddd8f9fd27e8b4f71bef</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210967</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hadith Compilation">
      <data key="d0">Hadith Compilation</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Hadith Compilation refers to the process by which oral traditions were written down during the Abbasid period, changing the balance of authority and knowledge within Islam.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a3cce5ba44faddd8f9fd27e8b4f71bef</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
