    </node>
    <node id="Abbasid period">
      <data key="d0">Abbasid period</data>
      <data key="d1">time_period</data>
      <data key="d2">Abbasid period is mentioned as a context for understanding the pact, noting that Islam was still new during this time.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-395768da0b846b0a0ea31c551387d142</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210967</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pact of Umar">
      <data key="d0">Pact of Umar</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">The Pact of Umar is a legal agreement established by Caliph Umar II to outline the rights and obligations of non-Muslims living under Islamic rule, ensuring their safety and freedom to practice their religion.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-395768da0b846b0a0ea31c551387d142</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210967</data>
    </node>
    <node id="non-Muslim communities'">
      <data key="d0">non-Muslim communities'</data>
      <data key="d1">cultural_or_religious_movement</data>
      <data key="d2">Non-Muslims living in predominantly Islamic territories during the Abbasid period maintained their religious identities while adopting certain cultural aspects of Islam and Arab customs.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-395768da0b846b0a0ea31c551387d142</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210967</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Jordan and Egypt">
      <data key="d0">Jordan and Egypt</data>
      <data key="d1">geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">These regions were part of the broader Abbasid Empire during the Abbasid period, where significant cultural exchanges and adaptations occurred.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-395768da0b846b0a0ea31c551387d142</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210967</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Islamic State">
      <data key="d0">Islamic State</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">The Islamic State refers to a historical concept where an empire or state operates under Muslim rule, exercising control and imposing governance according to Islamic law (Sharia). This entity is associated with the Delhi Sultanate, implementing various policies such as alcohol prohibition, land confiscation, and price controls. These measures were part of its administrative approach during its governance over the Delhi Sultanate period.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0f18152fa4fc7ac767c2d8eb0df0af8e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-17ddaf0fb532418dfd517db22df102e9</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210971</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Quran">
      <data key="d0">Quran</data>
      <data key="d1">source_or_artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">The Quran is a religious text central to Islam believed to be revealed by God through the angel Gabriel to Muhammad.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-17ddaf0fb532418dfd517db22df102e9</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210967</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Arab Culture">
      <data key="d0">Arab Culture</data>
      <data key="d1">cultural_or_religious_movement</data>
      <data key="d2">Arab culture refers to the tradition, language, art, science, and religious beliefs especially of the Arab peoples, having a rich heritage in literature and religion including the development of Islam.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-17ddaf0fb532418dfd517db22df102e9</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210967</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mawali">
