    <node id="Indian Ocean Basin">
      <data key="d0">Indian Ocean Basin</data>
      <data key="d1">geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">The Indian Ocean Basin is a major geographical location for early medieval trade and cultural exchange.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ff2834c0656da75835dd726c9d9fbb32</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210967</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Shah Abbasi caravansary">
      <data key="d0">Shah Abbasi caravansary</data>
      <data key="d1">source_or_artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">The Shah Abbasi caravansary is a historical artifact that served as an inn for traders on the Silk Roads, now recognized as a national heritage site in Nishapur, Iran.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ff2834c0656da75835dd726c9d9fbb32</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210967</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Caravansary">
      <data key="d0">Caravansary</data>
      <data key="d1">textbook_structure</data>
      <data key="d2">A Caravansary is an inn that played a crucial role as a stopping point for travelers and traders along ancient routes, particularly significant in the early medieval period. Described in detail in Figure 12.1 of the textbook, these establishments served as key points of exchange in Central Asia, acting both as important parts of the trade network during the early medieval era and providing essential accommodation and services to those traversing these historical pathways.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-76d4974f6a32742560ccbc66f2b65d1b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ff2834c0656da75835dd726c9d9fbb32</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210973</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Early Middle Ages">
      <data key="d0">Early Middle Ages</data>
      <data key="d1">time_period</data>
      <data key="d2">The Early Middle Ages, spanning approximately from the 5th to the late 10th century, was a significant historical period marked by important political and cultural developments in South Asia and China. This era also witnessed the expansion of long-distance trade during the first half of the Middle Ages, which facilitated increased connections across continents and the development of maritime networks centered on the Indian Ocean. These interconnected trends underscored the dynamic transformations taking place worldwide during this time.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-42369c47f2f60a9ab4ac526953bf6b40&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-582dc813b509020dd77ed6ee9db0e981&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ff2834c0656da75835dd726c9d9fbb32</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210974</data>
    </node>
    <node id="East-West Interactions">
      <data key="d0">East-West Interactions</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">East-West Interactions refers to the cultural, economic, and ideological exchanges between Eastern and Western regions during the early medieval period.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ff2834c0656da75835dd726c9d9fbb32</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210969</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Sogdiana">
      <data key="d0">Sogdiana</data>
      <data key="d1">civilization_or_empire</data>
      <data key="d2">Sogdiana was a significant region along the Silk Roads, operating alongside cities such as Samarkand and Panjikent as important trading hubs from the 4th century CE until its period of greatest wealth in central Asia. During the Early Middle Ages, Sogdiana functioned as a border state with notable interactions and influences extending even to territories like Korea and Japan.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e0e7cff15604f2aa13915047df90d715&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ff2834c0656da75835dd726c9d9fbb32</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210974</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Khyber Pass">
      <data key="d0">Khyber Pass</data>
      <data key="d1">geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">Khyber Pass is a geographical location that facilitated the spread of religions like Islam to northern India from Afghanistan.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-42369c47f2f60a9ab4ac526953bf6b40</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210969</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Sui Dynasty">
      <data key="d0">Sui Dynasty</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">The Sui Dynasty, an influential dynasty that preceded the Tang Dynasty, had a significant impact on religious practices such as Buddhism. This dynasty played a crucial role in shaping the religious landscape, setting the groundwork for the broader influence of Buddhism under the subsequent Tang Empire. The context provided by the descriptions emphasizes the historical importance and contributions of the Sui Dynasty to religious developments during this period.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-42369c47f2f60a9ab4ac526953bf6b40&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e2224dbd0185a7c0eadc02caea19f029</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210976</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hinduism">
      <data key="d0">Hinduism</data>
      <data key="d1">cultural_or_religious_movement</data>
      <data key="d2">Hinduism was another significant religious and philosophical tradition that emerged in South Asia before the Middle Ages and continues to be influential even today.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-42369c47f2f60a9ab4ac526953bf6b40</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210971</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ghaznavid Dynasty">
      <data key="d0">Ghaznavid Dynasty</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">"The Ghaznavid Dynasty was a powerful empire that controlled a vast region of central Asia during the 10th and 11th centuries."|</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3940f5bfcfa4bc96e49773142fb8d2a0</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210971</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Muhammad of Ghur">
      <data key="d0">Muhammad of Ghur</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">Muhammad of Ghur was a ruler and military leader who played a pivotal role in expanding Islamic rule into northern India during the late 12th century.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3940f5bfcfa4bc96e49773142fb8d2a0</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210971</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ghaznavid Empire">
      <data key="d0">Ghaznavid Empire</data>
      <data key="d1">civilization_or_empire</data>
      <data key="d2">The Ghaznavid Empire was a major power that dominated central Asia, encompassing regions across modern-day Afghanistan and parts of Pakistan from 977 to 1186, renowned for its governance and military prowess.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3940f5bfcfa4bc96e49773142fb8d2a0</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210971</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Samarkand">
      <data key="d0">Samarkand</data>
      <data key="d1">geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">Samarkand is an ancient city located in modern-day Uzbekistan that has played significant roles during various historical periods, notably serving as Timur's capital and holding importance as a key hub on the Silk Roads. It features notable architectural landmarks such as the Registan and three madrasas. Samarkand also emerged as a pivotal center for trade and cultural exchanges during the Ghaznavid period and along with nearby city-states like Sogdiana and Panjikent, facilitated extensive commercial activities from the 4th century CE through its zenith within the Sogdian sphere.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0e02f2af9c7712235155281a18fd34d7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3940f5bfcfa4bc96e49773142fb8d2a0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e0e7cff15604f2aa13915047df90d715</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210978</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Merv">
      <data key="d0">Merv</data>
      <data key="d1">geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">Merv, now part of Turkmenistan, was a key city along the Silk Roads during the height of the Ghaznavid Empire, playing a crucial role in trade and cultural exchange.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3940f5bfcfa4bc96e49773142fb8d2a0</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210972</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bodhisattvas">
      <data key="d0">Bodhisattvas</data>
      <data key="d1">cultural_or_religious_movement</data>
      <data key="d2">Bodhisattvas are spiritually advanced individuals in Buddhism on the path toward Buddhahood. Maitreya, a twelfth-century depiction in manuscript painting, is a notable bodhisattva.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8029d5dedb01e018d49edf5ba4d5039c</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210972</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Karma">
      <data key="d0">Karma</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">Karma operates under dual incentives: positive accumulation for higher varna and eventual moksha; negative accumulation leading to lower varna reincarnation or even chaos and destruction.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8029d5dedb01e018d49edf5ba4d5039c</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210972</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Samsara">
      <data key="d0">Samsara</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Samsara refers to the continued existence of the soul after death, its transformation, and the cycle of rebirth based on accumulated karma.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8029d5dedb01e018d49edf5ba4d5039c</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210972</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Moksha">
      <data key="d0">Moksha</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">Moksha is the ultimate goal in Hindu philosophy, signifying release from the karmic cycle of rebirth (samsara) and achieving a complete understanding of the world. This concept has had a significant broader cultural impact on Indian society and potentially extends to other cultures influenced by Hinduism.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-76d4974f6a32742560ccbc66f2b65d1b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8029d5dedb01e018d49edf5ba4d5039c</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210976</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Caste System">
      <data key="d0">Caste System</data>
      <data key="d1">cultural_or_religious_movement</data>
      <data key="d2">The caste system creates varna-communities in Indian society with mixed roles, providing social stability but also leading to exclusion based on birth. The rigidity of dharma helps maintain societal balance and prevent chaos.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8029d5dedb01e018d49edf5ba4d5039c</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210973</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Dharma">
      <data key="d0">Dharma</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">Dharma is a crucial moral or religious law in Hinduism that every individual must follow. This concept is essential for maintaining cosmic order and balancing the varna-based social structure. Failure to adhere to Dharma can result in karmic consequences, potentially leading to societal instability and destruction. At its core, Dharma serves as a central principle guiding the way of life in Hinduism, emphasizing cosmic law and duty. This concept is fundamental to the structured organization of Indian civilization, deeply embedding itself in the social, cultural, and religious fabric of society.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-76d4974f6a32742560ccbc66f2b65d1b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8029d5dedb01e018d49edf5ba4d5039c</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210981</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Varna-Based Social Structure">
      <data key="d0">Varna-Based Social Structure</data>
      <data key="d1">geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">The caste system’s varna-communities provide social support but also create hierarchical rigidities, affecting social mobility in Indian society. This system influenced both personal lives and larger social structures.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8029d5dedb01e018d49edf5ba4d5039c</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210974</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Political Instability">
      <data key="d0">Political Instability</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">The political upheaval in India was exacerbated by the impact of foreign invasions and internal conflicts, which the caste system attempted to mitigate through its rigid varna communities.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8029d5dedb01e018d49edf5ba4d5039c</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210974</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Han Empire">
      <data key="d0">Han Empire</data>
      <data key="d1">civilization_or_empire</data>
      <data key="d2">Han Empire refers to an empire that ruled China from 206 BCE to 220 CE, prior to its collapse in 220 CE.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-60db003690e75609ab22675a54f60648</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210974</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Wei, Wu, Shu States">
      <data key="d0">Wei, Wu, Shu States</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">Wei, Wu, and Shu states refer to the three northern Chinese regimes that briefly united parts of China from 220-316 CE after the fall of the Han Empire.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-60db003690e75609ab22675a54f60648</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210974</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Western Jin Dynasty">
      <data key="d0">Western Jin Dynasty</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">The Western Jin dynasty was a Chinese dynastic state that ruled from 265 to 316, temporarily reunifying China.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-60db003690e75609ab22675a54f60648</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210974</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Buddhism along the Silk Road">
      <data key="d0">Buddhism along the Silk Road</data>
      <data key="d1">cultural_or_religious_movement</data>
      <data key="d2">Buddhism along the Silk Road refers to the spread of Buddhism from India to China via trade routes, influencing and being adapted by local cultures.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-60db003690e75609ab22675a54f60648</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210974</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Sui Dynasty (Figure 12.8)">
      <data key="d0">Sui Dynasty (Figure 12.8)</data>
      <data key="d1">time_period</data>
      <data key="d2">Sui Dynasty, which ruled from 581-618 CE, is a significant period in Chinese history marked by empire-building and large-scale public works under Emperor Wen.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-60db003690e75609ab22675a54f60648</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210974</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Grand Canal">
      <data key="d0">Grand Canal</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">The Grand Canal was an extensive waterway system developed during the Sui Dynasty that connected major rivers across China for military and commercial purposes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-60db003690e75609ab22675a54f60648</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210974</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Yang Jian (Wen Emperor)">
      <data key="d0">Yang Jian (Wen Emperor)</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">Yang Jian, known after his reign as Emperor Wen of the Sui dynasty, was a key figure in reunifying China under the Sui empire through political acumen and military prowess.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-60db003690e75609ab22675a54f60648</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210974</data>
