    <node id="&lt;&quot;fifth century BCE&quot;&lt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"fifth century BCE"&lt;</data>
      <data key="d1">time_period"&lt;</data>
      <data key="d2">The fifth century BCE was a period in history that spans from 401 to 301 BCE, characterized by significant political and cultural developments in various regions of the world, including the decline of many Greek city-states and the rise of influential philosophers such as Confucius. This era also saw the emergence of new forms of government and social structures.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-19589951cfafe65a99e4e3c221837fdf</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210974</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Sui dynasty&quot;&lt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Sui dynasty"&lt;</data>
      <data key="d1">civilization_or_empire"&lt;</data>
      <data key="d2">The Sui dynasty was a brief but significant imperial dynasty in Chinese history, spanning from 581 to 618 CE. Famed for its efficient governance, ambitious military campaigns, particularly the unification of China under Emperor Wen and later the expansion efforts of Emperor Yang, and most notably, the construction of the Grand Canal, which linked northern and southern China.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-19589951cfafe65a99e4e3c221837fdf</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210974</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Grand Canal&quot;&lt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Grand Canal"&lt;</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event"&lt;</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;"The Grand Canal is an ancient network of artificial waterways in China that extends from Hangzhou to Luoyang, with branches reaching as far north as Beijing. Constructed initially under the Sui dynasty, it served primarily for transportation and logistics, facilitating the flow of goods and fostering trade between different regions."</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-19589951cfafe65a99e4e3c221837fdf</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210974</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Sui Empire&quot;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Sui Empire"</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;"civilization_or_empire</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;"The Sui Empire (581-618 AD</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c2414b2f38693a948721448090e1894e</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210975</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Tang Empire&quot;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Tang Empire"</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;"civilization_or_empire</data>
      <data key="d2">The Tang Empire was a powerful and prosperous dynasty that ruled over China from 618 to 907 AD. It is renowned for its cultural achievements, political stability, and economic prosperity during this period.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c2414b2f38693a948721448090e1894e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a0a9b6086a054e6f70e1dcba3f5f47af</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210978</data>
    </node>
    <node id="868 CE">
      <data key="d0">868 CE</data>
      <data key="d1">time_period</data>
      <data key="d2">The year 868 marks the creation of the Diamond Sutra, the world’s first block-printed book.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e2224dbd0185a7c0eadc02caea19f029</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210975</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pure Land Buddhism">
      <data key="d0">Pure Land Buddhism</data>
      <data key="d1">cultural_or_religious_movement</data>
      <data key="d2">Pure Land Buddhism is a significant branch of Mahayana Buddhism that focuses on salvation through faith. This practice has spread widely and is recognized as an important form of Buddhist tradition. It emphasizes devotion to Amida Buddha, offering a path to salvation that is accessible to all individuals regardless of their level of scholarly study or understanding of sacred texts.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e2224dbd0185a7c0eadc02caea19f029&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-76d4974f6a32742560ccbc66f2b65d1b</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210979</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Chan Buddhism">
      <data key="d0">Chan Buddhism</data>
      <data key="d1">cultural_or_religious_movement</data>
      <data key="d2">Chan Buddhism, known in Japanese as Zen, focuses on meditation practices and the example set by Chan masters.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e2224dbd0185a7c0eadc02caea19f029</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210975</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tang Empire">
      <data key="d0">Tang Empire</data>
      <data key="d1">civilization_or_empire</data>
      <data key="d2">The Tang Empire, a powerful and influential entity in Chinese history, primarily focused on its golden age during the zenith of the Tang Dynasty, which was subsequently followed by periods of decline due to various internal and external challenges. These challenges were significantly influenced by religious movements both within and outside the empire, with the Tang rulers implementing notable policies that particularly targeted Buddhism. These internal and external religious factors played a crucial role in shaping the dynasty's transition from its height to its eventual downward spiral.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e2224dbd0185a7c0eadc02caea19f029&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c7fc526d4d7b88eb3c5457b58cd983ec</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210981</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tang period (8th-10th centuries)">
      <data key="d0">Tang period (8th-10th centuries)</data>
      <data key="d1">time_period</data>
      <data key="d2">This period saw the rapid growth of Pure Land and Chan Buddhism in China, leading to a significant transformation in religious practices and cultural influence.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e2224dbd0185a7c0eadc02caea19f029</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210976</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Emperor Wuzong">
      <data key="d0">Emperor Wuzong</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">Under Emperor Wuzong's reign from 841-847 CE, policies led to the destruction of Buddhist temples and monasteries.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e2224dbd0185a7c0eadc02caea19f029</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210976</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Buddhism under Tang Empire">
      <data key="d0">Buddhism under Tang Empire</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">The development of Buddhism during the Tang period marked a significant phase in its spread within China and beyond.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e2224dbd0185a7c0eadc02caea19f029</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210976</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Tang period&quot;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Tang period"</data>
      <data key="d1">time_period</data>
      <data key="d2">"The Tang period (618-907 AD) was a golden age of Chinese history, marked by political stability, economic prosperity, and cultural flourishing. It is known for its advancements in poetry, art, technology, and philosophy, as well as the spread of Buddhism and other religions."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a0a9b6086a054e6f70e1dcba3f5f47af</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210976</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;China&quot;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"China"</data>
      <data key="d1">geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">"China, a vast nation with rich historical and cultural heritage, was the center of the Tang Empire during the 7th to 10th centuries. The empire contributed significantly to the spread of its traditions through trade routes such as the Silk Road."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a0a9b6086a054e6f70e1dcba3f5f47af</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210976</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Civil service exam&quot;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Civil service exam"</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">"The civil service exam system during the Tang period was an important method of selecting government officials based on merit. It tested candidates' knowledge of literature, classics, and administration skills."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a0a9b6086a054e6f70e1dcba3f5f47af</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210976</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Emperor Wuzong&quot;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Emperor Wuzong"</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">"Emperor Wuzong of the Tang dynasty (reigned 840-846 AD) was known for his campaign against Buddhism and efforts to revive Confucianism. He sought to strengthen the state by reducing Buddhist influence."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a0a9b6086a054e6f70e1dcba3f5f47af</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210976</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Emperor Gaozong&quot;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Emperor Gaozong"</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">"Emperor Gaozong of the Tang dynasty (reigned 649-683 AD) was a key figure in early Tang history, known for his patronage of Confucianism and legal reforms."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a0a9b6086a054e6f70e1dcba3f5f47af</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210977</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Wu Zetian&quot;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Wu Zetian"</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">"Wu Zetian (624-705 AD), empress dowager and later Emperor of the Tang dynasty from 690 to 705 AD, was a prominent figure who rose to power through her intelligence and political acumen. She is known for declaring Buddhism as the state religion and founding the Wu Zhou dynasty."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a0a9b6086a054e6f70e1dcba3f5f47af</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210977</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Empress Wei&quot;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Empress Wei"</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">"Empress Wēi (died 653 AD) was a prominent figure in the Tang court, she had significant influence over Emperor Gaozong. She played a crucial role in shaping early Tang policies and her daughter became Wu Zetian."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a0a9b6086a054e6f70e1dcba3f5f47af</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210977</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Buddhism, Daoism, Confucianism&quot;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Buddhism, Daoism, Confucianism"</data>
      <data key="d1">cultural_or_religious_movement</data>
      <data key="d2">"During the Tang period, Buddhism, Daoism, and Neo-Confucianism were influential religious and philosophical movements. They played a significant role in shaping the cultural and societal norms of the time."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a0a9b6086a054e6f70e1dcba3f5f47af</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210977</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Figure 12.12 Emperor Wuzong&quot;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Figure 12.12 Emperor Wuzong"</data>
      <data key="d1">source_or_artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">"Figure 12.12 showcases Emperor Wuzong, who was involved in the revival of Confucian teachings and efforts to limit Buddhist influence during his reign."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a0a9b6086a054e6f70e1dcba3f5f47af</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210977</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;poetry period (7-9th century)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"poetry period (7-9th century)"</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">"The poetry period during the Tang dynasty is renowned for its rich literary output, featuring poets such as Wang Wei, Li Bai, and Du Fu who shaped subsequent Chinese literature with their works."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a0a9b6086a054e6f70e1dcba3f5f47af</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210977</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;poets: Wang Wei, Li Bai (Li Bo), Du Fu&quot;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"poets: Wang Wei, Li Bai (Li Bo), Du Fu"</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">"Wang Wei, Li Bai, and Du Fu were renowned Tang dynasty poets whose work reflected the influences of Buddhism, Daoism, and Confucianism. They played a significant role in shaping poetic traditions."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a0a9b6086a054e6f70e1dcba3f5f47af</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210977</data>
    </node>
    <node id="early eighth century">
      <data key="d0">early eighth century</data>
      <data key="d1">time_period</data>
      <data key="d2">"During this period, the Tang Empire reached its zenith, but also began facing internal and external challenges that would later lead to its decline."|</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c7fc526d4d7b88eb3c5457b58cd983ec</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210977</data>
    </node>
    <node id="central Asia">
      <data key="d0">central Asia</data>
      <data key="d1">geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">Central Asia played a significant role in historical events influencing both the decline of the Tang Empire, as evidenced by its defeat at the Battle of Talas River against Turkic and Arab forces, and in the strategic military objectives of Timur, who targeted Central Asia for his control during his campaigns.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c7fc526d4d7b88eb3c5457b58cd983ec&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2c0fbb619a916ac387c46ee41fe1af47</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210981</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Islam spread">
      <data key="d0">Islam spread</data>
      <data key="d1">cultural_or_religious_movement</data>
      <data key="d2">"The spread of Islam is mentioned as one of the cultural interactions facilitated through the Silk Roads, alongside trade and technology exchange."|</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c7fc526d4d7b88eb3c5457b58cd983ec</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210977</data>
    </node>
    <node id="An Lushan rebellion">
      <data key="d0">An Lushan rebellion</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">"The An Lushan rebellion significantly weakened the Tang Empire, starting in 755 and lasting for eight years."|</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c7fc526d4d7b88eb3c5457b58cd983ec</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210977</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Battle of Talas River">
      <data key="d0">Battle of Talas River</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">The Battle of Talas River took place in 751 AD on the Talas River between Tang forces from China and coalition armies consisting of Turkic and Arab forces, with a significant presence from both Abbasid and Umayyad factions. This engagement is regarded as a pivotal military defeat for the Tang Empire, contributing to its terminal decline.

The battle marked a critical turning point that facilitated Islamic expansion into Central Asia, ultimately solidifying the Abbasids' control over extensive territories previously dominated by the Umayyads. The conflict thus serves as a landmark event in both Chinese and Middle Eastern history, reflecting the broader geopolitical landscape of the late 8th century AD.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-68886ae6bf0868f51bdd288cf9f2091f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c7fc526d4d7b88eb3c5457b58cd983ec&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c28e7cd00f07a73fa79b2c59e74b6ef9</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210984</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Later Middle Ages">
      <data key="d0">Later Middle Ages</data>
      <data key="d1">time_period</data>
      <data key="d2">Later Middle Ages includes the period of trade developments after the early period mentioned above.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-582dc813b509020dd77ed6ee9db0e981</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210977</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Six Dynasties (220–589 CE)">
      <data key="d0">Six Dynasties (220–589 CE)</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">Six Dynasties is the period when Buddhism arrived in China during this time.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-582dc813b509020dd77ed6ee9db0e981</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210977</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Silk Roads Trade Network">
      <data key="d0">Silk Roads Trade Network</data>
      <data key="d1">textbook_structure</data>
      <data key="d2">This term refers to the interconnected roads that facilitated both trade and cultural exchange during the Middle Ages.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-582dc813b509020dd77ed6ee9db0e981</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210977</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bactria">
      <data key="d0">Bactria</data>
      <data key="d1">geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">Bactria, located in eastern regions, was another market city connected by the Silk Roads.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-582dc813b509020dd77ed6ee9db0e981</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210977</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Yungang Grottoes">
      <data key="d0">Yungang Grottoes</data>
      <data key="d1">source_or_artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">Yungang Grottoes represents a key stop along the Silk Roads, featuring Buddha statues that signify religious and cultural diffusion.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-582dc813b509020dd77ed6ee9db0e981</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210977</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;Bamboo Strips&quot;|">
      <data key="d0">"Bamboo Strips"|</data>
      <data key="d1">source_or_artifact"|</data>
      <data key="d2">Fibers from bamboo strips were used for writing before paper was invented.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-deb2934cdba0dce973f283674d9d1d27</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210977</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Papermaking|">
      <data key="d0">Papermaking|</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event"|</data>
      <data key="d2">The process of converting raw materials into usable paper, perfected in China during the second century CE.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-deb2934cdba0dce973f283674d9d1d27</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210977</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mulberry tree bark|">
      <data key="d0">Mulberry tree bark|</data>
      <data key="d1">source_or_artifact"|</data>
      <data key="d2">Fibers from mulberry tree bark were pounded into pulp and spread to dry as sheets to make paper.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-deb2934cdba0dce973f283674d9d1d27</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210977</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hemp rags and fishing nets|">
      <data key="d0">Hemp rags and fishing nets|</data>
      <data key="d1">source_or_artifact"|</data>
      <data key="d2">These materials added strength to the paper, creating parchment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-deb2934cdba0dce973f283674d9d1d27</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210977</data>
    </node>
    <node id="751 Battle of Talas River|">
      <data key="d0">751 Battle of Talas River|</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_event"|</data>
      <data key="d2">An event where Chinese prisoners were taken to Samarkand and Khurasan, leading to the transfer of papermaking knowledge.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-deb2934cdba0dce973f283674d9d1d27</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210977</data>
    </node>
    <node id="First paper mill in Abbasid Caliphate (794–795)|">
      <data key="d0">First paper mill in Abbasid Caliphate (794–795)|</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event"|</data>
      <data key="d2">The first known paper mill was established during this period.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-deb2934cdba0dce973f283674d9d1d27</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210977</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Spanish production of paper by tenth century|">
      <data key="d0">Spanish production of paper by tenth century|</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event"|</data>
      <data key="d2">By the tenth century, papermaking techniques had spread to Spain and began being produced locally.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-deb2934cdba0dce973f283674d9d1d27</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210977</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;Silk Roads&quot;|">
      <data key="d0">"Silk Roads"|</data>
      <data key="d1">trade_network"|</data>
      <data key="d2">Trade routes that facilitated the exchange of goods, ideas, and technologies between different regions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-deb2934cdba0dce973f283674d9d1d27</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210977</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Abbasid dynasty">
      <data key="d0">Abbasid dynasty</data>
      <data key="d1">civilization_or_empire</data>
      <data key="d2">The Abbasid dynasty was a Arab Muslim family that ruled the Islamic Caliphate from 750 to 1258. The capital of their empire was Baghdad, which served as the center for trade in the Indian Ocean and Persian Gulf during this period.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c1dd00b0ec1039c40b565a8910073d14</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210977</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Chola Architecture of the Golden Age">
      <data key="d0">Chola Architecture of the Golden Age</data>
      <data key="d1">source_or_artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">This Hindu temple represents the stepped-pyramidal shape characteristic of Chola architecture during its golden age.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-612cbc64f4b3c4c91b0c0f131b3b9fe1</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210978</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;civilization_or_empire&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;civilization_or_empire&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">civilization_or_empire</data>
      <data key="d2">The Kingdom_of_Aksum was an ancient civilization centered in present-day Ethiopia and Eritrea, influential during the 1st to the 8th centuries AD for its significant role in trade and cultural exchanges. It is renowned for its advanced political structure. The Mongol Empire under Chinggis Khan was a vast political entity founded by Chinggis Khan in the 13th century that expanded across much of Eurasia, marking a period of extensive territorial conquest and influence. Both entities were pivotal in their respective eras through their roles in political organization and cultural or territorial expansion.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b8ad3f8265df80ff0cbd95a7914a6123&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-89576c0d4225b9ce6218c684fd4f58b7</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210985</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;King Ezna&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;King Ezna&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;King Ezna was a significant ruler in Aksum who expanded Aksumite control to its greatest extent through military conquests south of Egypt and north of Ethiopia, and converted the kingdom to Christianity.&gt;"</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6ea12f4a3376daf41b860db682d4f0a3</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210978</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Red Sea trade routes&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Red Sea trade routes&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">historic_trade_route</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;Red Sea trade routes were crucial during the 4th century CE for the Kingdom of Aksum to control trade, including goods like gold, silver, ivory, and spices.&gt;</data>
