    <node id="Sasanid Empire">
      <data key="d0">Sasanid Empire</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">The Sasanid Empire is an historical event and a civilization that existed before another group came to power.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c01c8cf58623d713a64f359bd7fee42a</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210981</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Post-Roman West">
      <data key="d0">Post-Roman West</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">The Post-Roman West refers to the period following the decline of Roman power and authority in Western Europe, during which the political landscape fragmented and gave rise to new socio-political structures, including feudalism. This transitional era witnessed a significant shift away from the centralized governance of the Roman Empire, characterized by the emergence of various local powers and the eventual establishment of feudal systems that would shape medieval European societies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0cff5e2853da981c04b0b2b4aa611137&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ba4f5d4caaa0f599fff45f792b8e6178&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f0c63666ae15748bbc6f819be670c287</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210984</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Crusading Movement">
      <data key="d0">Crusading Movement</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">The Crusading Movement represents the collective effort and goals of various groups during the Crusades, comprising a series of religiously justified military expeditions that originated in Western Europe with the primary purpose of capturing Jerusalem. This movement began around a specific historical timeline, marking a significant period in medieval European history characterized by widespread religious motivation and coordinated action among diverse factions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0cff5e2853da981c04b0b2b4aa611137&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b7df43c8d0f05694f4eca13a6b562ce4</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210987</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Saint Radegonde">
      <data key="d0">Saint Radegonde</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">Saint Radegonde is depicted as a figure within the context of the Post-Roman West and the crusading movement.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0cff5e2853da981c04b0b2b4aa611137</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210981</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Charlemagne">
      <data key="d0">Charlemagne</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">Charlemagne was a significant ruler during the medieval period in Europe, known for his complex relationship with the papacy. His reign exemplified the use of religious legitimization as a strategic tool to promote political integration and consolidate power. The crowning and anointing ceremonies during his reign underscored the intricate dynamics between rulers and religious authorities, highlighting the mutual reliance and interplay between secular and spiritual leadership in medieval Europe.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0cff5e2853da981c04b0b2b4aa611137&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e4ed21ba18d39c10570d17123a56560a</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210987</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Sigismund">
      <data key="d0">Sigismund</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">Imperial Sigismund, as mentioned here, is part of the sequence depicting historical rulers and figures in relation to the post-Roman West and crusades.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0cff5e2853da981c04b0b2b4aa611137</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210981</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cluny III">
      <data key="d0">Cluny III</data>
      <data key="d1">source_or_artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">Consécration de Cluny III par Urbain II” by Bibliothèque Nationale de France/Wikimedia Commons, Public Domain represents an important religious artifact central to the context of the timeline.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0cff5e2853da981c04b0b2b4aa611137</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210981</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Peter the Hermit Preaching the First Crusade">
      <data key="d0">Peter the Hermit Preaching the First Crusade</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">The event where Peter the Hermit preached for the first crusade is marked on this timeline.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0cff5e2853da981c04b0b2b4aa611137</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210981</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Post-Roman Western Eurasia">
      <data key="d0">Post-Roman Western Eurasia</data>
      <data key="d1">geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">Post-Roman Western Eurasia refers to the region that experienced significant political and cultural changes following the decline of Roman authority in the western Mediterranean around 500 CE.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-774c1aebb34d8cee02655e76bf182dd9</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210981</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Successor Kingdoms">
      <data key="d0">Successor Kingdoms</data>
      <data key="d1">civilization_or_empire</data>
      <data key="d2">Successor kingdoms were the Germanic states established after the fall of the Western Roman Empire, replacing its political authority in large parts of Europe.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-774c1aebb34d8cee02655e76bf182dd9</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210981</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Acculturation">
      <data key="d0">Acculturation</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Acculturation is the process by which a group adopts elements from another culture, leading to blending and eventual social change.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-774c1aebb34d8cee02655e76bf182dd9</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210981</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Christianity">
      <data key="d0">Christianity</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">Christianity played a significant role in reducing cultural differences between Roman populations and the Germanic invaders through conversion efforts.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-774c1aebb34d8cee02655e76bf182dd9</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210981</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Franks,Ostrogoths,Visigoths">
      <data key="d0">Franks,Ostrogoths,Visigoths</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_figure,civilization_or_empire</data>
      <data key="d2">The Franks, Ostrogoths, and Visigoths are specific Germanic tribes that established successor kingdoms after the fall of the Western Roman Empire.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-774c1aebb34d8cee02655e76bf182dd9</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210981</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Huns,Avars">
      <data key="d0">Huns,Avars</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">Huns and Avars were non-Germanic tribes that invaded the Roman Empire but did not speak a Germanic language, showing the fluidity of ethnic identity during this period.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-774c1aebb34d8cee02655e76bf182dd9</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210981</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Oral Culture">
      <data key="d0">Oral Culture</data>
      <data key="d1">concept</data>
      <data key="d2">Oral culture refers to the tradition of transmitting information and traditions through spoken word prior to widespread literacy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-774c1aebb34d8cee02655e76bf182dd9</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210982</data>
    </node>
    <node id="SongofHildebrand,SongoftheNibelungs">
      <data key="d0">SongofHildebrand,SongoftheNibelungs</data>
      <data key="d1">source_or_artifact,textbook_structure</data>
      <data key="d2">The Song of Hildebrand and the Song of the Nibelungs are epic poems that reflect oral traditions before being recorded in writing, representing cultural narratives passed down through generations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-774c1aebb34d8cee02655e76bf182dd9</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210982</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Post-Roman Period">
      <data key="d0">Post-Roman Period</data>
      <data key="d1">time_period</data>
      <data key="d2">The Post-Roman period refers to the era following the decline and fall of the Western Roman Empire around 500 CE. During this time, significant political, social, and cultural changes defined the region as various successor states emerged and interactions among different groups increased.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-774c1aebb34d8cee02655e76bf182dd9</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210982</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Franks">
      <data key="d0">Franks</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">The Franks were Germanic tribes that established a powerful kingdom in the post-Roman period. They played a significant role in European history, contributing to the shaping of the Middle Ages through their culture and institutions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-774c1aebb34d8cee02655e76bf182dd9</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210982</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Western Roman Empire">
      <data key="d0">Western Roman Empire</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">The Western Roman Empire, which officially fell in 476 CE, was the context from which the Post-Roman period emerged. Its decline left a vacuum filled by various successor states and the complex interplay of political entities.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-774c1aebb34d8cee02655e76bf182dd9</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210982</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Literacy">
      <data key="d0">Literacy</data>
      <data key="d1">cultural_or_religious_movement</data>
      <data key="d2">The limited presence of literacy during the Post-Roman period influenced how information and knowledge were preserved and transmitted. The lack thereof prompted greater reliance on oral culture for maintaining traditions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-774c1aebb34d8cee02655e76bf182dd9</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210982</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Historical Accounts">
      <data key="d0">Historical Accounts</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_or_literary_works</data>
      <data key="d2">Through various historical accounts, the Post-Roman period has been chronicled, providing insights into political dynamics and social changes during this transformative era.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-774c1aebb34d8cee02655e76bf182dd9</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210982</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Anglo-Saxon_settlements_and_literature&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Anglo-Saxon_settlements_and_literature&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">civilization_or_empire</data>
      <data key="d2">Settled by Germanic peoples in the British Isles from the late 5th to early 11th centuries AD, known for their literature and riddles.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c72ef5521fa6a1751f4ea6b0d2ef51af</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210982</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Clovis period">
      <data key="d0">Clovis period</data>
      <data key="d1">time_period</data>
      <data key="d2">Clovis period refers to the leadership of Clovis and his reign as a key figure in early Frankish history.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2419060d66509b1cd45b21e948345d70</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210982</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Merovingian rulers">
      <data key="d0">Merovingian rulers</data>
      <data key="d1">civilization_or_empire</data>
      <data key="d2">Merovingian rulers represent the dynasty following Clovis, known for internal conflicts and ineffective succession.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2419060d66509b1cd45b21e948345d70</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210982</data>
    </node>
    <node id="partible inheritance">
      <data key="d0">partible inheritance</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">Partible inheritance is a legal practice where each son receives an equal share of his father’s estate, leading to reduced land size over generations unless new conquests are made.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2419060d66509b1cd45b21e948345d70</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210982</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Carolingian dynasty">
      <data key="d0">Carolingian dynasty</data>
      <data key="d1">civilization_or_empire</data>
      <data key="d2">The Carolingian dynasty took control of the Frankish kingdom after the Merovingians and became key figures in shaping medieval Western Europe.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2419060d66509b1cd45b21e948345d70</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210982</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pépin le Bref">
      <data key="d0">Pépin le Bref</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">Pépin le Bref, or Pippin the Short, was a significant Carolingian ruler who helped establish the Carolingian dynasty as the dominant power in the Frankish kingdom.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2419060d66509b1cd45b21e948345d70</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210982</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Donation of Pepin">
      <data key="d0">Donation of Pepin</data>
      <data key="d1">source_or_artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">The Donation of Pepin is a historical document that granted land to the Pope and provided the legal basis for the Papal States, marking an important religious-political alliance between the Carolingians and the papacy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2419060d66509b1cd45b21e948345d70</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210982</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Papal States">
      <data key="d0">Papal States</data>
      <data key="d1">geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">Papal States refer to the territories controlled by the Catholic Church during medieval times, established through the Donation of Pepin.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2419060d66509b1cd45b21e948345d70</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210982</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Einhard&quot;&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Einhard"&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;historical_figure&gt;</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;"Einhard was a close associate of Charlemagne and served in his administration. He is best known for writing 'Vita Karoli Magni' (The Life of CharlestheGreat), which provides insight into the life and reign of Charlemagne, often praising him while excusing some of his faults."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0770e5b4119dd765a7b5288439c14adb</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210982</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Vita Karoli Magni&quot;&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Vita Karoli Magni"&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;source_or_artifact&gt;</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;"TheLifeofCharlestheGreat is a biographical work written by Einhard. It provides detailed information on Charlemagne's life and reign, emphasizing his achievements and the impact of his rule."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0770e5b4119dd765a7b5288439c14adb</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210982</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Charlemagne&quot;&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Charlemagne"&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;historical_figure&gt;</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;"Charlemagne was the King of the Franks and later became the first Holy Roman Emperor. He ruled from 768 to 814 and is known for his extensive conquests, administrative reforms, revival of learning, and the strengthening of church influence."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0770e5b4119dd765a7b5288439c14adb</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210982</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Pepin the Short&quot;&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Pepin the Short"&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;historical_figure&gt;</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;"Pepin the Short was the King of the Franks from 751 to 768. He was Charles Martel's son, and Charlemagne's father. His rule laid the groundwork for Charlemagne's later expansion."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0770e5b4119dd765a7b5288439c14adb</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210982</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Pope Leo III&quot;&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Pope Leo III"&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;historical_figure&gt;</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;"Pope Leo III crowned Charlemagne as Emperor of the Romans in 800 AD at St. Peter's Basilica, Rome. This event signified Charlemagne’s authority and influence over much of Europe."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0770e5b4119dd765a7b5288439c14adb</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210982</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Byzantine rulers&quot;&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Byzantine rulers"&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;historical_figure&gt;</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;"The Byzantine Empire was a significant power in the east during Charlemagne's reign. Their relationship with Charlemagne was often tense, particularly regarding his imperial coronation and territorial ambitions."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0770e5b4119dd765a7b5288439c14adb</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210982</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Abbasid Caliphate&quot;&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Abbasid Caliphate"&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;civilization_or_empire&gt;</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;"The Abbasid Caliphate ruled much of the Middle East and Central Asia during Charlemagne's time. Its ruler Harun al-Rashid sent diplomatic gifts, such as an elephant, to Charlemagne."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0770e5b4119dd765a7b5288439c14adb</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210982</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Harun al-Rashid&quot;&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Harun al-Rashid"&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;historical_figure&gt;</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;"Harun al-Rashid was the Abbasid Caliph and is known for sending a symbolic gift (an elephant) to Charlemagne, reflecting ties between the East and West."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0770e5b4119dd765a7b5288439c14adb</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210982</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Alfonso II King of Galicia and Asturias&quot;&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Alfonso II King of Galicia and Asturias"&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;historical_figure&gt;</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;"Alfonso II was a figure in Iberian history during the same period as Charlemagne. His relationship with Charlemagne is evidenced by correspondence related to their interactions."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0770e5b4119dd765a7b5288439c14adb</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210982</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Han dynasty&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Han dynasty&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_period</data>
      <data key="d2">Founded by Liu Bang in 202 BCE, the Han dynasty succeeded the Qin dynasty and is divided into the Western and Eastern Han periods. Known for promoting Confucianism, economic stability, and significant cultural achievements.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9e0565ea301887f653d4d7d463873cc0</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210982</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;img-0&gt;[Chu Ci]">
      <data key="d0">&lt;img-0&gt;[Chu Ci]</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;cultural_or_religious_movement&gt;</data>
      <data key="d2">Chu Ci is a collection of poems and songs from the State of Chu during the Warring States period. It reflects the cultural, social, and political conditions of the time and includes works by well-known poets like Qu Yuan.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-86ee84211a107644a48bbdb3505e9a8d</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210982</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;img-0&gt;[Qu Yuan]">
      <data key="d0">&lt;img-0&gt;[Qu Yuan]</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;historical_figure&gt;</data>
      <data key="d2">Qu Yuan was a Chinese poet and statesman from the State of Chu during the Warring States period. He is best known for his work "Li Sao," a lengthy poem included in the "Chu Ci" collection, which explores themes of loyalty and despair.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-86ee84211a107644a48bbdb3505e9a8d</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210982</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;img-0&gt;[Warring States period]">
      <data key="d0">&lt;img-0&gt;[Warring States period]</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;time_period&gt;</data>
      <data key="d2">The Warring States period was an era during the late Zhou dynasty (771–256 BCE) when various states in ancient China vied for dominance. It is known for political intrigue, militaristic advancements, and flourishing of culture and thought.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-86ee84211a107644a48bbdb3505e9a8d</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210982</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;img-0&gt;[State of Chu]">
      <data key="d0">&lt;img-0&gt;[State of Chu]</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;geographical_location&gt;</data>
      <data key="d2">The State of Chu was one of the major states during the Warring States period in East Asia. It stretched from the Yangtze River basin to Shandong Peninsula and was known for its strong military, cultural innovations, and significant role in early Chinese history.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-86ee84211a107644a48bbdb3505e9a8d</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210983</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Serfs">
      <data key="d0">Serfs</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">Serfs were unfree, servile laborers tied to the land who provided labor and goods for their lord, and occupied a low social status.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-997b1992dbcfe1fe016997656821d078</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210983</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Lord">
      <data key="d0">Lord</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">Lords were required to protect serfs, resolve disputes, and administer work, receiving service days from serfs annually.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-997b1992dbcfe1fe016997656821d078</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210983</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Manorialism/Mansion System">
      <data key="d0">Manorialism/Mansion System</data>
      <data key="d1">cultural_or_religious_movement</data>
      <data key="d2">Manorialism is a system where serfs are grouped into villages around the lord's manor house to cooperatively tend land and provide labor services.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-997b1992dbcfe1fe016997656821d078</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210983</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Clergy">
      <data key="d0">Clergy</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">Clergy were members of the church, separate from serf society, often in positions of spiritual authority and service.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-997b1992dbcfe1fe016997656821d078</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210983</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Tithe">
      <data key="d0">Tithe</data>
      <data key="d1">textbook_structure</data>
      <data key="d2">Tithe is a mandatory tax of usually 10 percent of produce paid by serfs to lords or churches for services and legitimacy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-997b1992dbcfe1fe016997656821d078</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210983</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Rural Society">
      <data key="d0">Rural Society</data>
      <data key="d1">geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">Rural society characterized much of Western Europe, with people producing goods primarily at the subsistence level and engaging in cooperative labor under manorialism.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-997b1992dbcfe1fe016997656821d078</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210983</data>
    </node>
    <node id="World War II">
      <data key="d0">World War II</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">Worldwide military conflict involving many nations in the 1940s. Fought between the Axis powers (Nazi Germany, Fascist Italy, and Japan) and the Allies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2a458530cae95d6afec55adc910e949a</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210983</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Figure 13.9 Queen Radegund">
      <data key="d0">Figure 13.9 Queen Radegund</data>
      <data key="d1">source_or_artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">This illustration depicts Queen Radegund of the Franks, who later became a founder of a monastery. She is shown seated with a book, symbolizing her learning and piety. The artifact highlights the role of religious women in the medieval period.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e4ed21ba18d39c10570d17123a56560a</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210983</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Queen Radegund">
      <data key="d0">Queen Radegund</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">Radegund was the queen of the Franks who later founded a monastery, demonstrating her influence as a religious leader through her education and piety. She exemplifies the role of influential women in the medieval period.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e4ed21ba18d39c10570d17123a56560a</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210983</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Monastic communities">
      <data key="d0">Monastic communities</data>
      <data key="d1">cultural_or_religious_movement</data>
      <data key="d2">Monasteries were critical centers for the preservation of knowledge and education in the post-Roman world, functioning as refuges, places of administration, and nodes in communication networks. They played a significant role in training priests and serving newly converted populations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e4ed21ba18d39c10570d17123a56560a</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210983</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Preservation of learning">
      <data key="d0">Preservation of learning</data>
      <data key="d1">cultural_or_religious_movement</data>
      <data key="d2">("Monks copied ancient texts to preserve knowledge for future generations, contributing significantly to the continuation of classical learning during a time of upheaval.")</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e4ed21ba18d39c10570d17123a56560a</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210983</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Conversion to Christianity">
      <data key="d0">Conversion to Christianity</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">("The conversion of Germanic kings like Charlemagne to Catholic Christianity helped integrate them into their Roman populations, while also providing bishops with administrative roles within the new social hierarchies. Monasteries were central in educating and preserving Christian culture."</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e4ed21ba18d39c10570d17123a56560a</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210983</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Jewish communities">
      <data key="d0">Jewish communities</data>
      <data key="d1">cultural_or_religious_movement</data>
      <data key="d2">("Judaism existed as a non-Christian religion during this period but faced restrictions from both clergy and kings, leading to challenges such as forced conversions or expulsions. Their position was often precarious due to Christian beliefs and anti-Semitic rhetoric."</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e4ed21ba18d39c10570d17123a56560a</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210983</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Roman Empire collapse">
      <data key="d0">Roman Empire collapse</data>
      <data key="d1">time_period</data>
      <data key="d2">The fall of Rome marked a significant transition in the political landscape and allowed for the rise of new religious and cultural movements, including Christianity's increasing dominance over previously established ideologies. Monasteries became crucial institutions during this period.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e4ed21ba18d39c10570d17123a56560a</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210983</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Papacy authority">
      <data key="d0">Papacy authority</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">("The power of bishops, especially the Pope, was undisputed in matters such as crowning monarchs and performing anointing ceremonies. The relationship between rulers like Charlemagne and papal authorities reflected broader themes of religious legitimacy."</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e4ed21ba18d39c10570d17123a56560a</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210983</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Visigoth Rulers">
      <data key="d0">Visigoth Rulers</data>
      <data key="d1">civilization_or_empire</data>
      <data key="d2">The Visigoth Rulers attempted to create written law codes like the Ostrogoths, but they maintained an uneasy relationship with their Hispano-Roman subjects and eventually converted to Catholicism under King Recared.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-981b9ba171235c0eda85615bee2f8630</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210983</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Theodoric">
      <data key="d0">Theodoric</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">Theodoric is compared to Visigoth Rulers for his successful integration with Roman institutions while the Visigoths remained apart from their Hispano-Roman subjects.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-981b9ba171235c0eda85615bee2f8630</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210983</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Clovis">
      <data key="d0">Clovis</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">Clovis, like Theodoric, successfully integrated into Roman institutions but contrasts with the isolationist stance of the Visigoth Rulers towards Hispano-Romans.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-981b9ba171235c0eda85615bee2f8630</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210983</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Visigoths (Arian Christians)">
      <data key="d0">Visigoths (Arian Christians)</data>
      <data key="d1">civilization_or_empire</data>
      <data key="d2">The Visigoths were Arian Christians who tolerated non-Arian subjects and converted to Catholicism under King Recared for better integration with the population.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-981b9ba171235c0eda85615bee2f8630</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210983</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Recared (King of Visigoths)">
      <data key="d0">Recared (King of Visigoths)</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">King Recared converted to Catholicism and gained support from the church, facilitating stronger ties with the population after tolerating non-Arian subjects.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-981b9ba171235c0eda85615bee2f8630</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210983</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Umayyad Caliphate">
      <data key="d0">Umayyad Caliphate</data>
      <data key="d1">civilization_or_empire</data>
      <data key="d2">The Umayyad Caliphate's armies invaded Spain in 711 but did not control the entire peninsula, just like Visigoths failed to fully control it.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-981b9ba171235c0eda85615bee2f8630</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210983</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hispano-Roman Subjects">
      <data key="d0">Hispano-Roman Subjects</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">Refers to people of Roman descent who lived under Visigothic rule during the period and their complex relationship with Visigoth rulers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-981b9ba171235c0eda85615bee2f8630</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210983</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Non-Arian Subjects">
      <data key="d0">Non-Arian Subjects</data>
      <data key="d1">cultural_or_religious_movement</data>
      <data key="d2">These were subjects who did not follow Arian Christianity, living under Visigothic rule before the conversion to Catholicism by King Recared.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-981b9ba171235c0eda85615bee2f8630</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210983</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Abbasid Dynasty">
      <data key="d0">Abbasid Dynasty</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">The Abbasid dynasty overthrew the Umayyads in a significant political event that led Abd al-Rahman to flee and set up his own rule in Al-Andalus.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-981b9ba171235c0eda85615bee2f8630</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210983</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Abd al-Rahman I">
      <data key="d0">Abd al-Rahman I</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">Abd al-Rahman I fled from Syria after his family's destruction and used discontent among non-Arab soldiers to form alliances and establish himself as the ruler of Al-Andalus in 756 AD.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-981b9ba171235c0eda85615bee2f8630</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210983</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Al-Andalus">
      <data key="d0">Al-Andalus</data>
      <data key="d1">geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">Al-Andalus refers to the territories occupied during the period of Muslim rule in the Iberian Peninsula, primarily between the 8th and early 13th centuries. Specifically, it denotes the region governed by Umayyad offshoots who fled from Syria after their downfall, where Islamic law was established and trade expanded with other regions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bcb575f31b05c6035003e7300c15bfb5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-981b9ba171235c0eda85615bee2f8630</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210988</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cordoba">
      <data key="d0">Cordoba</data>
      <data key="d1">geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">The city of Cordoba became the capital of Al-Andalus under Abd al-Rahman I and grew into a center for commerce, learning, and arts in early medieval times.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-981b9ba171235c0eda85615bee2f8630</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210984</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Figure 13.10 (Map or Image)">
      <data key="d0">Figure 13.10 (Map or Image)</data>
      <data key="d1">source_or_artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">This refers to an image or map mentioned in the text that depicts the cities and regions of Al-Andalus during this period, likely illustrating its extent and significance.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-981b9ba171235c0eda85615bee2f8630</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210984</data>
    </node>
    <node id="King Recared (Visigoth King)">
      <data key="d0">King Recared (Visigoth King)</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">King Recared converted to Catholicism and gained support from the church, facilitating stronger ties with the population after tolerating non-Arian subjects.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-981b9ba171235c0eda85615bee2f8630</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210984</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Umayyad Rule">
      <data key="d0">Umayyad Rule</data>
      <data key="d1">textbook_structure</data>
      <data key="d2">The Umayyads' rule, which followed after the Visigoths in the Iberian Peninsula, marked a significant transition from Visigoth to Muslim governance.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-981b9ba171235c0eda85615bee2f8630</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210984</data>
    </node>
    <node id="King Abdul Rahman Al Ghafiqi">
      <data key="d0">King Abdul Rahman Al Ghafiqi</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">Abdul Rahman al-Ghafiqi was the leader who initiated Umayyad rule in the Iberian Peninsula after overthrowing the Visigoths. He established a new dynasty that lasted until 756 AD.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-981b9ba171235c0eda85615bee2f8630</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210984</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Maya civilization&quot;&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Maya civilization"&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;historical_civilization&gt;</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;"The Maya civilization was a Mesoamerican civilization developed by the Maya peoples, and noted for its hieroglyphic script-the only known fully developed writing system of the pre-Columbian Americas-as well as for its12-month year calendar. It spanned various territories in what is now Mexico, Guatemala, Belize, Honduras, and El Salvador."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-33cf612417535ca257ea83d557f527a5</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210984</data>
    </node>
    <node id="13.2 The Seljuk Migration and the Call from the East">
      <data key="d0">13.2 The Seljuk Migration and the Call from the East</data>
      <data key="d1">textbook_structure</data>
      <data key="d2">Chapter or Section in a textbook focusing on the Seljuk migration and its relevance to the Islamic world.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-70a588c1e9025f193747e47553aa2ca6</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210984</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Great Library of Baghdad">
      <data key="d0">Great Library of Baghdad</data>
      <data key="d1">source_or_artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">A significant repository of works during the Abbasid era, contributing to cultural and scientific advancements.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-70a588c1e9025f193747e47553aa2ca6</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210984</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Fatimids">
      <data key="d0">Fatimids</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_entity</data>
      <data key="d2">The Fatimids were a rival caliphate that emerged in North Africa and competed with the Abbasids for control over Islamic territories. Known for establishing a Shia caliphate, the Fatimid dynasty played a significant role in the political landscape of the time by challenging the authority of the Sunni-led Abbasid Caliphate. These descriptions collectively highlight the Fatimids' emergence in North Africa, their rivalry with the Abbasids, and their status as leaders in the Shia Islamic faith.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-70a588c1e9025f193747e47553aa2ca6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9cea715d402110c49c739e324bc54567</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210990</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Abbasid Caliphate&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Abbasid Caliphate&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">civilization_or_empire</data>
      <data key="d2">The Abbasid Caliphate was a major Islamic empire and one of the most significant periods in Islamic history, established by Abu al-ʿAbbās Abdullah ibn Muhammad in 750 AD. It had its capital at Baghdad and later moved to Samarra. The caliphate was known for its cosmopolitan nature and notable intellectual advancements during the time period.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a51f56123d8110f2ea915388f363a913</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210984</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Mamluks&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Mamluks&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">The Mamluk Sultanate was an Islamic sultanate based in Egypt that existed from 1250 to 1517. Initially composed of foreign slaves, the Mamluks rose through military ranks and eventually seized power, establishing a powerful government with significant influence over much of the eastern Mediterranean region. They played a crucial role in repelling Crusader invasions and defending Islamic lands against external threats. These individuals were known for their loyalty to the caliph and often served as military commanders. The Mamluks also actively quelled domestic rebellions and conducted foreign campaigns against Byzantine rivals, highlighting their dual role of internal pacification and external defense.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2d6af87b20cd5c5556a01a3b05d06cc0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a51f56123d8110f2ea915388f363a913</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210992</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Al-Mutasim&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Al-Mutasim&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">Abu Jaʿfar al-Mutawakkil's brother, Al-Mutasim was the 6th Abbasid caliph who reigned from 833 to 842 AD. He moved the capital of the Abbasid Caliphate from Baghdad to Samarra and was known for his support of translations into Arabic.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a51f56123d8110f2ea915388f363a913</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210984</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Samarra&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Samarra&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">Samarra became the capital of the Abbasid Caliphate after the move from Baghdad in 836 AD. Known for its grand architecture, it was a center of religious and intellectual life during this time period.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a51f56123d8110f2ea915388f363a913</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210984</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Arab elites&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Arab elites&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">cultural_or_religious_movement</data>
      <data key="d2">During the Abbasid Caliphate, Arab elites played an influential role as the ruling class who maintained their cultural identity despite the cosmopolitan nature of society. They were key players in shaping policies and cultural practices throughout this period.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a51f56123d8110f2ea915388f363a913</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210985</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Byzantine rivals&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Byzantine rivals&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">During the Abbasid Caliphate, there was ongoing conflict with the Byzantine Empire as both empires sought dominance over territories in the Middle East. These conflicts had a significant impact on military and political strategies during this period.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a51f56123d8110f2ea915388f363a913</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210985</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;philosophers like al-Farabi and Ibn Sina&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;philosophers like al-Farabi and Ibn Sina&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">Al-Farabi and Ibn Sina (Avicenna) were prominent philosophers who contributed significantly to Islamic scholarship during the Abbasid Caliphate. Al-Farabi focused on speculative philosophy, while Ibn Sina made substantial advancements in medicine, logic, and metaphysics.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a51f56123d8110f2ea915388f363a913</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210985</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;translation into Arabic&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;translation into Arabic&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">source_or_artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">The process of translating philosophical and scientific texts from Greek into Arabic during the Abbasid period was crucial for preserving and expanding knowledge. This activity facilitated a bridge between ancient Greek thought and later European scholars.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a51f56123d8110f2ea915388f363a913</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210985</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;XXI&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;XXI&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">time_period</data>
      <data key="d2">8th-13th centuries, marks a significant period in Islamic history with cultural and scientific advancements</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-309095f0336265497cf6191180cb1924</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210985</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;XXII&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;XXII&gt;</data>
