    </node>
    <node id="Seljuk Empire">
      <data key="d0">Seljuk Empire</data>
      <data key="d1">civilization_or_empire</data>
      <data key="d2">The Seljuk Empire was a significant central Asian power that expanded from India to Anatolia, challenging and taking territories from the Byzantine Empire and ruling over Persia, Mesopotamia, Syria, and much of Anatolia during the 10th-12th centuries.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ef569534db6161860971327c3bc8b184</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210985</data>
    </node>
    <node id="First Crusade">
      <data key="d0">First Crusade</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">The First Crusade, initiated in 1095 by European Christian kingdoms, was a pivotal historical event that aimed to capture Jerusalem and establish Crusader States in the region. This crusade had far-reaching implications for both the religious and political landscapes of the time, particularly affecting the Muslim world and serving as a crucial test for the medieval Christian states against their Byzantine counterparts. Despite initial internal conflicts among participating Christian factions, the First Crusade successfully took Jerusalem, marking it as a significant milestone in the broader crusading movement. This event provided context for the later separation between eastern and western churches, reflecting its complex interactions with both the Byzantine Empire and the Muslim world, ultimately shaping medieval history significantly.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9cea715d402110c49c739e324bc54567&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0cff5e2853da981c04b0b2b4aa611137&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aeb38d9c22dbab062c46fb4a7bdfb465&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b150c3e9d15dec1eaca94b8168bda0ba&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-70a588c1e9025f193747e47553aa2ca6</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210989</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Caliphate">
      <data key="d0">Caliphate</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">The concept of Caliphate refers to the Islamic institution of religious-political leadership, particularly challenging for non-Arab Islamic leaders like the Seljuks who adopted the title 'sultan' instead, indicating their new authority and independent rule.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ef569534db6161860971327c3bc8b184</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210985</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Sultans">
      <data key="d0">Sultans</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">The Sultans were the rulers of the Seljuk Empire, adopting the title 'authority' and exercising real power despite acknowledging the position but not the authority of the Abbasid Caliph.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ef569534db6161860971327c3bc8b184</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210985</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Sunni Islam">
      <data key="d0">Sunni Islam</data>
      <data key="d1">cultural_or_religious_movement</data>
      <data key="d2">Sunni Islam denotes the largest branch of Islam, emphasized by the Seljuks in their defense and promotion of Islamic culture, arts, and sciences through scholars like al-Ghazali and Omar Khayyam.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ef569534db6161860971327c3bc8b184</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210985</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Caravansaries">
      <data key="d0">Caravansaries</data>
      <data key="d1">source_or_artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">Caravansaries were structures built along trade routes to provide lodging, services, and a hub for news and trading activities for travelers such as merchants, diplomats, and pilgrims during the Seljuk period.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ef569534db6161860971327c3bc8b184</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210985</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Strict Sunnis">
      <data key="d0">Strict Sunnis</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">"The term represents a specific period of Sunni orthodoxy and policy during which strict policies were enacted against non-Sunni groups.&lt;"|&gt;""&lt;|</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c7ba4063d51063344788ef9e47fac211</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210985</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Macedonian Renaissance">
      <data key="d0">Macedonian Renaissance</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">"Macedonian Renaissance is depicted through an illuminated manuscript from Constantinople during the tenth century, which shows innovations in art and setting realism.")&lt;|</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-99442760cad93ba2b6238a5063d5e7a3</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210985</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Dynatoi">
      <data key="d0">Dynatoi</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">Dynatoi were powerful elites in the Byzantine Empire whose influence was curbed by measures taken during the Macedonian period but eventually supported by less capable emperors, weakening peasant military support and empowering these elites.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-99442760cad93ba2b6238a5063d5e7a3</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210985</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Macedonian Dynasty">
      <data key="d0">Macedonian Dynasty</data>
      <data key="d1">civilization_or_empire</data>
      <data key="d2">The Macedonian Dynasty ruled over the Byzantine Empire with rulers like Basil II who enacted laws against power elites; however, their successors reversed these policies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-99442760cad93ba2b6238a5063d5e7a3</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210985</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cluny">
      <data key="d0">Cluny</data>
      <data key="d1">geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">Cluny refers to the Abbey of Cluny, a significant twelfth-century religious establishment where reforms were initiated and spread to influence the Roman Catholic Church.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-65da8baf696cc67fe70482eefac175f5</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210985</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Abbey of Cluny">
      <data key="d0">Abbey of Cluny</data>
      <data key="d1">source_or_artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">Abbey of Cluny is a specific source or artifact from the twelfth century that played a crucial role in church reform movements, particularly through its third abbey consecration by Pope Urban II.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-65da8baf696cc67fe70482eefac175f5</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210985</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Pope Urban II">
      <data key="d0">Pope Urban II</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">Pope Urban II is an historical figure pivotal to the Cluniac movement and church reforms, noted for his involvement in the consecration of the third Abbey of Cluny and his influence on papal reform programs.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-65da8baf696cc67fe70482eefac175f5</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210985</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cluniac Movement">
      <data key="d0">Cluniac Movement</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">The Cluniac movement is a religious movement that emerged from the Abbey of Cluny, promoting reforms such as ending simony and encouraging celibacy among clergy, which had wider implications for church governance and practices.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-65da8baf696cc67fe70482eefac175f5</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210985</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Simony">
      <data key="d0">Simony</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">Simony is a historical concept denoting the act of buying or selling ecclesiastical offices, which became a target for reform efforts initiated by the Cluniac movement and later adopted by the Roman Catholic Church.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-65da8baf696cc67fe70482eefac175f5</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210985</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Celibacy">
      <data key="d0">Celibacy</data>
      <data key="d1">cultural_or_religious_movement</data>
      <data key="d2">Celibacy is a cultural and religious practice that was encouraged among clergy during the church reforms, with its enforcement becoming increasingly emphasized by reforming popes and monks to address the issue of simony and maintain ecclesiastical purity.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-65da8baf696cc67fe70482eefac175f5</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210985</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bishops Elected by Clergy">
      <data key="d0">Bishops Elected by Clergy</data>
      <data key="d1">cultural_or_religious_movement</data>
      <data key="d2">The movement towards electing bishops in a manner that is independent of lords, reflecting a significant change instigated by Cluniac reforms within the church hierarchy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-65da8baf696cc67fe70482eefac175f5</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210986</data>
    </node>
    <node id="King Excommunication">
      <data key="d0">King Excommunication</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">King excommunication is a concept associated with the reform efforts, where leaders of Europe who attempted to interfere in ecclesiastical appointments could be expelled from the church's communion and sacraments, signaling the increasing power and autonomy of the clergy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-65da8baf696cc67fe70482eefac175f5</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210986</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Chivalry">
      <data key="d0">Chivalry</data>
      <data key="d1">cultural_or_religious_movement</data>
      <data key="d2">Chivalry is a cultural concept that emerged as part of the broader reforms initiated by Cluniac movements, meant to Christianize knightly behavior and violence through codes of conduct such as protecting women and observing religiously significant days.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-65da8baf696cc67fe70482eefac175f5</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210986</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Code of Conduct for Christians">
      <data key="d0">Code of Conduct for Christians</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">A code of conduct specifically for Christian soldiers that aimed to limit violent behavior in accordance with church values, contributing to the broader chivalric culture influenced by cluny reforms.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-65da8baf696cc67fe70482eefac175f5</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210986</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Warfare Ethics">
      <data key="d0">Warfare Ethics</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">The concept of distinguishing between legitimate and illegitimate warfare based on Christian ethics and church values, shaping the ideals of chivalry and knightly behavior.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-65da8baf696cc67fe70482eefac175f5</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210986</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Secular Interest in Chivalric Culture">
      <data key="d0">Secular Interest in Chivalric Culture</data>
      <data key="d1">cultural_or_religious_movement</data>
      <data key="d2">Secular interest in a newly defined chivalric culture reflected the broadening impact of cluny reforms beyond strictly ecclesiastical concerns, integrating knights' roles with broader cultural values.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-65da8baf696cc67fe70482eefac175f5</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210986</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Christianized Warfare">
      <data key="d0">Christianized Warfare</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">The idea of Christianized warfare is a specific concept within the broader context of cluniac reform, focusing on using military might in accordance with religious and ethical principles.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-65da8baf696cc67fe70482eefac175f5</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210986</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Textbook Structure">
      <data key="d0">Textbook Structure</data>
      <data key="d1">textbook_structure</data>
      <data key="d2">This structure outlines key points discussed in this text, including the origins, nature, and impacts of the Cluniac movement, which are pivotal moments and concepts for understanding church history.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-65da8baf696cc67fe70482eefac175f5</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210986</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Muslim inhabitants">
      <data key="d0">Muslim inhabitants</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">The massacre of Muslim inhabitants represents an event during the First Crusade where significant violence was committed against Muslims, sparking shock among both religious groups.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-aeb38d9c22dbab062c46fb4a7bdfb465</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210986</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Jewish inhabitants">
      <data key="d0">Jewish inhabitants</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">The massacre of Jewish inhabitants also occurred as a result of the actions taken by crusaders, indicating the scale and nature of the conflict.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-aeb38d9c22dbab062c46fb4a7bdfb465</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210986</data>
    </node>
    <node id="County of Edessa">
      <data key="d0">County of Edessa</data>
      <data key="d1">cultural_or_religious_movement</data>
      <data key="d2">The County of Edessa is one of the four Crusader States established after the success of the First Crusade, signifying a new religious and political entity in the region.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-aeb38d9c22dbab062c46fb4a7bdfb465</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210986</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Principality of Antioch">
      <data key="d0">Principality of Antioch</data>
      <data key="d1">geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">Principality of Antioch was another important territory taken by crusaders during the First Crusade, becoming part of the newly formed Crusader States.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-aeb38d9c22dbab062c46fb4a7bdfb465</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210986</data>
    </node>
    <node id="(&quot;County of Tripoli&quot;">
      <data key="d0">("County of Tripoli"</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">The County of Tripoli is one of the four Crusader States, indicating a region under Crusader control after the victory in the First Crusade, blending political and religious governance.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-aeb38d9c22dbab062c46fb4a7bdfb465</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210986</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Kingdom of Jerusalem">
      <data key="d0">Kingdom of Jerusalem</data>
      <data key="d1">historic_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">Established as the capital of Outremer, the Kingdom of Jerusalem represented the highest level of political organization among the Crusader States, symbolizing their most significant territorial achievement.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-aeb38d9c22dbab062c46fb4a7bdfb465</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210986</data>
    </node>
    <node id="2005 Ridley Scott Crusader Film">
      <data key="d0">2005 Ridley Scott Crusader Film</data>
      <data key="d1">source_or_artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">The 2005 Ridley Scott crusader film 'Kingdom of Heaven' is often criticized for its modern sentiments and dramatized depictions, moving away from a realistic portrayal of the Crusades.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b150c3e9d15dec1eaca94b8168bda0ba</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210986</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Kingdom of Heaven">
      <data key="d0">Kingdom of Heaven</data>
      <data key="d1">source_or_artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">The 2005 Ridley Scott crusader film 'Kingdom of Heaven' is often criticized for its modern sentiments and dramatized depictions, moving away from a realistic portrayal of the Crusades.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b150c3e9d15dec1eaca94b8168bda0ba</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210986</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Seljuks">
      <data key="d0">Seljuks</data>
      <data key="d1">civilization_or_empire</data>
      <data key="d2">The Selujks were a Turkic nomadic confederation that established a powerful empire in the Middle East and played a crucial role in opposing the Crusaders, notably blocking many newly arriving knights from reaching Outremer.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b150c3e9d15dec1eaca94b8168bda0ba</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210986</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Imad al-Din Zengi">
      <data key="d0">Imad al-Din Zengi</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">Imad al-Din Zengi was a Turkic aristocrat who began his career as an enemy of the Crusaders by taking Edessa but later gained recognition as a defender of Islam, though he was assassinated before continuing his campaign.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b150c3e9d15dec1eaca94b8168bda0ba</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210986</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Second Crusade">
      <data key="d0">Second Crusade</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">The Second Crusade, called for by Pope Eugene III and led by powerful rulers like King Louis VII of France and King Conrad III of Germany, culminated in significant defeats for the Christian armies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b150c3e9d15dec1eaca94b8168bda0ba</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210986</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Knights Templar">
      <data key="d0">Knights Templar</data>
      <data key="d1">cultural_or_religious_movement</data>
      <data key="d2">The Knights Templar was a religious order of monks founded by Saint Bernard of Clairvaux to protect Christian pilgrims and support Crusader efforts.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b150c3e9d15dec1eaca94b8168bda0ba</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210986</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bernard of Clairvaux">
      <data key="d0">Bernard of Clairvaux</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">Bernard of Clairvaux was an influential 12th-century monk, abbot, and doctor of the Church who played a significant role in founding the Knights Templar and inspiring the Second Crusade.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b150c3e9d15dec1eaca94b8168bda0ba</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210986</data>
    </node>
