      <data key="d0">Figure 13.21 King Louis VII on Crusade</data>
      <data key="d1">textbook_structure</data>
      <data key="d2">Figure 13.21 illustrates King Louis VII's participation in the Second Crusade, welcoming him to Antioch.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5a5b2b430185118342f9094519e5e975</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210986</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Imad al-Din Zengi's successors">
      <data key="d0">Imad al-Din Zengi's successors</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">The leaders who succeeded Imad al-Din Zengi and sought to unite Muslim princes in jihad, notable among them was Salah al-Din (Saladin).</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5a5b2b430185118342f9094519e5e975</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210986</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Salah al-Din or Saladin">
      <data key="d0">Salah al-Din or Saladin</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">A notable leader who united the Muslims under his banner, fought against Crusaders, and founded the Ayyubid dynasty. Revered in both Muslim and Christian contexts for his chivalrous conduct.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5a5b2b430185118342f9094519e5e975</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210986</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Shia Fatimid Caliphate">
      <data key="d0">Shia Fatimid Caliphate</data>
      <data key="d1">civilization_or_empire</data>
      <data key="d2">A Muslim caliphate that faced the rise of the Ayyubids under Salah al-Din, which was in decline and gave way to Salah al-Din's new dynasty.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5a5b2b430185118342f9094519e5e975</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210986</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Battle of the Horns of Hattin">
      <data key="d0">Battle of the Horns of Hattin</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">A significant battle in 1187 where Saladin defeated the Crusaders, leading to the fall of Jerusalem. Marked a crucial turning point in the Crusades with heavy losses for Christian forces.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5a5b2b430185118342f9094519e5e975</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210986</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Richard the Lionhearted">
      <data key="d0">Richard the Lionhearted</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">A significant king who played a crucial role in both personal combat and treaty negotiations during the Third Crusade. Celebrated for his martial prowess but ultimately had to negotiate with Saladin.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5a5b2b430185118342f9094519e5e975</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210986</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Jerusalem fall to Saladin">
      <data key="d0">Jerusalem fall to Saladin</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">The capture of Jerusalem by Salah al-Din, marking a significant setback for Christian crusaders.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5a5b2b430185118342f9094519e5e975</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210987</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Third Crusade outcome - Cyprus and Coastline">
      <data key="d0">Third Crusade outcome - Cyprus and Coastline</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Achievements of the Third Crusade, including victories at the island of Cyprus and coastal regions but lack of progress inland. Despite initial successes, Christian forces were unable to advance farther into the Middle East.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5a5b2b430185118342f9094519e5e975</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210987</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Treaty between Richard and Saladin">
      <data key="d0">Treaty between Richard and Saladin</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">A negotiated settlement that allowed Christians free access to Jerusalem while maintaining Muslim control. Represented a diplomatic victory for Salah al-Din, preserving Christian pilgrim rights without full military engagement.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5a5b2b430185118342f9094519e5e975</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210987</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Richard the Lionhearted and Salah al-Din's relationship or encounter">
      <data key="d0">Richard the Lionhearted and Salah al-Din's relationship or encounter</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">A meeting between Richard the Lionhearted and Saladin during the Third Crusade. Highlighting their interaction in historical narratives, though not a direct combat but rather a treaty making.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5a5b2b430185118342f9094519e5e975</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210987</data>
    </node>
    <node id="King Louis VII and Richard the Lionhearteds relationship or exchange">
      <data key="d0">King Louis VII and Richard the Lionhearteds relationship or exchange</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">Historical figures interacting with each other on different Crusades, both participating in the Second Crusade. Their roles as leaders of Christian forces and their respective outcomes are key elements of narratives from this period.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5a5b2b430185118342f9094519e5e975</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210987</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Baltic regions">
      <data key="d0">Baltic regions</data>
      <data key="d1">geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">Baltic regions refer to the historical area north of the Baltic Sea where Christian kingdoms expanded during this period.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d6659f444d2496d6a86c3c2494b6b2e4</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210987</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Iberian Peninsula">
      <data key="d0">Iberian Peninsula</data>
      <data key="d1">geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">The Iberian Peninsula is a region located in southwestern Europe known for its historical significance during the expansion of Christian kingdoms. This geographic area also served as a critical location for one of the invasion routes from Egypt, where Arab soldiers traversed through it to push further into Christian territories.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d6659f444d2496d6a86c3c2494b6b2e4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-70d03803311b31d7e11f7ace07e0a57f</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210992</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mamluks">
      <data key="d0">Mamluks</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">The Mamluks were a historical group that held power in Egypt from the 13th century until the Ottoman conquest in the 16th century. They were a powerful Islamic dynasty based in Egypt and played significant roles in resisting Crusader incursions during this period.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d6659f444d2496d6a86c3c2494b6b2e4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-72d7bb40fa0aa36bf5b0bb66bf7975fe</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210992</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ottoman Turks">
      <data key="d0">Ottoman Turks</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">Ottoman Turks, who emerged as a major political power in Anatolia, challenged the idea of crusade by establishing control over key territories previously claimed during the crusades.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d6659f444d2496d6a86c3c2494b6b2e4</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210987</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Christian kingdoms">
      <data key="d0">Christian kingdoms</data>
      <data key="d1">civilization_or_empire</data>
      <data key="d2">Christian kingdoms refer to the centralized states and polities that emerged and expanded across Europe during this period as a result of geopolitical shifts and religious motivations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d6659f444d2496d6a86c3c2494b6b2e4</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210987</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Chivalry&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Chivalry&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;historical_concept_or_event&gt;</data>
      <data key="d2">Chivalry is a set of ideals and practices that developed in medieval Europe. It emphasized principles such as honor, bravery, loyalty, and respect for women. Chivalric codes were often associated with the upper classes, especially members of the knightly class, and included specific rules for conduct during warfare, tournaments, and courtly love. The concept played a significant role in the formation of medieval social structures and cultural values.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2d6af87b20cd5c5556a01a3b05d06cc0</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210987</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Cluniac_reform&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Cluniac_reform&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;historical_concept_or_event&gt;</data>
      <data key="d2">The Cluniac reform was a movement within Western Christianity that aimed to revive monastic life according to stricter observance of Benedictine rules. It began at the Abbey of Cluny in France and spread across Europe by the 11th century, leading to the founding of numerous new abbeys with increased dedication to prayer, study, and evangelism. The reform emphasized liturgical purity, monastic discipline, and the role of the abbey as a center for religious education.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2d6af87b20cd5c5556a01a3b05d06cc0</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210987</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Feudalism&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Feudalism&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;historical_concept_or_event&gt;</data>
      <data key="d2">Feudalism was a complex and hierarchical system that emerged in medieval Europe following the decline of the Roman Empire. It centered around land-based relationships, where lords granted fiefs to vassals who provided military service or other forms of loyalty in return. The system structured society into multiple layers, from the monarchy down to serfs tied to their land. Feudalism was characterized by its rigid social order, interdependent lord-vassal relationships, and the exchange of protective benefits for land ownership.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2d6af87b20cd5c5556a01a3b05d06cc0</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210988</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Great_Schism_of_1054&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Great_Schism_of_1054&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;historical_concept_or_event&gt;</data>
      <data key="d2">The Great Schism or East-West Schism of 1054 was a significant event in Christian history that resulted in the formal split between Eastern Orthodox and Roman Catholic churches. It began with theological disputes, ecclesiastical issues, and political conflicts, ultimately leading to a permanent division on key matters such as papal authority, the nature of Christ, and liturgical practices. The event marked the end of centuries of shared faith and cooperation among Christians from both western and eastern traditions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2d6af87b20cd5c5556a01a3b05d06cc0</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210988</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Jihad&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Jihad&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;historical_concept_or_event&gt;</data>
      <data key="d2">Jihad refers to struggle or strife in Islam, traditionally understood as a form of struggle for the moral, spiritual, or religious betterment of oneself. In specific contexts, it can denote armed conflict against infidels, particularly in defense of Islamic territory or faith. Historical use includes both internal and external struggles, often associated with military campaigns during the time of Muhammad and subsequent Muslim conquests.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2d6af87b20cd5c5556a01a3b05d06cc0</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210988</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Manorialism&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Manorialism&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;historical_concept_or_event&gt;</data>
      <data key="d2">Manorialism was an organizational system that structured agricultural productivity during the Middle Ages in Europe, particularly after the fall of Rome. It involved a hierarchy where lords held land and provided for serfs who cultivated it. In exchange for labor and protection, peasants enjoyed certain rights and privileges. Manorial units acted as self-sufficient communities integrating both economic and administrative functions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2d6af87b20cd5c5556a01a3b05d06cc0</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210988</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Serfs&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Serfs&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;historical_concept_or_event&gt;</data>
      <data key="d2">Serfs were bound to the land in medieval Europe, primarily serving large landowners who provided them with protection and resources. Serfs had limited mobility and faced various legal limitations; however, they also often enjoyed certain rights like heritable land ownership and the ability to marry freely. Serfdom was a fundamental aspect of feudal society, defining economic, social, and political relationships.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2d6af87b20cd5c5556a01a3b05d06cc0</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210988</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Sufism&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Sufism&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;cultural_or_religious_movement&gt;</data>
      <data key="d2">Sufism is an esoteric mystical dimension within Islam that emphasizes personal experience of God, devotion (dhikr), and ascetic practices. Sufi orders or brotherhoods spread widely across the Islamic world, offering spiritual guidance through various methods like meditation, poetry, music, and dance. Sufi mystics sought to interpret Islamic teachings in a more individualistic manner, often emphasizing love as a gateway to divine presence.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2d6af87b20cd5c5556a01a3b05d06cc0</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210988</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Sultan&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Sultan&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;historical_figure&gt;</data>
      <data key="d2">A sultan was an autocratic ruler who typically headed various Muslim states and empires throughout the Middle Ages. The title conveyed both sovereignty over territory and spiritual authority within Islam. Sultans wielded significant political and military power, often maintaining complex administrative structures to govern their realms. Notable examples include leaders like Saladin, who fought against Crusaders in the Holy Land, and Bayezid I of the Ottoman Empire.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2d6af87b20cd5c5556a01a3b05d06cc0</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210988</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Cluny&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Cluny&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;place&gt;</data>
      <data key="d2">The Abbey of Cluny was a major Benedictine abbey located in Burgundy, France. Established around 910 AD, it became one of the wealthiest and most influential monasteries during its era. The abbey was known for its elaborate architecture, extensive library, and significant role in initiating the Cluniac reform movement, which sought to revitalize monastic life according to Benedictine rules.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2d6af87b20cd5c5556a01a3b05d06cc0</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210988</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Crusades&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Crusades&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;historical_concept_or_event&gt;</data>
      <data key="d2">The Crusades were a series of religious wars launched by Western Christendom from 1095 onwards, primarily in the name of recapturing Holy Land Jerusalem from Muslim rule. They involved multiple campaigns, mobilizing armed Christian pilgrims and soldiers to engage with forces of Islam, often involving complex political alliances and conflicts. The Crusades had significant impacts on medieval politics, religion, and socio-economic conditions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2d6af87b20cd5c5556a01a3b05d06cc0</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210988</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Eastern_Orthodox_Church&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Eastern_Orthodox_Church&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;religion&gt;</data>
      <data key="d2">The Eastern Orthodox Church is one of the oldest Christian traditions, rooted in early Christianity and maintaining distinct liturgical, theological, and pastoral practices. It includes various autocephalous churches spread across Eastern Europe, the Balkans, Middle East, Asia Minor, and diaspora. The church emphasizes the importance of sacraments, liturgy, holy icons, and ecclesial hierarchy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2d6af87b20cd5c5556a01a3b05d06cc0</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210988</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Renaissance&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Renaissance&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;historical_event&gt;</data>
      <data key="d2">The Renaissance was a period characterized by significant cultural revival focused on classical knowledge and values in early 14th-century Europe, marking the transition between the later Middle Ages and modern history. During this era, advances were made in art, exploration, humanism, science, literature, and philosophy. Prominently personified figures like Leonardo da Vinci demonstrated a revival of interest in Greco-Roman classical antiquity, contributing to a new emphasis on individual achievement.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2d6af87b20cd5c5556a01a3b05d06cc0</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210988</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Islam&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Islam&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;religion&gt;</data>
      <data key="d2">Islam is a monotheistic religion based on the belief that there is only one God (Allah) and that Muhammad is His final prophet. It originated in the 7th century with the revelations conveyed to Prophet Muhammad, who was seen as a reformer of previous religions. Muslims follow the Quran as holy scripture, maintain five pillars of faith, and believe in angels, prophets, revelation, judgment, and predestination.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2d6af87b20cd5c5556a01a3b05d06cc0</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210988</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Saladin&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Saladin&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;historical_figure&gt;</data>
      <data key="d2">Saladin (1138-1193) was a Kurdish military leader who became Sultan of Egypt and Syria. He is best known for his role as the first Ayyubid ruler and for his leadership in the Third Crusade where he defeated the Christians, especially during the major victory at the Battle of Hattin (1187). Known for his piety, generosity, and military prowess, Saladin was instrumental in re-establishing Muslim control over Jerusalem until its fall to the Crusaders a decade later.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2d6af87b20cd5c5556a01a3b05d06cc0</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210988</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Bayezid_I&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Bayezid_I&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;historical_figure&gt;</data>
      <data key="d2">Bayezid I (1360-1404) was the third Sultan of the Ottoman Empire and ruled from 1389 to 1402. Under his leadership, the Ottomans significantly expanded their territories, conquering the Serene Republic of Genoa in Bodrum on the Aegean coast. He faced defeat at the Battle of Ankara (1402</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2d6af87b20cd5c5556a01a3b05d06cc0</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210988</data>
    </node>
    <node id="The Crusading Movement">
      <data key="d0">The Crusading Movement</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_movement</data>
      <data key="d2">The Crusades were a movement that signaled the growth of the papacy’s influence in western Europe and helped to stimulate trade, the growth of the Italian city-states, and contact with peoples across Afro-Eurasia.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8d439bb45b85431dc5d851d56b5b4932</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210988</data>
    </node>
