    <node id="&lt;Temujin&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Temujin&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">“Entity” Temujin, later known as Chinggis Khan, was a Mongol leader who united disparate steppe tribes and established one of the largest contiguous empires in history. He implemented reforms to create a classless society through oaths of loyalty and promoted merit-based leadership structures."</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6323d37e41c8fe5abe6e45e92d74deb5</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210992</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Chinggis Khan&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Chinggis Khan&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;historical_figure&gt;</data>
      <data key="d2">Chinggis Khan, also known as Genghis Khan, was a pivotal figure in Mongolian history who lived from around 1162 to 1227. His leadership encompassed military campaigns, trade negotiations, and the expansion of the Mongol Empire, making it one of the largest empires in world history. As Temujin, he unified the various Mongol tribes and went on to establish the Mongol Empire as a formidable force through his strategic conquests, economic policies, and cultural reforms. Chinggis Khan is celebrated for his role in shaping the foundations of this expansive empire during his reign from 1207 to 1227.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c065aace43270eef7d2c60439e6d228b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6323d37e41c8fe5abe6e45e92d74deb5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4e57e6349a7929fcf289f8da82a3abde</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210997</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Ögedei Khan&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Ögedei Khan&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">“Entity” Ögedei Khan succeeded Chinggis Khan as the second Great Khan. He continued to expand the Mongol Empire, aiming to implement his father’s vision for a Eurasian trading empire and maintain the cohesion of diverse ethnic groups under the felt walls."</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6323d37e41c8fe5abe6e45e92d74deb5</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210992</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Möngke Khan&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Möngke Khan&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">“Entity” Möngke Khan, also known as Mengge or Monke, succeeded Ögedei Khan. As the fourth Great Khan, he continued to expand the Mongol Empire and maintained its unity under a central authority while facing the increasing challenge of internal dissent and external pressures."</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6323d37e41c8fe5abe6e45e92d74deb5</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210992</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;People of the Felt Walls&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;People of the Felt Walls&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">civilization_or_empire</data>
      <data key="d2">“Entity” People of the Felt Walls refers to the unified Mongol Empire under Chinggis Khan. It is characterized by a hierarchical but merit-based leadership structure, a sense of national unity and loyalty expressed in the Baljuna Covenant, and military successes through strategic unification."</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6323d37e41c8fe5abe6e45e92d74deb5</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210992</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Baljuna Covenant&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Baljuna Covenant&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">“Entity” The Baljuna Covenant was an oath that symbolized Mongol nationalism and renewed loyalty among the tribes under Chinggis Khan. It played a crucial role in maintaining unity and discipline within the People of the Felt Walls."</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6323d37e41c8fe5abe6e45e92d74deb5</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210992</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;class warfare&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;class warfare&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">“Entity” Class warfare refers to sociopolitical conflict between different social classes or strata, such as the opposition between Chinggis Khan’s reforms and traditional aristocratic power. It was a critical aspect of Mongol unification efforts."</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6323d37e41c8fe5abe6e45e92d74deb5</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210992</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Yasa&quot;&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Yasa"&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;historical_concept_or_event&gt;</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;"Yasa was the legal code of the Mongol Empire. It removed many sources of strife, including traditional raiding activities, which helped stabilize the empire but also restricted some traditional activities that led to social mobility."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-405d14b235f769c3d78504b096eb8659</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210992</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Chinggis Khan&quot;&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Chinggis Khan"&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;historical_figure&gt;</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;"Chinggis Khan was a historical leader who significantly influenced the Mongol Empire through his conquests and governance. He is known for his innovative strategies, including the use of Yasa, and for his role in the expansion of the Mongol Empire."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-405d14b235f769c3d78504b096eb8659</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210992</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Conquest Movement of Chinggis Khan&quot;&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Conquest Movement of Chinggis Khan"&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;historical_concept_or_event&gt;</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;"The Conquest Movement of Chinggis Khan was both a response to traditional customs and an expansion strategy aimed at acquiring wealth from conquered territories, such as the Jin Dynasty, by leveraging internal dissent among other cultures."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-405d14b235f769c3d78504b096eb8659</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210992</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Kara-Khitan&quot;&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Kara-Khitan"&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;cultural_or_religious_movement&gt;</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;"The Kara-Khitans were a cultural or religious movement within the Mongol Empire. They are mentioned in relation to Chinggis Khan’s strategies of portraying the Mongols as liberators for linguistic kin."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-405d14b235f769c3d78504b096eb8659</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210992</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Jin Dynasty&quot;&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Jin Dynasty"&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;civilization_or_empire&gt;</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;"The Jin Dynasty was a civilization that coexisted with and was conquered by the Mongol Empire. The Mongols used internal dissent among the Jin to expand their territories."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-405d14b235f769c3d78504b096eb8659</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210992</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Song Dynasty&quot;&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Song Dynasty"&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;civilization_or_empire&gt;</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;"The Song Dynasty was another empire coexisting with the Mongols; it suffered from Mongol invasions and eventually fell to the Yuan dynasty, established by Kublai Khan, Chinggis Khan's grandson."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-405d14b235f769c3d78504b096eb8659</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210992</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Afro-Eurasian Societies&quot;&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Afro-Eurasian Societies"&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;geographical_location&gt;</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;"Afro-Eurasian Societies refers to the vast region that includes parts of Africa, Asia, and Europe. The Mongol Empire expanded across this area, interacting with these diverse societies."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-405d14b235f769c3d78504b096eb8659</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210992</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Xi Xia&quot;&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Xi Xia"&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;civilization_or_empire&gt;</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;"The Xi Xia was a civilization in present-day China that existed alongside the Song Dynasty and was eventually conquered by the Mongols. It was ruled by the Tangut people."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-405d14b235f769c3d78504b096eb8659</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210992</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Khitan&quot;&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Khitan"&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;historical_figure&gt;</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;"The Khitan were a nomadic Turkic people who established their own empire before being absorbed into the larger Mongol Empire. They are mentioned in relation to Chinggis Khan's strategies of portraying the Mongols as liberators for linguistic kin."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-405d14b235f769c3d78504b096eb8659</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210992</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Chinggis Khan's Coronation&quot;&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Chinggis Khan's Coronation"&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;historical_event&gt;</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;"The coronation of Chinggis Khan marked a significant event when he officially became the leader of the Mongol people, establishing his authority and legitimacy over the empire."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-405d14b235f769c3d78504b096eb8659</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210992</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Zhongdu&quot;&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Zhongdu"&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;source_or_artifact&gt;</data>
      <data key="d2">Zhongdu was a pivotal capital city with a rich historical legacy. Initially serving as an important capital during the Jin Dynasty, Zhongdu later assumed significance under Kublai Khan, who made it one of the capitals for the Yuan dynasty. This city played a crucial role in shaping the political and cultural landscape of the Mongol Empire before becoming the main capital alongside Beijing (formerly known as Khanbaliq). Its historical importance lies in its contributions to the governance and cultural development under both the Jin Dynasty and the early years of the Yuan dynasty.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4b991f2f74ca0591e586ba942c9fa90b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-405d14b235f769c3d78504b096eb8659</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210996</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;gender roles in Mongol society&quot;&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"gender roles in Mongol society"&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;historical_concept_or_event&gt;</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;"The gender roles within Mongol society were impacted by laws such as Yasa. While stability was achieved, traditional opportunities for mobility among women were limited by new legal constraints."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-405d14b235f769c3d78504b096eb8659</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210992</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;political strategy of Chinggis Khan&quot;&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"political strategy of Chinggis Khan"&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;historical_figure&gt;</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;"Chinggis Khan employed a multifaceted political strategy that included military conquests, legal reforms like Yasa, and cultural narratives to stabilize and expand the empire."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-405d14b235f769c3d78504b096eb8659</data>
