    <node id="&lt;Great Mongol Nation&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Great Mongol Nation&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;cultural_or_religious_movement&gt;</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;The Great Mongol Nation refers to the empire founded by Chinggis Khan, characterized by its vast territorial expansion, complex administrative practices, and cultural influence across multiple regions.&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4e57e6349a7929fcf289f8da82a3abde</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210993</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Ogedei Khan&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Ogedei Khan&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;historical_figure&gt;</data>
      <data key="d2">Ogedei Khan was the third Great Khan of the Mongol Empire and the son of Genghis Khan. He ruled from 1229 to 1241. His reign saw significant administrative reforms aimed at stabilizing the empire, including bureaucratic practices for resource extraction and tax collection. However, his focus on wealth accumulation through tribute led him to delegate military engagements like the inconclusive war against the Song Dynasty.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-14626ecd14bc7017e18a8413e9db611d</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210993</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Xi Xia&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Xi Xia&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;geographical_location&gt;</data>
      <data key="d2">The Xi Xia was a confederation of Tangut tribes located in what is now western China. It was one of Ogedei Khan's conquests, and he received the conquered lands as part of his empire.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-14626ecd14bc7017e18a8413e9db611d</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210993</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Jin Dynasty&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Jin Dynasty&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;historical_concept_or_event&gt;</data>
      <data key="d2">The Jin Dynasty (1115–1234) was a confederation in northern China. It was one of the territories Ogedei Khan's forces conquered, leading to the integration of its lands into his empire.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-14626ecd14bc7017e18a8413e9db611d</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210993</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Qubilai Khan&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Qubilai Khan&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;historical_figure&gt;</data>
      <data key="d2">Qubilai Khan was Ogedei's grandson and later became Khagan (emperor) of the Mongol Empire. He expanded the empire further, particularly in East Asia.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-14626ecd14bc7017e18a8413e9db611d</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210993</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Chagatai Khanate&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Chagatai Khanate&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;civilization_or_empire&gt;</data>
      <data key="d2">The Chagatai Khanate was a division of the Mongol Empire established by Ogedei's brother Chagatai. It covered a vast area, including present-day Central Asia and parts of Pakistan.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-14626ecd14bc7017e18a8413e9db611d</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210993</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Batu Khan (Golden Horde)&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Batu Khan (Golden Horde)&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;historical_figure&gt;</data>
      <data key="d2">Batu Khan led one of the Golden Horde forces and expanded Mongol rule into Eastern Europe during Ogedei's reign.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-14626ecd14bc7017e18a8413e9db611d</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210993</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Song Dynasty&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Song Dynasty&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;civilization_or_empire&gt;</data>
      <data key="d2">The Song Dynasty ruled China from 960 to 1279. It was an ongoing target for Mongol conquests, leading to the inconclusive war that began in 1241.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-14626ecd14bc7017e18a8413e9db611d</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210993</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Yelu Chucai&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Yelu Chucai&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;historical_figure&gt;</data>
      <data key="d2">Yelu Chucai was a minister during Ogedei's reign and played a significant role in advising him on the administration and governance of the empire. He convinced Ogedei to transition from nomadic rule to a more bureaucratic system.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-14626ecd14bc7017e18a8413e9db611d</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210993</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Tolui Khanate&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Tolui Khanate&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;civilization_or_empire&gt;</data>
      <data key="d2">The Tolui Khanate was one of the divisions of the Mongol Empire, established by Genghis Khan's son Tolui. Ogedei received lands and resources from this division.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-14626ecd14bc7017e18a8413e9db611d</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210993</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Ogedei&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Ogedei&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">Chinggis Khan’s grandson who expanded the yamon system and continued his grandfather's legacy. He ruled during a period of significant trade, diplomatic relations, and expansion of Pax Mongolica.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c9ac7d67ec4740a5b51c39c9c49e5d54</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210993</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Yamon_system&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Yamon_system&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">civilization_or_empire</data>
      <data key="d2">A network of relay stations originally established by Chinggis Khan for military communication which Ogedei expanded to support trade and administration. It facilitated long-distance travel, commerce, and communication across the empire.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c9ac7d67ec4740a5b51c39c9c49e5d54</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210993</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Pax_Mongolica&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Pax_Mongolica&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">Mongol Peace, an era of extensive trade and cultural exchange under Mongol rule during Ogedei's reign, marked by peace enforced through systematic taxation. It spanned much of the world and united various regions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c9ac7d67ec4740a5b51c39c9c49e5d54</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210993</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;The_Hu&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;The_Hu&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">cultural_or_religious_movement</data>
      <data key="d2">A modern Mongolian heavy-metal band known for spreading Mongol culture globally through their music and international tours. Their work often references historical figures like Chinggis Khan, intertwining history with contemporary expression.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c9ac7d67ec4740a5b51c39c9c49e5d54</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210994</data>
    </node>
    <node id="1294">
      <data key="d0">1294</data>
      <data key="d1">time_period</data>
      <data key="d2">The year following the death of Kublai Khan, marking a significant change in the governance and sovereignty of the Mongol Empire into separate sovereign states.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-348d4b146145587a270aa17c65022fed</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210994</data>
    </node>
    <node id="The Year Following Kublai Khan's Death">
      <data key="d0">The Year Following Kublai Khan's Death</data>
      <data key="d1">time_period</data>
      <data key="d2">This period marks a significant change in the governance and sovereignty of the Mongol Empire into separate sovereign states.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-348d4b146145587a270aa17c65022fed</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210994</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Four Khanates of the Mongol Empire">
      <data key="d0">Four Khanates of the Mongol Empire</data>
      <data key="d1">geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">After the death of Kublai Khan, the empire fragmented into four distinct khanates: Il-Khanate, Chagatai Khanate, Khanate of the Golden Horde, and Yuan China.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-348d4b146145587a270aa17c65022fed</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210994</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Kublai Khan">
      <data key="d0">Kublai Khan</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">Kublai Khan, a grandson of Genghis Khan, ruled the Yuan Dynasty and was committed to upholding his grandfather's vision for Eurasian trade and unity. As the founder of the Yuan Dynasty, Kublai transferred the capital to Beijing, embraced Chinese naming conventions, and undertook the conquest of the Song dynasty to unify China under Mongol leadership.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-dfd43568e4817f7de7fa5fd0bbb3f2e0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-348d4b146145587a270aa17c65022fed</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210998</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ariq Boke">
      <data key="d0">Ariq Boke</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">A son of Möngke Khan, he claimed the throne against Kublai, representing a traditional nomadic culture opposed to settled life.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-348d4b146145587a270aa17c65022fed</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210994</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Settled Life vs. Nomadic Culture">
      <data key="d0">Settled Life vs. Nomadic Culture</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">The division between Mongols willing to adopt aspects of settled life and those who preferred nomadic traditions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-348d4b146145587a270aa17c65022fed</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210994</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mongol Empire Fragmentation">
      <data key="d0">Mongol Empire Fragmentation</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">The split of the Mongol Empire into separate khanates after Kublai Khan's death, each with distinct governance and culture.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-348d4b146145587a270aa17c65022fed</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210994</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Islam in the Mongol Empire">
      <data key="d0">Islam in the Mongol Empire</data>
      <data key="d1">cultural_or_religious_movement</data>
      <data key="d2">The adoption of Islam by some Mongol rulers, including those of the Il-Khanate, Chagatai Khanate, and Khanate of the Golden Horde, despite internal conflicts.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-348d4b146145587a270aa17c65022fed</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210994</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Yuan China">
      <data key="d0">Yuan China</data>
      <data key="d1">geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">A khanate within the broader context of the Yuan Dynasty ruled by Kublai Khan, focusing on integrating traditional Chinese culture with Mongol rule.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-348d4b146145587a270aa17c65022fed</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210994</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mongke Khan">
      <data key="d0">Mongke Khan</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">The third Great Khan who died in 1259, aiming to continue his grandfather's legacy but ultimately failing due to internal conflicts among his successors.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-348d4b146145587a270aa17c65022fed</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210994</data>
    </node>
    <node id="14th century">
      <data key="d0">14th century</data>
      <data key="d1">time_period</data>
      <data key="d2">The 14th century witnessed significant internal divisions within China as the Western Mongol Empire fragmented into four separate factions. This period, marked by the year 1300s, saw concurrent social and political turbulence both in China and among the former territories of the Western Mongol Empire. Additionally, an illustration dating from this era is available to provide visual insights into the cultural or artistic landscape of the time.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c71a838af77fbaa35a3faa2b0287f52d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-803afe7d2b36623c7ac458fe075bafa3</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210999</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;14th century&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"14th century"</data>
      <data key="d1">time_period</data>
      <data key="d2">The 14th century is a period where serious internal divisions in China were occurring while the Western Mongol Empire was fragmenting into four parts.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c71a838af77fbaa35a3faa2b0287f52d</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210994</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;Delhi Sultanate&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"Delhi Sultanate"</data>
      <data key="d1">civilization_or_empire</data>
      <data key="d2">The Delhi Sultanate refers to areas where sporadic efforts were made to expand against but failed, likely in a geographical context west of the Indus River.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c71a838af77fbaa35a3faa2b0287f52d</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210994</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;Kublai Khan&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"Kublai Khan"</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">Kublai Khan is known for conquering China and establishing the Yuan dynasty as the Great Khanate of Yuan China in the 1270s.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c71a838af77fbaa35a3faa2b0287f52d</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210994</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;Sufism&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"Sufism"</data>
      <data key="d1">cultural_or_religious_movement</data>
      <data key="d2">Sufism is described as a strain of Islam emphasizing asceticism and meditation for achieving divine understanding and deep reflection on God.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c71a838af77fbaa35a3faa2b0287f52d</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210994</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;Delhi Sultanate expansion&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"Delhi Sultanate expansion"</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_event</data>
      <data key="d2">The Delhi Sultanate expanded its territory, though largely unsuccessful, in the geographical context of areas west of the Indus River.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c71a838af77fbaa35a3faa2b0287f52d</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210994</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;Islamization of Mongols&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"Islamization of Mongols"</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_process</data>
      <data key="d2">The process through which Mongols embraced Islam under the influence of Sufism, Kublai Khan's rule, and broader religious changes during the 13th and 14th centuries.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c71a838af77fbaa35a3faa2b0287f52d</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210994</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;Internal Divisions in China&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"Internal Divisions in China"</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_event</data>
      <data key="d2">Serious internal divisions influenced the political stability of the Yuan Dynasty during Kublai Khan's rule.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c71a838af77fbaa35a3faa2b0287f52d</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210994</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;Delhi Sultanate expansion failure&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"Delhi Sultanate expansion failure"</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_event</data>
      <data key="d2">Failed attempts to expand Delhi Sultanate territory west of the Indus.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c71a838af77fbaa35a3faa2b0287f52d</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210994</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;Golden Horde fragmentation&quot;">
      <data key="d0">"Golden Horde fragmentation"</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_event</data>
      <data key="d2">The Golden Horde began to fragment into independent khanates after the death of Kublai Khan, reflecting the broader trend of Mongol Empire disintegration.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c71a838af77fbaa35a3faa2b0287f52d</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210994</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;source_or_artifact&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;source_or_artifact&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">source_or_artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">The Canterbury Tales, authored by Geoffrey Chaucer, is a collection of stories written in Middle English during the late 14th century. This work consists of various tales composed to be read or heard during a pilgrimage from London to Canterbury.

The Spirit of the Laws, penned by Montesquieu, is a political treatise published in 1748 that explores principles of governance and introduces the concept of separation of powers among different branches of government.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-89576c0d4225b9ce6218c684fd4f58b7</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783211000</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Kublai Khan&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Kublai Khan&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">Kublai Khan was the fifth Great Khan of the Mongol Empire and the founder and first emperor of the Yuan dynasty, which he ruled from 1260 to 1294. During his reign, he conducted significant military campaigns such as the invasions of Vietnam and Japan (though the latter failed), implemented administrative reforms, and experienced succession struggles which impacted the stability of the empire.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-475ef7f0df0edf6dfe1fe0174a98af67</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210994</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Marco Polo&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Marco Polo&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">Marco Polo was an Italian merchant, explorer, writer, and businessman who spent over 25 years under Mongol rule during the 13th century, playing a role as a hostage bureaucrat and traveling extensively throughout Asia. His numerous experiences and observations were compiled into "Il Milione," a detailed account of his adventures along the Silk Road and his time in China under Kublai Khan.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-330a687273081b34f8925b1af4e2c2b3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-475ef7f0df0edf6dfe1fe0174a98af67</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783211000</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Ibn Battuta&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Ibn Battuta&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">Ibn Battuta was a Moroccan scholar-traveler who extensively documented his travels through various regions of the Islamic world, including the Mamluk territories and the Yuan dynasty. His journeys enabled him to compile detailed records of the societal, economic, political, and religious structures prevalent during his time. These descriptions, while not explicitly mentioned in earlier texts, are inferred from his comprehensive accounts that provide valuable insights into the civilizations he encountered.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-224bdda0e90e6002ca09f2027f71a671&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-475ef7f0df0edf6dfe1fe0174a98af67</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783211003</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Yuan dynasty&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Yuan dynasty&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">civilization_or_empire</data>
      <data key="d2">The Yuan dynasty ruled China from 1271 to 1368 and was founded by Kublai Khan. It is known for implementing administrative segments based on racial divides, the reinstatement of a mandarins' exam system, economic policies including paper currency, and military expeditions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-475ef7f0df0edf6dfe1fe0174a98af67</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210997</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;paper currency&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;paper currency&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">Paper currency was introduced during the Yuan dynasty but led to inflation and economic instability due to excessive printing by the government. Its use and devaluation were managed through various fiscal policies in an attempt to control transactions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-475ef7f0df0edf6dfe1fe0174a98af67</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210997</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Khitan rules for administration of different types of people">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Khitan rules for administration of different types of people</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">The Khitan rulers established administrative practices segmented based on racial divides, which later influenced the Yuan dynasty's governance structure. This practice differentiated treatment and roles among various populations under Mongol rule.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-475ef7f0df0edf6dfe1fe0174a98af67</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210997</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Buyantu Khan&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Buyantu Khan&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">Buyantu Khan was a ruler of the Yuan dynasty who reinstated the mandarins' written exam system with adjusted regulations to accommodate non-Chinese subjects differently from traditional Han candidates.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-475ef7f0df0edf6dfe1fe0174a98af67</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210997</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Yunnan and Vietnam campaigns">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Yunnan and Vietnam campaigns</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_event</data>
      <data key="d2">Kublai Khan's military campaigns against Yunnan, parts of Southeast Asia, and his failed attempt at an invasion of Japan. These campaigns were costly and drained the economy, leading to inflation and financial instability.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-475ef7f0df0edf6dfe1fe0174a98af67</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210997</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Yuan census records">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Yuan census records</data>
      <data key="d1">source_or_artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">Censuses conducted during the Yuan dynasty provided comprehensive data on governance, demographics, and economic activities. These records played a crucial role in understanding administrative structures and implementing policies across diverse populations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-475ef7f0df0edf6dfe1fe0174a98af67</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210997</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;The Two Sicilies&quot;&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"The Two Sicilies"&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;geographical_location&gt;</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;"A kingdom in Southern Italy that experienced a significant shift towards secular governance through royal bureaucratic control and measures such as the Constitutions of Melfi, which restricted church influence."</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5477449078cf65c87acf77960989f513</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210997</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Constitutions of Melfi (1231)&quot;&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Constitutions of Melfi (1231)"&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;historical_concept_or_event&gt;</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;"The oldest surviving written constitution in the world, establishing a framework for increased monarchical power and centralized governance in the Two Sicilies by placing local administrative and judicial roles under royal bureaucrats and enhancing royal financial control through monopolies and taxation."</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5477449078cf65c87acf77960989f513</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210997</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;King Philip II&quot;&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"King Philip II"&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;historical_figure&gt;</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;"A French king who initiated significant restructuring of local government to strengthen the monarchy's control and resources, setting a precedent for later kings and impacting the church-state relationship in France. He was also involved in the conflict with Pope Boniface VIII."</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5477449078cf65c87acf77960989f513</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210997</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Pope Boniface VIII&quot;&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Pope Boniface VIII"&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;historical_figure&gt;</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;"The Pope who faced opposition from King Philip IV of France, culminating in an act that threatened papal authority and led to a crisis when a French successor was chosen for the papacy, moving the seat of power to Avignon."</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5477449078cf65c87acf77960989f513</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210997</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;King John&quot;&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"King John"&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;historical_figure&gt;</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;"An English king who faced significant challenges and limitations on royal power as described in the Magna Carta, which protected basic rights of commoners and established some early forms of parliamentary representation."</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5477449078cf65c87acf77960989f513</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210997</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Magna Carta (1215)&quot;&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Magna Carta (1215)"&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;source_or_artifact&gt;</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;"A seminal document that limited the monarch's power over vassals and church officials, protecting fundamental rights and establishing precedent for parliamentary representation in England from 1215 onwards."</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5477449078cf65c87acf77960989f513</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210997</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Almohad Caliphate&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Almohad Caliphate&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;civilization_or_empire&gt;</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;The Almohad Caliphate ruled over part of the Iberian Peninsula from the 1170s to the mid-13th century, with Imazighen (Berbers</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fb294c4779330d013102242472a1d584</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210997</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Third Crusade&quot;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Third Crusade"</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;time_period</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;"The Third Crusade (1189-1192) was a major military expedition by European王朝against the Islamic powers, primarily focusing on retaking Jerusalem from Saladin's Ayyubid dynasty. The crusade involved significant political and military figures of the time and marked a period of tension between Catholic Europe and the Muslim world."</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6364e06fdbc45845d82e5119ef391b50</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210997</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Fourth Crusade&quot;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Fourth Crusade"</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">The Fourth Crusade was a pivotal event in the history of the crusades, originally initiated with the goal of recapturing Jerusalem from Muslim control but became ensnared in intricate political intrigues within the Byzantine Empire. Although the expedition's initial target was Egypt, it was redirected to Constantinople, resulting in its siege and capture in 1204. The city's downfall led to its looting and the establishment of a Latin Empire, despite the intended religious focus of the crusade. This complex endeavor encompassed diverse motivations, including political maneuvering and religious zeal, coupled with substantial military engagements.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6364e06fdbc45845d82e5119ef391b50&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-18da3eb589b5768bb0689278b2d9503c</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783211005</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Levant&quot;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Levant"</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;"The Levant is a geographical region that includes parts of modern-day Lebanon, Syria, Israel, Palestine, Jordan, and Cyprus. During the Crusades, it was an area of significant strategic importance for both Christian and Muslim powers."</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6364e06fdbc45845d82e5119ef391b50</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210997</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Byzantine Empire&quot;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Byzantine Empire"</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;civilization_or_empire</data>
      <data key="d2">The Byzantine Empire, also officially known as the Eastern Roman Empire, was an empire centered on Byzantium or Constantinople, characterized by its Greek-speaking Christian populace. It endured from 330 to 1453 and played a crucial role during the Crusades, engaging in significant conflicts with both Western Christian kingdoms and Islamic powers. Additionally, the Byzantine Empire was noted for its pivotal position in safeguarding Western Europe and the Crusaders against Muslim invasions throughout its lengthy history.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6364e06fdbc45845d82e5119ef391b50&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-18da3eb589b5768bb0689278b2d9503c</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783211005</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Pope Innocent III&quot;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Pope Innocent III"</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;"Pope Innocent III (1161-1216) was a powerful pope who sought to maintain the focus of the Crusades on retaking Jerusalem from the Ayyubids, but his efforts were ultimately unsuccessful as he could not prevent the diversion of the Fourth Crusade to Constantinople."</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6364e06fdbc45845d82e5119ef391b50</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210998</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Alexius IV&quot;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Alexius IV"</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;"Alexius IV Angelos (1195-1196) was an emperor of Byzantium who attempted to gain support from the West through various means, including promising financial aid for the Crusades. He initially supported the Fourth Crusade but was overthrown and later executed."</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6364e06fdbc45845d82e5119ef391b50</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210998</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;installation of Alexius IV on the throne of Byzantium&quot;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"installation of Alexius IV on the throne of Byzantium"</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;"Alexius IV attempted to gain support from Western powers by making promises, leading to his installation as emperor. However, he faced internal opposition and was overthrown shortly after."</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6364e06fdbc45845d82e5119ef391b50</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210998</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;crusaders and Venetian merchants&quot;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"crusaders and Venetian merchants"</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;entities_group</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;"The crusaders and Venetian merchants were key participants in the Fourth Crusade, both seeking their own interests such as financial gain through conquests and support for Alexius IV's reign in exchange for aid."</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6364e06fdbc45845d82e5119ef391b50</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210998</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Zadar (in modern Croatia)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Zadar (in modern Croatia)"</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;"Zadar is a city on the Adriatic coast of present-day Croatia. It was an important target during the Fourth Crusade, where Venetian merchants and crusaders sought to repay debts through conquests."</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6364e06fdbc45845d82e5119ef391b50</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783210998</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Constantinople (Byzantium)&quot;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Constantinople (Byzantium)"</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;"The capital city of the Byzantine Empire, Constantinople, was at the heart of the conflicts during the Fourth Crusade. The Fourth Crusade famously ended with the sacking of the city in 1204."</data>
