    <node id="&lt;&quot;Niger River&quot;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Niger River"</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;"river</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;"The Niger River originates in the Guinea Highlands and flows through the interior of West Africa, emptying into the Atlantic Ocean. It plays a crucial role as a water source for regions like the western Sahara and Sahel, contributing to trading centers at Gao and Timbuktu."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1b09d86a943a3a531e994b41d0267303</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783211002</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Tropical Rainforest&quot;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Tropical Rainforest"</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;"environment</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;"The tropical rainforest covers about 2 million square miles of Africa, is rich in biodiversity including pygmy hippopotamuses, giant forest hogs, canopy monkeys, chimpanzees, and thousands of plant species. It lies south of the savanna."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1b09d86a943a3a531e994b41d0267303</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783211002</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Bantu&quot;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Bantu"</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;"civilization_or_empire</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;"The Bantu people began their migration possibly as early as 3000 BCE, spreading throughout southern Africa after acquiring ironworking technology from the Nok people. They developed self-sufficient economies and adapted local environments, maintaining a stateless society organized along kinship lines."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1b09d86a943a3a531e994b41d0267303</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783211002</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Nok people&quot;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Nok people"</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;"civilization_or_empire</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;"The Nok civilization was one of the earliest ironworking societies in West Africa, whose technological advancements were passed on to the Bantu people during their migration."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1b09d86a943a3a531e994b41d0267303</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783211002</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;tenth century&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;tenth century&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;time_period&gt;</data>
      <data key="d2">The tenth century is a time period in history that marks the transition from the late medieval to early medieval periods. During this time, Bantu kingdoms began to emerge and expand their influence in the Central African region.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-910128b658445e8077f4da4134f40da2</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783211002</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Central_African_rainforest&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Central_African_rainforest&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;geographical_location&gt;</data>
      <data key="d2">Central African rainforest is a tropical rainforest located primarily in the Congo Basin, spanning several countries including Angola, Democratic Republic of the Congo, and parts of Cameroon, Gabon, and others. The rainforest provides a rich ecosystem with significant biodiversity and supports early Bantu settlements.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-910128b658445e8077f4da4134f40da2</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783211002</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Bantu_kingdoms&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Bantu_kingdoms&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;civilization_or_empire&gt;</data>
      <data key="d2">Bantu kingdoms refer to political entities established by the Bantu peoples in Central Africa during the medieval period. These kingdoms, such as those in Congo and Zimbabwe, were characterized by large settlements, economic dominance, and cultural assimilation of earlier inhabitants.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-910128b658445e8077f4da4134f40da2</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783211002</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Neolithic_cultivation_and_animal_husbandry&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Neolithic_cultivation_and_animal_husbandry&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;historical_concept_or_event&gt;</data>
      <data key="d2">The introduction of Neolithic cultivation and animal husbandry during the tenth century facilitated agricultural practices among Bantu peoples, supporting their social and economic development in the region.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-910128b658445e8077f4da4134f40da2</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783211002</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Iron_Age_technologies&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Iron_Age_technologies&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;historical_concept_or_event&gt;</data>
      <data key="d2">Iron Age technologies, tools, and weapons were adopted by Bantu kingdoms during the tenth century, leading to advancements in agriculture, warfare, and overall societal organization.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-910128b658445e8077f4da4134f40da2</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783211002</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Limpopo River&quot;&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Limpopo River"&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;"geographical_location"&gt;</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;"The Limpopo River is a major river in southern Africa, stretching approximately 1,609 km from north to south. It forms part of the boundaries between several countries including South Africa, Botswana, Zimbabwe, and Mozambique.")</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9885f9e0c1965c95ff90139b9fdaedf2</data>
