    <node id="&lt;entity_name&gt;Mali Empire">
      <data key="d0">&lt;entity_name&gt;Mali Empire</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;entity_type&gt;civilization_or_empire</data>
      <data key="d2">The Mali Empire was formed under Sundiata's leadership in the 13th century. It expanded through strategic alliances and conquests, becoming a major West African empire with significant control over trade routes, particularly the trans-Saharan trade. The empire was known for its diverse economy, including gold mining, agriculture, and religious practices such as the spread of Islam.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fc3ce939e47db3c28ccabb86ce4b9084</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783211005</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;entity_name&gt;Sundiata">
      <data key="d0">&lt;entity_name&gt;Sundiata</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;entity_type&gt;historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">Sundiata was a key leader in the formation of the Mali Empire. He convinced other Malinke kings to surrender their title, mansa, and thus unified the region under his rule, becoming the sole mansa and religious and secular leader over all Malinke people.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fc3ce939e47db3c28ccabb86ce4b9084</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783211005</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;entity_name&gt;Niani">
      <data key="d0">&lt;entity_name&gt;Niani</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;entity_type&gt;geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">Niani is a city located in the southern savanna country of the upper Niger valley. It served as the imperial capital of Mali, advantageous for its location near goldfields and agricultural areas.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fc3ce939e47db3c28ccabb86ce4b9084</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783211005</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;entity_name&gt;Bure Goldfields">
      <data key="d0">&lt;entity_name&gt;Bure Goldfields</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;entity_type&gt;geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">The Bure goldfields are situated in the region controlled by the Mali Empire. They were a crucial source of gold dust which was traded along the trans-Saharan routes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fc3ce939e47db3c28ccabb86ce4b9084</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783211005</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;entity_name&gt;Upper Niger Valley">
      <data key="d0">&lt;entity_name&gt;Upper Niger Valley</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;entity_type&gt;geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">The upper Niger valley is where Niani, the imperial capital of the Mali Empire, is located. It also contains rich agricultural and gold regions, contributing to Mali's economic wealth.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fc3ce939e47db3c28ccabb86ce4b9084</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783211005</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;entity_name&gt;Sahelian Grasslands">
      <data key="d0">&lt;entity_name&gt;Sahelian Grasslands</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;entity_type&gt;geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">Sahelian grasslands are drier than the southern savanna and located more northerly. They were used for grazing camels, sheep, and goats, contributing to the Mali Empire's economy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fc3ce939e47db3c28ccabb86ce4b9084</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783211005</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;entity_name&gt;Gambia Valley">
      <data key="d0">&lt;entity_name&gt;Gambia Valley</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;entity_type&gt;geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">The Gambia valley is known for its fertile lands where rice flourished, adding diversity to Mali's agricultural sector.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fc3ce939e47db3c28ccabb86ce4b9084</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783211005</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;entity_name&gt;Farming Communities">
      <data key="d0">&lt;entity_name&gt;Farming Communities</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;entity_type&gt;civilization_or_empire</data>
      <data key="d2">Farming communities within the Mali Empire practiced different crops suited to their environment. Sorghum and millet grew on the savannah, while rice thrived in the Gambia valley and around the upper Niger floodplain.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fc3ce939e47db3c28ccabb86ce4b9084</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783211005</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Taghaza">
      <data key="d0">Taghaza</data>
      <data key="d1">geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">Description of Taghaza housing in rock and salt houses with roofs of camel skins, located in a sandy environment with no trees and containing a salt mine.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-653e423a3526fec9af080bbf288c37ac</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783211005</data>
    </node>
    <node id="gold and salt trade">
      <data key="d0">gold and salt trade</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">Trade between Mali Empire and other regions involving gold and salt, crucial for the wealth of Mali during its zenith under Mansa Musa’s rule and after, significantly impacting regional economics and history.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-653e423a3526fec9af080bbf288c37ac</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783211005</data>
    </node>
    <node id="mansa">
      <data key="d0">mansa</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">Title given to rulers of the Mali Empire, including notable figures like Mansa Musa who engaged in significant political, economic, and charitable activities during his reign.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-653e423a3526fec9af080bbf288c37ac</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783211005</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Catalan Atlas">
      <data key="d0">Catalan Atlas</data>
      <data key="d1">source_or_artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">A medieval map that provided visual representation during the 14th century, featuring Mansa Musa's depiction by Majorcan mapmaker Abraham Cresques.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-653e423a3526fec9af080bbf288c37ac</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783211005</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Abraham Cresques">
      <data key="d0">Abraham Cresques</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">A prominent Catalan mapmaker active in the 14th century who contributed to the creation of important geographical texts and was known for his depictions such as those on the Catalan Atlas.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-653e423a3526fec9af080bbf288c37ac</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783211005</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mali">
      <data key="d0">Mali</data>
      <data key="d1">civilization_or_empire</data>
      <data key="d2">By 1438, Mali was a civilization that had lost control over its key northern cities, specifically Gao and Timbuktu. However, Mali still maintained some level of influence and control over its traditional heartland in the Mande region as well as the southern grasslands. This description highlights the geographical extent of Mali's power dynamics at the time, emphasizing both its losses and persisting influence.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0574b1f8c885767e594c6d93856176c8</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783211009</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Timbuktu">
      <data key="d0">Timbuktu</data>
      <data key="d1">geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">Timbuktu, a city that was once an important center of trade and learning, was abandoned along with Gao by the Mali Empire in 1438. This abandonment marks a significant shift in the historical landscape of the region, as both cities played crucial roles prior to their joint desertion.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0574b1f8c885767e594c6d93856176c8</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783211010</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Sundiata's Alliance">
      <data key="d0">Sundiata's Alliance</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">Sundiata's Alliance is a term used to describe a series of political and military alliances that emerged in an earlier historical period. One key event associated with this concept involves the kingdom of Mema, which gained independence as part of this alliance following the year 1438. The repeated descriptions highlight the importance of these alliances in the political landscape of the region during a significant时间段，这些联盟在该地区的政治格局中占据重要地位。</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0574b1f8c885767e594c6d93856176c8</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783211012</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mema">
      <data key="d0">Mema</data>
      <data key="d1">cultural_or_religious_movement</data>
      <data key="d2">Mema is a kingdom that broke away from Sundiata's Alliance and became independent in 1438, showing early signs of emerging autonomy within the empire.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0574b1f8c885767e594c6d93856176c8</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783211006</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Songhai Empire">
      <data key="d0">Songhai Empire</data>
      <data key="d1">civilization_or_empire</data>
      <data key="d2">The Songhai Empire was one of the significant medieval kingdoms in southern Africa, emerging as a powerful entity that rivaled the Malian Empire during its golden age. Centered around the city of Gao, the empire played a crucial role in controlling vital trade routes across the Sahara following the decline of Mali. Notably, the founding and development of the Songhai Empire are not attributed to Bantu-speaking peoples, indicating a distinct historical and cultural background for this influential African kingdom.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0574b1f8c885767e594c6d93856176c8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ad97ea1ef8e2c731dddc24e3eecbe4f2</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783211012</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Zimbabwean Plateau">
      <data key="d0">Zimbabwean Plateau</data>
      <data key="d1">geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">The Zimbabwean Plateau is an area in southern Africa where Bantu migration led to significant cultural developments during the Later Iron Age from 900-1600 CE.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0574b1f8c885767e594c6d93856176c8</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783211006</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Later Iron Age (c. 900–1600)">
      <data key="d0">Later Iron Age (c. 900–1600)</data>
      <data key="d1">time_period</data>
      <data key="d2">The period known as the Later Iron Age, spanning approximately 900 to 1600 CE, saw significant cultural shifts and developments in southern Africa, including the rise of Mapungubwe.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0574b1f8c885767e594c6d93856176c8</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783211006</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bantu Migration">
      <data key="d0">Bantu Migration</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_event</data>
      <data key="d2">During the Later Iron Age, Bantu groups migrated from central Africa to areas like the Zimbabwean Plateau, leading to the development of various states such as Mapungubwe and Great Zimbabwe.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0574b1f8c885767e594c6d93856176c8</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783211006</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mapungubwe Kingdom">
      <data key="d0">Mapungubwe Kingdom</data>
      <data key="d1">civilization_or_empire</data>
      <data key="d2">Mapungubwe was a kingdom that developed in southern Africa between the 11th and 13th centuries, marked by complex social structures and terracing farming methods for soil conservation on the Zimbabwean Plateau.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0574b1f8c885767e594c6d93856176c8</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783211006</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Great Zimbabwe Kingdom">
      <data key="d0">Great Zimbabwe Kingdom</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">Great Zimbabwe is often considered an advanced state and a cultural center that succeeded Mapungubwe in southern Africa, reflecting more mature societal structures compared to earlier states.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0574b1f8c885767e594c6d93856176c8</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783211006</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Leopard's Kopje">
      <data key="d0">Leopard's Kopje</data>
      <data key="d1">cultural_or_religious_movement</data>
      <data key="d2">Leopard's Kopje likely refers to both a specific location and an individual associated with early mining operations at Mapungubwe, although this could also denote a cattle-keeping culture that initiated the origins of Mapungubwe around the tenth century on the Zimbabwean Plateau. This culture practiced a mixed economy involving livestock and agriculture, contributing significantly to the beginnings of Mapungubwe as an important historical settlement.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0574b1f8c885767e594c6d93856176c8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ef5829225aa0fe5157b728e0142f7c68</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783211013</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Terracing Farming">
      <data key="d0">Terracing Farming</data>
      <data key="d1">technique</data>
      <data key="d2">Mapungubwe developed terrace farming as an innovative agricultural technique to prevent soil erosion on the southern slopes of the large sandstone plateau in the 12th century.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0574b1f8c885767e594c6d93856176c8</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783211008</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mapela Hill">
      <data key="d0">Mapela Hill</data>
      <data key="d1">geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">Mapela Hill is a site where, during the twelfth century, Mapungubwe established extensive terrace farming and other stone structures for various purposes including housing, defense, and cultivation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0574b1f8c885767e594c6d93856176c8</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783211008</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Southern African Societal Evolution">
      <data key="d0">Southern African Societal Evolution</data>
      <data key="d1">cultural_context</data>
      <data key="d2">The socio-cultural context of southern Africa during this period involved complex interactions among various groups, leading to diverse cultural expressions including the development of significant cities like Mapungubwe and Great Zimbabwe.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0574b1f8c885767e594c6d93856176c8</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783211008</data>
    </node>
    <node id="LaterIronAge (c. 900–1600)">
      <data key="d0">LaterIronAge (c. 900–1600)</data>
      <data key="d1">TimePeriod</data>
      <data key="d2">The period known as the Later Iron Age, spanning approximately 900 to 1600 CE, saw significant cultural shifts and developments in southern Africa, including the rise of Mapungubwe.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0574b1f8c885767e594c6d93856176c8</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783211008</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mapungubwe">
      <data key="d0">Mapungubwe</data>
      <data key="d1">geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">Mapungubwe, a region in southern Africa, was an important center during the Later Iron Age that significantly contributed to cultural and economic development. Specifically, Mapungubwe functioned as a medieval kingdom from around 1200 to 1300 AD, flourishing under Bantu-speaking peoples who introduced innovations and facilitated regional trade networks.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ad97ea1ef8e2c731dddc24e3eecbe4f2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ef5829225aa0fe5157b728e0142f7c68</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783211013</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Great Zimbabwe">
      <data key="d0">Great Zimbabwe</data>
      <data key="d1">geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">Great Zimbabwe, like Mapungubwe, was a significant center during the Later Iron Age in southern Africa.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ef5829225aa0fe5157b728e0142f7c68</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783211008</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Later Iron Age">
      <data key="d0">Later Iron Age</data>
      <data key="d1">time_period</data>
      <data key="d2">The Later Iron Age is a distinct period characterized by significant developments such as the growth of cattle herds, agricultural innovations like cotton cultivation, and gold mining activities.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ef5829225aa0fe5157b728e0142f7c68</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783211009</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Gold Mining">
      <data key="d0">Gold Mining</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">Gold mining during the Later Iron Age included innovative techniques for extracting and processing gold from rich deposits in southern Africa.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ef5829225aa0fe5157b728e0142f7c68</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783211009</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mapungubwe Culture">
      <data key="d0">Mapungubwe Culture</data>
      <data key="d1">civilization_or_empire</data>
      <data key="d2">The culture of Mapungubwe, a significant civilization or empire in the region, developed complex economic and social structures that facilitated long-distance trade.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ef5829225aa0fe5157b728e0142f7c68</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783211009</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ivory and Animal Hides Trade">
      <data key="d0">Ivory and Animal Hides Trade</data>
      <data key="d1">textbook_structure</data>
      <data key="d2">The discovery of ivory splinters and animal hides at Mapungubwe supports the existence of an established trade network for these goods.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ef5829225aa0fe5157b728e0142f7c68</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783211009</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Great Enclosure">
      <data key="d0">Great Enclosure</data>
      <data key="d1">geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">The Great Enclosure, dating from the thirteenth to fourteenth centuries, is a significant ancient monument in Zimbabwe and one of the largest in Africa south of the Sahara. It features a drystone wall that is elliptical in shape and slightly inward-pitched at the top.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a1f6ebe577e7c514981ac9ee685fefde</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783211009</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hill Complex">
      <data key="d0">Hill Complex</data>
      <data key="d1">geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">Another important site near the Great Enclosure, possibly used for religious or military purposes based on archaeological findings.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a1f6ebe577e7c514981ac9ee685fefde</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783211009</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Valley Ruins">
      <data key="d0">Valley Ruins</data>
      <data key="d1">source_or_artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">These ruins lie between the Great Enclosure and the Hill Complex, suggesting significant growth in the settlement during the height of Great Zimbabwe's flourishing. They may have been part of a dense urban area.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a1f6ebe577e7c514981ac9ee685fefde</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783211009</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Great Zimbabwe flourished">
      <data key="d0">Great Zimbabwe flourished</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_event</data>
      <data key="d2">This period saw the construction of impressive stone structures, the establishment of a hierarchical civilization, and the presence of luxury artifacts.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a1f6ebe577e7c514981ac9ee685fefde</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783211009</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Zimbabwean civilization">
      <data key="d0">Zimbabwean civilization</data>
      <data key="d1">civilization_or_empire</data>
      <data key="d2">The Zimbabwean civilization refers to the society that constructed the Great Enclosure during the thirteenth to fourteenth centuries. This period saw major renovations of the Great Enclosure, including the addition of its elliptical drystone wall.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a1f6ebe577e7c514981ac9ee685fefde</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783211009</data>
    </node>
    <node id="drystone wall">
      <data key="d0">drystone wall</data>
      <data key="d1">source_or_artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">The main feature of the Great Enclosure is a drystone wall that is elliptical in shape and slightly inward-pitched at the top.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a1f6ebe577e7c514981ac9ee685fefde</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783211009</data>
    </node>
    <node id="1325-1430">
      <data key="d0">1325-1430</data>
      <data key="d1">time_period</data>
      <data key="d2">This period covers the annexation of Gao by Mali and subsequent struggles that caused the Malian rulers to withdraw from Gao in the 1430s.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-915948e7d5ef4ba882727621163189e3</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783211009</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Timbuktu&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Timbuktu&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;geographical_location&gt;</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;Timbuktu is situated on the Niger River, known for its grain production, livestock, and trade in salt from Tegaza.&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-42a7884af18b1be9360e3134246f2ec8</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783211009</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Tegaza&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Tegaza&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;geographical_location&gt;</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;Tegaza is the distant source of salt for Timbuktu, located about 500 miles away from the city.&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-42a7884af18b1be9360e3134246f2ec8</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783211009</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Islamic expansion&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Islamic expansion&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;historical_concept_or_event&gt;</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;Islamic armies expanded control over North Africa starting from the late seventh century, leading to further conquests and cultural changes.&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-42a7884af18b1be9360e3134246f2ec8</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783211009</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Kahina's defeat&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Kahina's defeat&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;historical_event&gt;</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;The defeat of al-Kahina opened up routes into Western Niger, impacting the spread of Islamic influence.&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-42a7884af18b1be9360e3134246f2ec8</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783211009</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Fatimids&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Fatimids&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;civilization_or_empire&gt;</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;The Fatimids established unified Islamic rulership after nearly two centuries, unifying North Africa under their rule.&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-42a7884af18b1be9360e3134246f2ec8</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783211009</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Leo Africanus&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Leo Africanus&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;historical_figure&gt;</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;Leonardo Alansari, commonly known as Leo Africanus, was an explorer and writer who provided detailed accounts of Timbuktu and other regions.&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-42a7884af18b1be9360e3134246f2ec8</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783211009</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;time_periodAlmoravid period&quot;&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"time_periodAlmoravid period"&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">time_period</data>
      <data key="d2">The Almoravid period during which the Almoravids founded their empire and expanded it to include various locations in North Africa and parts of Spain, known for their Islamic reforms and zealous religious approach</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-896a5fa643e1e53896e494a5806c81e7</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783211009</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;geographical_locationDjuddala&quot;&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"geographical_locationDjuddala"&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">A region controlled by the Almoravids as part of their expansion in North Africa, known for its strategic position along trade routes</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-896a5fa643e1e53896e494a5806c81e7</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783211009</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;geographical_locationLamtuna&quot;&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"geographical_locationLamtuna"&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">A confederation and Berber tribe allied with the Almoravids during their rise to power in North Africa</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-896a5fa643e1e53896e494a5806c81e7</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783211009</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;geographical_locationMaghreb&quot;&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"geographical_locationMaghreb"&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">The region of North Africa ruled by the Almoravids, including present-day Morocco, Algeria, and Tunisia, which they expanded into as part of their empire-building efforts</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-896a5fa643e1e53896e494a5806c81e7</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783211009</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;geographical_locationSijilmasa&quot;&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"geographical_locationSijilmasa"&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">A key city controlled by the Almoravids along the trans-Saharan trade route, serving as a strategic point for capturing and controlling commercial activity between North Africa and sub-Saharan regions</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-896a5fa643e1e53896e494a5806c81e7</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783211009</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;geographical_locationAwdaghost&quot;&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"geographical_locationAwdaghost"&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">Another important city in the Almoravid Empire located on the trans-Saharan trade route, known for its control over the movement of goods and people in that region</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-896a5fa643e1e53896e494a5806c81e7</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783211009</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;geographical_locationFez modern-day Morocco&quot;&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"geographical_locationFez modern-day Morocco"&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">Fez, a significant geographical location in modern-day Morocco, was the capital of the Almoravids before they shifted their capital to Marrakesh. This city played a crucial role in North Africa during the early expansion efforts of the Almoravid dynasty. The relocation from Fez to Marrakesh marked a key moment in the consolidation of centralized governance and administration within the expanding state network under Almoravid rule, reflecting broader changes in the organizational structure of their empire.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-896a5fa643e1e53896e494a5806c81e7</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783211015</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;geographical_locationMarrakesh&quot;&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"geographical_locationMarrakesh"&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">The capital of the Almoravid Empire and later the Almohad Empire, symbolizing the centralization of power during these periods</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-896a5fa643e1e53896e494a5806c81e7</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783211009</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;geographical_locationZaragoza on the Ebro River in Spain&quot;&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"geographical_locationZaragoza on the Ebro River in Spain"&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">A significant city captured by the Almoravids as part of their invasion into Umayyad Spain, marking a northern extension of their influence and control</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-896a5fa643e1e53896e494a5806c81e7</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783211009</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;civilization_or_empireAlmoravids Empire&quot;&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"civilization_or_empireAlmoravids Empire"&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">civilization_or_empire</data>
      <data key="d2">The empire founded by Islamic reformers who emerged from the Masmuda tribe to build a powerful state in North Africa and parts of Spain, characterized by zealous religious practices and expansionist policies</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-896a5fa643e1e53896e494a5806c81e7</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783211009</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;civilization_or_empireAlmohads Empire&quot;&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"civilization_or_empireAlmohads Empire"&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">civilization_or_empire</data>
      <data key="d2">The empire founded by the successors to the Almoravids who continued their aggressive expansion and Islamic reforms, including significant military conquests in parts of North Africa and Spain</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-896a5fa643e1e53896e494a5806c81e7</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783211010</data>
