    <node id="Almoravids">
      <data key="d0">Almoravids</data>
      <data key="d1">civilization_or_empire</data>
      <data key="d2">Almoravids were a Muslim dynasty that ruled parts of the Maghreb and Al-Andalus from the 11th to mid-12th centuries.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bcb575f31b05c6035003e7300c15bfb5</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783211010</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Masmuda nomads">
      <data key="d0">Masmuda nomads</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">Masmuda nomads lived in the Atlas Mountains and played a crucial role in the formation of the Almohad movement under Ibn Tumart.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bcb575f31b05c6035003e7300c15bfb5</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783211010</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mali Empire">
      <data key="d0">Mali Empire</data>
      <data key="d1">civilization_or_empire</data>
      <data key="d2">Mali Empire was a powerful West African kingdom that flourished from the 13th to the 14th century, known for its vast gold fields and active salt trade, marking a significant transformation from earlier Ghanaians' influence under the leadership of Sundiata Keita. The Mali Empire reached its pinnacle of power during this period, becoming the largest and richest state in medieval Africa. Governed by the Masa ruling dynasty, which included Mansa Musa's brother's reign, the empire expanded through strategic conquests of recently subdued states.

Although Mali Empire is an option among West African kingdoms, it did not specifically originate with the Sanhaja and Masmuda groups as per the provided information. Nevertheless, internal challenges eventually led to its decline.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-653e423a3526fec9af080bbf288c37ac&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-42e94d3dccf53e38234edf815e468339&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d32d7ef2fac445505a9bd6f9b878a6f4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ad97ea1ef8e2c731dddc24e3eecbe4f2</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783211011</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ibn Tumart">
      <data key="d0">Ibn Tumart</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">Ibn Tumart was a religious leader who initiated the Almohad movement by recruiting the Masemuda tribes into his cause to oppose the Almoravids.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bcb575f31b05c6035003e7300c15bfb5</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783211010</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Almohad Empire">
      <data key="d0">Almohad Empire</data>
      <data key="d1">civilization_or_empire</data>
      <data key="d2">The Almohad Empire was a historical Islamic empire that controlled parts of North Africa and al-Andalus (present-day Spain) during the 13th century. Founded by Ibn Tumart, the empire succeeded the Almoravids in overseeing key territories such as Marrakesh, Spain, and Morocco. The Almohads were established with the goal of promoting the spread of Malikite legalism and expanded significantly from their base in Marrakesh, covering a large portion of North Africa and Muslim Spain around 1200.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-5f105692039d53c800b15e8de2086313&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bcb575f31b05c6035003e7300c15bfb5</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783211016</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Marrakesh">
      <data key="d0">Marrakesh</data>
      <data key="d1">geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">Marrakesh served as the capital during key periods in the Almohad Empire’s history, notably when it was captured from the Almoravids in 1147.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bcb575f31b05c6035003e7300c15bfb5</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783211010</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Atlas Mountains">
      <data key="d0">Atlas Mountains</data>
      <data key="d1">geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">The Atlas Mountains presented a challenge for the Almoravid armies but were home to the Masemuda nomads, who helped form the Almohad movement.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bcb575f31b05c6035003e7300c15bfb5</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783211010</data>
    </node>
