    </node>
    <node id="Kublai Khan's Rule">
      <data key="d0">Kublai Khan's Rule</data>
      <data key="d1">time_period</data>
      <data key="d2">The period during which Kublai Khan ruled over the Yuan dynasty and attempted to unify and control the Mongol Empire, particularly focusing on China.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-050e5acc95e8e8a8dba391a9f249062f</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783211012</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mongol Empire Splintering">
      <data key="d0">Mongol Empire Splintering</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_event</data>
      <data key="d2">The process where the vast Mongol Empire fragmented into four separate khanates following internal conflicts and external pressures.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-050e5acc95e8e8a8dba391a9f249062f</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783211012</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Yuan Dynasty Succession Crisis">
      <data key="d0">Yuan Dynasty Succession Crisis</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A critical moment during which there was confusion and strife over who would succeed Kublai Khan after his death in 1294, leading to political instability and vulnerability.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-050e5acc95e8e8a8dba391a9f249062f</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783211013</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mongol Taxation Practices">
      <data key="d0">Mongol Taxation Practices</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">The policies of taxation and land expropriation used by Mongol authorities that contributed significantly to economic tensions and resistance within the empire.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-050e5acc95e8e8a8dba391a9f249062f</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783211013</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Chinese Peasant Unrest">
      <data key="d0">Chinese Peasant Unrest</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Widespread local rebellions driven by Chinese subjects' dissatisfaction with unfavorable governmental policies, particularly high taxes and land grabs, during the Yuan dynasty.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-050e5acc95e8e8a8dba391a9f249062f</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783211013</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Yuan Dynasty Collapse">
      <data key="d0">Yuan Dynasty Collapse</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_event</data>
      <data key="d2">The formal end of the Yuan dynasty in 1368, marking its complete collapse and transition to the Ming dynasty.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-050e5acc95e8e8a8dba391a9f249062f</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783211013</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Il-Khanate's Leadership Shift">
      <data key="d0">Il-Khanate's Leadership Shift</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">"A significant political change where Mongol leadership moved from a remote detachment to full assimilation into local governance structures in the Il-Khanate during the early 1300s.")</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-050e5acc95e8e8a8dba391a9f249062f</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783211013</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Middle East and North Africa under Mongol Control">
      <data key="d0">Middle East and North Africa under Mongol Control</data>
      <data key="d1">geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">An area with Mongol influence extending to the Middle East and central Asia, characterized by political instability and complex relationships with local populations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-050e5acc95e8e8a8dba391a9f249062f</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783211013</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Il-Khanate&quot;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Il-Khanate"</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;"historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;"The Il-Khanate is a historical administrative division and political entity that emerged after the Mongol conquests in Iran, Iraq, Egypt, and parts of Turkey. It served as an autonomous branch of the Mongol Empire under its own dynasty, the Il-khans, who were primarily descendants of Hulagu Khan, Genghis Khan's grandson. The Il-Khanate existed from approximately 1256 to around 1335 and was characterized by a blend of Mongol administrative practices with local Persian cultural elements. Notable rulers include Abu Sa‘id (died in 1335) and Mahmud Ghazan, who played crucial roles in the political and religious history of the region."&lt;&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c8c392726f8376468eaf5e1d578baaa5</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783211013</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Abu Said&quot;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Abu Said"</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;"historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;"Abu Sa'id was an Il-Khanate ruler from around 1304 to 1335. He is notable for the stability and relative peace he brought to his realm during a period of political turmoil, but also for his limited achievements in reforming the administration or strengthening Mongol culture."&lt;&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c8c392726f8376468eaf5e1d578baaa5</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783211013</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Mahmud Ghazan&quot;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Mahmud Ghazan"</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;"historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;"Mahmud Ghazan was an Il-Khanate ruler who ascended to power in 1295 and reigned until his death in 1304. He is most famous for converting to Islam, a significant moment that marked the shift of the Il-Khanate from a predominantly Buddhist-Mongol state to one influenced by Islamic culture."&lt;&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c8c392726f8376468eaf5e1d578baaa5</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783211013</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Ayurbarwada&quot;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Ayurbarwada"</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;"historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;"Ayurbarwada was an Emperor of the Yuan Dynasty, a Chinese empire that ruled much of East Asia. He is known for his efforts in restoring Confucianism and civil service examination systems in China after a period of Mongol rule."&lt;&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c8c392726f8376468eaf5e1d578baaa5</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783211013</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;&quot;Jani Beg&quot;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Jani Beg"</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;"historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;"Jani Beg was a ruler of the Golden Horde, a significant state that ruled much of Eastern Europe and Central Asia. He is noted for his religious policies, which included conversion to Islam towards the end of his reign."&lt;&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c8c392726f8376468eaf5e1d578baaa5</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783211013</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Il-Khanate regime">
      <data key="d0">Il-Khanate regime</data>
      <data key="d1">time_period</data>
      <data key="d2">"Il-Khanate regime is a time period representing the rulers and their rule in the 13th-14th centuries, specifically under Mongol control."|</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9962856ee7ea0d7a7a21ef65c30faca6</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783211013</data>
    </node>
    <node id="High Sophistication of Islamic Scientific Scholarship and Mechanical Knowledge">
      <data key="d0">High Sophistication of Islamic Scientific Scholarship and Mechanical Knowledge</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">"High sophistication of Islamic scientific scholarship and mechanical knowledge at the height of the Mamluk Empire, exemplified by al-Jazari’s elephant clock."|</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c136dfeff6dede53a0a73af631e7957d</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783211013</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cairo">
      <data key="d0">Cairo</data>
      <data key="d1">geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">Cairo served as a prominent center of Muslim intellectual culture and architecture during the Mamluk era.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c136dfeff6dede53a0a73af631e7957d</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783211013</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mamluk Empire">
      <data key="d0">Mamluk Empire</data>
      <data key="d1">civilization_or_empire</data>
      <data key="d2">The Mamluk Empire controlled regions including access to the eastern Mediterranean, the Red Sea, the Nile, and holy cities such as Mecca, Medina, and Jerusalem. This empire was prominent from 1260 to 1341 and experienced periods of both prosperity and instability under the leadership of figures like Sultan al-Nasir Muhammad.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-065db1aa182e4a6eb02755ddc80375ab&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c136dfeff6dede53a0a73af631e7957d</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783211018</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Death of Sultan al-Nasir Muhammad">
      <data key="d0">Death of Sultan al-Nasir Muhammad</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">The death of Sultan al-Nasir Muhammad in 1341 marked an end to a period of Mamluk prosperity and preceded the empire's decline.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c136dfeff6dede53a0a73af631e7957d</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783211013</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ottomans">
      <data key="d0">Ottomans</data>
      <data key="d1">civilization_or_empire</data>
      <data key="d2">The Ottoman Empire was a powerful state that emerged as one of the major forces in the later part of the Middle Ages and the beginning of the Early Modern Period. It succeeded both the Mamluk Empire in the sixteenth century after defeating them and the Byzantine Empire, establishing itself as a significant gatekeeper between East and West. At its peak, the Ottoman Empire was rivals with Timur, leading to notable conflicts such as the Battle of Ankara. This empire continued to grow in territory and influence throughout this period, significantly impacting the region both politically and culturally.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c136dfeff6dede53a0a73af631e7957d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-325c2f087ce183f26e739c5d7d7a119e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-72d7bb40fa0aa36bf5b0bb66bf7975fe</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783211019</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Early Fourteenth Century Europe">
      <data key="d0">Early Fourteenth Century Europe</data>
      <data key="d1">time_period</data>
      <data key="d2">The early fourteenth century was marked by widespread crises, including crop failures and food shortages leading to profound societal transformations across Europe.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c136dfeff6dede53a0a73af631e7957d</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783211013</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Crop Failures and Food Shortages">
      <data key="d0">Crop Failures and Food Shortages</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">Worsening weather conditions during the early 14th century led to significant food shortages and decreased crop yields in Europe.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c136dfeff6dede53a0a73af631e7957d</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783211013</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Europe">
      <data key="d0">Europe</data>
      <data key="d1">geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">Europe is depicted as a region experiencing political fragmentation during the beginning of the fourteenth century.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1c35939e482e2a77512480fac9dae902</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783211013</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Papal Authority">
      <data key="d0">Papal Authority</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">The Papal Authority is challenged by increasing tensions with national monarchs, leading to a weakening of papal power and internal church divisions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1c35939e482e2a77512480fac9dae902</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783211013</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Conflict between Pope Boniface VIII and King Philip IV of France">
      <data key="d0">Conflict between Pope Boniface VIII and King Philip IV of France</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A notable conflict occurs in 1303 when King Philip IV of France attempts to impose taxes on the clergy without papal approval, resulting in a confrontation over ecclesiastical authority.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1c35939e482e2a77512480fac9dae902</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783211013</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Papacy in Avignon">
      <data key="d0">Papacy in Avignon</data>
      <data key="d1">location</data>
      <data key="d2">Despite political autonomy and bureaucratic structures, the Papacy's relocation to Avignon in 1309 further weakens its spiritual prestige and leads to doubt about church administration.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1c35939e482e2a77512480fac9dae902</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783211013</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Great Western Schism&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Great Western Schism&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;historical_event&gt;</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;The Great Western Schism was a period of Catholic Church history from 1378 to 1417, during which there were multiple popes claiming legitimacy. This schism significantly affected the unity and authority of the papacy, leading to the Council of Constance in 1415 where two of these claimants agreed to resign, paving way for a new pope.&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6970ff4a745f78f3ab3bcab2da4945d1</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783211013</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Hundred Years' War&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Hundred Years' War&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;conflict&gt;</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;The Hundred Years' War was a protracted conflict between England and France that lasted from 1337 until 1453. It was marked by numerous battles, treaties, and interruptions, primarily over claims of English sovereignty in France. Major technological developments like longbows and firearms played significant roles. The war ended with the recognition of Charles VII as king of France, leaving England a minor player in European power dynamics.&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6970ff4a745f78f3ab3bcab2da4945d1</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783211014</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;The LandofCockaigne&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;The LandofCockaigne&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;cultural_or_religious_movement&gt;</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;"The Land of Cockaigne" is an English satirical poem that depicts a place of extreme luxury and abundance, used as a criticism of the clergy's perceived wealth and greed during times of food scarcity. It served as an allegory for the moral decay of society and religious institutions.&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6970ff4a745f78f3ab3bcab2da4945d1</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783211014</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Holy Roman Empire&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Holy Roman Empire&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;civilization_or_empire&gt;</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;The Holy Roman Empire was a multi-ethnic complex of territories in Central Europe that developed during the Early Middle Ages, founded by Charlemagne, and lasted until its dissolution in 1806. It included entities like Italy, Germany, Burgundy, and Bohemia, selected through an elective process amongst noble factions, leading to frequent instability among competing powers.&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6970ff4a745f78f3ab3bcab2da4945d1</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783211014</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Pope Gregory XI&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Pope Gregory XI&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;historical_figure&gt;</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;Pope Gregory XI (1370–1378) served as the 209th pope and was instrumental in ending the conflict between Rome and Avignon, bringing the papal court back to Rome. His efforts laid the groundwork for later reforms and helped improve church unity.&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6970ff4a745f78f3ab3bcab2da4945d1</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783211014</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;King Charles IV of France&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;King Charles IV of France&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;historical_figure&gt;</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;Charles IV (1328–1378) was king of France and became Holy Roman Emperor, significantly influencing both French and German politics during his reign. He played a crucial role in the early phases of the Hundred Years' War.&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6970ff4a745f78f3ab3bcab2da4945d1</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783211014</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;King Edward III of England&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;King Edward III of England&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;historical_figure&gt;</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;Edward III (1327–1377) led England into several key conflicts, including the beginning stages of the Hundred Years' War. Known for his military prowess and strategic decisions, he helped define English claims in France.&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6970ff4a745f78f3ab3bcab2da4945d1</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783211014</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Emperor Charles IV&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Emperor Charles IV&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;historical_figure&gt;</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;Charles IV (1316–1378) was a German king and Holy Roman Emperor who contributed to the late medieval period's political structure through his reign and influence over various territories, often engaging in negotiations and conflicts.&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6970ff4a745f78f3ab3bcab2da4945d1</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
