    <node id="&lt;&quot;Jacquerie Revolt&quot; (Historical Figure/Sub-event)">
      <data key="d0">&lt;"Jacquerie Revolt" (Historical Figure/Sub-event)</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;historical_concept_or_event&gt;</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;"A major peasant rebellion that occurred in 1358 during the Black Death, highlighting peasant resistance against feudalism due to economic hardships and social oppression."</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6e5db5c52f4fc1bd5d65714070a352ef</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783211017</data>
    </node>
    <node id="fourteenth century travel">
      <data key="d0">fourteenth century travel</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">Travel in the fourteenth century was significantly more difficult than today, due to limited transportation and the high cost of travel. The period saw challenges for travelers across Europe amid worsening environmental conditions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f485cc659c371a55149fcc8ff3f200a5</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783211017</data>
    </node>
    <node id="plagues spread">
      <data key="d0">plagues spread</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">The primary method of plague's rapid spread during the fourteenth century was through small rodents, which traveled with transported goods. This facilitated its widespread impact across regions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f485cc659c371a55149fcc8ff3f200a5</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783211017</data>
    </node>
    <node id="tree ring data">
      <data key="d0">tree ring data</data>
      <data key="d1">source_or_artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">Tree ring data is a valuable source for historical climatology, providing insight into past climate conditions through measurements of tree growth layers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f485cc659c371a55149fcc8ff3f200a5</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783211017</data>
    </node>
    <node id="the Silk Roads">
      <data key="d0">the Silk Roads</data>
      <data key="d1">trade route</data>
      <data key="d2">The Silk Roads played a pivotal role in enabling the plague to spread from central Asia to western Europe and North Africa.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d85eeeedc638b2dc40a70eec24196c37</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783211017</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Italy and France">
      <data key="d0">Italy and France</data>
      <data key="d1">regions</data>
      <data key="d2">In the 1330s, the plague made its debut in both Italy and France. This widespread outbreak began during this decade, affecting public health significantly in these two countries.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d85eeeedc638b2dc40a70eec24196c37</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783211019</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Many peasants left rural areas in search of employment in towns and cities.">
      <data key="d0">Many peasants left rural areas in search of employment in towns and cities.</data>
      <data key="d1">factor</data>
      <data key="d2">Many peasants left rural areas in search of employment in towns and cities following the Black Death, hastening the decline of feudalism in western Europe. This movement of people played a significant role in accelerating the transition away from the feudal system, as documented in multiple descriptions provided.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d85eeeedc638b2dc40a70eec24196c37</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783211021</data>
    </node>
    <node id="by reinforcing the role of Confucianism">
      <data key="d0">by reinforcing the role of Confucianism</data>
      <data key="d1">restoration efforts</data>
      <data key="d2">Ming emperors, notably including Zhu Di, sought to restore Chinese cultural traditions following the fall of the Mongol Yuan dynasty by emphasizing and reinforcing the role of Confucianism. This consistent effort across multiple descriptions underscores their commitment to reviving key elements of traditional Chinese culture through the promotion of Confucian values and principles.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d85eeeedc638b2dc40a70eec24196c37</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783211022</data>
    </node>
    <node id="wages">
      <data key="d0">wages</data>
      <data key="d1">regulating workers'</data>
      <data key="d2">To address labor shortages caused by the Black Death, countries including England passed laws regulating workers' wages. These measures were implemented to manage the economic challenges arising from the significant reduction in available workforce due to the aforementioned pandemic.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d85eeeedc638b2dc40a70eec24196c37</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783211021</data>
    </node>
    <node id="It made it easier for people to set up craft shops, undermining the guild system.">
      <data key="d0">It made it easier for people to set up craft shops, undermining the guild system.</data>
      <data key="d1">effect on social structure</data>
      <data key="d2">The absence of laborers in towns and cities impacted the social structure in Europe by facilitating the establishment of craft shops, thereby challenging and weakening the established guild system. This process made it more accessible for individuals to engage in crafts and trade, thereby disrupting traditional professional associations and their control over trades and craftsmanship.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d85eeeedc638b2dc40a70eec24196c37</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783211023</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Yuan leaders selected China as the center of their empire">
      <data key="d0">Yuan leaders selected China as the center of their empire</data>
      <data key="d1">reason for selection</data>
      <data key="d2">The Yuan leaders chose China as the center of their empire for strategic, logistical, and cultural reasons. While no specific details are provided in the descriptions regarding why they selected China, it can be inferred that factors such as the extensive historical and cultural significance of China, its central location facilitating control over a large territory, and perhaps its rich resources and established administrative structures played crucial roles. These considerations collectively supported China’s position as the core of their expansive empire.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d85eeeedc638b2dc40a70eec24196c37</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783211024</data>
    </node>
    <node id="The Silk Roads">
      <data key="d0">The Silk Roads</data>
      <data key="d1">trade route</data>
      <data key="d2">The Silk Roads played a pivotal role in enabling the plague to spread from central Asia to western Europe and North Africa. As trade routes connecting various regions, they facilitated not only commercial exchanges but also the movement of people, goods, and diseases.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d85eeeedc638b2dc40a70eec24196c37</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783211019</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Trade networks">
      <data key="d0">Trade networks</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">Trade networks are interconnected pathways forming a global economic system during the medieval period that facilitated the exchange of goods and ideas. These networks were crucial for disseminating diseases like the plague.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d85eeeedc638b2dc40a70eec24196c37</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783211019</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Han culture">
      <data key="d0">Han culture</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">Han culture refers to the cultural traditions, values, and practices associated with the Han ethnic group in China, which were central in the Ming Dynasty's effort to restore traditional ways after Yuan rule.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d85eeeedc638b2dc40a70eec24196c37</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783211019</data>
    </node>
