    <node id="Florentine architect Filippo Brunelleschi">
      <data key="d0">Florentine architect Filippo Brunelleschi</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">Filippo Brunelleschi was instrumental in rediscovering and popularizing the technique of linear perspective, which inspired artists like Masaccio.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bf5cfe6916b81abc088c0896d934f008</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783211030</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Linear perspective experiment">
      <data key="d0">Linear perspective experiment</data>
      <data key="d1">experiment</data>
      <data key="d2">Brunelleschi's experiments with linear perspective and his influential drawings played a key role in its spread to Renaissance art.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bf5cfe6916b81abc088c0896d934f008</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783211030</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Leonardo da Vinci">
      <data key="d0">Leonardo da Vinci</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">Leonardo da Vinci, among other artists, conducted detailed studies of animal and human anatomy for more realistic depictions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bf5cfe6916b81abc088c0896d934f008</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783211030</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Michelangelo">
      <data key="d0">Michelangelo</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">Michelangelo idealized the body in his artworks, setting a new standard for Renaissance sculpture and painting.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bf5cfe6916b81abc088c0896d934f008</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783211030</data>
    </node>
    <node id="13th Century">
      <data key="d0">13th Century</data>
      <data key="d1">time_period</data>
      <data key="d2">During the 13th century, a new state came into existence in Egypt and the Levant, known as the Mamluk Sultanate.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-47fec5d364392047eb02361961d78934</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783211030</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Timur">
      <data key="d0">Timur</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">Timur, also known as Tamerlane, was a 14th-century Central Asian conqueror and military leader renowned for his exceptional military prowess. Born into the Barlas tribe, he sought to revive the Mongol Empire established by Chinggis Khan. Timur successfully expanded his territorial control by defeating numerous rivals and establishing the Timurid Empire. His conquests extended into Persia, Iraq, Afghanistan, and Syria. Notably, Timur achieved a significant victory against Bayezid I at the Battle of Ankara in 1402, further solidifying his threats to the Ottomans with his extensive Mongol campaigns.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7a54827dcff91e0c3be7735cbd32331f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-325c2f087ce183f26e739c5d7d7a119e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-51a6e6c0f619a3b37e3ee2a7e7f19b5a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3781905eca1839631b0bed57e233e84b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2c0fbb619a916ac387c46ee41fe1af47</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783211030</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Istanbul">
      <data key="d0">Istanbul</data>
      <data key="d1">geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">Istanbul is a significant city with deep historical roots, known for its pivotal role in the transformation and governance under Mehmed II's rule during the mid-15th century. The city attracted artisans and prisoners from various regions, who contributed significantly to its rebuilding, exemplified by iconic structures such as the Suleymaniye Mosque and Hagia Sophia. Concurrently, Istanbul became a crucial center for religious communities' autonomy within the Ottoman Empire. Historically, modern-day Istanbul, formerly Constantinople, served as a central location that bridged the Byzantine and Ottoman empires, emphasizing its strategic importance in the political landscape of the Eastern Mediterranean as well as acting as a geographical point of transition between East and West during the Ottoman period.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-51a6e6c0f619a3b37e3ee2a7e7f19b5a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-72d7bb40fa0aa36bf5b0bb66bf7975fe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8adac32853d2cbcb9cfc409ace493450</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783211026</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mamluk Sultanate">
      <data key="d0">Mamluk Sultanate</data>
      <data key="d1">civilization_or_empire</data>
      <data key="d2">The Mamluk Sultanate was a medieval state that emerged in the 13th century in Egypt and the Levant, playing a crucial role against the Mongols during the 14th century. This sultanate was known for its governance by trained, formerly enslaved elite who served as soldiers and administrators. Through their strategic use of early cannons, the Mamluk Sultanate successfully defended against Mongol invasions, highlighting its military prowess and strategic foresight during this period.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c5b0a78bf038c5aff1201d9ae1d7d241&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-47fec5d364392047eb02361961d78934</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783211036</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Slavery in Islamic World">
      <data key="d0">Slavery in Islamic World</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">A system of slavery within the Islamic world relied on enslaved or formerly enslaved elite soldiers and administrators.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-47fec5d364392047eb02361961d78934</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783211030</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Oxus River">
      <data key="d0">Oxus River</data>
      <data key="d1">geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">The Oxus River, located in central Asia, was a source for the purchase of enslaved soldiers by Islamic states in western Asia.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-47fec5d364392047eb02361961d78934</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783211030</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Caucasus">
      <data key="d0">Caucasus</data>
      <data key="d1">geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">Caucasian individuals were among those from whom slaves were taken and trained to become mamluks.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-47fec5d364392047eb02361961d78934</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783211030</data>
    </node>
    <node id="15th Century">
      <data key="d0">15th Century</data>
      <data key="d1">time_period</data>
      <data key="d2">The 15th century saw the rule of Ming China, during which time they had responses to foreign influences.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-47fec5d364392047eb02361961d78934</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783211030</data>
    </node>
    <node id="young mamluks">
      <data key="d0">young mamluks</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">Young men trained as mamluks were taught to ride at a gallop and aim projectiles at a swinging target as part of their rigorous training.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-803afe7d2b36623c7ac458fe075bafa3</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783211030</data>
    </node>
    <node id="H_o#_ a gallop">
      <data key="d0">H_o#_ a gallop</data>
      <data key="d1">cultural_or_religious_movement</data>
      <data key="d2">This phase represents the speed at which young mamluks trained, symbolizing the intensity of their physical training.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-803afe7d2b36623c7ac458fe075bafa3</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783211030</data>
    </node>
    <node id="freed people of ancient Rome">
      <data key="d0">freed people of ancient Rome</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">The analogy drawn between freed people in ancient Roman society and mamluks suggests a similar relationship with former owners.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-803afe7d2b36623c7ac458fe075bafa3</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783211030</data>
    </node>
    <node id="province governor and commander of the yearly pilgrimage to Mecca">
      <data key="d0">province governor and commander of the yearly pilgrimage to Mecca</data>
      <data key="d1">source_or_artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">These were positions often held by mamluks in Mamluk Egypt, highlighting their political influence.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-803afe7d2b36623c7ac458fe075bafa3</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783211030</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Manual of the Arts of Horsemanship by al-Aqsara'i">
      <data key="d0">Manual of the Arts of Horsemanship by al-Aqsara'i</data>
      <data key="d1">source_or_artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">This historical document from the 14th century describes training methods for young mamluks, focusing on horsemanship and combat skills.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-803afe7d2b36623c7ac458fe075bafa3</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783211030</data>
    </node>
    <node id="mamluk Sultanate">
      <data key="d0">mamluk Sultanate</data>
      <data key="d1">civilization_or_empire</data>
      <data key="d2">The Mamluk Sultanate was a powerful dynasty in Egypt that ruled after deposing the Ayyubid Sultan. It maintained control over major regions through appointed officials and military prowess.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-803afe7d2b36623c7ac458fe075bafa3</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783211030</data>
    </node>
    <node id="appointed mamluks">
      <data key="d0">appointed mamluks</data>
      <data key="d1">civilization_or_empire</data>
      <data key="d2">Mamluks who were appointed to various positions were often granted greater authority and responsibility compared to biological sons. This practice was a key feature of the Mamluk Sultanate’s governance structure.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-803afe7d2b36623c7ac458fe075bafa3</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783211030</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ayyubids">
      <data key="d0">Ayyubids</data>
      <data key="d1">civilization_or_empire</data>
      <data key="d2">The Ayyubids were a dynasty that founded their own state in the 11th century by breaking away from the Abbasid state, and they used enslaved men, known as mamluks, to fill important governmental and military positions because of their advantages.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ba4ea489d2ce3a29d9caae0afece9397</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783211030</data>
    </node>
    <node id="mamluks">
      <data key="d0">mamluks</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">Mamluks were highly trained and well educated slaves who could be chosen by a ruler based on ability rather than heredity. They were likely loyal to their 'father' and the state due to their upbringing, ensuring loyalty in positions of authority.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ba4ea489d2ce3a29d9caae0afece9397</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783211030</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Abbasid state">
      <data key="d0">Abbasid state</data>
      <data key="d1">civilization_or_empire</data>
      <data key="d2">The Abbasid state refers to the former empire where the Ayyubids broke away from to found their own dynasty.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ba4ea489d2ce3a29d9caae0afece9397</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783211030</data>
    </node>
    <node id="devshirme">
      <data key="d0">devshirme</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">Devshirme was a system in the Ottoman Empire where Christian boys were recruited as part of a tax on European subjects, and they were educated, converted to Islam, and trained for various governmental positions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ba4ea489d2ce3a29d9caae0afece9397</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783211030</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ottoman sultans">
      <data key="d0">Ottoman sultans</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">Ottoman Sultans were rulers who implemented the devshirme system and used it to gather Christian boys for various governmental positions, including those in the palace where they could rise to important roles.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ba4ea489d2ce3a29d9caae0afece9397</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783211030</data>
    </node>
    <node id="timars">
      <data key="d0">timars</data>
      <data key="d1">source_or_artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">Timars were awards given to selected mamluks or devshirme recruits as a form of land ownership, which offered a means to wealth and status within the Ottoman administration.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ba4ea489d2ce3a29d9caae0afece9397</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783211030</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Grand Vizier">
      <data key="d0">Grand Vizier</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">Grand Vizier was an important administrative position that some devshirme trainees could rise to within the Ottoman government hierarchy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ba4ea489d2ce3a29d9caae0afece9397</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783211030</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ottoman agents">
      <data key="d0">Ottoman agents</data>
      <data key="d1">cultural_or_religious_movement</data>
      <data key="d2">Ottoman agents were individuals responsible for implementing and maintaining the devshirme system, recruiting Christian boys from occupied European territories.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ba4ea489d2ce3a29d9caae0afece9397</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783211030</data>
    </node>
    <node id="1558">
      <data key="d0">1558</data>
      <data key="d1">time_period</data>
      <data key="d2">"The Ottoman miniature painting dates back to 1558."&lt;&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3781905eca1839631b0bed57e233e84b</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783211031</data>
    </node>
    <node id="The Ottoman miniature painting">
      <data key="d0">The Ottoman miniature painting</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">The Ottoman miniature paintings are a form of visual art from the Ottoman Empire that date back to 1558. These paintings often depict scenes from Ottoman life, battles, and court life in great detail using traditional techniques.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3781905eca1839631b0bed57e233e84b</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783211031</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Novo Brdo&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Novo Brdo&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;geographical_location&gt;</data>
      <data key="d2">Novo Brdo is a city historically located in what is now Montenegro that was significant for its silver and gold deposits. It was conquered by the Ottoman Sultan during the 16th century as part of the devshirme system. The city played a strategic role due to its rich resources.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-12239487bfd15d0a7af1a8f3aa38e412</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783211031</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ottoman Empire">
      <data key="d0">Ottoman Empire</data>
      <data key="d1">civilization_or_empire</data>
      <data key="d2">The Ottoman Empire was a powerful state that emerged in the late Middle Ages and extended its influence across Asia Minor, Southeast Europe, and North Africa. It is particularly noted for its military successes, including the conquest of Constantinople by Mehmed II in 1453, where cannons were used to breach the city's walls, marking a significant expansion of Ottoman territories.

The empire faced continuous challenges with other regional powers but maintained its influence through various means such as the devshirme system. This system involved the recruitment of Christian boys from occupied European lands to serve within the empire’s military and administrative structures, serving to counter the power of Turkish nobles. Additionally, the use of enslaved people or prisoners of war for labor began in the 14th century, further reinforcing the Ottoman Empire's complex socio-political and administrative systems.

As a significant player on the geopolitical stage during both its medieval and early modern periods, the Ottoman Empire is remembered for its military prowess under leaders like Mehmed II and its expansive territory that included vast regions of Asia Minor, Southeast Europe, and North Africa.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0f34176fe0a68fb23d86164ec84def78&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f3acbd758cf8087488127fa375dda8fa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-81aaa6bf9b333d06305b8e02f056d212&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-51a6e6c0f619a3b37e3ee2a7e7f19b5a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9659ee5a3cec87c5f65828322cee3bbd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ba4ea489d2ce3a29d9caae0afece9397</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783211032</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Ottoman Empire&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Ottoman Empire&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;civilization_or_empire&gt;</data>
      <data key="d2">The Ottoman Empire, which existed from the 14th to the early 20th century, was centered in Southeast Europe and the Middle East. During the 16th century, it expanded its territory through conquests and employed various methods of incorporating conquered peoples into its administrative and military structures.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-12239487bfd15d0a7af1a8f3aa38e412</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783211031</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Devshirme System&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Devshirme System&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;historical_concept_or_event&gt;</data>
      <data key="d2">The devshirme system was a practice in the Ottoman Empire where Christian boys were forcibly recruited from their communities, often for service as Janissaries or members of the Sultan's harem. It was a key method used to maintain elite military forces and officials within the empire.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-12239487bfd15d0a7af1a8f3aa38e412</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783211031</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Janissaries&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Janissaries&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;historical_concept_or_event&gt;</data>
      <data key="d2">Janissaries were elite Islamic soldiers in the Ottoman Empire, recruited through the devshirme system. They held significant social and military status but were also enslaved as part of their service.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-12239487bfd15d0a7af1a8f3aa38e412</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783211031</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Konstantin Mihailović&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Konstantin Mihailović&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;historical_figure&gt;</data>
      <data key="d2">Konstantin Mihailović was a Christian convert to Islam who served as a Janissary. His memoirs provide insight into the experiences and perspectives of individuals involved in the devshirme system.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-12239487bfd15d0a7af1a8f3aa38e412</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783211031</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Ottoman Sultan&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Ottoman Sultan&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;historical_figure&gt;</data>
      <data key="d2">The Ottoman Sultan was the ruler of the Ottoman Empire during this period, responsible for military conquests and administrative decisions. The sultan's actions included promising non-enforced enslavement of civilians after a successful siege.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-12239487bfd15d0a7af1a8f3aa38e412</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783211031</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Sultan death or deposition">
      <data key="d0">Sultan death or deposition</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_event</data>
      <data key="d2">"The sultan's death or deposition led to the mamluks' indecision about obeying the new ruler."|</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-065db1aa182e4a6eb02755ddc80375ab</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783211031</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mamluk Sultanate succession uncertainty">
      <data key="d0">Mamluk Sultanate succession uncertainty</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">Succession in the Mamluk Sultanate was consistently uncertain due to military forces asserting their right to choose rulers.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-065db1aa182e4a6eb02755ddc80375ab</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783211031</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Military control over succession">
      <data key="d0">Military control over succession</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">The army continuously had a significant role in selecting and deposing rulers in the Mamluk Sultanate.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-065db1aa182e4a6eb02755ddc80375ab</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783211031</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Bahri Dynasty">
      <data key="d0">Bahri Dynasty</data>
      <data key="d1">civilization_or_empire</data>
      <data key="d2">During the Bahri dynasty, the Mamluk Sultanate experienced a period of flourishing from the first century of Mamluk rule.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-065db1aa182e4a6eb02755ddc80375ab</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783211031</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mongol invasion of Syria">
      <data key="d0">Mongol invasion of Syria</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_event</data>
      <data key="d2">The Mongols attempted to invade Syria but were defeated by the Mamluks in 1313.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-065db1aa182e4a6eb02755ddc80375ab</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783211031</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mamluk-Safavid peace treaty">
      <data key="d0">Mamluk-Safavid peace treaty</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">In 1322, the Mamluks established a peace treaty with the Ilkhanate Mongols of Persia.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-065db1aa182e4a6eb02755ddc80375ab</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783211031</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Abbasid family refuge in Egypt">
      <data key="d0">Abbasid family refuge in Egypt</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_event</data>
      <data key="d2">After fleeing from Baghdad, members of the Abbasid dynasty took refuge in Egypt in 1261 and Baybars proclaimed al-Mustansir as their caliph.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-065db1aa182e4a6eb02755ddc80375ab</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783211031</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Mamluk sultans&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Mamluk sultans&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;historical_figure&gt;</data>
      <data key="d2">Protectors of the caliphate and pious Muslims who supported various institutions and faced political threats.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-224bdda0e90e6002ca09f2027f71a671</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783211031</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;caliph&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;caliph&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;historical_concept_or_event&gt;</data>
      <data key="d2">Symbolic head of Islam, protected by Mamluk sultans but not directly claiming the caliphate for themselves.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-224bdda0e90e6002ca09f2027f71a671</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783211031</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Mamluk Rulers&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Mamluk Rulers&gt;</data>
      <data key="d1">&lt;historical_figure&gt;</data>
