    <node id="Hongwu Emperor">
      <data key="d0">Hongwu Emperor</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">The Hongwu Emperor, whose full name was Zhu Yuanzhang and who founded the Ming dynasty, ascended to the throne and subsequently took an initial step in establishing diplomatic relations by sending an announcement to the Byzantine emperor. This action marked his early efforts to project influence beyond China's borders. Given that two of the descriptions are identical, we can confidently consolidate the information provided into a single, coherent summary.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cb1aed2461d605387294e530dbb57f99&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ac97c61bc79f0fafcdc0133b3f827bda</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783211036</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Chief Minister Position">
      <data key="d0">Chief Minister Position</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_concept_or_event</data>
      <data key="d2">The office of chief minister that was abolished by Hongwu to consolidate his power without interference from advisors.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cb1aed2461d605387294e530dbb57f99</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783211032</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Secret Police Force">
      <data key="d0">Secret Police Force</data>
      <data key="d1">cultural_or_religious_movement</data>
      <data key="d2">The establishment and use of a force of secret police by Hongwu to protect himself and expand Chinese influence.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cb1aed2461d605387294e530dbb57f99</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783211032</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;Rebellions'">
      <data key="d0">"Rebellions'</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_event</data>
      <data key="d2">Rebels and revolts, notably the Miao ethnic group revolt in Hunan Province which was crushed by Hongwu.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cb1aed2461d605387294e530dbb57f99</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783211032</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Kingdom of Dali">
      <data key="d0">Kingdom of Dali</data>
      <data key="d1">geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">The Kingdom of Dali, where Mongol forces had taken refuge, located in what is now Yunnan Province during the reign of Hongwu.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cb1aed2461d605387294e530dbb57f99</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783211032</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Manchuria Invasion">
      <data key="d0">Manchuria Invasion</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_event</data>
      <data key="d2">Ming forces invading Manchuria to counterloyalists of Yuan dynasty who established a foothold there under Hongwu's rule.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cb1aed2461d605387294e530dbb57f99</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783211032</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Hereditary Military Service">
      <data key="d0">Hereditary Military Service</data>
      <data key="d1">policy</data>
      <data key="d2">Military service becoming hereditary in certain regions under the command of Hongwu to protect borders and respond to overpopulation issues.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cb1aed2461d605387294e530dbb57f99</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783211032</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Mass Peasant Relocation">
      <data key="d0">Mass Peasant Relocation</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_event</data>
      <data key="d2">The forced relocation of peasants from southern China by Hongwu to increase population in affected areas of central and northern China.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cb1aed2461d605387294e530dbb57f99</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783211032</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Yongle Emperor">
      <data key="d0">Yongle Emperor</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">Zhu Di, the son and successor of Hongwu who ruled as the Yongle emperor after 1406.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cb1aed2461d605387294e530dbb57f99</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783211032</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Southeast Asia Invasion">
      <data key="d0">Southeast Asia Invasion</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_event</data>
      <data key="d2">The unsuccessful invasions and attempts to subjugate Vietnam by Chinese forces under Yongle from 1406-1427.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cb1aed2461d605387294e530dbb57f99</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783211032</data>
