    <node id="&quot;Great Wall of China'">
      <data key="d0">"Great Wall of China'</data>
      <data key="d1">cultural_or_religious_movement</data>
      <data key="d2">Repairing and lengthening the Great Wall under the control of Yongle to counter Mongol threats, as part of an expansionist effort.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cb1aed2461d605387294e530dbb57f99</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783211032</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;Maritime Trade Ban'">
      <data key="d0">"Maritime Trade Ban'</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_event</data>
      <data key="d2">Hongwu's policy of forbidding nearly all maritime trade to isolate China from foreign influences, aimed at protecting domestic stability and reducing exposure to foreign risks.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cb1aed2461d605387294e530dbb57f99</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783211032</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;Admiral Zheng He'">
      <data key="d0">"Admiral Zheng He'</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">Zheng He, the admiral dispatched by Yongle on naval expeditions between 1405-1433, aimed at establishing foreign relations and showcasing Chinese wealth and power.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cb1aed2461d605387294e530dbb57f99</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783211032</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;Foreign Gifts to Impress Other Rulers'">
      <data key="d0">"Foreign Gifts to Impress Other Rulers'</data>
      <data key="d1">policy</data>
      <data key="d2">Sending gifts of porcelain, silk, and gold from Chinese fleets during Zheng He’s expeditions to impress upon rulers the splendor of the Ming Dynasty's wealth and culture.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cb1aed2461d605387294e530dbb57f99</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783211032</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;Tribute with Exotic Animals'">
      <data key="d0">"Tribute with Exotic Animals'</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_event</data>
      <data key="d2">Returning with tribute such as ivory and exotic animals like zebras and ostriches, used by Yongle for the emperor’s collection or display.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cb1aed2461d605387294e530dbb57f99</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783211032</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;Manchuria Invasion'">
      <data key="d0">"Manchuria Invasion'</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_event</data>
      <data key="d2">Hongwu ordered military expeditions aimed at Manchuria in order to counter the support given by Yuan loyalists who had established footholds there, pushing back Mongol influence and expanding China's borders.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cb1aed2461d605387294e530dbb57f99</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783211032</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;Hereditary Military Service'">
      <data key="d0">"Hereditary Military Service'</data>
      <data key="d1">policy</data>
      <data key="d2">Hereditary military service was introduced under Hongwu as a means of ensuring loyalty and control over certain regions, while also addressing the issue of border defense and population surplus in those areas.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cb1aed2461d605387294e530dbb57f99</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783211032</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;Mass Peasant Relocation'">
      <data key="d0">"Mass Peasant Relocation'</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_event</data>
      <data key="d2">To increase the population density in less populated central and northern China, Hongwu ordered the forced relocation of peasants from southern provinces into these areas.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cb1aed2461d605387294e530dbb57f99</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783211032</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&quot;Yongle Emperor '">
      <data key="d0">"Yongle Emperor '</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">Yongle was the third emperor of the Ming dynasty, succeeding his father Hongwu. He continued many of his predecessor's policies but also initiated significant construction projects and territorial expansions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cb1aed2461d605387294e530dbb57f99</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783211032</data>
    </node>
    <node id="14th-15th century">
      <data key="d0">14th-15th century</data>
      <data key="d1">time_period</data>
      <data key="d2">Refers to the period during which significant historical events and actions occurred, such as the establishment of diplomatic relations and construction activities undertaken by Zhu Di and the Yongle Emperor.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ac97c61bc79f0fafcdc0133b3f827bda</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783211032</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Shah Rukh, Timur's son">
      <data key="d0">Shah Rukh, Timur's son</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">Shah Rukh, as the son and heir of Timur, played a role in establishing diplomatic relations with China during his time.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ac97c61bc79f0fafcdc0133b3f827bda</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
