    </node>
    <node id="Yongle Emperor's reign">
      <data key="d0">Yongle Emperor's reign</data>
      <data key="d1">time_period</data>
      <data key="d2">The Yongle Emperor's period of rule saw significant diplomatic and architectural initiatives, including the establishment of a new capital and the construction of the Forbidden City.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ac97c61bc79f0fafcdc0133b3f827bda</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783211032</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Nanjing to Beijing relocation">
      <data key="d0">Nanjing to Beijing relocation</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_event</data>
      <data key="d2">The relocation of China’s capital from Nanjing to Beijing during the Yongle Emperor's reign marks an important historical event in this period.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ac97c61bc79f0fafcdc0133b3f827bda</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783211032</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Forbidden City">
      <data key="d0">Forbidden City</data>
      <data key="d1">source_or_artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">The Forbidden City is a palace complex that served as the primary residence for emperors of the Ming Dynasty and was later during the Qing Dynasty, located in central Beijing. This grand architectural accomplishment not only symbolizes imperial governance and China's historical might but also represents significant cultural and political significance. Constructed during the reign of the Yongle Emperor, it stands as a testament to early Chinese dynastic power and architectural excellence.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b48f1c75c48a630bdea282765ade371c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ac97c61bc79f0fafcdc0133b3f827bda&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c01365a1973af158b0d688c24d6fe6d2</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783211037</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ming Tombs">
      <data key="d0">Ming Tombs</data>
      <data key="d1">historical_figure</data>
      <data key="d2">Historical figures of the Ming dynasty, particularly emperors, built tombs near Beijing as their final resting places.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ac97c61bc79f0fafcdc0133b3f827bda</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783211032</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Jundu Mountains">
      <data key="d0">Jundu Mountains</data>
      <data key="d1">geographical_location</data>
      <data key="d2">The Jundu Mountains serve as a geographical reference for the location where some of the emperor's tombs were constructed.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ac97c61bc79f0fafcdc0133b3f827bda</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783211033</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Meridian Gate">
      <data key="d0">Meridian Gate</data>
      <data key="d1">source_or_artifact</data>
      <data key="d2">A specific structure, Meridian Gate, within the Forbidden City is described with historical significance, being reserved exclusively for the emperor and key individuals on special occasions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ac97c61bc79f0fafcdc0133b3f827bda</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783211033</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Ming dynasty">
      <data key="d0">Ming dynasty</data>
      <data key="d1">time_period</data>
      <data key="d2">The Ming dynasty is a period in Chinese history during which the emperors held court and lived in specific palaces within the Forbidden City.</data>
