    <node id="&lt;civil war supported by mamluks&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;civil war supported by mamluks&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a51f56123d8110f2ea915388f363a913</data>
      <data key="d2">The civil war that occurred with the support of Mamluks was a significant event during Al-Mutasim's reign, indicating internal unrest and the influence of military factions within the caliphate.</data>
      <data key="d1">UNKNOWN</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783203334</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Islamic scholars translating texts into Arabic&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Islamic scholars translating texts into Arabic&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a51f56123d8110f2ea915388f363a913</data>
      <data key="d2">The activity of Islamic scholars translating ancient philosophical texts from Greek into Arabic was crucial for preserving knowledge and spreading it throughout the Abbasid Caliphate. These translations later influenced Christian thought in Europe through further translations.</data>
      <data key="d1">UNKNOWN</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783203334</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Byzantine rivals' influence&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Byzantine rivals' influence&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a51f56123d8110f2ea915388f363a913</data>
      <data key="d2">The conflict with Byzantium influenced translators, as they sought to preserve and propagate knowledge against the perceived threat of their cultural counterparts. This tension drove translation efforts as a form of assertive intellectual diplomacy.</data>
      <data key="d1">UNKNOWN</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783203335</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Arab elites'&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Arab elites'&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a51f56123d8110f2ea915388f363a913</data>
      <data key="d2">The shift from Baghdad to Samarra signified changing social and political dynamics; Arab elites had to adapt to new administrative centers. This move reflected their continued relevance in Abbasid court culture.</data>
      <data key="d1">UNKNOWN</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783203335</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Baghdad's transition of power base&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Baghdad's transition of power base&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a51f56123d8110f2ea915388f363a913</data>
      <data key="d2">The shift from Baghdad to Samarra signified changing social and political dynamics; Arab elites had to adapt to new administrative centers. This move reflected their continued relevance in Abbasid court culture.</data>
      <data key="d1">UNKNOWN</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783203335</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Mamluks'&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Mamluks'&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a51f56123d8110f2ea915388f363a913</data>
      <data key="d2">The act of translating texts during the Abbasid period provided Mamluk military support with intellectual and cultural capital, potentially enhancing their social status among other elites. This partnership highlighted a unique alliance between military might and academic pursuit.</data>
      <data key="d1">UNKNOWN</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783203335</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Al-Mutasim's reign&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Al-Mutasim's reign&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a51f56123d8110f2ea915388f363a913</data>
      <data key="d2">During Al-Mutasim’s rule, the intellectual output of philosophers including Al-Farabi and Ibn Sina flourished. His support for translations into Arabic also contributed to this period.</data>
      <data key="d1">UNKNOWN</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783203335</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Baghdad's capital status&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Baghdad's capital status&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a51f56123d8110f2ea915388f363a913</data>
      <data key="d2">While Baghdad was the capital, Arab elites operated within its political framework, contributing to governance and culture before Samarra became the new seat of power. This continuity symbolized a unified intellectual community under one administration.</data>
      <data key="d1">UNKNOWN</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783203335</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Baghdad's capital status'&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Baghdad's capital status'&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a51f56123d8110f2ea915388f363a913</data>
      <data key="d2">Even as Baghdad lost its capital status, translation work continued. This indicates that the Abbasid leadership maintained a commitment to cultural and scholarly endeavors through various means, ensuring continuity of such activities.</data>
      <data key="d1">UNKNOWN</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783203335</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;translation into Arabic'&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;translation into Arabic'&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a51f56123d8110f2ea915388f363a913</data>
      <data key="d2">Even as Baghdad lost its capital status, translation work continued. This indicates that the Abbasid leadership maintained a commitment to cultural and scholarly endeavors through various means, ensuring continuity of such activities.</data>
      <data key="d1">UNKNOWN</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783203335</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Baghdad's transition of power base'&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Baghdad's transition of power base'&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a51f56123d8110f2ea915388f363a913</data>
      <data key="d2">Translation efforts continued as Baghdad lost its status as the capital. This practice underlined the resilience and adaptability of scholarly pursuits in changing political environments, ensuring knowledge remained central to Abbasid culture.</data>
      <data key="d1">UNKNOWN</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783203335</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Byzantine rivals'&gt;">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Byzantine rivals'&gt;</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a51f56123d8110f2ea915388f363a913</data>
      <data key="d2">The philosophers’ works reflected the need to assert Islamic philosophy against Byzantine influence, showcasing their role in maintaining cultural identity through intellectual endeavors.&lt;SEP&gt;These philosophers addressed their concerns about the Byzantine Empire partly through their writings, aiming to define Islamic philosophy as distinct from Greco-Roman thought. This relationship can be seen in their work's critical stance towards the Christian-dominated power.</data>
      <data key="d1">UNKNOWN</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783203335</data>
