    <node id="&lt;Great Western Schism">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Great Western Schism</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6970ff4a745f78f3ab3bcab2da4945d1</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;The Great Western Schism created divisions that could potentially exacerbate existing tensions between France and England, as both nations had interests in the Papal state's stability during this time. This relationship is moderate due to indirect connections through papacy influence on politics.</data>
      <data key="d1">UNKNOWN</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783203342</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Hundred Years' War">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Hundred Years' War</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6970ff4a745f78f3ab3bcab2da4945d1</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;The Hundred Years' War and the Great Western Schism both occurred during a period where religious and political upheaval were intertwined. The Papacy's weakened state due to schism could impact decisions and strategies taken by kings, including Edward III of England and Charles IV of France.</data>
      <data key="d1">UNKNOWN</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783203342</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;Pope Gregory XI">
      <data key="d0">&lt;Pope Gregory XI</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6970ff4a745f78f3ab3bcab2da4945d1</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;As a papal figure during the Great Western Schism, Pope Gregory XI's efforts to unify and reform the church could be seen as part of broader historical criticism, which The Land of Cockaigne parodies. This relationship is thematic rather than direct but reflects the larger social and religious context.</data>
      <data key="d1">UNKNOWN</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783203342</data>
    </node>
    <node id="&lt;The LandofCockaigne">
      <data key="d0">&lt;The LandofCockaigne</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6970ff4a745f78f3ab3bcab2da4945d1</data>
      <data key="d2">&lt;Satirical works like The Land of Cockaigne could indirectly reflect contemporary societal conflicts, including those between England and France in the Hundred Years' War. While not direct, this relationship highlights the broader socio-political critiques present during the period.</data>
      <data key="d1">UNKNOWN</data>
      <data key="d4">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1783203342</data>
    </node>
