      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211043</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Kalahari Desert" target="San people">
      <data key="d6">6.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The San people live within the harsh, arid conditions of the Kalahari Desert, continuing their hunter-gatherer lifestyle adapted to this environment.</data>
      <data key="d8">"habitat_adaptation"|</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-9556c4c62dde493845781e64a44ca00c</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211043</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Kalahari Desert" target="southern Africa">
      <data key="d6">6.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The Kalahari Desert is geographically located in southern Africa, where the San people live practicing hunter-gatherer traditions.</data>
      <data key="d8">"region_location"|</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-9556c4c62dde493845781e64a44ca00c</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211043</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Kalahari Desert" target="inhabited by San people">
      <data key="d6">6.0</data>
      <data key="d7">San people live in the Kalahari, demonstrating an egalitarian structure within their hunter-gatherer society.</data>
      <data key="d8">"social_adaptation"|</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-9556c4c62dde493845781e64a44ca00c</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211043</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Kalahari Desert" target="Nomads">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The nomadic lifestyle of people like the Tuareg and Teda is influenced by environmental factors including deserts such as the Kalahari."&lt;&lt;br&gt;&gt;"environmental_influence_on_lifestyle</data>
      <data key="d8">4</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-fe1025f0b9f819b6ebc89df353b87d27</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211071</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Inuit people" target="Alaska">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The Inuit people inhabit parts of Alaska as well as other Arctic regions, maintaining their traditional subsistence lifestyle based on hunting and gathering.</data>
      <data key="d8">"habitat_distribution"|</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-9556c4c62dde493845781e64a44ca00c</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211043</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Inuit people" target="practicing traditional hunting">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The Inuit engage in subsistence hunting using caribou as a primary resource, maintaining cultural heritage and traditions.</data>
      <data key="d8">"cultural_practice"|</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-9556c4c62dde493845781e64a44ca00c</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211043</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Neolithic Age" target="Llamas">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The Neolithic Age saw the domestication of llamas in Peru, marking a significant shift from hunter-gatherer lifestyles to settled agricultural communities.</data>
      <data key="d8">agriculture,domestication</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-a4d6f532dbc20aade1c93b0700c3b84d</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211043</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Neolithic Age" target="Hunter-gatherers">
      <data key="d6">6.0</data>
      <data key="d7">During the Neolithic Age, there was a transition from hunter-gatherer societies to agricultural ones, though not all groups adopted farming practices.</data>
      <data key="d8">agricultural transition,lifestyle change</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-a4d6f532dbc20aade1c93b0700c3b84d</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211043</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Peru" target="Llamas">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Llamas were domesticated in Peru and played a significant role in the local economy and culture.</data>
      <data key="d8">cultural impact,domestication</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-a4d6f532dbc20aade1c93b0700c3b84d</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211043</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Peru" target="Norte Chico Sites">
      <data key="d6">1.7</data>
      <data key="d7">Norte Chico sites are located in Peru, setting their geographical context within the country.</data>
      <data key="d8">Geographical Location</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-01fbacc5fd9d868563c8694a482de678</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211066</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Domestication of Humans" target="Plant Domestication">
      <data key="d6">6.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The mutual domestication process between plants and humans may have influenced each other, with both the selection of advantageous plant traits and human behavioral changes contributing to agricultural development.</data>
      <data key="d8">mutual domestication</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-93c39510d11fc933bbd94a1b6ff98dad</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211043</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Domestication of Humans" target="Agriculture">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The concept of Human Domestication Theory relates directly to Agriculture as it is closely linked with the social, economic, and biological changes brought about by farming communities.</data>
      <data key="d8">domestication-phenomenon</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-93c39510d11fc933bbd94a1b6ff98dad</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211043</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Domestication of Humans" target="Docility and Cooperativeness">
      <data key="d6">6.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Traits such as docility and cooperativeness are selected through the process of Domestication, indicating a shift towards more peaceful interactions and group cooperation among humans.</data>
      <data key="d8">trait-selection</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-93c39510d11fc933bbd94a1b6ff98dad</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211043</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Domestication of Humans" target="Modern Humans Less Aggressive and Cooperative">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The theory of Human Domestication suggests that modern human traits like less aggression and greater cooperativeness can be traced back to the Agricultural Revolution.</data>
      <data key="d8">evolutionary-shift</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-93c39510d11fc933bbd94a1b6ff98dad</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211044</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Plant Domestication" target="Neolithic Peoples">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Through labor-intensive cultivation by Neolithic people, plants like wheat grew into successful crops that influenced human behavior and contributed to the establishment of agricultural societies.</data>
      <data key="d8">interaction-cultivation</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-93c39510d11fc933bbd94a1b6ff98dad</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211043</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Plant Domestication" target="Wheat">
      <data key="d6">6.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Wheat is a key example used in discussions of plant domestication, symbolizing the mutual process between humans and plants.</data>
      <data key="d8">plant-food-connection</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-93c39510d11fc933bbd94a1b6ff98dad</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211043</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Çatalhöyük" target="bull_sacrifices">
      <data key="d6">10.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Çatalhöyük inhabitants performed bull sacrifices, which were enabled by an agricultural surplus and religious specialization.</data>
      <data key="d8">bulletsacrifices</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-2b63f6d20ffb060f0c413763b316d411</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211044</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Çatalhöyük" target="mother-deity">
      <data key="d6">6.0</data>
      <data key="d7">People of Çatalhöyük may have offered bull sacrifices to a mother-deity, possibly symbolized by small figurines found at the site.</data>
      <data key="d8">motherdeitybullsacrifices</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-2b63f6d20ffb060f0c413763b316d411</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211044</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Jericho" target="ancestor_veneration">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The community of Jericho may have been united by the veneration of ancestors, as evidenced by decorated skulls revered as idols.</data>
      <data key="d8">ancestorsveneration</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-2b63f6d20ffb060f0c413763b316d411</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211044</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Indus River" target="Kushan Empire">
      <data key="d6">15.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The Indus River influenced the geographical extent and resource access of the Kushan Empire, impacting its trade networks and cultural exchanges.</data>
      <data key="d8">natural boundary,trade routes</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-bceaa7979e3d60d1deb8d4848dbe01fa</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211076</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Yellow River" target="Yangtze River">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Both major river systems played significant roles in supporting early societies. They were seen as integral to cultural and agricultural development." &lt;|"major rivers, support, development</data>
      <data key="d8">4</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-990942779714eb1b55832fae0d8365e4</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211055</data>
    </edge>
