    </edge>
    <edge source="Human migration" target="geographical_location">
      <data key="d6">6.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Migration routes to North America were altered by changes in sea levels during the last ice age, making previously underwater areas accessible.</data>
      <data key="d8">ice age,land exposure,migration</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-5d60f602c690bd1716c66d15ec766306</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211043</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Lower sea levels" target="time_period">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">This concept refers to a time period when sea levels dropped significantly due to glaciation and ice ages, affecting human migration patterns and settlement opportunities.&lt;"|&gt;ice age, landbridge, expansion</data>
      <data key="d8">4</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-5d60f602c690bd1716c66d15ec766306</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211043</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Archaeologists uncover evidence" target="geographical_location">
      <data key="d6">6.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Eastern Europe is the region where archaeologists have found remains of shelters built from mammoth bones during the Paleolithic period.</data>
      <data key="d8">evidence,mammoths,paleolithic,shelter</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-5d60f602c690bd1716c66d15ec766306</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211044</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Agriculture adoption" target="time_period">
      <data key="d6">6.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The widespread adoption of agriculture from nomadic lifestyles led to more stable communities and improved food security over time."&lt;|"agriculture, stability, food security</data>
      <data key="d8">3</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-5d60f602c690bd1716c66d15ec766306</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211044</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Rice agriculture first development" target="source_or_artifact">
      <data key="d6">6.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Neolithic communities in East Asia, particularly in the Yangtze River valley, were early adopters of rice cultivation which became a cornerstone of their agricultural practices."&lt;|"rice, yangtze river valley, agriculture</data>
      <data key="d8">3</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-5d60f602c690bd1716c66d15ec766306</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211044</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Maize cultivation" target="historical_concept_or_event">
      <data key="d6">6.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The independent domestication of maize in pre-Columbian America significantly influenced regional diets and agricultural diversity.&lt;"|&gt;maize, prehistory, food diversification</data>
      <data key="d8">3</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-5d60f602c690bd1716c66d15ec766306</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211044</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Men's tasks in agricultural communities" target="textbook_structure">
      <data key="d6">4.0</data>
      <data key="d7">In early farming societies, men often performed roles related to agriculture such as plowing fields or constructing irrigation systems."&lt;|"farming, agriculture, gender roles</data>
      <data key="d8">2</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-5d60f602c690bd1716c66d15ec766306</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211044</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Sumer" target="Art of Ur Box">
      <data key="d6">6.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The Art of Ur box is a representation of Sumerian civilization and society.</data>
