    </edge>
    <edge source="&lt;img-1&gt;" target="&lt;img-0&gt;">
      <data key="d6">58.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The images &lt;img-0&gt; and &lt;img-1&gt; together provide a comprehensive view of the historical, theological, and cultural context during the era influenced by Augustine's work "The City of God." Image &lt;img-0&gt; represents a paleolithic site from North Africa, which was historically significant at the time when Augustine discussed the fall of Rome and theological disputes such as those between Donatists, Dyophysites, and Monophysites. Image &lt;img-1&gt;, depicting material evidence of Greek influence alongside artifacts showcasing later cultural exchanges with other regions, illustrates how these influences shaped North African society during this period.

Augustine’s "The City of God" addresses key historical events and theological disputes relevant to North Africa, including the challenges posed by the fall of Rome. These discussions are particularly pertinent to understanding the regional conflicts and religious movements prevalent in the area. The combination of archaeological context provided by &lt;img-0&gt; and material evidence from &lt;img-1&gt; underscores the complex interplay between historical events, theological debates, and cultural interactions during this transformative era.</data>
      <data key="d8">Cultural Exchange,Historical context and theological discussion,Paleolithic location,Religious philosophy and history,contextual_relationship</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-b8ad3f8265df80ff0cbd95a7914a6123&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-959d0e5d4763f5423c8c6e422ec0cdb2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a72dedb2114605de59f4327cf6088215&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1e09ce349d4a0e40bee9c4bf3685382c</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211047</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&lt;img-1&gt;" target="Dyphysites">
      <data key="d6">16.0</data>
      <data key="d7">"The City of God" covers theological disputes between Dyophysites and Monophysites, highlighting their doctrinal differences</data>
      <data key="d8">Theological debate and historical works</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-1e09ce349d4a0e40bee9c4bf3685382c</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211095</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&lt;img-1&gt;" target="Monophysites">
      <data key="d6">32.0</data>
      <data key="d7">"The City of God" likely references Monophysite beliefs in contrast to Dyophysite teachings, reflecting Augustine's stance against these heretical beliefs. The work also delves into the historical context surrounding the split and the creation of independent churches, such as the Coptic Church, which emerged from Monophysite beliefs.</data>
      <data key="d8">Historical movements and divisions in Christianity,Theological debate and historical works</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-1e09ce349d4a0e40bee9c4bf3685382c</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211098</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&lt;img-1&gt;" target="&lt;black death&gt;">
      <data key="d6">4.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The image shows historical documentation and records related to the spread and impact of the Black Death.</data>
      <data key="d8">documentation-history</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-5010e0f708f284a5e25e323f470ad4d6</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211098</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Fourth Millennium BCE" target="urbanization">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Urban areas flourished during the fourth millennium BCE due to advancements like cuneiform script, trade records, and construction techniques.</data>
      <data key="d8">urbanization</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-4475a92fdbe351d1be51fc471c84fa37</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211044</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Fourth Millennium BCE" target="technological_innovation">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Innovations such as irrigation techniques and marketplaces flourished during this period, enhancing agricultural productivity and trade.</data>
      <data key="d8">technological_efficiency</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-4475a92fdbe351d1be51fc471c84fa37</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211045</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Uruk" target="Sargon_of_Akkad">
      <data key="d6">16.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Sargon of Akkad expanded his empire to cover Uruk, making it part of the Akkadian Empire.</data>
      <data key="d8">political_influence</data>
