    </edge>
    <edge source="Sumerian civilization" target="cuneiform script">
      <data key="d6">18.0</data>
      <data key="d7">"Cuneiform script emerged as a writing system in Sumer, primarily used for economic, legal, religious, and cultural documentation of the Sumerian civilization."|</data>
      <data key="d8">"script-civilization"|</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-ce95f4fd988337875531ba7f4b862b38</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211046</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Sumerian civilization" target="tokens">
      <data key="d6">12.0</data>
      <data key="d7">"Tokens were an important part of early accounting systems in Sumer. Their usage reflects the economic activities and record-keeping practices during the Sumerian civilization."|</data>
      <data key="d8">"accounting-practices"|</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-ce95f4fd988337875531ba7f4b862b38</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211046</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Sumerian civilization" target="bullae">
      <data key="d6">12.0</data>
      <data key="d7">"Bullae functioned as a part of the transition from early accounting systems to written records in Sumer. They represent key stage in the development of civilizational record-keeping practices."|</data>
      <data key="d8">"transitional-records"|</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-ce95f4fd988337875531ba7f4b862b38</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211047</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="cuneiform script" target="tokens">
      <data key="d6">32.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The cuneiform script emerged after the use of tokens as an earlier method of accounting and record-keeping. Tokens represented abstract symbols within an accounting system before transitioning into a more sophisticated form of written communication through the use of a stylus on clay tablets. This shift marked a significant advancement in written records, reflecting the evolution from simple symbolic representations to complex recorded information. The development from tokens to cuneiform was indicative of societal progress in terms of written communication and administration.</data>
      <data key="d8">"accounting-to-writing-system"|,"evolution-to-writing"|</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-ce95f4fd988337875531ba7f4b862b38</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211046</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="cuneiform script" target="bullae">
      <data key="d6">14.0</data>
      <data key="d7">"Bullae symbolized a step towards using symbols and marks on materials, which eventually led to the development of cuneiform. They paved the way from simple representation methods to more complex writing systems."|</data>
      <data key="d8">"transitional-stage"|</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-ce95f4fd988337875531ba7f4b862b38</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211046</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="cuneiform script" target="clay tablets">
      <data key="d6">18.0</data>
      <data key="d7">"Cuneiform script was recorded on clay tablets, which served as an enduring medium for transmitting knowledge, history, laws, and other significant information of Sumerian civilization."|</data>
      <data key="d8">"script-medium"|</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-ce95f4fd988337875531ba7f4b862b38</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211047</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="cuneiform script" target="Sumerian language">
      <data key="d6">18.0</data>
      <data key="d7">"Cuneiform script was developed specifically to write in the Sumerian language, allowing for the documentation of religious texts, laws, and literature that are central to Sumerian culture."|</data>
      <data key="d8">"script-language"|</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-ce95f4fd988337875531ba7f4b862b38</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211047</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="cuneiform script" target="Sumerian culture">
      <data key="d6">18.0</data>
      <data key="d7">"Cuneiform script served to document and preserve the rich cultural heritage, religious texts, and historical accounts of Sumer. The script is a crucial element through which Sumerian culture has been understood by later generations."|</data>
      <data key="d8">"script-cultural-heritage"|</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-ce95f4fd988337875531ba7f4b862b38</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211047</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="cuneiform script" target="Mesopotamian economy">
      <data key="d6">18.0</data>
      <data key="d7">"Cuneiform script provided documentation for various aspects of the Mesopotamian economy, ensuring the preservation and management of commercial, legal, and financial activities."|</data>
      <data key="d8">"document-economy"|</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-ce95f4fd988337875531ba7f4b862b38</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211047</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="cuneiform script" target="clay materials">
      <data key="d6">18.0</data>
      <data key="d7">"The use of clay as a medium for writing facilitated the documentation process during the Sumerian era, making it possible to create long-lasting records using cuneiform script."|</data>
      <data key="d8">"script-materials"|</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-ce95f4fd988337875531ba7f4b862b38</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211047</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="tokens" target="Mesopotamian economy">
      <data key="d6">16.0</data>
      <data key="d7">"Tokens were an integral part of early economic practices in Mesopotamia, used for record-keeping, trade, and financial transactions. They reflect the complexity and sophistication of the ancient Mesopotamian economic system."|</data>
      <data key="d8">"economic-practices"|</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-ce95f4fd988337875531ba7f4b862b38</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211046</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="tokens" target="Sumerian language">
      <data key="d6">14.0</data>
      <data key="d7">"Tokens played a role in the early stages of developing the Sumerian writing system. They laid the groundwork for the script and contributed to its linguistic complexity."|</data>
      <data key="d8">"language-evolution"|</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-ce95f4fd988337875531ba7f4b862b38</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211047</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="tokens" target="Sumerian culture">
      <data key="d6">14.0</data>
      <data key="d7">"Tokens played a role in the early stages of developing Sumerian cultural practices, including trade and religious rituals. Their use reflects the interplay between material and symbolic elements in Sumerian society."|</data>
      <data key="d8">"cultural-practices"|</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-ce95f4fd988337875531ba7f4b862b38</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211047</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="tokens" target="clay materials">
      <data key="d6">16.0</data>
      <data key="d7">"Clay was also used in the creation of tokens, making them a material artifact that served multiple purposes within Sumerian society. Its properties as an abundant and moldable material were essential."|</data>
      <data key="d8">"token-materials"|</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-ce95f4fd988337875531ba7f4b862b38</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211047</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="bullae" target="Mesopotamian economy">
      <data key="d6">14.0</data>
      <data key="d7">"Bullae were used as a part of the accounting systems in the Mesopotamian economy. They represent a significant stage in the evolution of economic record-keeping and financial practices."|</data>
      <data key="d8">"economic-record-keeping"|</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-ce95f4fd988337875531ba7f4b862b38</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211047</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="bullae" target="clay materials">
      <data key="d6">14.0</data>
      <data key="d7">"Clay was the primary component in the production of bullae, indicating its importance in early accounting systems. The use of clay facilitated the transition from oral to written record-keeping methods."|</data>
      <data key="d8">"clay-records"|</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-ce95f4fd988337875531ba7f4b862b38</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211047</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="bullae" target="Sumerian culture">
      <data key="d6">12.0</data>
      <data key="d7">"Bullae were used in various cultural practices, including religious ceremonies. Their presence indicates the integration of material objects with symbolic expression and ritual practice within Sumerian culture."|</data>
      <data key="d8">"cultural-rituals"|</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-ce95f4fd988337875531ba7f4b862b38</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211047</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&lt;Figure 3.11 The Ziggurat of Ur&gt;" target="Sumeria">
      <data key="d6">10.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The ziggurat in Figure 3.11 demonstrates the significant architectural achievements of Sumerian civilization, showing that they were deeply involved in building monumental structures to honor their deities.</data>
      <data key="d8">architecture-religion-building</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-a8ea083f226618d1b7e5a85496f09e4d</data>
