    <edge source="Senusret III" target="leader">
      <data key="d6">14.0</data>
      <data key="d7">As the ruler of Egypt during its cultural and economic zenith, Senusret III's actions shaped this period.</data>
      <data key="d8">7</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-58fa5bf2acfbf424dd2563814c0f0472</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211050</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Temple of Amon-Re" target="ruined_structure">
      <data key="d6">16.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The temple served as a religious hub but was later devastated; its ruins remain standing to indicate historical significance.</data>
      <data key="d8">8</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-58fa5bf2acfbf424dd2563814c0f0472</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211050</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Karnak Temple Complex at Luxor, Egypt" target="related_structure">
      <data key="d6">14.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The complex is an extension of the Temple of Amon-Re and also suffered from plundering over time but has preserved some of its original grandeur through restoration efforts.</data>
      <data key="d8">7</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-58fa5bf2acfbf424dd2563814c0f0472</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211050</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="centralized state" target="state_policy">
      <data key="d6">14.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Senusret III's policies aimed to consolidate power by diminishing nomarchs' authority, leading to a more centralized state apparatus.</data>
      <data key="d8">7</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-58fa5bf2acfbf424dd2563814c0f0472</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211050</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Treaty Power" target="control_mechanism">
      <data key="d6">16.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Through military campaigns and administrative reforms, Senusret III increased Egypt's treaty power, extending its influence over Kush and the Levant.</data>
      <data key="d8">8</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-58fa5bf2acfbf424dd2563814c0f0472</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211050</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Nomarchs" target="subordinate">
      <data key="d6">14.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Local rulers or officials under Senusret III had their authority diminished to strengthen centralized state control through policy implementation and military conquests.</data>
      <data key="d8">7</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-58fa5bf2acfbf424dd2563814c0f0472</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211050</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Cultural flourishing" target="result_of_policy">
      <data key="d6">16.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Senusret III's governance, including administrative reforms and military successes, contributed to a period of cultural refinement during the Middle Kingdom.</data>
      <data key="d8">8</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-58fa5bf2acfbf424dd2563814c0f0472</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211050</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&lt;&quot;Ming Dynasty&quot;" target="Qing Dynasty">
      <data key="d6">6.0</data>
      <data key="d7">"The Qing Dynasty succeeded and replaced the Ming Dynasty in 1644."|&lt;end_of_ming_start_of_qing&gt;</data>
      <data key="d8">3</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-be37fc10d5448a07bdf21b06248f3471</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211050</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Indus Valley Culture" target="Harappa">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Harappa is an integral part of the larger Indus Valley Culture, contributing to its understanding through archaeological discoveries.</data>
      <data key="d8">archaeological discovery,civilization</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-33b80661398bbb8712dfa331df14ca97</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211050</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Indus Valley Culture" target="Mohenjo-Daro">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Similarly, Mohenjo-Daro provides another key component for comprehending the Indus Valley Culture through detailed study and analysis of its urban structure.</data>
      <data key="d8">archaeological discovery,civilization</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-33b80661398bbb8712dfa331df14ca97</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211050</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Harappa" target="trade">
      <data key="d6">10.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The city of Harappa served as a crucial gateway for trade and commerce, symbolizing the interconnected nature of Ancient Asian civilizations through its role in facilitating exchange with neighboring regions.</data>
      <data key="d8">Cultural Exchange,Trade</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-96a9973262a04f62e37d1ec6cfe70c42</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211054</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Mohenjo-Daro" target="2600–1900 BCE">
      <data key="d6">10.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The time period of 2600 to 1900 BCE is associated with the discovery of seals at Mohenjo-Daro.</data>
      <data key="d8">time_period_location_relation</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-e2b97f3e59f7a42ed6560944da478375</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211050</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&lt;&quot;Shang Dynasty&quot;" target="&lt;&quot;Yellow River&quot;">
      <data key="d6">2.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The Shang Dynasty was centered around the Yellow River valley; the river provided significant resources for their advanced bronze work and socio-political structures.</data>
      <data key="d8">river-sacred-ground</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-dff0e11d470077fc40e0d05ff82a3124</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211050</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&lt;&quot;Shang Dynasty&quot;" target="&lt;&quot;Qin Dynasty&quot;">
      <data key="d6">2.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Both the Shang and Qin Dynasties were crucial periods in Chinese history for their political unification efforts and socio-political organization.</data>
      <data key="d8">unity-political-unification</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-dff0e11d470077fc40e0d05ff82a3124</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211050</data>
    </edge>
