    </edge>
    <edge source="1500-1000 BCE" target="Aryan Invasion Theory">
      <data key="d6">6.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The time period when the Aryans are believed to have invaded India around 1500 BCE overlaps with significant changes in historical narratives and cultural contexts, impacting the understanding of events like the decline of Indus Valley Civilization.</data>
      <data key="d8">scholarly_controversy,theory_period</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-eda2368f7c0ef00cbce121f87fbbdaa1</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211050</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="1500-1000 BCE" target="Environmental Degradation">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Environmental issues such as resource depletion and high population density are believed to have exacerbated the decline of the Indus Valley Civilization during this period.</data>
      <data key="d8">environmental_impact,period_of_degradation</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-eda2368f7c0ef00cbce121f87fbbdaa1</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211050</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="1500-1000 BCE" target="Rigveda, Mohenjo-Daro, Aryan Invasion Theory, Environmental Degradation, Climate Change, Indus Valley Civilization">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">All these elements converge during the 1500-1000 BCE time period to provide a complex and contentious historical context for understanding the decline of the Indus Valley civilization.</data>
      <data key="d8">converging_factors,scholarly_controversy_comprehensive</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-eda2368f7c0ef00cbce121f87fbbdaa1</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211050</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&lt;Bronze Age&gt;" target="&lt;City-States&gt;">
      <data key="d6">2.0</data>
      <data key="d7">During the Bronze Age, city-states emerged and developed complex social structures and technologies.</data>
      <data key="d8">development_of_civilizations|&gt;2</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-bacddcb0411fbd18f80694e00c36c414</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211050</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&lt;Bronze Age&gt;" target="&lt;Cuneiform&gt;">
      <data key="d6">2.0</data>
      <data key="d7">In the Bronze Age, cuneiform was invented for writing and record-keeping purposes.</data>
      <data key="d8">invention_and_usage|&gt;1.5</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-bacddcb0411fbd18f80694e00c36c414</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211050</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&lt;City-States&gt;" target="&lt;Code of Hammurabi&gt;">
      <data key="d6">2.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The Code of Hammurabi was implemented in one of these city-states, serving as a legal framework for society.</data>
      <data key="d8">legal_influence_and_rule|&gt;2</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-bacddcb0411fbd18f80694e00c36c414</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211050</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&lt;Cuneiform&gt;" target="&lt;Harappan Civilization&gt;">
      <data key="d6">2.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Harappan civilization used advanced infrastructure and possibly cuneiform script for record-keeping or communication purposes.</data>
      <data key="d8">innovation_and_usage|&gt;1.5</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-bacddcb0411fbd18f80694e00c36c414</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211050</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&lt;Hieratic Script&gt;" target="&lt;Monsoon Season&gt;">
      <data key="d6">2.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Egyptians developed hieratic as a practical script during the monsoon season, when they were active in writing and administration.</data>
      <data key="d8">seasonality_and_utility|&gt;1.2</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-bacddcb0411fbd18f80694e00c36c414</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211050</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&lt;Nomarchs (Regional Governors)" target="&lt;Papyrus&gt;">
      <data key="d6">2.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Nomarchs utilized papyrus for administrative records and correspondence, indicating its importance in governance.</data>
      <data key="d8">record-keeping_and_governance|&gt;2</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-bacddcb0411fbd18f80694e00c36c414</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211050</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&lt;Pastoralists (Nomadic Herders)" target="&lt;Cultural Concept of Mobility">
      <data key="d6">2.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Pastoralists reflect a lifestyle characterized by nomadism, which is a form of cultural mobility and adaptation to environment.</data>
      <data key="d8">nomadism_and_adaptation|&gt;1.8</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-bacddcb0411fbd18f80694e00c36c414</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211050</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="nomarchs" target="Second_Intermediate_Period_of_Egypt">
      <data key="d6">14.0</data>
      <data key="d7">During this period, nomarchs gained increased power and autonomy in their regions.</data>
