    <edge source="Trade Routes" target="Cultural Exchange">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Interconnected trade routes supported not only economic interaction but also the transmission of ideas, religious practices, and artistic styles across Eurasia during this period.</data>
      <data key="d8">Cultural Diffusion,Trade Networks</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-582dc813b509020dd77ed6ee9db0e981</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211081</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Trade Routes" target="Sultans">
      <data key="d6">6.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The sultans used trade routes to strengthen their empire's economic and political position.</data>
      <data key="d8">economy,politics,strengthen</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-ef569534db6161860971327c3bc8b184</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211093</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Trade Routes" target="Caravansaries">
      <data key="d6">4.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The caravansaries served as key nodes on established trade routes.</data>
      <data key="d8">node,route,serve</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-ef569534db6161860971327c3bc8b184</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211092</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Trade Routes" target="Sunni Islam">
      <data key="d6">6.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The propagation of Sunni Islam was facilitated via trade routes, spreading cultural influence.</data>
      <data key="d8">culture,facilitate,influence,propagate</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-ef569534db6161860971327c3bc8b184</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211089</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Trade Routes" target="Seljuk Empire">
      <data key="d6">10.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The Seljuk Empire featured extensive trade routes that were crucial for the movement of goods and people within its territory. The Caravansaries, important facilities for this purpose, were essential in facilitating these movements. Furthermore, the Seljuk sultans played a significant role in maintaining these trade routes to promote economic growth and foster cultural interactions across the empire.</data>
      <data key="d8">facilitate,goods,growth,interactions,maintain,movement</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-ef569534db6161860971327c3bc8b184</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211092</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Second Temple" target="Contemporary Model ">
      <data key="d6">18.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The contemporary model represents how the Second Temple might have appeared after 1st century BCE expansions.</data>
      <data key="d8">representation,visualization</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-273ea8b020a60935dc711b6d8902f7a6</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211054</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&lt;&quot;Hittite Empire&quot;" target="&lt;&quot;Neo-Assyrian Empire&quot;">
      <data key="d6">4.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The Hittite and Neo-Assyrian Empires are both Near Eastern empires from similar historical periods in Anatolia.</data>
      <data key="d8">Near Eastern Empires</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-e206803b8187f4934c68619b5564e588</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211054</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&lt;&quot;Neo-Assyrian Empire&quot;" target="&lt;&quot;preceded_by&quot;">
      <data key="d6">2.0</data>
      <data key="d7">&lt;"Achaemenid Empire"</data>
      <data key="d8">&lt;"Overthrown by Cyrus II who reigned as the first monarch of the Persian Empire,ending Assyrian dominion in modern-day Iraq and Syria."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-b7685a6f4e6e873aa232bfe92f1fbd33</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211053</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&lt;&quot;Neo-Assyrian Empire&quot;" target="&lt;&quot;Neo-Babylonian Empire&quot;">
      <data key="d6">4.0</data>
      <data key="d7">"Both empires are related through periods of dominance in the same region.".</data>
      <data key="d8">Succession and Continuity</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-e206803b8187f4934c68619b5564e588</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211054</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Hyksos people origin" target="Seqenenre Tao II">
      <data key="d6">12.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Seqenenre Tao II led efforts against the Hyksos, who were believed to have originated from Canaan.</data>
      <data key="d8">conflict</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-4579f206ae885a591950ddee175aec35</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211054</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Cyrus II conquests" target="Neo-Babylonians">
      <data key="d6">18.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Cyrus II conquered the Neo-Babylonians during his reign.</data>
      <data key="d8">conquest</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-4579f206ae885a591950ddee175aec35</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211054</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="India" target="cultural_or_religious_movement">
      <data key="d6">6.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The civilization centered in present-day India is marked as a major cultural pole during ancient times, known for its city planning, standardized practices, and religious innovations. It, alongside Ancient China, would have engaged in or been subject to significant cross-pollination of ideas through travelers, merchants, and monks.</data>
      <data key="d8">Cultural Exchanges,Religious Influence</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-96a9973262a04f62e37d1ec6cfe70c42</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211054</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="India" target="Warring States period">
      <data key="d6">2.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Although the Warring States period is primarily about Chinese history, it is related to India through the spread of philosophical and cultural practices that influenced later Indian traditions.</data>
      <data key="d8">cultural_influence</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-92d40e92f75ab6af9420387ca6d2a691</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211058</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Steppes" target="migration">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The Steppes acted as a pathway for migration and cultural diffusion, linking different parts of ancient Asia through nomadic movements that carried goods and ideas across vast distances.</data>
      <data key="d8">Cultural Diffusion,Migration</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-96a9973262a04f62e37d1ec6cfe70c42</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211054</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Koreans" target="trade">
      <data key="d6">6.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Through the migration processes involving Steppes and cultural exchanges between India and Ancient China, Koreans could have established significant trading networks. They would have contributed to trade and cultural exchange in Southeast Asia and possibly back to Central Asian regions via their trade hubs.</data>
      <data key="d8">Cultural Exchange,Trade</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-96a9973262a04f62e37d1ec6cfe70c42</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211054</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Japanese" target="trade">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Similar to the process involving Korean migratory patterns, Japanese people engaged in migrations across Steppes and along with cultural exchanges. They contributed to and benefited from extensive trade networks and cultural exchange routes including Southeast Asian hubs.</data>
      <data key="d8">4</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-96a9973262a04f62e37d1ec6cfe70c42</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211054</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Southeast Asia" target="cultural_or_religious_movement">
      <data key="d6">6.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Southeast Asia represents another region that benefited from cultural exchanges and migrations as influenced by India, China, Koreans, and Japanese people. It would likely have been involved in trade and religious movements through this network of cultures.</data>
      <data key="d8">Cultural Exchanges,Trade</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-96a9973262a04f62e37d1ec6cfe70c42</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211054</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Ancient China" target="cultural_or_religious_movement">
      <data key="d6">6.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Ancient China represents one of the dominant civilizational poles in Asia, along with India, characterized by impressive cities, kingdoms, and empires that significantly influenced regional history and culture for millennia. As such, it likely engaged in or was subject to cultural exchanges through monks traveling between regions.</data>
      <data key="d8">Cultural Exchanges,Religious Influence</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-96a9973262a04f62e37d1ec6cfe70c42</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211054</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Ancient China" target="Early Dynasties">
      <data key="d6">4.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The term Ancient China includes early political organizations like the Xia and Shang dynasties which are referred to as Early Dynasties. The rise of these early dynasties marked the beginning of Chinese civilization.</data>
      <data key="d8">dynasty,organization</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-793114b9aad7a4e7d97590998af0342f</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211054</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Ancient China" target="Terracotta Army">
      <data key="d6">4.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The Terracotta Army was created during the Qin Empire under Emperor Qin Shi Huang, making it a significant artifact from ancient times within the geographical area now known as Ancient China.</data>
      <data key="d8">artifact,period</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-793114b9aad7a4e7d97590998af0342f</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211054</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Ancient China" target="Great Wall">
      <data key="d6">4.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The Great Wall of China is one of the iconic structures that existed during the Warring States Period and was significantly expanded by later dynasties like the Ming, all within the geographical area now referred to as Ancient China.</data>
      <data key="d8">period,structure</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-793114b9aad7a4e7d97590998af0342f</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211055</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Ancient China" target="Qin Empire">
      <data key="d6">4.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The Qin dynasty (221-206 BC) unified China and initiated monumental projects like the Terracotta Army and early construction of parts of the Great Wall. This makes it a key period in Ancient China history.</data>
      <data key="d8">project,unification</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-793114b9aad7a4e7d97590998af0342f</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211055</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Ancient China" target="Warring States Period">
      <data key="d6">3.6</data>
      <data key="d7">The Warring States Period followed early dynastic periods within Ancient China and represents a significant historical phase marked by constant warfare between states as they vied for dominance.</data>
      <data key="d8">evolution,warfare</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-793114b9aad7a4e7d97590998af0342f</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211055</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&lt;Ancient Greece&gt;" target="&lt;Plato&gt;">
      <data key="d6">10.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Plato was a prominent philosopher from Ancient Greece.</data>
      <data key="d8">originating_from</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-d074051902a427319c33ea918573bbf9</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211054</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&lt;Plato&gt;" target="&lt;Academy of Plato&gt;">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The Academy of Plato is named after and associated with its founder, Plato.</data>
      <data key="d8">foundational</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-d074051902a427319c33ea918573bbf9</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211054</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&lt;Plato&gt;" target="&lt;Socrates&gt;">
      <data key="d6">14.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Socrates was Plato's teacher and mentor, influencing his philosophical ideas deeply.</data>
      <data key="d8">instructor_of</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-d074051902a427319c33ea918573bbf9</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211055</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&lt;Egypt&gt;" target="&lt;Nubia and Syria control">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">*Control over Nubia and Syria extended Egypt’s sphere of influence. Mentions indicate a long-term focus on expanding territory beyond traditional borders.</data>
      <data key="d8">military_campaigns,regency,succession</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-fd503ba9613920462a3a6dc53ae595a8</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211052</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&lt;Egypt&gt;" target="&lt;TheCollapseoftheBronzeAgeWorld&gt;">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Egyptian documents often describe interactions and transactions with other Late Bronze Age civilizations that declined during this time.</data>
      <data key="d8">interactions_decline</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-31f3de74d24f9f5ce534259a76865d74</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211059</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&lt;Egypt&gt;" target="&lt;Sumer&gt;">
      <data key="d6">2.0</data>
      <data key="d7">&lt;The influence of Sumerian culture and writing, such as cuneiform, may have influenced the development of Egyptian hieroglyphics.</data>
      <data key="d8">&lt;cultural_influence&gt;</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-2b5f6c1ce60c5a963cfeed75216a1324</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211106</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&lt;Egypt&gt;" target="&lt;Greece (Ancient)&gt;">
      <data key="d6">2.0</data>
      <data key="d7">&lt;Both ancient Egypt and Greece contributed significantly to world history through architecture, philosophy, religion, etc., and had overlapping influence. Greek historians such as Herodotus wrote extensively on the cultures of both regions.</data>
      <data key="d8">&lt;cultural_influence&gt;</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-2b5f6c1ce60c5a963cfeed75216a1324</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211106</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Terracotta Army" target="Qin Empire">
      <data key="d6">4.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The creation of the Terracotta Army is directly linked to the Qin Empire during its rule under Emperor Qin Shi Huang. The army was made to protect him posthumously.</data>
      <data key="d8">emperor,protection</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-793114b9aad7a4e7d97590998af0342f</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211055</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Great Wall" target="Qin Empire">
      <data key="d6">4.0</data>
      <data key="d7">While early versions of the Great Wall existed before, the more prominent parts were constructed during and after the Qin period. The building of this wall was a monumental project initiated by the Qin Emperor.</data>
      <data key="d8">construction,emperor</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-793114b9aad7a4e7d97590998af0342f</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211055</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Peking Man" target="Prehistory">
      <data key="d6">4.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Peking Man lived in prehistoric times in the region that is now part of modern-day China, marking one segment of human history leading up to more recent periods like Neolithic China.</data>
      <data key="d8">era,humanity</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-793114b9aad7a4e7d97590998af0342f</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211055</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Homo sapiens" target="Prehistory">
      <data key="d6">4.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The appearance of Homo sapiens in Asia and China is a key event in the prehistoric period, contributing to the cultural evolution that eventually led to dynasties and empires.</data>
      <data key="d8">era,humanity</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-793114b9aad7a4e7d97590998af0342f</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211055</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Paleolithic period" target="rivers">
      <data key="d6">6.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Ancient rivers provided resources and influenced development during the era." &lt;|"waterways, resources, development</data>
      <data key="d8">3</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-990942779714eb1b55832fae0d8365e4</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211055</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="rivers" target="Mountains">
      <data key="d6">6.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Rivers and mountains shaped the geography of ancient China, significantly influencing where early societies could thrive or survive." &lt;|"geography, environment, development</data>
      <data key="d8">3</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-990942779714eb1b55832fae0d8365e4</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211055</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="rivers" target="&quot;lowlands regions'&lt;&lt;">
      <data key="d6">6.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Lowland areas adjacent to major rivers were heavily influenced by the presence of nearby water resources for agriculture and trade." &lt;|"influence, resources, development</data>
      <data key="d8">3</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-990942779714eb1b55832fae0d8365e4</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211055</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="deserts" target="regional variations">
      <data key="d6">4.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Desert regions posed unique challenges but also contributed to regional cultural diversity among early Chinese societies." &lt;|"diversity, challenge, survival</data>
      <data key="d8">2</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-990942779714eb1b55832fae0d8365e4</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211055</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="grasslands" target="regional variations">
      <data key="d6">6.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Grasslands supported nomadic or semi-nomadic groups and similarly influenced the development of distinct cultures in China." &lt;|"diversity, survival, cultural</data>
      <data key="d8">3</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-990942779714eb1b55832fae0d8365e4</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211055</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="high plateaus" target="region of Harbin">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">High plateaus, like those around Harbin, often hosted unique ecosystems that shaped local customs and survival strategies. Their influence extended to regional diversity." &lt;|"ecosystems, culture, diversity</data>
      <data key="d8">4</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-990942779714eb1b55832fae0d8365e4</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211055</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Egypt" target="Annona">
      <data key="d6">10.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The role of Egypt in grain production was crucial as it functioned as an essential part of the Annona distribution system, providing a significant portion of grain required for Rome's large population.</data>
      <data key="d8">Annona,grain supply</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-26ccbf3441b6b8fad246e6cd56535b53</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211063</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&lt;&quot;Qiang&quot;" target="&lt;&quot;Shang kings&quot;">
      <data key="d6">2.0</data>
      <data key="d7">&lt;"Possible opposition or interactions between these groups during the Shang period"</data>
      <data key="d8">&lt;"interaction"</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-17c7ceed9ec0b5e033838e24607d90cc</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211055</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&lt;&quot;Zhou Dynasty&quot;" target="&lt;&quot;human sacrifice decline under Zhou rule&quot;">
      <data key="d6">2.0</data>
      <data key="d7">&lt;"Change from Shang practices to non-sacrificial policies under the Zhou"</data>
      <data key="d8">&lt;"decline"</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-17c7ceed9ec0b5e033838e24607d90cc</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211055</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&lt;&quot;Zhou Dynasty&quot;" target="&lt;&quot;heaven&quot;">
      <data key="d6">2.0</data>
      <data key="d7">&lt;"Heaven's influence over the Zhou concept of mandate from heaven, legitimating their rule"</data>
      <data key="d8">&lt;"mandate"</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-17c7ceed9ec0b5e033838e24607d90cc</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211055</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&lt;&quot;Zhou Dynasty&quot;" target="&lt;&quot;Shaanxi province in north-central China&quot;">
      <data key="d6">2.0</data>
      <data key="d7">&lt;"Political and cultural centers of the Zhou Dynasty were often established here, symbolizing its importance"</data>
      <data key="d8">&lt;"importance"</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-17c7ceed9ec0b5e033838e24607d90cc</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211055</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&lt;&quot;Zhou Dynasty&quot;" target="&lt;&quot;Table 5.1 Chinas Dynasties&quot;">
      <data key="d6">2.0</data>
      <data key="d7">&lt;"Overlapping historical frameworks for studying Chinese antiquity"</data>
      <data key="d8">&lt;"overlap"</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-17c7ceed9ec0b5e033838e24607d90cc</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211055</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&lt;&quot;Shang kings&quot;" target="&lt;&quot;Shang Dynasty era human sacrifice rituals&quot;">
      <data key="d6">2.0</data>
      <data key="d7">&lt;"Practices continued or influenced by the Shang rulers into their dynasty's legacy"</data>
      <data key="d8">&lt;"human_sacrifice"</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-17c7ceed9ec0b5e033838e24607d90cc</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211055</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&lt;&quot;heaven&quot;" target="&lt;&quot;cosmos&quot;">
      <data key="d6">2.0</data>
      <data key="d7">&lt;"Interconnected concepts, both influencing the perception of legitimacy and order in early China"</data>
      <data key="d8">&lt;"interconnection"</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-17c7ceed9ec0b5e033838e24607d90cc</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211055</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Shang Dynasty" target="caused by">
      <data key="d6">2.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Zhou Dynasty</data>
      <data key="d8">The fall of the Shang Dynasty due to despotic leadership led to the ascendance of the Zhou dynasty,as recorded in various classical texts supporting the Mandate of Heaven.</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-3f1b435017b04eb71afccb13dbfc34c9</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211055</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Mandate of Heaven" target="justified by">
      <data key="d6">2.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Zhou Victory</data>
      <data key="d8">The victory of the Zhou and defeat of the Shang were used to justify the Mandate of Heaven,signaling heaven's approval for the new ruling dynasty.</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-3f1b435017b04eb71afccb13dbfc34c9</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211055</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&lt;Western Zhou&gt;" target="&lt;Territorial Expansion&gt;">
      <data key="d6">2.0</data>
      <data key="d7">&lt;"During Western Zhou period, early Zhou rulers expanded their territory eastward over the Yellow River through military campaigns, establishing and expanding their influence."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d8">&lt;"Territorial Growth,Military Campaigns"&gt;</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-8fed1d6dd15981d033c4b2d57d29efbc</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211055</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&lt;Mandate of Heaven&gt;" target="&lt;Zhou Rulers&gt;">
      <data key="d6">2.0</data>
      <data key="d7">&lt;"The Zou rulers believed in and utilized the mandate to legitimize their rule, providing them with divine sanction for governance."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d8">&lt;"Legitimation,Divine Right,Rule Justification"&gt;</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-8fed1d6dd15981d033c4b2d57d29efbc</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211055</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&lt;Eastern Zhou Dynasty&gt;" target="&lt;Spring and Autumn Annals&gt;">
      <data key="d6">4.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Spring and Autumn Annals recorded events during the Spring and Autumn period, which was part of the Eastern Zhou dynasty. These records reflect the political turmoil and power struggles that shaped this era.</data>
      <data key="d8">recorded_events</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-8d279577288a6b48e916b34e9cf14a35</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211055</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&lt;Eastern Zhou Dynasty&gt;" target="&lt;Confucius&gt;">
      <data key="d6">4.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Confucius lived during the Eastern Zhou dynasty, a time characterized by societal upheaval and the beginnings of philosophical thought. His teachings were influenced by the changing political landscape of this era.</data>
      <data key="d8">influenced_by</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-8d279577288a6b48e916b34e9cf14a35</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211055</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&lt;Eastern Zhou Dynasty&gt;" target="&lt;Mozi&gt;">
      <data key="d6">2.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Mozi lived during the Eastern Zhou dynasty, but his philosophy emerged as a significant alternative to Confucianism during the Warring States period. Their teachings shared some intellectual space in this era.</data>
      <data key="d8">shared_period</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-8d279577288a6b48e916b34e9cf14a35</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211055</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&lt;Eastern Zhou Dynasty&gt;" target="&lt;Sun Wu&gt;">
      <data key="d6">4.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Sun Wu's treatise "The Art of War" was written during the Eastern Zhou dynasty, reflecting the widespread interest in military strategy and warfare that characterized this period.</data>
      <data key="d8">influenced_by_context</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-8d279577288a6b48e916b34e9cf14a35</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211056</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&lt;Eastern Zhou Dynasty&gt;" target="&lt;Warring States period&gt;">
      <data key="d6">4.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The Eastern Zhou dynasty ended with the Warring States period (475–221 BCE), marking a continuation and intensification of the political struggles that began during the Spring and Autumn period.</data>
      <data key="d8">succession</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-8d279577288a6b48e916b34e9cf14a35</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211056</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&lt;Eastern Zhou Dynasty&gt;" target="&lt;The Art of War&gt;">
      <data key="d6">4.0</data>
      <data key="d7">"The Art of War" was authored during the Eastern Zhou dynasty, reflecting the era’s intense interest in military strategy and tactics that were crucial for interstate competition during this period.</data>
      <data key="d8">context_influence</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-8d279577288a6b48e916b34e9cf14a35</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211056</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&lt;Eastern Zhou Dynasty&gt;" target="&lt;Mohism&gt;">
      <data key="d6">2.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Mohism emerged as a significant philosophical alternative during the Eastern Zhou dynasty, competing with and influencing trends set by Confucianism.</data>
      <data key="d8">intellectual_competition</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-8d279577288a6b48e916b34e9cf14a35</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211056</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&lt;Spring and Autumn Annals&gt;" target="&lt;Mozi&gt;">
      <data key="d6">2.0</data>
      <data key="d7">As a historical document, the Spring and Autumn Annals may have included events and philosophical ideas that contrasted with or directly engaged Mozi's teachings. Their presence in the chronicle indicates they were significant during this period.</data>
      <data key="d8">parallel_era</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-8d279577288a6b48e916b34e9cf14a35</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211055</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&lt;Spring and Autumn Annals&gt;" target="&lt;Sun Wu&gt;">
      <data key="d6">2.0</data>
      <data key="d7">"The Art of War" by Sun Wu, though later than Spring and Autumn Annals, reflected military practices and ideas that may have been recorded or referenced in the chronicle. The timing suggests overlapping themes.</data>
      <data key="d8">contemporary_themes</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-8d279577288a6b48e916b34e9cf14a35</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211055</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&lt;Spring and Autumn Annals&gt;" target="&lt;Confucius&gt;">
      <data key="d6">6.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The Spring and Autumn Annals are historical records that document events and philosophies which have directly or indirectly influenced Confucian thought. It is believed that Confucius likely had access to or was inspired by some of the records preserved in this chronicle. These annals, along with their topics related to societal issues and moral conduct, overlap significantly with the teachings of Confucius, suggesting potential direct influences between these two sources.</data>
      <data key="d8">influence_source,similar_topics</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-8d279577288a6b48e916b34e9cf14a35</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211057</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&lt;Spring and Autumn Annals&gt;" target="&lt;Warring States period&gt;">
      <data key="d6">2.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The Spring and Autumn Annals document events up to the start of the Warring States period, providing historical context and records for this tumultuous transition.</data>
      <data key="d8">historical_linkage</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-8d279577288a6b48e916b34e9cf14a35</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211057</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&lt;Confucius&gt;" target="&lt;Mozi&gt;">
      <data key="d6">4.0</data>
      <data key="d7">While both were philosophers active in later Zhou periods, Confucianism and Mohism provided philosophical alternatives to each other during the Warring States period. Their teachings contrasted significantly but still coexisted.</data>
      <data key="d8">philosophical_alternatives</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-8d279577288a6b48e916b34e9cf14a35</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211057</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&lt;Confucius&gt;" target="&lt;Sun Wu&gt;">
      <data key="d6">2.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Both figures lived in a time of great change, influenced by warfare and societal transformation. Confucianism focused on moral philosophy while Sun Wu's work emphasized military strategy, both critical for leaders during the Eastern Zhou era.</data>
      <data key="d8">parallel_context</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-8d279577288a6b48e916b34e9cf14a35</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211058</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&lt;Mozi&gt;" target="&lt;Sun Wu&gt;">
      <data key="d6">2.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Mozi’s focus on non-aggression and universal love contrasts significantly with Sun Wu’s strategic approach to warfare. Their lives overlapped in a chaotic period where both forms of philosophy gained traction but remained distinct.</data>
      <data key="d8">distinct_paths</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-8d279577288a6b48e916b34e9cf14a35</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211056</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&lt;&quot;The Analects of Confucius&quot;&gt;" target="&lt;&quot;Mandate of Heaven&quot;&gt;">
      <data key="d6">2.0</data>
      <data key="d7">&lt;"Both philosophies utilize the concept of Mandate of Heaven as a basis for ethical leadership, indicating that leaders should rule according to cosmic principles."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d8">&lt;"ethics,governance,heavenly mandate"&gt;</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-d49b5280ff80378cd7267e9b7173e02e</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211056</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&lt;&quot;The Analects of Confucius&quot;&gt;" target="&lt;&quot;Confucianism&quot;&gt;">
      <data key="d6">2.0</data>
      <data key="d7">&lt;"Originates from the teachings of Confucius and focuses on ethical behavior, personal integrity, and moral duty, aligning with the core themes of The Analects."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d8">&lt;"ethics,moral principles,philosophy"&gt;</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-d49b5280ff80378cd7267e9b7173e02e</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211056</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&lt;&quot;The Analects of Confucius&quot;&gt;" target="&lt;&quot;Daoism&quot;&gt;">
      <data key="d6">2.0</data>
      <data key="d7">&lt;"Influenced by Daoist concepts such as harmony with nature (yin-yang) and living in accordance with natural laws, promoting a balanced approach to governance."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d8">&lt;"harmony,governance"&gt;,natural law</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-d49b5280ff80378cd7267e9b7173e02e</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211056</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&lt;&quot;Confucius&quot;&gt;" target="&lt;&quot;Laozi&quot;&gt;">
      <data key="d6">2.0</data>
      <data key="d7">&lt;"Influences later Daoist philosophy through ideas of ethical governance and the importance of moral principles in leadership, mirroring similar themes to Laozi."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d8">&lt;"moral principles,governance,influence"&gt;</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-d49b5280ff80378cd7267e9b7173e02e</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211056</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&lt;&quot;Confucius&quot;&gt;" target="&lt;&quot;Zhuang Zhou&quot;&gt;">
      <data key="d6">2.0</data>
      <data key="d7">&lt;"Their philosophies overlap with each other as both emphasized the importance of personal integrity and ethical leadership, contributing to discussions on harmony and virtue."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d8">&lt;"ethics,influence"&gt;,philosophy</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-d49b5280ff80378cd7267e9b7173e02e</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211056</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&lt;&quot;Laozi&quot;&gt;" target="&lt;&quot;Zhuang Zhou&quot;&gt;">
      <data key="d6">2.0</data>
      <data key="d7">&lt;"The writings of Laozi influenced Zhuang Zhou's philosophical approach, both advocating for natural and harmonious living in society."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d8">&lt;"influence,natural law"&gt;,philosophy</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-d49b5280ff80378cd7267e9b7173e02e</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211056</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="China" target="Tang Dynasty">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The Tang Dynasty worked with Silla against their mutual enemies and later became rivals as both sought control over the Korean peninsula. The relationship between the Tang Dynasty and Silla encompassed periods of cooperation and competition.</data>
      <data key="d8">ally,conflict</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-2ffc1f0ad0cbf2629423c1e50ba8c211</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211057</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="China" target="Sogdiana">
      <data key="d6">12.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Both are influential in the Silk Roads network, with Sogdiana operating alongside key trading hubs that included Chinese goods. Chinese entities like gold, ivory, and rare animals were traded through these routes.</data>
      <data key="d8">Trade Route Hub</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-e0e7cff15604f2aa13915047df90d715</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211084</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="China" target="Panjikent">
      <data key="d6">12.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Panjikent operates in the same network of trade as China, likely trading similar goods such as those mentioned for Sogdiana. Both entities are part of the vast Silk Roads network, connecting different regions through trade.</data>
      <data key="d8">Trade Route Hub</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-e0e7cff15604f2aa13915047df90d715</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211084</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="China" target="Korea">
      <data key="d6">10.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Korea received influences from both China and Sogdiana through trade routes like the Silk Roads, indicating a direct relationship where Chinese cultural elements reached Korea via intermediaries such as Sogdiana.</data>
      <data key="d8">Cultural Influence</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-e0e7cff15604f2aa13915047df90d715</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211084</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="China" target="Japan">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Japan's unique traditions were influenced by both Korea and China, with Korean influence being a conduit for Chinese culture. This indicates a strong connection between China and Japan through intermediary cultures like Korea.</data>
      <data key="d8">Cultural Bridging</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-e0e7cff15604f2aa13915047df90d715</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211085</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&lt;Qin Empire&gt;" target="&lt;collapse of the Qin Empire&gt;">
      <data key="d6">16.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The collapse was due to Shihuangdi's harsh and oppressive rule, alienating powerful generals like Wang Jian and the populace, leading to widespread revolts soon after his death.</data>
      <data key="d8">Authoritarian regime downfall</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-e282907e1ce23dfd30fb1de511b8e168</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211056</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&lt;Qin Empire&gt;" target="&lt;Shihuangdi&gt;">
      <data key="d6">18.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Key decision-maker who established the Qin Dynasty and initiated policies that shaped its legacy, but whose brutal methods contributed to its decline upon his death.</data>
      <data key="d8">Author of the empire's rise and fall</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-e282907e1ce23dfd30fb1de511b8e168</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211056</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&lt;Qin Empire&gt;" target="&lt;Terracotta Army&gt;">
      <data key="d6">16.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The Terracotta Army was commissioned as part of Emperor Qin Shi Huang’s mausoleum complex, symbolizing his power and his belief in an afterlife with an army to protect him.</data>
      <data key="d8">Eternal accompaniment</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-e282907e1ce23dfd30fb1de511b8e168</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211056</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&lt;Qin's mausoleum&gt;" target="&lt;collapse of the Qin Empire&gt;">
      <data key="d6">14.0</data>
      <data key="d7">During the fall of the Qin Dynasty, looters damaged parts of the Terracotta Warriors site, but UNESCO's intervention helped preserve these archaeological treasures.</data>
      <data key="d8">Heritage rescue mission</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-e282907e1ce23dfd30fb1de511b8e168</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211056</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&lt;Qin's mausoleum&gt;" target="&lt;UNESCO World Heritage site designation&gt;">
      <data key="d6">18.0</data>
      <data key="d7">This historical recognition has been instrumental in preserving and promoting Qin’s cultural legacy globally through tourism, research, and historical education efforts.</data>
      <data key="d8">Global awareness campaign</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-e282907e1ce23dfd30fb1de511b8e168</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211056</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&lt;Qin's mausoleum&gt;" target="&lt;UNESCO website&gt;">
      <data key="d6">16.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The official documentation on UNESCO’s website outlines extensive details about why this site is a valuable part of world heritage, fostering international interest in protecting and learning from these historical treasures.</data>
      <data key="d8">Documentation and promotion platform</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-e282907e1ce23dfd30fb1de511b8e168</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211056</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&lt;collapse of the Qin Empire&gt;" target="&lt;Legalist philosophy and practices&gt;">
      <data key="d6">16.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The extreme policies advocated by Legalism under Shihuangdi contributed significantly to social unrest, making it unsustainable in the face of widespread resentment and rebellion across the empire.</data>
      <data key="d8">Ideological clash</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-e282907e1ce23dfd30fb1de511b8e168</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211056</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&lt;Shihuangdi&gt;" target="&lt;Prince Fusu&gt;">
      <data key="d6">14.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Despite appointing him as first in line for succession, Shihuangdi rejected Fusu’s vision for a less oppressive government, leading to Fusu's ultimate death during the Qin regime's fall.</data>
      <data key="d8">Succession rivalry</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-e282907e1ce23dfd30fb1de511b8e168</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211056</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&lt;&quot;Silk Roads&quot;" target="&lt;&quot;Sasanians&quot;">
      <data key="d6">2.0</data>
      <data key="d7">&lt;"'The Silk Roads played a crucial role in shaping the economic and cultural interactions between the Sasanians and other empires. It facilitated trade, diplomacy, and transmission of ideas."</data>
      <data key="d8">&lt; "trade &amp; international relations"</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-0f3483082d977617409e75f5948382d4</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211075</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Northern Wei Dynasty" target="Xiaowen's reign">
      <data key="d6">10.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Xiaowen’s rule during the Northern Wei Dynasty was characterized by significant policies promoting cultural exchange and assimilation between Xianbei and Han Chinese nobles.</data>
      <data key="d8">Cultural Integration</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-7933b681356fd848600cd95d60b6d201</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211056</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Goguryeo" target="Silla">
      <data key="d6">18.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Goguryeo initially overcame Han influence but was later absorbed by Silla, which eventually unified Korea.</data>
      <data key="d8">culture,unification</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-464eb2e1546acfe2e39b57d49cd04088</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211083</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Goguryeo" target="Baekje">
      <data key="d6">16.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The kingdoms of Goguryeo and Baekje were rivals, eventually leading to Baekje's defeat by Silla with Tang support. It highlights the complex power dynamics.</data>
      <data key="d8">rivalry-power</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-464eb2e1546acfe2e39b57d49cd04088</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211084</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Baekje" target="Silla">
      <data key="d6">23.6</data>
      <data key="d7">The Kings of Silla, along with support from the Tang dynasty, conquered Baekje in 660 CE following years of conflict. This victory marked a significant turning point where both Silla and Baekje fell to the Tang Empire's forces. Although initially described as merging into a single kingdom after their respective defeats, it is important to note that Baekje was already defeated by this time, so the description should reflect that only Silla contributed to forming a unified entity post-conflict with the support of the Tang dynasty. Both entities experienced a shared cultural and political destiny in the aftermath, as suggested by later descriptions, highlighting the enduring impact of this historical event on their joint future developments.</data>
      <data key="d8">conflict-unification,unification of kingdoms</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-c4306b2ba6966f8e780acc5a0a8ddd2f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-464eb2e1546acfe2e39b57d49cd04088</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211060</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Silla" target="Unified Rule">
      <data key="d6">6.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Silla emerged as the dominant kingdom and established a period of unified rule over much of Korea that lasted from 668 to 892 CE. During this time, Silla consolidated its power through strategic alliances, military victories, and cultural development.</data>
      <data key="d8">diplomacy,power struggle,unification</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-2ffc1f0ad0cbf2629423c1e50ba8c211</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211057</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Silla" target="Queen Seondeok">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">As queen during a critical period, Queen Seondeok's actions set Silla on a path to dominance when she leveraged the conflict between Goguryeo and China to ally with Tang forces.</data>
      <data key="d8">rise to power,strategic alliance</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-2ffc1f0ad0cbf2629423c1e50ba8c211</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211057</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Silla" target="Baekje &amp; Goguryeo">
      <data key="d6">10.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Through alliance with Tang forces, Silla helped to eliminate its competition from Baekje and Goguryeo in the north. This strategic cooperation resulted in the eventual fall of both kingdoms.</data>
      <data key="d8">alliance,conquest</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-2ffc1f0ad0cbf2629423c1e50ba8c211</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211057</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Queen Seondeoks reign" target="celadon ceramics">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">During Queen Seondeok's reign, Korean artisans learned to create celadon ceramics, a tradition that continued and expanded in subsequent periods.</data>
      <data key="d8">cultural innovation</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-c4306b2ba6966f8e780acc5a0a8ddd2f</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211057</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Queen Seondeoks reign" target="cultural exchange">
      <data key="d6">7.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Cultural exchange facilitated between China during the Qin dynasty's decline and Korea, influencing pottery techniques like celadon ceramics development.</data>
      <data key="d8">extraction influence</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-c4306b2ba6966f8e780acc5a0a8ddd2f</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211057</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Queen Seondeoks reign" target="tombs of Goguryeo">
      <data key="d6">6.4</data>
      <data key="d7">The period of Queen Seondeok saw the rise in tomb construction techniques that can be seen in the tombs of later Goguryeo rulers, demonstrating a continuity of cultural practices.</data>
      <data key="d8">cultural continuity</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-c4306b2ba6966f8e780acc5a0a8ddd2f</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211057</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&lt;&quot;Goguryeo Tombs Murals&quot;" target="&lt;&quot;Anak Tomb No. 3&quot;">
      <data key="d6">2.0</data>
      <data key="d7">&lt;"Located within the specific tomb site where the murals are found, providing context for the artifact's location and significance."</data>
      <data key="d8">&lt;"locational_context"</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-977f854853b555ebe9aa7924aa9d1777</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211057</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&lt;&quot;Goguryeo Tombs Murals&quot;" target="&lt;&quot;Chinese-style decorative elements&quot;">
      <data key="d6">2.0</data>
      <data key="d7">&lt;"Showcases Chinese cultural influence through decorative motifs, suggesting interactions or exchanges between Goguryeo and neighboring states."</data>
      <data key="d8">&lt;"cultural_exchange"</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-977f854853b555ebe9aa7924aa9d1777</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211057</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Early Waves of Foragers and Hunters" target="Climate Shift">
      <data key="d6">10.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The arrival of early waves was influenced by the significant climate change that isolated Japan, leading to adaptation among these groups.</data>
      <data key="d8">adaptation,climate_change</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-eb95afa387a7f6959f4e43eac7aed572</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211057</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Jōmon Pottery" target="Dogu Figurines">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Both artifacts represent advancements in technology and culture during the Jōmon period, with the pottery serving functional roles while the dogu served spiritual or ritualistic purposes.</data>
      <data key="d8">culture,technology</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-eb95afa387a7f6959f4e43eac7aed572</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211057</data>
