    </edge>
    <edge source="&lt;TheCollapseoftheBronzeAgeWorld&gt;" target="&lt;AegeanRegion&gt;">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The Aegean region, where both Mycenaean civilization and Troy were located, was impacted by the end of the Bronze Age era.</data>
      <data key="d8">regional_impact</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-31f3de74d24f9f5ce534259a76865d74</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211059</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&lt;Libyans&gt;" target="&lt;Bronze AgeCollapse&gt;">
      <data key="d6">10.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Libyan invasions contributed to the disruption leading to the collapse of central Egyptian governments during the Late Bronze Age. This resulted in a significant political and social crisis that lasted until 1100 BCE.</data>
      <data key="d8">collapse_of_egypt</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-bb886d541386cb6fe2ba6006b55bcddc</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211059</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&lt;Libyans&gt;" target="&lt;Phoenicians&gt;">
      <data key="d6">6.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Libyan invasions and Phoenician trade networks coexisted around 1200 BCE as significant geopolitical events occurring in the same region, both contributing to the cultural diversity of Mediterranean coastlines.</data>
      <data key="d8">trade_and_invasion</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-bb886d541386cb6fe2ba6006b55bcddc</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211059</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&lt;Phrygians&gt;" target="&lt;LateBronzeAgeCollapse&gt;">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Phrygian migrations into Asia Minor (Turkey) occurred during the same period as other significant movements of peoples that contributed to the Late Bronze Age Collapse around 1200 BCE.</data>
      <data key="d8">movement_of_peoples</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-bb886d541386cb6fe2ba6006b55bcddc</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211059</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&lt;Aramaeans&gt;" target="&lt;LateBronzeAgeCollapse&gt;">
      <data key="d6">10.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The Aramaean migrations into Syria and Mesopotamia began as a response to the turmoil of the Late Bronze Age Collapse, influencing local cultures and economies during this period.</data>
      <data key="d8">migration_and_influence</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-bb886d541386cb6fe2ba6006b55bcddc</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211059</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&lt;Aramaeans&gt;" target="&lt;Phoenicians&gt;">
      <data key="d6">6.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Both peoples were active during the Late Bronze Age Collapse, representing different waves in the movement of Semitic speaking groups into key areas such as Canaan and the Levant.</data>
      <data key="d8">migration_and_culture</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-bb886d541386cb6fe2ba6006b55bcddc</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211059</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&lt;Phoenicians&gt;" target="&lt;Carthage&gt;">
      <data key="d6">10.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Carthage was established by Phoenician migrants who brought their culture, technological advances (including shipbuilding), and commercial networks to North Africa around 800 BCE. This relationship highlights the continuity of cultural influence across generations.</data>
      <data key="d8">cultural_continuity</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-bb886d541386cb6fe2ba6006b55bcddc</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211059</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&lt;Phoenicians&gt;" target="&lt;LateBronzeAgeCollapse&gt;">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">During the Late Bronze Age Collapse, Phoenician cities faced various disruptions including invasions and trade shifts. However, they continued to evolve culturally and technologically through the development of their alphabet system.</data>
      <data key="d8">cultural_evolution</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-bb886d541386cb6fe2ba6006b55bcddc</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211059</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&lt;Carthage&gt;" target="&lt;Phoenician Empire&gt;">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Carthage was the imperial capital of the broader Phoenician Empire, centering its trade and political power in this region.</data>
      <data key="d8">Capital and Empire</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-556925683aa4f212797f42e3798eb53b</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211073</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&lt;&quot;6.2 Ancient Greece&quot;&gt;" target="&lt;&quot;Greek city-state development&quot;&gt;">
      <data key="d6">2.0</data>
      <data key="d7">&lt;"The period from 1100 BCE to 323 BCE encompasses the development of independent city-states and their political systems."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d8">&lt;"development,city-states,periods"&gt;</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-deeeaa267faae20e29663a365d84657c</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211059</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&lt;&quot;6.2 Ancient Greece&quot;&gt;" target="&lt;&quot;Mycenaean kingdoms collapse&quot;&gt;">
      <data key="d6">2.0</data>
      <data key="d7">&lt;"The collapse marks the beginning of Ancient Greece proper, setting the stage for developments throughout this period."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d8">&lt;"origin,foundation"&gt;,impact</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-deeeaa267faae20e29663a365d84657c</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211059</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&lt;&quot;Greek city-state development&quot;&gt;" target="&lt;&quot;Archaic Greece (800 - 500 BCE)&quot;&gt;">
      <data key="d6">2.0</data>
      <data key="d7">&lt;"The Archaic period saw significant developments in the political and social structures of Greek city-states."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d8">&lt;"evolution,development"&gt;,period</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-deeeaa267faae20e29663a365d84657c</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211059</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&lt;&quot;Innovative consensual governments&quot;&gt;" target="&lt;&quot;Archaic Greece (800 - 500 BCE)&quot;&gt;">
      <data key="d6">2.0</data>
      <data key="d7">&lt;"The period saw the emergence of diverse forms of governance which were precursors to more advanced systems in later times."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d8">&lt;"emergence,evolution"&gt;,history</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-deeeaa267faae20e29663a365d84657c</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211059</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&lt;&quot;Athenian and Spartan governance styles&quot;&gt;" target="&lt;&quot;innovative consensual governments&quot;&gt;">
      <data key="d6">2.0</data>
      <data key="d7">&lt;"Both Athenian democracy and Spartan oligarchy are examples of the diverse and evolving forms of governance seen in Ancient Greece. Both systems influenced the political landscape of the time, with Athens being particularly influential."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d8">&lt;"governance styles,influence"&gt;,innovation</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-deeeaa267faae20e29663a365d84657c</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211059</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&lt;&quot;Polis (city-states)&quot;&gt;" target="&lt;&quot;Acropolis&quot;&gt;">
      <data key="d6">2.0</data>
      <data key="d7">&lt;"Acropolises were common features in city-states and served as significant centers of political and cultural activities."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d8">&lt;"centers,features"&gt;,importance</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-deeeaa267faae20e29663a365d84657c</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211059</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&lt;&quot;Delphi&quot;&gt;" target="&lt;&quot;Olympia&quot;&gt;">
      <data key="d6">2.0</data>
      <data key="d7">&lt;"Both sites held spiritual significance in Ancient Greece and were key locations for major events such as oracle pronouncements and the Olympic Games."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d8">&lt;"significance,events,spirituality"&gt;</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-deeeaa267faae20e29663a365d84657c</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211059</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Olympia" target="Modern Olympic Games">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">References to ancient Olympia in naming modern Olympics suggest continuity and reverence for the original site.</data>
      <data key="d8">continuity,reverence</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-f530a5f4d471fe3bf47b9e7f89b1521f</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211059</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Archaic period" target="Ancient Olympic Games">
      <data key="d6">14.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The Ancient Olympic Games emerged during the Archaic period when societies began to specialize and develop more sophisticated artistic practices.</data>
      <data key="d8">artistry,culture,specialization,sports</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-f530a5f4d471fe3bf47b9e7f89b1521f</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211059</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Archaic period" target="Modern Olympic Games">
      <data key="d6">6.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Both Ancient and Modern Olympic Games have a historical lineage that traces back to developments during the Archaic period.</data>
      <data key="d8">continuity,legacy</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-f530a5f4d471fe3bf47b9e7f89b1521f</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211059</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Archaic period" target="Pindar">
      <data key="d6">6.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Pindar flourished during the Archaic period, contributing significantly to the cultural and artistic developments of the time.</data>
      <data key="d8">cultural impact,literary</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-f530a5f4d471fe3bf47b9e7f89b1521f</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211059</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Archaic period" target="Theodosius I">
      <data key="d6">4.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The end of the Ancient Olympic Games during Theodosius I's tenure relates to societal changes and pressures that emerged after the Archaic period.</data>
      <data key="d8">end,transition</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-f530a5f4d471fe3bf47b9e7f89b1521f</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211059</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Archaic period" target="Phoenician alphabet">
      <data key="d6">6.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The Phoenician alphabet played a vital role in the advancements made during the Archaic period of Greek history, including literacy and trade developments.</data>
      <data key="d8">innovation,literacy</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-f530a5f4d471fe3bf47b9e7f89b1521f</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211060</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Archaic period" target="Ancient Egyptian Art">
      <data key="d6">6.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Athenian artists during the Archaic period were influenced by Ancient Egyptian conventions, such as those seen in kouroi carvings.</data>
      <data key="d8">influence</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-d1b923a222fa396828cfac46f7a026c9</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211060</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Archaic period" target="Greek Theatre">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Theater developed during the Archaic period, incorporating elements of masks and performances inspired by cultural exchanges.</data>
      <data key="d8">evolution_influence</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-d1b923a222fa396828cfac46f7a026c9</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211060</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&lt;&quot;Pisistratus&quot;" target="&lt;&quot;Council of Four Hundred&quot;">
      <data key="d6">4.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The Council of Four Hundred and its establishment are closely associated with the period during which Pisistratus held significant influence over Athens. These councils were established as a measure to stabilize the city-state following the Pisistratid tyranny, serving as an indirect consequence of Pisistratus's rule. The formation of the Council sought to address ongoing political instability within Athens and aimed to prevent any future tyrannies that might emulate Pisistratus’s autocratic governance.</data>
      <data key="d8">&lt;"governance reforms post-tyranny",&lt;"post-tyrant governance"</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-a0e8d0356f28f7848ea4b63b26a9e3d1</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211062</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&lt;&quot;Pisistratus&quot;" target="&lt;&quot;Jury Courts&quot;">
      <data key="d6">2.0</data>
      <data key="d7">&lt;"During Pisistratus' rule, efforts were made to reform judicial processes, potentially influencing the creation or further development of jury courts. His governance aimed to gain popular support and legitimacy."</data>
      <data key="d8">&lt;"tyranny resilience"</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-a0e8d0356f28f7848ea4b63b26a9e3d1</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211062</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&lt;&quot;Solon&quot;" target="&lt;&quot;Jury Courts&quot;">
      <data key="d6">2.0</data>
      <data key="d7">&lt;"Jury courts were introduced during Solon's tenure, reflecting his commitment to promoting democratic elements. These institutions provided a platform for citizen involvement in the judicial process."</data>
      <data key="d8">&lt;"democracy and justice"</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-a0e8d0356f28f7848ea4b63b26a9e3d1</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211060</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&lt;&quot;Solon&quot;" target="&lt;&quot;Council of Four Hundred&quot;">
      <data key="d6">2.0</data>
      <data key="d7">&lt;"The Council of Four Hundred was a direct outcome of Solon's reformation efforts. His implementation of new governance structures and democratic elements helped pave the way for the establishment of this more inclusive body."</data>
      <data key="d8">&lt;"demos and citizenship"</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-a0e8d0356f28f7848ea4b63b26a9e3d1</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211062</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&lt;&quot;Council of Four Hundred&quot;" target="&lt;&quot;Jury Courts&quot;">
      <data key="d6">2.0</data>
      <data key="d7">&lt;"The Council and the jury courts both represent aspects of democratic reforms in Athens. While the council focused on government oversight, jury courts were significant for involving citizens directly in decision-making processes."</data>
      <data key="d8">&lt;"democratic institutions"</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-a0e8d0356f28f7848ea4b63b26a9e3d1</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211062</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Tang Dynasty" target="Abbasids">
      <data key="d6">10.0</data>
      <data key="d7">During the Tang Dynasty, the Abbasid Caliphate under Emperor Wuzong's persecution and subsequent military advancement into Central Asia challenged Chinese cultural and religious influence. The relationship is seen through historical records of Islamic spread impacting East Asian states.</data>
      <data key="d8">Islamic expansion</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-68886ae6bf0868f51bdd288cf9f2091f</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211084</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Tang Dynasty" target="Buddhism">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The Tang Dynasty experienced a significant persecution of Buddhism by Emperor Wuzong, driven by beliefs that threatened state order. This relationship underscores the tension between foreign religions and native traditions.</data>
      <data key="d8">religious conflict</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-68886ae6bf0868f51bdd288cf9f2091f</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211084</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Tang Dynasty" target="Aksumite Empire">
      <data key="d6">6.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The trade networks and cultural exchanges during Queen Ezana's reign extended to East Asia, facilitating interactions with empires like the Tang. Their relationship is best seen through shared economic interests.</data>
      <data key="d8">trade relations</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-68886ae6bf0868f51bdd288cf9f2091f</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211085</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Tang Dynasty" target="Srivijaya Kingdom">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Both empires were part of a broader trading network that facilitated exchange across Southeast Asia and beyond, impacting both political and commercial landscapes.</data>
      <data key="d8">trade routes</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-68886ae6bf0868f51bdd288cf9f2091f</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211085</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Tang Dynasty" target="Khitan Liao">
      <data key="d6">10.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The fall of the Tang Dynasty led to the rise of Khitan Liao, which controlled much of the former Tang domains and existed in competition with Song China.</data>
      <data key="d8">Competition,Succession</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-65567519161cbb48515c5592fd7dfa04</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211091</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;Ancient Rome&quot;|" target="&quot;Julius Caesar&quot;|">
      <data key="d6">2.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Julius Caesar was a significant leader during the late Roman Republic, leading military campaigns and political reforms before his rise to power. He became an integral figure in the transition from republic to empire.</data>
      <data key="d8">&lt; relationship_keywords="Leadership,Transformation"</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-629b8eb2a542c32ccebdf708239587e0</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211060</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;Ancient Rome&quot;|" target="&quot;Battle of Alexandria&quot;|">
      <data key="d6">2.0</data>
      <data key="d7">A critical event that marked the end of Roman Civil Wars and signified the dominance of Octavian as the ruler, thus marking the start of Pax Romana.</data>
      <data key="d8">&lt; relationship_keywords="Empire Formation,End of Conflict"</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-629b8eb2a542c32ccebdf708239587e0</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211060</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;Julius Caesar&quot;|" target="&quot;Rome&quot;|">
      <data key="d6">2.0</data>
      <data key="d7">His conquests and policies shaped Rome's destiny, contributing to its expansion and eventual decline after his assassination.</data>
      <data key="d8">&lt; relationship_keywords="Influence,Legacy"</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-629b8eb2a542c32ccebdf708239587e0</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211060</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="431BCE" target="Athenian Aggression">
      <data key="d6">16.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Athens' aggressive actions around 431 BCE were perceived as a direct provocation by Sparta, leading to the outbreak of the Peloponnesian War.</data>
      <data key="d8">aggression-conflict</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-7f10b984844e9f3bf6227734f9ce29f8</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211060</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Peloponnesian League" target="Sparta">
      <data key="d6">18.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The Peloponnesian League, led by Sparta, was instrumental in initiating the Peloponnesian War against Athens. The league's formation marked the strategic opposition of Athens through collective action.</data>
      <data key="d8">league-war</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-7f10b984844e9f3bf6227734f9ce29f8</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211060</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Peloponnesian League" target="Alcibiades">
      <data key="d6">12.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Alcibiades's involvement with the Peloponnesian League influenced strategic decisions and furthered conflicts, as seen in the Sicilian expedition.</data>
      <data key="d8">strategic-influence</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-7f10b984844e9f3bf6227734f9ce29f8</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211060</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Sparta" target="Peloponnesian War">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Sparta was a key participant and leader in the Peloponnesian League during the war.</data>
      <data key="d8">conflict_participant</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-3097b7631833d13406561b217f399efa</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211106</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Peace of Nicias" target="Sparta and Athens">
      <data key="d6">14.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The Peace of Nicias in 421 BCE was a peace agreement between Sparta and Athens, aiming to end significant hostilities during the Peloponnesian War although its effectiveness was limited.</data>
      <data key="d8">peace-war</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-7f10b984844e9f3bf6227734f9ce29f8</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211060</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Plague of 426BCE" target="Pericles">
      <data key="d6">14.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Pericles' leadership faced challenges due to the Plague of 426 BCE, which led to significant loss of life within Athens including his own.</data>
      <data key="d8">leadership-crisis</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-7f10b984844e9f3bf6227734f9ce29f8</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211060</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Socrates" target="Plato">
      <data key="d6">10.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Plato wrote dialogues featuring Socrates, who significantly influenced his philosophical system.</data>
      <data key="d8">philosophical_influence</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-c8a46da47c7b3c4b8e674ef868767514</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211060</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Socrates" target="Aristotle">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Aristotle was a pupil of Plato and later disagreed with him; both were heavily influenced by Socrates' teaching methods and ideas.</data>
      <data key="d8">philosophical_influence</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-c8a46da47c7b3c4b8e674ef868767514</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211060</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Plato" target="The Academy">
      <data key="d6">10.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The Academy was established by Plato as a center for philosophical study and discussion.</data>
      <data key="d8">establishment</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-c8a46da47c7b3c4b8e674ef868767514</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211060</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Aristotle" target="Lyceum">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Aristotle founded the Lyceum, which served similar functions to Plato's Academy but with different emphases in philosophy.</data>
      <data key="d8">founding_and_similarity_in_mission</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-c8a46da47c7b3c4b8e674ef868767514</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211060</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Greek Theatre" target="Golden Age">
      <data key="d6">10.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Greek Theatre flourished during the Golden Age, a period marked by significant cultural achievements including drama and literature.</data>
      <data key="d8">flourishment_within_period</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-d1b923a222fa396828cfac46f7a026c9</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211060</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Dionysus" target="Terracotta figurine">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The terracotta figurine depicts Dionysus, the god of wine and an influence on Greek theatre and theatrical masks.</data>
      <data key="d8">depiction_of_god</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-d1b923a222fa396828cfac46f7a026c9</data>
