    </edge>
    <edge source="&lt;historical_figure&gt;" target="&lt;Al-Mutasim&gt;">
      <data key="d6">12.0</data>
      <data key="d7">During his reign, Al-Mutasim had Mamluks serving as key military commanders and loyal to him. This relationship helped maintain stability during turbulent periods within the caliphate's history.</data>
      <data key="d8">loyalty</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-a51f56123d8110f2ea915388f363a913</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211085</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&lt;historical_figure&gt;" target="&lt;source_or_artifact&gt;">
      <data key="d6">10.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Geoffrey Chaucer portrayed Chinggis Khan in a positive and idealized manner, reflecting his understanding of the 14th-century perception of the Mongol ruler. This depiction is consistent across both descriptions provided for Chaucer. On the other hand, Montesquieu depicted Chinggis Khan with a cynical and critical view due to Enlightenment ideology, showcasing a more skeptical perspective typical of that period. Thus, while Chaucer's portrayal was favorable and reflective of his era’s generally admiring attitude towards the Mongol ruler, Montesquieu's depiction was influenced by the critical viewpoint characteristic of the Enlightenment era.</data>
      <data key="d8">cynicism,high_praise,idealization,skepticism</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-89576c0d4225b9ce6218c684fd4f58b7</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211098</data>
    </edge>
