    </edge>
    <edge source="Maya Calendar System" target="Maya Great Cycle">
      <data key="d6">6.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The cyclical nature of the Maya Calendar revealed important dates for rituals and was related to the start of a new cycle in 2012, according to scholars.</data>
      <data key="d8">cyclical_calendar_rituals_new_cycle_start</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-a3469f37ac0d52cc0b4327a6357a2e63</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211067</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Classic Period" target="Divine Lords">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">During the Classic Period, divine lords controlled religious life and political authority over city-states through wars and tribute systems.</data>
      <data key="d8">religious_and_political_authority_war_tribute_systems</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-a3469f37ac0d52cc0b4327a6357a2e63</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211067</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Urbanization" target="Tribute System">
      <data key="d6">6.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The growth of urban centers during the Classic Period was supported by a tribute system that provided resources from conquered or subdued enemies.</data>
      <data key="d8">urban_growth_resources_conquered_city_states</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-a3469f37ac0d52cc0b4327a6357a2e63</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211067</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Maya World" target="Maya Civilization">
      <data key="d6">10.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The 'Maya World' represents the geographical extent of the civilization during its zenith.</data>
      <data key="d8">civilization_location</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-d9cbdf3b7df94a4de04479b6873b5dfd</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211067</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Maya World" target="Family Ancestors">
      <data key="d6">10.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The Maya world's religious practices centered around honoring family ancestors as bridges between heaven and earth.</data>
      <data key="d8">ancestor_veneration</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-d9cbdf3b7df94a4de04479b6873b5dfd</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211067</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Family Ancestors" target="Ritual Bloodletting">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Ancestral reverence through rituals including bloodletting to communicate with deceased family members.</data>
      <data key="d8">ancestor_r礼worship</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-d9cbdf3b7df94a4de04479b6873b5dfd</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211067</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Maya Calendar" target="Ritual Bloodletting">
      <data key="d6">6.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Calendar influenced specific ritual practices, which included bloodletting and prayer directed at the ancestors based on seasonal timing.</data>
      <data key="d8">ritual_practice</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-d9cbdf3b7df94a4de04479b6873b5dfd</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211067</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Maya Ball Court" target="City-States of Maya Civilization">
      <data key="d6">10.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Every city-state within the Maya civilization had its own ball court reflecting a shared cultural practice.</data>
      <data key="d8">ball courts,city-states,cultural practice</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-86b8b74f4921d905c9deab7414c00e0c</data>
