    </edge>
    <edge source="Moche pottery" target="historical_concept_or_event">
      <data key="d6">17.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The Moche culture, which thrived between approximately 300 and 700 CE, is known for its depiction of ceremonial violence and militaristic practices through their elaborate pottery. The pottery showcases scenes featuring bound prisoners as a testament to the cultural rituals and conflicts during this period. These depictions not only highlight the Moche's engagement in warfare but also emphasize their incorporation of violent ceremonies into their societal and religious practices, providing valuable insights into the militarism that was integral to Moche culture.</data>
      <data key="d8">ceremonial violence,conflict,militarism,ritual</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-61af3f4c1b1189c9bb78051c623bf7d1</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211070</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="pilgrimage" target="historical_concept_or_event">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Nazca people made pilgrimages to Cahuachi, a significant religious and ceremonial site, for spiritual activities.</data>
      <data key="d8">pilgrimage,religion,rituals</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-61af3f4c1b1189c9bb78051c623bf7d1</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211070</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Nazca geoglyphs" target="source_or_artifact">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Geoglyphs were created by Nazca people to be seen from the sky or used in their religious practices since 200 BCE.</data>
      <data key="d8">geography,religion</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-61af3f4c1b1189c9bb78051c623bf7d1</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211068</data>
    </edge>
