    <edge source="Aztec Empire Tribute System" target="Codex Mendoza">
      <data key="d6">16.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The Codex Mendoza provides visual evidence supporting the description of the tribute system, highlighting various aspects such as warrior depictions. This relationship helps understand both the military and economic structures of the Aztec Empire.</data>
      <data key="d8">evidence,tribute,visual</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-87d72ac3e78c4c472db6934632abf483</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211068</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Aztec Warriors" target="Gulf Coast Area">
      <data key="d6">14.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The Gulf Coast area contributed rubber to the tribute system, which likely required coordination with Aztec warriors who could ensure its timely delivery and protection during transportation. This relationship reflects the interplay between military and economic systems.</data>
      <data key="d8">rubber,tribute</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-87d72ac3e78c4c472db6934632abf483</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211068</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Imperial Capitals" target="Aztec Tribute System">
      <data key="d6">18.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Tribute from provinces was submitted to imperial capitals, where these goods were processed or distributed further. This demonstrates the role of centers like Tenochtitlan in maintaining and reinforcing the empire’s centralized power structure.</data>
      <data key="d8">distribution,tribute</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-87d72ac3e78c4c472db6934632abf483</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211068</data>
    </edge>
