    <edge source="The Inca Empire" target="Chinchansuyu, Antisuyu, Cuntinsuyu, Collasuyu">
      <data key="d6">16.0</data>
      <data key="d7">These administrative regions were integral parts of the Inca Empire's governance structure. Each suyu was responsible for different aspects of resource management and ensured efficient administration across the empire.</data>
      <data key="d8">administration,territorial divisions</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-3ff58dc29260ce90c12894688e1e2ac5</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211069</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="The Inca Empire" target="resource management">
      <data key="d6">14.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Resource management in each of the four sunyas (Chinchansuyu, Antisuyu, Cuntinsuyu, Collasuyu) was a key aspect of Inca governance and ensured efficient use of resources across the empire.</data>
      <data key="d8">administration,resource distribution</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-3ff58dc29260ce90c12894688e1e2ac5</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211069</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="The Inca Empire" target="governance systems">
      <data key="d6">16.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The governance systems of the Inca Empire included administrative divisions like suyas and quipu-based record-keeping, which were essential for maintaining control and order within the empire.</data>
      <data key="d8">administration,governance structure</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-3ff58dc29260ce90c12894688e1e2ac5</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211069</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="The Inca Empire" target="resource distribution">
      <data key="d6">14.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Efficient resource distribution was a key aspect of Inca administrative practices, ensuring that resources from each suyu (Chinchansuyu, Antisuyu, Cuntinsuyu, Collasuyu) were utilized effectively to support the empire's needs.</data>
      <data key="d8">administration,resource management</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-3ff58dc29260ce90c12894688e1e2ac5</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211069</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Chinchansuyu, Antisuyu, Cuntinsuyu, Collasuyu" target="chuncho plants">
      <data key="d6">12.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Each suyu had unique resources like chuncho plants that were important for local agricultural production.</data>
      <data key="d8">agriculture,distribution</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-3ff58dc29260ce90c12894688e1e2ac5</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211069</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Quipu" target="resource management">
      <data key="d6">14.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The use of quipus in resource management ensured accurate and efficient tracking of resources across the empire.</data>
      <data key="d8">administration,tracking systems</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-3ff58dc29260ce90c12894688e1e2ac5</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211069</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="resource distribution" target="chuncho plants">
      <data key="d6">12.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The efficient management and distribution of resources like chuncho plants ensured a stable food supply throughout the Inca Empire, contributing to its stability and growth.</data>
      <data key="d8">administration,agriculture</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-3ff58dc29260ce90c12894688e1e2ac5</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211069</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Mississippian period" target="time_period_of_Cahokia">
      <data key="d6">6.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The Mississippian period encompasses the flourishing of Cahokia and its large urban center.</data>
      <data key="d8">Complex societal development</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-cd9c7f6e58d53fe2d7895baaf718081c</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211069</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Cahokia site" target="central_to_Mississippian_culture">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Cahokia was a central location for complex societal development during the Mississippian period.</data>
      <data key="d8">Urban center</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-cd9c7f6e58d53fe2d7895baaf718081c</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211069</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Monks Mound" target="part_of_Cahokia urban center">
      <data key="d6">10.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Monks Mound, a large earthen structure, is part of the Cahokia's urban center and serves as an example of complex societal development during this period.</data>
      <data key="d8">Urban architecture</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-cd9c7f6e58d53fe2d7895baaf718081c</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211069</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Burial mounds of Cahokia" target="related_to_cultural_practices">
      <data key="d6">6.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The burial mounds at Cahokia were used for significant burials, reflecting complex cultural or religious practices within the Mississippian period society.</data>
      <data key="d8">Cremation practices</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-cd9c7f6e58d53fe2d7895baaf718081c</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211069</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Chunkey game" target="part_of_Cahokia cultural practices">
      <data key="d6">6.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The ritual ball game of Chunkey played at Cahokia reflects important cultural and social activities during the Mississippian period, similar to ancient Mesoamerican ball games.</data>
      <data key="d8">Cultural activity</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-cd9c7f6e58d53fe2d7895baaf718081c</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211069</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Defensive wall around Cahokia site" target="part_of_Cahokia site infrastructure">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The defensive wall with watchtowers that surrounded the central complex at Cahokia demonstrated organized societal structures and protection mechanisms of the urban center.</data>
      <data key="d8">Structural defense</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-cd9c7f6e58d53fe2d7895baaf718081c</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211069</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Cahokia" target="Mississippian settlements">
      <data key="d6">2.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The settlement of Cahokia was part of the larger Mississippian tradition characterized by organized chiefdoms with complex social structures.</data>
      <data key="d8">chiefdom,tradition</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-ceba2c93f185573d710c16b29e1b461f</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211069</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Cahokia" target="Nobility">
      <data key="d6">2.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The nobility class in Cahokia had access to luxury goods and surplus food, reflecting the stratified society structure of the Mississippian settlements.</data>
      <data key="d8">social structure,stratification</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-ceba2c93f185573d710c16b29e1b461f</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211069</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Cahokia" target="Tribute system">
      <data key="d6">2.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Cahokia's power was based on its ability to exact tribute from surrounding groups through control of long-distance trade routes, illustrating the role of the tribute system in maintaining power.</data>
      <data key="d8">power,trade</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-ceba2c93f185573d710c16b29e1b461f</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211069</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Cahokia" target="Long-distance trade">
      <data key="d6">2.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Trade played a crucial role in the rise and fall of Cahokia, bringing exotic items from distant lands indicating the importance of long-distance networks.</data>
      <data key="d8">networks,trade</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-ceba2c93f185573d710c16b29e1b461f</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211070</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Cahokia" target="Moundville in Alabama">
      <data key="d6">2.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Both Moundville and Cahokia were part of the larger Mississippian tradition centered on complex ceremonial structures.</data>
      <data key="d8">ceremonial,tradition</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-ceba2c93f185573d710c16b29e1b461f</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211070</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Cahokia" target="Etowah in Georgia">
      <data key="d6">2.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Both Etowah and Cahokia were key examples of the Mississippian tradition, demonstrating the spread and consistency across different regions.</data>
      <data key="d8">spread,tradition</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-ceba2c93f185573d710c16b29e1b461f</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211070</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Mississippian settlements" target="Nobility">
      <data key="d6">2.0</data>
      <data key="d7">In Mississippian settlements like Cahokia, the nobility held a special status with access to luxury goods and surplus food due to their positions in society.</data>
      <data key="d8">rank,status</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-ceba2c93f185573d710c16b29e1b461f</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211069</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Mississippian settlements" target="Tribute system">
      <data key="d6">2.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The tribute system operated within Mississippian societies like Cahokia, where rulers exacted payments from subject groups for political or economic gain.</data>
      <data key="d8">control,payment</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-ceba2c93f185573d710c16b29e1b461f</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211069</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Mississippian settlements" target="Long-distance trade">
      <data key="d6">2.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Trade was integral to the functioning of Mississippian settlements, allowing for exchange of goods across vast distances and reinforcing social hierarchies through luxury items.</data>
      <data key="d8">exchange,hierarchies</data>
