    <edge source="Kushite kings" target="historical_figure">
      <data key="d6">14.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Kushite kings refer to rulers who governed the Nubian Kingdom of Kush during its history, some even ascending to rule Egypt in the eighth century BCE.</data>
      <data key="d8">rule_ascendancy</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-fd4afb589af42f7e98b4b08ee50decdd</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211071</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Nile delta" target="geographical_location">
      <data key="d6">10.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The Nile delta was part of the territory covered by the Kingdom of Kush, contributing to its trading network and expansion southward.</data>
      <data key="d8">territorial_expansion</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-fd4afb589af42f7e98b4b08ee50decdd</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211071</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Khartoum" target="geographical_location">
      <data key="d6">12.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Khartoum functioned both as the capital of present-day northern Sudan and part of the Kingdom of Kush's southern boundary in later centuries, highlighting its strategic importance.</data>
      <data key="d8">capital_strategic</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-fd4afb589af42f7e98b4b08ee50decdd</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211071</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Trade with Egypt" target="historical_concept_or_event">
      <data key="d6">10.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Trade between Nubia and ancient Egypt involved goods like ostrich feathers, ivory, ebony, incense, and gold.</data>
      <data key="d8">trade_goods</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-fd4afb589af42f7e98b4b08ee50decdd</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211071</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Ostrich feathers" target="source_or_artifact">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Ostrich feathers were traded from Nubia to ancient Egypt during the Old Kingdom period.</data>
      <data key="d8">trade_feathers</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-fd4afb589af42f7e98b4b08ee50decdd</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211071</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Pharaonic ritual and ceremony" target="historical_concept_or_event">
      <data key="d6">12.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Nubian gold played a significant role in Egyptian pharaonic rituals, as seen through the sarcophagus of Tutankhamun.</data>
      <data key="d8">ritual_importance</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-fd4afb589af42f7e98b4b08ee50decdd</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211071</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Sarcophagus mask of Tutankhamun" target="source_or_artifact">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The mask of Tutankhamun is a testament to the use of Nubian gold in Egyptian funerary practices.</data>
      <data key="d8">mask_gold</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-fd4afb589af42f7e98b4b08ee50decdd</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211071</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&lt;&quot;Kerma&quot;" target="&lt;&quot;Western Deffufa&quot;">
      <data key="d6">4.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The Western Deffufa served as a significant symbol and functional element in the urban center of Kerma. This major structure likely played an integral role in the city's administrative and ceremonial functions, making it a central and important feature of Kerma’s urban layout.</data>
      <data key="d8">&lt;"cultural_symbol',&lt;"urban_structure'</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-d7edb5c90c1347b6f59e1607916d8804</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
