    <edge source="new Muslim community in Medina" target="tensions within Medina">
      <data key="d6">6.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Internal tensions arose as the community grew and interacted with Jews and other groups within Medina, affecting stability.</data>
      <data key="d8">interaction,stability</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-33708cfaea8613ac13f575367841658f</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211078</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="new Muslim community in Medina" target="conflict with former brethren in Mecca">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The new Muslim community had to contend with their former allies in Mecca who later converted or were defeated, leading to a shift in allegiances.</data>
      <data key="d8">allegiance,conversion</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-33708cfaea8613ac13f575367841658f</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211079</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="conflict with former brethren in Mecca" target="surrender of Quraysh tribe">
      <data key="d6">10.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The surrender was a key event in resolving conflicts and leading to widespread conversions among the Quraysh and other tribes.</data>
      <data key="d8">reconciliation,resolution</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-33708cfaea8613ac13f575367841658f</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211079</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="conversion to Islam" target="united majority of Arab tribes under his leadership">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Conversion played a crucial role in drawing more tribes into the expanding Muslim community under Muhammad's leadership.</data>
      <data key="d8">conversion,leadership</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-33708cfaea8613ac13f575367841658f</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211078</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="surrender of Quraysh tribe" target="Battle outside Mecca">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The battle was a precursor to the eventual surrender of Quraysh, showcasing the efficacy of Muhammad's leadership in leading his community towards victory.</data>
      <data key="d8">efficacy,leadership</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-33708cfaea8613ac13f575367841658f</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211079</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="cleansing the sanctuary of Mecca" target="spread of Islam and death in 632">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The expulsion of idols and establishment of monotheism facilitated the spread of Islam by clearing obstacles to its message and practices.</data>
      <data key="d8">expansion,obstacle removal</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-33708cfaea8613ac13f575367841658f</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211079</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="cleansing the sanctuary of Mecca" target="Muhammad destroying idols">
      <data key="d6">6.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Muhammad's direct involvement in the cleansing event symbolizes his leadership role in the establishment of monotheism.</data>
      <data key="d8">leadership,symbol</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-33708cfaea8613ac13f575367841658f</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211079</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="cleansing the sanctuary of Mecca" target="Cleansing the Sanctuary of Mecca (depiction, Figure 11.12)">
      <data key="d6">6.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The artwork symbolizes and commemorates the event, reflecting its significance in Islamic history.</data>
      <data key="d8">commemoration,symbolism</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-33708cfaea8613ac13f575367841658f</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211079</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="united majority of Arab tribes under his leadership" target="spread of Islam and death in 632">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The spread of Islam and death of Muhammad marked the completion of a unification project spanning multiple generations and various tribes.</data>
      <data key="d8">completion,legacy</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-33708cfaea8613ac13f575367841658f</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211079</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="620s and 630s" target="Arab-Muslim armies expansion">
      <data key="d6">30.0</data>
      <data key="d7">During the 620s and 630s, Arab-Muslim armies were engaged in an expansionary campaign that aimed to consolidate and extend the reach of the early Islamic state. This period witnessed active military movements by Arab-Muslim forces to expand into nearby territories, marking a crucial phase in the growth and spread of Islam. These efforts continued the broader strategy of extending Islamic influence beyond the initial Arabian Peninsula, reflecting the dynamic and strategic nature of early Muslim community expansion.</data>
      <data key="d8">conflicts,consolidation,expansion,time</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-c7e3d55eb6e21abdcb5964c1eb485603</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211081</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="620s and 630s" target="Nomadic and Seminomadic Societies">
      <data key="d6">12.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Societies, including Arab-Muslims, were nomadic or seminomadic in nature and sought resources for survival.</data>
      <data key="d8">resources,survival</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-c7e3d55eb6e21abdcb5964c1eb485603</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211081</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Arab-Muslim armies expansion" target="Hardiness and Capability of the Arab-Muslims as a Fighting Force">
      <data key="d6">16.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The hardiness and capability were crucial for the success of expansionist movements.</data>
      <data key="d8">fighting,strength</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-c7e3d55eb6e21abdcb5964c1eb485603</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211081</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Arab-Muslim armies expansion" target="Weakness of Byzantine and Sasanian Empires at a Critical Juncture">
      <data key="d6">14.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The weakness provided opportunities for Arab-Muslims to launch successful raids during this critical juncture.</data>
      <data key="d8">opportunity,weakness</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-c7e3d55eb6e21abdcb5964c1eb485603</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211081</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Arab-Muslim armies expansion" target="Caliphate (an area under the control of a caliph)">
      <data key="d6">14.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The expansion contributed to the establishment and expansion of the Caliphate.</data>
      <data key="d8">expansion,state</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-c7e3d55eb6e21abdcb5964c1eb485603</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211081</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Early Islamic Art and Architecture" target="Byzantine Gold Solidus">
      <data key="d6">2.0</data>
      <data key="d7">("The Byzantine Gold Solidus exemplifies pre-Islamic influences on early Islamic art, serving as a reference artifact for understanding the cultural and artistic developments before the Umayyad era. The coin provides context for studying the transition from Greco-Roman to Islamic iconography."))</data>
      <data key="d8">("influence,transition"</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-8515eeaece70ecd2cb37ce5d05f004bd</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211079</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Early Islamic Art and Architecture" target="Umayyad family">
      <data key="d6">2.0</data>
      <data key="d7">("The Umayyad dynasty's governance significantly impacted the region’s art and architecture, introducing new styles and materials that reflected their rule. Their reign marked a critical period of transformation in Arabic-speaking regions."))</data>
      <data key="d8">("influence,innovation"</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-8515eeaece70ecd2cb37ce5d05f004bd</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211079</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Early Islamic Art and Architecture" target="Arabic script on coins and buildings">
      <data key="d6">2.0</data>
      <data key="d7">("The introduction of Arabic script on coins and buildings during the Umayyad era symbolized a shift away from pre-Islamic scripts. These inscriptions serve as historical evidence for understanding linguistic developments in early Islamic art."))</data>
      <data key="d8">("transition,language"</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-8515eeaece70ecd2cb37ce5d05f004bd</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211079</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Early Islamic Art and Architecture" target="Persian empire">
      <data key="d6">2.0</data>
      <data key="d7">("There are indications of shared architectural styles between the Persian and early Islamic periods. This connection suggests a continuation of cultural practices even as rulers changed."))</data>
      <data key="d8">("continuity,transformation"</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-8515eeaece70ecd2cb37ce5d05f004bd</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211079</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Early Islamic Art and Architecture" target="Christian crosses">
      <data key="d6">2.0</data>
      <data key="d7">("The removal of Christian crosses from coins by Umayyad caliphs marked a definitive shift in iconography. This change reflects the broader cultural and religious changes under Islamic rule."))</data>
      <data key="d8">("religious transition,iconographic change"</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-8515eeaece70ecd2cb37ce5d05f004bd</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211079</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Early Islamic Art and Architecture" target="Arabic language">
      <data key="d6">2.0</data>
      <data key="d7">("The adoption of Arabic as an official language under the Umayyads had a profound impact on art and architecture. Arabic inscriptions became prevalent in public monuments."))</data>
      <data key="d8">("linguistic transition,administrative innovation"</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-8515eeaece70ecd2cb37ce5d05f004bd</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211079</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Islam" target="Caliph">
      <data key="d6">20.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The religious and cultural movement known as Islam was driven by the leadership of the caliph, who governed the expanding empire during the Umayyad period.</data>
      <data key="d8">leadership</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-70d03803311b31d7e11f7ace07e0a57f</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211096</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Abbasids" target="Battle of Talas River">
      <data key="d6">10.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The Abbasid victory in the Battle of Talas marked their ascension as a major power that would eventually eclipse the Umayyads. The strategic position gained from this battle illustrates the dynamic political landscape between Islam and other powers.</data>
      <data key="d8">empire expansion</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-68886ae6bf0868f51bdd288cf9f2091f</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211084</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Baghdad" target="translation movement">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Baghdad, as part of the Abbasid Caliphate, supported and facilitated the translation movement.</data>
      <data key="d8">cultural center,support,translation</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-58a412da38bd50aef179fd14786b22f7</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211079</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Abbasids patronage of scholarly work" target="Papermaking techniques from China to Middle East">
      <data key="d6">6.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Chinese papermaking technology, introduced to the Middle East via the Abbasids, was supported by their patronage which facilitated extensive scholarly activities.</data>
      <data key="d8">increased learning,support,technological transfer</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-58a412da38bd50aef179fd14786b22f7</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211079</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Abbasids patronage of scholarly work" target="translation movement">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The translation movement was enabled by the Abbasid patronage as they fostered an environment conducive for translating works from various cultures.</data>
      <data key="d8">facilitation,support,translation</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-58a412da38bd50aef179fd14786b22f7</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211079</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="translation movement" target="Arabic translation of Dioscoridess treatise">
      <data key="d6">6.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The translation movement included translating works like Dioscorides's *Demateriae Medica*, contributing to the preservation and spread of knowledge.</data>
      <data key="d8">knowledge expansion,preservation,translation</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-58a412da38bd50aef179fd14786b22f7</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211079</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="medieval learning explosion" target="madrasas">
      <data key="d6">6.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Madrasas were established to support the increased scholarly activity driven by the medieval learning explosion, enhancing education across the region.</data>
      <data key="d8">education expansion,knowledge preservation,support</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-58a412da38bd50aef179fd14786b22f7</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211079</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&lt;University Libraries K&gt;" target="&lt;Baghdad&gt;">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Baghdad housed the University Libraries, key educational centers during the early Abbasid period.</data>
      <data key="d8">center-of-learning</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-4809fc11398ec2c07172fc5d5bf5447f</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211079</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&lt;Baghdad&gt;" target="&lt;Mamluks&gt;">
      <data key="d6">10.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Before Samarra's capital status, Mamluks played essential roles in Baghdad, indicating their importance within the Abbasid Caliphate's administration and defense mechanisms before the change of power base.</data>
      <data key="d8">administration</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-a51f56123d8110f2ea915388f363a913</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211086</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&lt;Baghdad&gt;" target="&lt;Samarra&gt;">
      <data key="d6">10.0</data>
      <data key="d7">With Baghdad ceasing to be the capital, Samarra emerged as a new center of intellectual and political life for Arab elites. This transition marked a shift in cultural and administrative focus within the Abbasid Caliphate.</data>
      <data key="d8">transformation</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-a51f56123d8110f2ea915388f363a913</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211086</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&lt;Baghdad&gt;" target="&lt;Arab elites&gt;">
      <data key="d6">10.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Before moving the capital, Baghdad was a hub for Arab elites, where they could engage in politics and culture. This relationship highlights the city's role as both an economic and intellectual center of the Abbasid Caliphate.</data>
      <data key="d8">engagement</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-a51f56123d8110f2ea915388f363a913</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211086</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&lt;Translation Movement&gt;" target="&lt;Early Abbasid Period&gt;">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Translation efforts were initiated and flourished during this period.</data>
      <data key="d8">translation-initiation</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-4809fc11398ec2c07172fc5d5bf5447f</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211079</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&lt;Sunni-Shia Formation&gt;" target="&lt;Early Abbasid Period&gt;">
      <data key="d6">7.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The formation of Sunni and Shia sects had significant impacts on political and cultural landscapes in this era.</data>
      <data key="d8">sect-development</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-4809fc11398ec2c07172fc5d5bf5447f</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211079</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&lt;Abu Bakr&gt;" target="&lt;Ali ibn Abi Talib&gt;">
      <data key="d6">6.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Muhammad's close followers and family members played crucial roles in early Islamic political structures.</data>
      <data key="d8">family-closeness/</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-4809fc11398ec2c07172fc5d5bf5447f</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211079</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&lt;Ali ibn Abi Talib&gt;" target="&lt;Husayn ibn Ali&gt;">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Lineage between the Shia imams who led opposition movements against existing caliphates.</data>
      <data key="d8">family-continuation</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-4809fc11398ec2c07172fc5d5bf5447f</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211079</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Abbasid Period" target="Caliph">
      <data key="d6">2.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The Abbasid period saw changes in the caliphs' authority as religious scholars (Ulamā) gained prominence.</data>
      <data key="d8">"authority_shifts,scholar_power"\|&gt;&lt;3</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-a3cce5ba44faddd8f9fd27e8b4f71bef</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211079</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Abbasid Period" target="Hadith Compilation">
      <data key="d6">2.0</data>
      <data key="d7">During the Abbasid period, the process of compiling hadith impacted the traditional religious roles and powers held by the caliph.</data>
      <data key="d8">"legal_authority_changes,knowledge_transmission"\|&gt;&lt;4</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-a3cce5ba44faddd8f9fd27e8b4f71bef</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211080</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Abbasid Period" target="Ulaman">
      <data key="d6">2.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The Ulamā class became increasingly influential during the Abbasid period as it gained more control over Islamic legal interpretation.</data>
      <data key="d8">"scholarship_ascendancy,legal_interpretation"\|&gt;&lt;5</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-a3cce5ba44faddd8f9fd27e8b4f71bef</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211080</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Caliph" target="Islamic Caliphate">
      <data key="d6">20.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The caliph was the leader of the Islamic state during the Umayyad dynasty and played a crucial role in shaping its policies and military strategies.</data>
      <data key="d8">leadership_role</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-70d03803311b31d7e11f7ace07e0a57f</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211096</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Abbasid period" target="Pact of Umar">
      <data key="d6">6.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The Pact of Umar was implemented during the Abbasid period to govern non-Muslims living in Muslim territories. This relationship highlights the political and social context within which the Pact of Umar operates.</data>
      <data key="d8">context,implementation</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-395768da0b846b0a0ea31c551387d142</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211080</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Abbasid period" target="geographical_location">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The Abbasid period influenced the cultural and religious landscape of regions such as Jordan and Egypt through the adoption of Arab and Islamic culture.</data>
      <data key="d8">adaptation,influence</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-395768da0b846b0a0ea31c551387d142</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211080</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Pact of Umar" target="&quot;non-Muslim communities'">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The Pact of Umar directly affects non-Muslim communities, defining the conditions under which they can live, worship, and maintain cultural practices within a Muslim society.</data>
      <data key="d8">conditions,regulations</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-395768da0b846b0a0ea31c551387d142</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211080</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Quran" target="Revelations">
      <data key="d6">2.0</data>
      <data key="d7">("The revelations received by Muhammad became the basis of the Quran, the holy scripture of Islam.")</data>
      <data key="d8">("Scripture Formation")</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-d7b99215b9caa771a96c7b4fed40b3f5</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211078</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Mawali" target="Ridda Wars">
      <data key="d6">6.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The special legal status of Mawalis influenced their participation in conflicts such as the Ridda Wars, often fighting on the side of Muslims against rebellious groups.</data>
      <data key="d8">conflicts_involvement</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-1446ed4b941391bd4aa671377ea66c91</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211080</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Mawali" target="Process of Islamization during the Abbasid period">
      <data key="d6">4.0</data>
      <data key="d7">During the Abbasid period, Mawalis, due to their non-Arab status, played a significant role in cultural and legal aspects of Islamization through their integration into the Islamic society.</data>
      <data key="d8">cultural_integration</data>
