    </edge>
    <edge source="Umayyad Caliphate" target="King Recared (Visigoth King)">
      <data key="d6">14.0</data>
      <data key="d7">"The Visigoths' conversion to Catholicism under Recared could have indirectly affected their relationship with the Umayyads, creating potential alliances or tensions.")</data>
      <data key="d8">7</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-981b9ba171235c0eda85615bee2f8630</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211085</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Non-Arian Subjects" target="King Recared (Visigoth King)">
      <data key="d6">16.0</data>
      <data key="d7">King Recared, the Visigoth King, initially tolerated non-Arian subjects within his realm. However, following his own conversion, which is not specified but likely to an Arian faith, his rule over these non-Arian subjects became significantly strengthened through subsequent policies he implemented. This religious transition and corresponding governance reforms had a substantial impact on the lives of the non-Arian subjects under King Recared's jurisdiction.</data>
      <data key="d8">conversion-policies,tolerance-conversion</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-981b9ba171235c0eda85615bee2f8630</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211088</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Umayyad Rule" target="King Abdul Rahman Al Ghafiqi">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">King Abdul Rahman al-Ghafiqi was instrumental in initiating and establishing Umayyad rule.</data>
      <data key="d8">initiation-establishment</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-981b9ba171235c0eda85615bee2f8630</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211086</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&lt;&quot;Maya civilization&quot;&gt;" target="&lt;&quot;Central America&quot;&gt;">
      <data key="d6">2.0</data>
      <data key="d7">&lt;"The Maya civilization was centered around the regions of Central America, particularly the southern parts which were rich with natural resources for agriculture and trade."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d8">&lt;"centered in,territorial span"&gt;,trade hub</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-33cf612417535ca257ea83d557f527a5</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211085</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&lt;&quot;Maya civilization&quot;&gt;" target="&lt;&quot;Agricultural practices&quot;&gt;">
      <data key="d6">2.0</data>
      <data key="d7">&lt;"The Maya civilization involved advanced agricultural techniques such as slash-and-burn, terracing, and irrigation to sustain their population."&gt;</data>
      <data key="d8">&lt;"agriculture,farming,land use"&gt;</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-33cf612417535ca257ea83d557f527a5</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211085</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&lt;Abbasid Caliphate&gt;" target="&lt;Mamluks&gt;">
      <data key="d6">12.0</data>
      <data key="d7">During 850 AD, Mamluks were loyal to the Abbasid Caliph and played essential roles in the caliphate's administration and warfare. Their loyalty to the caliph contributed significantly to maintaining stability within the empire.</data>
      <data key="d8">loyalty</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-a51f56123d8110f2ea915388f363a913</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211085</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&lt;Mamluks&gt;" target="&lt;philosophers like al-Farabi and Ibn Sina&gt;">
      <data key="d6">6.0</data>
      <data key="d7">In the period of 850 AD, these philosophers likely received support from Mamluk military power. Their patronage highlighted the interdependence between intellectual achievements and military influence.</data>
      <data key="d8">support</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-a51f56123d8110f2ea915388f363a913</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211086</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&lt;Samarra&gt;" target="&lt;Arab elites&gt;">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">After becoming the capital, Samarra continued to be a place of significance for Arab elites. Though they had been loyal during Baghdad's administration, their status likely remained important in new political and social structures established by Al-Mutasim.</data>
      <data key="d8">continued importance</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-a51f56123d8110f2ea915388f363a913</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211086</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&lt;Arab elites&gt;" target="&lt;philosophers like al-Farabi and Ibn Sina&gt;">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">During the period, Arab elites supported philosophers including Al-Farabi and Ibn Sina, contributing to a vibrant intellectual culture within the Abbasid Caliphate.</data>
      <data key="d8">support</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-a51f56123d8110f2ea915388f363a913</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211086</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&lt;Arab elites&gt;" target="&lt;Baghdad's capital status&gt;">
      <data key="d6">10.0</data>
      <data key="d7">While Baghdad was the capital, Arab elites operated within its political framework, contributing to governance and culture before Samarra became the new seat of power. This continuity symbolized a unified intellectual community under one administration.</data>
      <data key="d8">administrate</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-a51f56123d8110f2ea915388f363a913</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211086</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&lt;Byzantine rivals&gt;" target="&lt;philosophers like al-Farabi and Ibn Sina&gt;">
      <data key="d6">6.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Philosophers were influenced by the ongoing conflict with Byzantium, creating works critical of their opponents and promoting Islamic philosophy as an alternative.</data>
      <data key="d8">influence</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-a51f56123d8110f2ea915388f363a913</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211086</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&lt;philosophers like al-Farabi and Ibn Sina&gt;" target="&lt;translation into Arabic&gt;">
      <data key="d6">12.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Philosophers such as Al-Farabi and Ibn Sina relied on translations from Greek philosophical works to develop their own ideas, showing the importance of this activity for intellectual growth during the Abbasid era.</data>
      <data key="d8">knowledge</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-a51f56123d8110f2ea915388f363a913</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211086</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&lt;philosophers like al-Farabi and Ibn Sina&gt;" target="&lt;Al-Mutasim's reign&gt;">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">During Al-Mutasim’s rule, the intellectual output of philosophers including Al-Farabi and Ibn Sina flourished. His support for translations into Arabic also contributed to this period.</data>
      <data key="d8">governmental patronage</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-a51f56123d8110f2ea915388f363a913</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211086</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&lt;philosophers like al-Farabi and Ibn Sina&gt;" target="&lt;Byzantine rivals'&gt;">
      <data key="d6">16.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The philosophers like al-Farabi and Ibn Sina, whose works reflected the need to assert Islamic philosophy against Byzantine influence, played a significant role in maintaining cultural identity through intellectual endeavors. Their writings addressed concerns about the Byzantine Empire, aiming to define Islamic philosophy as distinct from Greco-Roman thought. This is evident in their critical stance towards the Christian-dominated power, demonstrating their efforts to establish a unique philosophical tradition within an Islamic context.</data>
      <data key="d8">cultural assertion,philosophical debate</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-a51f56123d8110f2ea915388f363a913</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211088</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&lt;philosophers like al-Farabi and Ibn Sina&gt;" target="&lt;Arab elites'&gt;">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">During this era, Arab elites supported philosophers such as Al-Farabi and Ibn Sina, promoting a flourishing of both political and intellectual life. This support established strong ties between state patronage and personal scholarship.</data>
      <data key="d8">institutionalized support</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-a51f56123d8110f2ea915388f363a913</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211088</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&lt;translation into Arabic&gt;" target="&lt;Islamic scholars translating texts into Arabic&gt;">
      <data key="d6">16.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The activity of Islamic scholars translating ancient philosophical texts from Greek into Arabic was crucial for preserving knowledge and spreading it throughout the Abbasid Caliphate. These translations later influenced Christian thought in Europe through further translations.</data>
      <data key="d8">knowledge</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-a51f56123d8110f2ea915388f363a913</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211086</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&lt;translation into Arabic&gt;" target="&lt;civil war supported by mamluks&gt;">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Translation efforts contributed to intellectual life during periods of unrest. By preserving knowledge in the face of civil strife, scholars maintained continuity within the Abbasid Caliphate's cultural heritage.</data>
      <data key="d8">continuity</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-a51f56123d8110f2ea915388f363a913</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211086</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&lt;translation into Arabic&gt;" target="&lt;Byzantine rivals' influence&gt;">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The conflict with Byzantium influenced translators, as they sought to preserve and propagate knowledge against the perceived threat of their cultural counterparts. This tension drove translation efforts as a form of assertive intellectual diplomacy.</data>
      <data key="d8">threat assertion</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-a51f56123d8110f2ea915388f363a913</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211086</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&lt;translation into Arabic&gt;" target="&lt;Mamluks'&gt;">
      <data key="d6">10.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The act of translating texts during the Abbasid period provided Mamluk military support with intellectual and cultural capital, potentially enhancing their social status among other elites. This partnership highlighted a unique alliance between military might and academic pursuit.</data>
      <data key="d8">social alliance</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-a51f56123d8110f2ea915388f363a913</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211086</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&lt;translation into Arabic&gt;" target="&lt;Baghdad's transition of power base'&gt;">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Translation efforts continued as Baghdad lost its status as the capital. This practice underlined the resilience and adaptability of scholarly pursuits in changing political environments, ensuring knowledge remained central to Abbasid culture.</data>
      <data key="d8">scholarly continuity</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-a51f56123d8110f2ea915388f363a913</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211086</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&lt;XXIII&gt;" target="&lt;XXIV&gt;">
      <data key="d6">6.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The Abbasid Caliphate provided the backdrop for the growth and spread of Sufism through its cultural renaissance.</data>
      <data key="d8">Cultural renaissance,spirituality</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-309095f0336265497cf6191180cb1924</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211087</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&lt;XXIV&gt;" target="&lt;XXV&gt;">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Sufism gained momentum during the Abbasid era, contributing to the rich tapestry of Islamic culture and religion within the empire.</data>
      <data key="d8">Spirituality,growth</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-309095f0336265497cf6191180cb1924</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211087</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Seljuk Empire" target="Caravansaries">
      <data key="d6">14.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Caravansaries were crucial in maintaining and expanding trade networks within the Seljuk Empire, serving as key infrastructure for facilitating commerce and cultural exchange. The Seljuk Empire recognized their importance and supported the development of these establishments to enhance both economic and cross-cultural interactions throughout its territories.</data>
      <data key="d8">infrastructure,maintenance,network,support,trade,trade routes</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-ef569534db6161860971327c3bc8b184</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211089</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Seljuk Empire" target="Caliphate">
      <data key="d6">4.0</data>
      <data key="d7">While the Sultans of the Seljuk Empire recognized the Abbasid Caliph as a religious leader, they exercised independent political authority.</data>
      <data key="d8">politics,recognition,ruler</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-ef569534db6161860971327c3bc8b184</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211089</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Seljuk Empire" target="Sunni Islam">
      <data key="d6">6.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The Seljuk sultans patronized Sunni Islamic scholars and institutions as a means to assert control over religious doctrine.</data>
      <data key="d8">doctrine,patronization,scholar</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-ef569534db6161860971327c3bc8b184</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211089</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="First Crusade" target="County of Edessa">
      <data key="d6">10.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The County of Edessa was one of the territories taken during the First Crusade, forming part of the larger political landscape established by the crusaders.</data>
      <data key="d8">territorial_claims</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-aeb38d9c22dbab062c46fb4a7bdfb465</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211090</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="First Crusade" target="Principality of Antioch">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Principality of Antioch also emerged as a result of the success of the First Crusade, illustrating the broader impact of the historic event on regional governance and control.</data>
      <data key="d8">territorial_expansion</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-aeb38d9c22dbab062c46fb4a7bdfb465</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211090</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="First Crusade" target="(&quot;County of Tripoli&quot;">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The County of Tripoli was established during the time of the First Crusade, signifying the ongoing expansion and stabilization of Crusader states in the region.</data>
      <data key="d8">state_construction</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-aeb38d9c22dbab062c46fb4a7bdfb465</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211090</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="First Crusade" target="Kingdom of Jerusalem">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The establishment of the Kingdom of Jerusalem marked one of the main achievements of the First Crusade, setting a precedent for further territorial claims and governance by Crusaders in the region.</data>
      <data key="d8">state_formation</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-aeb38d9c22dbab062c46fb4a7bdfb465</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211090</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="First Crusade" target="Outremer">
      <data key="d6">9.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The Outremer represents the collective territories gained and administered during the First Crusade, making it a synthesis of the various Crusader states founded as a result of this event.</data>
      <data key="d8">territorial_unification</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-aeb38d9c22dbab062c46fb4a7bdfb465</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211090</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="First Crusade" target="Muslim inhabitants">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The mass violence against Muslim inhabitants reflects both a by-product and target of the First Crusade, highlighting its religious and military objectives.</data>
      <data key="d8">conflict_and_crusaders</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-aeb38d9c22dbab062c46fb4a7bdfb465</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211090</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Caliphate" target="Sultans">
      <data key="d6">12.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The Sultans and Caliphate maintained a relationship where the Sultans recognized the Caliph's religious authority, yet operated independently in matters of political governance. This arrangement involved a dual system of leadership: a clear spiritual hierarchy led by the Caliph, alongside a separate but concurrent political leadership structure managed by the Sultans.</data>
      <data key="d8">acknowledging,authoriy,autonomy,hierarchy,leadership,political,politics,recognized</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-ef569534db6161860971327c3bc8b184</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211092</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Caliphate" target="Sunni Islam">
      <data key="d6">6.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The Caliphate's recognition and support of Sunni Islam facilitated religious unity among diverse populations within the Seljuk Empire.</data>
      <data key="d8">diversity,recognition,unity</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-ef569534db6161860971327c3bc8b184</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211092</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Sultans" target="Sunni Islam">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The Sultans governed the Seljuk Empire while promoting Sunni Islamic principles through cultural, artistic, and scholarly pursuits.</data>
      <data key="d8">governance,promotion,rules</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-ef569534db6161860971327c3bc8b184</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211087</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Sultans" target="Caravansaries">
      <data key="d6">6.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The Sultans utilized caravansaries to oversee transportation and logistics in their empire.</data>
      <data key="d8">logistics,transportation,utilize</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-ef569534db6161860971327c3bc8b184</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211089</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Macedonian Renaissance" target="Paris Psalter">
      <data key="d6">10.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The Paris Psalter exemplifies the artistic advancements of the Macedonian Renaissance period through its illuminated manuscript from Constantinople in the tenth century.</data>
      <data key="d8">"Artistic Innovations"|</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-99442760cad93ba2b6238a5063d5e7a3</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211089</data>
    </edge>
