    <edge source="Ottoman Control Overland and Maritime Silk Routes" target="Fall of Constantinople">
      <data key="d6">10.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The control over trade routes established by the Ottomans contributed to the fall of Constantinople as it disrupted traditional trade patterns, leading to Greek refugees seeking sanctuary in western Europe.</data>
      <data key="d8">Trade Disruption</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-54c526f99d90b73a6903c122c8847aeb</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211101</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Fall of Constantinople" target="Trade Routes and European Response">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The fall of Constantinople and subsequent Ottoman control over trade routes prompted a major shift in European trading strategies, moving from overland to maritime routes.</data>
      <data key="d8">Strategic Adaptation</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-54c526f99d90b73a6903c122c8847aeb</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211101</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Fall of Constantinople" target="Anna Notaras">
      <data key="d6">10.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Following the fall of Constantinople many sought refuge; Anna Notaras helped Greek refugees including those coming to Venice, where she was instrumental in their resettlement.</data>
      <data key="d8">Refugee Assistance</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-54c526f99d90b73a6903c122c8847aeb</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211101</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Fall of Constantinople" target="Byzantine Migration to Italy">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The migration saw a significant number of people fleeing the destruction and instability post-Constantinople fall, with some finding homes in Italian city-states like Venice.</data>
      <data key="d8">Migration Route</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-54c526f99d90b73a6903c122c8847aeb</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211101</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Fall of Constantinople" target="Knowledge Transfer During Renaissance">
      <data key="d6">10.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Greek scholars fled Constantinople after its fall, bringing classical knowledge that fueled the Renaissance intellectual movement in Europe.</data>
      <data key="d8">Classical Knowledge Transfer</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-54c526f99d90b73a6903c122c8847aeb</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211101</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Fall of Constantinople" target="Petrarch Influence on Latin Language and Style">
      <data key="d6">10.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Many Greek refugees brought with them classical texts and learning, greatly influencing figures like Petrarch who advocated for a revival in Classical Latin during the Renaissance.</data>
      <data key="d8">Classical Revival</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-54c526f99d90b73a6903c122c8847aeb</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211101</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="1485" target="The Birth of Venus (1485)">
      <data key="d6">2.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Sandro Botticelli’s 1485 painting represents the artistic achievements of the Renaissance in that year.</data>
      <data key="d8">Artistic Achievement | 5</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-23659b0349306744ecfc24e78e40bee3</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
