    <edge source="&lt;geographical_location&gt;&lt;Red_Sea&gt;" target="&lt;trade_routes&gt;">
      <data key="d6">16.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The Red Sea region, encompassing important trade routes like those through Adulis, was at the heart of maritime commerce and cultural exchanges.</data>
      <data key="d8">red_sea_trade_routes</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-b8ad3f8265df80ff0cbd95a7914a6123</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211083</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&lt;geographical_location&gt;&lt;Red_Sea&gt;" target="&lt;historical_concept_or_event&gt;&lt;Silk_Roads&gt;">
      <data key="d6">16.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The Red Sea, which includes key ports like Adulis, was a crucial part of the Silk Roads network, facilitating trade and exchange across extensive distances.</data>
      <data key="d8">red_sea_in_silk_roads</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-b8ad3f8265df80ff0cbd95a7914a6123</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211083</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&lt;civilization_or_empire&gt;&lt;Kingdom_of_Aksum&gt;" target="&lt;geographical_location&gt;&lt;Aksum&gt;">
      <data key="d6">18.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Aksum was an important hub for trade routes connecting not only to other African regions but also facilitating connections to Europe and Asia during its height in the 1st to 8th centuries AD.</data>
      <data key="d8">trade_routes</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-b8ad3f8265df80ff0cbd95a7914a6123</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211081</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&lt;civilization_or_empire&gt;&lt;Kingdom_of_Aksum&gt;" target="&lt;historical_concept_or_event&gt;&lt;Silk_Roads&gt;">
      <data key="d6">32.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The Kingdom of Aksum was an influential entity that played a significant role in various trade networks, including those that eventually became part of the Silk Roads. The kingdom's substantial wealth enabled it to engage in long-distance trading, contributing essential goods and services that likely supported the broader functioning of the Silk Roads network.</data>
      <data key="d8">network_interconnections,trade_network_interactions</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-b8ad3f8265df80ff0cbd95a7914a6123</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211082</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&lt;civilization_or_empire&gt;&lt;Kingdom_of_Aksum&gt;" target="&lt;cultural_or_religious_movement&gt;&lt;Hinduism&gt;">
      <data key="d6">14.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Trade with Aksum often led to cultural exchanges, including influences from Hinduism that might have flowed into and through the region.</data>
      <data key="d8">cultural_exchange</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-b8ad3f8265df80ff0cbd95a7914a6123</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211083</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&lt;civilization_or_empire&gt;&lt;Kingdom_of_Aksum&gt;" target="&lt;geographical_location&gt;&lt;Gulf_of_Suez&gt;">
      <data key="d6">16.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The Kingdom of Aksum had interests and activities in the Gulf of Suez, given its broader trade footprint along the Red Sea.</data>
      <data key="d8">naval_interests</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-b8ad3f8265df80ff0cbd95a7914a6123</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211083</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&lt;cultural_or_religious_movement&gt;&lt;Islamization_of_indonesia&gt;" target="&lt;historical_figure&gt;&lt;Not_explicitly_mentioned_in_the_text&gt;">
      <data key="d6">18.0</data>
      <data key="d7">This relationship indicates the indirect role that ancient or medieval trade could have played in spreading Islam into far-flung areas like Indonesia.</data>
      <data key="d8">indirect_spread</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-b8ad3f8265df80ff0cbd95a7914a6123</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211081</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&lt;cultural_or_religious_movement&gt;&lt;Islamization_of_indonesia&gt;" target="&lt;historical_concept_or_event&gt;&lt;Silk_Roads&gt;">
      <data key="d6">18.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The Silk Roads played a crucial role in the spread of Islam, including to areas like Indonesia, through various trade and cultural interactions along these networks.</data>
      <data key="d8">silksroad_islamization</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-b8ad3f8265df80ff0cbd95a7914a6123</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211083</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&lt;historical_figure&gt;&lt;Not_explicitly_mentioned_in_the_text&gt;" target="&lt;cultural_or_religious_movement&gt;&lt;Hinduism&gt;">
      <data key="d6">12.0</data>
      <data key="d7">This relationship denotes the possibility of indirect influence or knowledge transfer related to Hinduism's practices through trade and cultural interactions.</data>
      <data key="d8">indirect_cultural_exchange</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-b8ad3f8265df80ff0cbd95a7914a6123</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211083</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&lt;geographical_location&gt;&lt;Yemeni_area_of_the_Arabian_Peninsula&gt;" target="&lt;cultural_or_religious_movement&gt;&lt;Hinduism&gt;">
      <data key="d6">28.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Hinduism's cultural and religious practices may have had an impact on the Yemeni area of the Arabian Peninsula during certain periods, particularly through cross-cultural interactions facilitated by trade networks. These interactions likely occurred via routes that passed near or directly through Adulis, a significant ancient port city in Yemen. The presence of Hindu cultural practices in this region suggests that trade activities played a crucial role in spreading religious and cultural influences across geographic distances during these historical times.</data>
      <data key="d8">cross_cultural_influences,hindu_culture_in_yemini</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-b8ad3f8265df80ff0cbd95a7914a6123</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211083</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&lt;cultural_or_religious_movement&gt;&lt;Hinduism&gt;" target="&lt;geographical_location&gt;&lt;Indian_Ocean&gt;">
      <data key="d6">14.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Hindu traditions and practices likely spread through maritime networks, including those connected to Indian Ocean trading routes, which might have involved Aksum.</data>
      <data key="d8">maritime_spread</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-b8ad3f8265df80ff0cbd95a7914a6123</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211083</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&lt;cultural_or_religious_movement&gt;&lt;Hinduism&gt;" target="&lt;historical_concept_or_event&gt;&lt;Silk_Roads&gt;">
      <data key="d6">16.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Through the Silk Roads, Hindu cultural practices and knowledge likely spread across vast distances, influencing various regions along these extensive trade routes.</data>
      <data key="d8">interactions_across_silk_roads</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-b8ad3f8265df80ff0cbd95a7914a6123</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211083</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&lt;cultural_or_religious_movement&gt;&lt;Hinduism&gt;" target="&lt;historical_concept_or_event&gt;&lt;Islamization_of_indonesia&gt;">
      <data key="d6">14.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Trade via the Silk Roads may have involved interactions where Hindu practices influenced cultural exchanges, potentially impacting regions like Indonesia.</data>
      <data key="d8">interactions_in_silk_roads</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-b8ad3f8265df80ff0cbd95a7914a6123</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211083</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&lt;historical_concept_or_event&gt;&lt;Silk_Roads&gt;" target="&lt;trade_routes&gt;">
      <data key="d6">16.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The trade routes operated by Aksum, including those through Adulis and along the Red Sea, were integral parts of the broader Silk Roads network, facilitating not only goods but also cultural exchanges.</data>
      <data key="d8">Trade_route_in_silk_roads</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-b8ad3f8265df80ff0cbd95a7914a6123</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211082</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&lt;trade_routes&gt;" target="&lt;geographical_location&gt;&lt;Red_Sea_region&gt;">
      <data key="d6">16.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Trade routes operating in the Red Sea region, such as those through Adulis, were vital components of broader maritime trade networks and cultural interactions.</data>
      <data key="d8">red_sea_trade_routes_network</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-b8ad3f8265df80ff0cbd95a7914a6123</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211083</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&lt;Arab elites'&gt;" target="&lt;Baghdad's transition of power base&gt;">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The shift from Baghdad to Samarra signified changing social and political dynamics; Arab elites had to adapt to new administrative centers. This move reflected their continued relevance in Abbasid court culture.</data>
      <data key="d8">adaptation</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-a51f56123d8110f2ea915388f363a913</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211086</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&lt;Arab elites'&gt;" target="&lt;philosophers like al-Farabi and Ibn Sina'&gt;">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Philosophical works from this period indicate the deep involvement of Arab elites in supporting intellectual figures, signifying a sustained interest and cooperation between aristocratic circles and scholars.</data>
      <data key="d8">scholar-elite collaboration</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-a51f56123d8110f2ea915388f363a913</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211088</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&lt;Arab elites'&gt;" target="&lt;translation into Arabic'&gt;">
      <data key="d6">6.0</data>
      <data key="d7">During the period in question, Arab elites played significant roles in supporting translation projects, demonstrating a commitment to preserving and enhancing knowledge through linguistic and intellectual efforts with official backing.</data>
      <data key="d8">patronage and support</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-a51f56123d8110f2ea915388f363a913</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211088</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&lt;Baghdad's capital status'&gt;" target="&lt;translation into Arabic'&gt;">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">Even as Baghdad lost its capital status, translation work continued. This indicates that the Abbasid leadership maintained a commitment to cultural and scholarly endeavors through various means, ensuring continuity of such activities.</data>
      <data key="d8">policy of patronage</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-a51f56123d8110f2ea915388f363a913</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211086</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&lt;Byzantine rivals'&gt;" target="&lt;philosophers like al-Farabi and Ibn Sina'&gt;">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">These philosophers engaged in direct or indirect responses to Byzantine scholars, developing their ideas to counteract foreign influences. Their works show a clear intent to differentiate Islamic philosophy from Greco-Roman traditions.</data>
      <data key="d8">intellectual dialogue</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-a51f56123d8110f2ea915388f363a913</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211088</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="(&quot;Fatimids&quot;, &quot;Abbasid Caliphate&quot;)" target="(&quot;challenge&quot;, &quot;conflict&quot;)">
      <data key="d6">10.0</data>
      <data key="d7">"The Fatimids and Abbasids were often at odds, with the former viewing the latter as legitimate rulers of the Islamic world. Their rivalry manifested in political and military conflicts.")</data>
      <data key="d8">conflict</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-9cea715d402110c49c739e324bc54567</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211091</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="(&quot;challenge&quot;, &quot;conflict&quot;)" target="(&quot;Abbasid Caliphate&quot;, &quot;Byzantine Empire&quot;)">
      <data key="d6">8.0</data>
      <data key="d7">"The Abbasids and Byzantines were often in confrontation over territories and doctrinal disputes. Their conflicts are evident in historical accounts of wars, political alliances, and the sack of Constantinople.")</data>
      <data key="d8">conflict</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-9cea715d402110c49c739e324bc54567</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
