      <data key="d6">2.0</data>
      <data key="d7">All three entities influenced the political and religious landscape of regions ruled by descendants of Genghis Khan, representing various aspects of Mongol Empire fragmentation.</data>
      <data key="d8">wider_geographical_impact</data>
      <data key="d9">chunk-c71a838af77fbaa35a3faa2b0287f52d</data>
      <data key="d10">dumps/World_History_Volume_1/pages_content.json</data>
      <data key="d11">1783211093</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="&quot;Golden Horde's separate religious trajectory&quot;" target="&quot;Sufism as a factor in Yuan Dynasty culture&quot;">
      <data key="d6">2.0</data>
      <data key="d7">While Sufism had an influence under the Yuan, it developed differently in the Golden Horde, indicating regional differences within a larger Mongol framework.</data>
